window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"gil-scott-heron-winter-in-america":["Nine songs and pieces, intimate and sparse, built around Brian Jackson's piano, electric piano and flute, with Scott-Heron's voice and sometimes his own piano. Peace Go with You, Brother is a slow, gospel-tinged farewell; Rivers of My Fathers is a meditation on ancestry; The Bottle, about alcoholism, is an upbeat, flute-led groove; H2Ogate Blues is a long, spoken satire of the Watergate scandal over a blues accompaniment.","Credited to Scott-Heron and Brian Jackson, this was recorded in 1973 near Washington, D.C., and released in 1974 on Strata-East, an independent, musician-run jazz label. It came out as Watergate was unfolding and as American cities were struggling with poverty and unrest. The title captured a mood of disillusion after the 1960s.","Listen to Brian Jackson's Fender Rhodes electric piano: its warm, bell-like tone defines the album's sound and became a signature of 1970s jazz-soul. The Bottle became an underground club hit, spreading through DJs rather than radio. H2Ogate Blues includes a spoken introduction about the blues as a response to crisis.","It was released on a small label but became an underground success. Critics now regard it as one of Scott-Heron's finest albums, and it is often cited as a key influence on neo-soul and conscious hip-hop. Some find H2Ogate Blues too long; others hear it as a vital political document.","Start with The Bottle for its groove, then hear Rivers of My Fathers and Peace Go with You, Brother. Listen for the electric piano. Compare with Stevie Wonder's Innervisions, from the year before, for another politically charged soul album. Then hear the whole album as one long, late-night set."],"joanna-newsom-the-milk-eyed-mender":["Twelve songs built almost entirely from one woman and a concert harp. Joanna Newsom plucks interlocking patterns in both hands, the left hand often walking a bass line while the right spins figures above it, and over this she sings in a high, cracked, wildly elastic voice that swoops, yelps and drawls. Peach Plum Pear swaps the harp for a clattering harpsichord; Sprout and the Bean is a light, skipping tune; Sadie is slow and grave. The sound is bare, close and unmistakable from the first phrase.","Newsom grew up in Nevada City, California, studied the harp from childhood, and briefly studied composition at Mills College before leaving to make her own songs. Early home recordings reached the Chicago label Drag City, which released this debut in March 2004, produced with her friend Noah Georgeson. It arrived amid a scene that the press loosely called freak folk, alongside Devendra Banhart, and her voice made her instantly famous and instantly divisive in indie-rock circles that had never heard a harp front a band.","Listen to the harp lines as counterpoint rather than accompaniment: her training is in the Celtic and classical harp, and the left hand often moves independently of the right in the way a Baroque keyboard player's would. Several songs, including Three Little Babes, borrow shapes from old Anglo-American ballads, and the record ends with En Gallop, the earliest of the songs, which already holds the rhythmic restlessness of everything to come.","Few debuts split listeners so cleanly. Many critics praised the originality of the writing and the harp playing; just as many reviewers wrote mainly about the voice, which some heard as childlike affectation and others as fearless. Over time the argument has settled in Newsom's favour, and the album is now heard as the seed of the large-scale work that followed rather than as a curiosity of the freak-folk moment.","Give the voice two or three songs before deciding; it grows familiar quickly and then becomes the point. Start with Bridges and Balloons, then Sadie for the slow, serious side, then Peach Plum Pear for the harpsichord. Follow the harp's lowest line through a whole song. Afterwards compare Alan Stivell's Celtic harp records and Kate Bush's early albums for other private worlds built on unusual voices."],"joanna-newsom-ys":["Five songs running between seven and seventeen minutes, each a long, through-composed narrative with almost no repeated choruses. Newsom's harp and voice carry everything, while a full orchestra, written by Van Dyke Parks, comments, swells, mutters and occasionally sweeps in like weather. Emily opens at twelve minutes; Monkey & Bear is a fable told in a lurching waltz; Sawdust & Diamonds is harp and voice alone; Only Skin, the longest, adds a second male voice near its end; Cosmia closes in a slow blaze of strings.","Released by Drag City in November 2006 and named after the legendary drowned city of Breton folklore. Newsom wrote the songs after a hard year that included a serious illness, the loss of people close to her and the end of a relationship. She recorded harp and voice with Steve Albini in Chicago, Parks then wrote and recorded orchestral arrangements in Los Angeles around the finished takes, and Jim O'Rourke mixed the result. Bill Callahan, her partner at the time, sings on Only Skin.","Because the orchestra was added after the harp and voice were recorded, the strings and winds are always reacting to Newsom's rubato rather than setting a beat, which is why they seem to breathe with her. Emily is addressed to her younger sister, an astrophysicist, and is full of accurate talk of meteors and meteorites. Only Skin quietly brings back melodic shapes from earlier songs, binding the record together.","Reviews were rapturous and the album appeared near the top of many end-of-year lists; it was widely compared with Joni Mitchell's Hejira and with Van Dyke Parks's own Song Cycle. Some listeners found the length and the density of the language exhausting. Two decades on it is regularly named among the most ambitious records of the century so far and the high point of the whole folk revival of the 2000s.","Set aside an hour, use headphones, and do not shuffle. Begin with Sawdust & Diamonds to hear the songs in their bare state, then go back to Emily and listen for the way the orchestra waits, sometimes for minutes, before it enters. Read the words afterward rather than during. Then compare The Smile Sessions for Parks's orchestral humour and Hejira for long-lined songwriting."],"joanna-newsom-have-one-on-me":["A triple album of eighteen songs lasting just over two hours, spread across three discs that each run like a separate evening. The writing is looser and warmer than on Ys: Newsom alternates harp and piano, her voice is lower and smoother, and the band arrangements by Ryan Francesconi bring in strings, horns, banjo and a drum kit. The title song is an eleven-minute portrait of a nineteenth-century dancer; Good Intentions Paving Company is close to a country-soul song; Baby Birch is long and devastating.","Drag City released the set in February 2010 after nearly four years of silence, during which Newsom had lost her voice for a period to vocal nodules and had to relearn how to sing, which accounts for the changed timbre. She insisted that the triple album be sold at roughly the price of a single record, taking the cost onto her own share, because she wanted listeners to hear the whole thing as one work rather than buying fragments.","The final song, Does Not Suffice, quietly reuses the melody of the opening song, Easy, so the two hours end where they began, but with the meaning turned around. The title track's subject is Lola Montez, the dancer and adventurer whose affair with King Ludwig I of Bavaria helped bring down his reign. Listen for the recurring imagery of birds and of California, which links songs across all three discs.","Most critics welcomed the album warmly, finding it more approachable than Ys and admiring the range of songwriting, and it sold well for a two-hour folk record. A few felt that the length diluted the impact and that a single disc would have been stronger. The album has aged well; many listeners now hold it as her richest and most human record, and Good Intentions Paving Company is probably her most popular song.","Treat it as three albums and hear one disc at a sitting. Start the first disc with Easy and stay through Good Intentions Paving Company, where the drums arrive. Later, come back for Baby Birch, which builds from near silence. Pay attention to the piano songs, a new colour for her. Compare Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark for a piano-driven turn toward warmth, then Laura Marling's Once I Was an Eagle."],"joanna-newsom-divers":["Eleven songs, shorter and more compressed than on her earlier albums, in which harp and piano share the lead with old synthesizers, Mellotron, a marxophone and a clavichord, with arrangements by several hands including Nico Muhly and Dave Longstreth. Sapokanikan bounces on a jaunty piano; Leaving the City surges on drums; the title song is a long, still harp ballad; Waltz of the 101st Lightborne races through a time-travel fantasy. The palette is brighter and stranger than before, but every song turns on loss and time.","Released by Drag City in October 2015, five years after Have One on Me, when Newsom was living in New York and newly married to the actor Andy Samberg. She co-produced the record with longtime collaborator Noah Georgeson and spent years on it. Its preoccupations, mortality, the unequal lengths of human lives, and history buried under city streets, run through Sapokanikan, named for a Lenape settlement that once stood where Greenwich Village is now.","The album is a loop. The last song, Time, as a Symptom, breaks off in the middle of a word, and that unfinished word completes itself when the first song begins again, so on repeat the record never quite ends. Birdsong recurs between songs as a structural thread. Sapokanikan is studded with references to Shelley's Ozymandias and to the forgotten dead of the Manhattan grid.","Critics gave Divers some of the strongest reviews of Newsom's career, praising its concision and the density of ideas packed into each song; it made many year-end lists. Some listeners who loved the orchestral sweep of Ys found the synthesizer colours less to their taste. It has since come to be heard as the most tightly constructed of her albums and, after a long silence that followed, her last for some years.","Begin with Sapokanikan, then the title track, then Leaving the City; afterwards play the whole thing twice in a row to hear the loop close. Listen for the birds in the gaps and for the clavichord, a delicate early keyboard rarely heard in popular music. Compare Kate Bush's Aerial for another domestic, time-haunted album, and Sufjan Stevens's Carrie & Lowell, released the same year, for grief handled very differently."],"iron-and-wine-the-creek-drank-the-cradle":["Eleven songs recorded at home on a four-track machine: a whispered double-tracked voice, a fingerpicked acoustic guitar, a little banjo and slide, and the hiss of the tape under everything. Sam Beam sings so softly he seems to be trying not to wake someone. The songs are short and circular, with Southern imagery of creeks, rooster crows, mothers, Bibles and dirt roads. Lion's Mane opens it, Upward Over the Mountain is the emotional centre, and Bird Stealing Bread is a perfect miniature.","Beam was teaching film and cinematography at a college in Miami and writing songs in his spare time, recording them alone at home. A demo reached Sub Pop, the Seattle label best known for grunge, which released this debut in September 2002 more or less as he had recorded it. The lo-fi sound connected him to the bedroom recordings then popular in indie rock, but the songs reached back to Appalachian and gospel forms and to Nick Drake.","The double-tracked vocal is the secret of the sound: Beam sang most lines twice, and the two slightly mismatched whispers make one ghostly voice. Upward Over the Mountain is written as a son speaking to his mother about leaving and being changed, and it frames the album's quiet family drama. Listen for small domestic noises and tape artefacts left in; the record was never cleaned up for release.","The album was a sleeper success, spread by word of mouth and warm reviews that compared Beam to Nick Drake and Elliott Smith. Some critics found the uniform hush monotonous over forty minutes. It became a template for a decade of bearded, home-recorded folk, and is now regarded as one of the founding records of 2000s indie folk alongside the early Sufjan Stevens and Bon Iver albums.","Play it quietly, late at night; it is designed for low volume. Start with Upward Over the Mountain and Bird Stealing Bread, then listen to the whole record. Try to separate the two voices in the double-tracking. Then hear Nick Drake's Pink Moon for the closest ancestor, Elliott Smith's self-titled album for another whispering voice, and Bon Iver's For Emma, Forever Ago for what followed."],"iron-and-wine-our-endless-numbered-days":["Twelve songs that keep the hush of the debut but trade the four-track hiss for a clean studio sound. Sam Beam's voice is still close and soft, now joined by his sister Sarah's harmonies, and the guitar picking is clearer and more intricate, ringed with banjo, slide, piano and brushed drums. Naked as We Came is a two-minute song about love and death that became his best known; Sodom, South Georgia and Passing Afternoon show him writing slow, shapely melodies with a gospel undertow.","Released by Sub Pop in March 2004, this was Beam's first album made in a proper studio, produced by Brian Deck in Chicago with a small band. The same year Beam's whispered cover of the Postal Service's electronic song Such Great Heights, issued on a single, was used in the film Garden State and in advertising, bringing him a far wider audience than most folk singers of the period could expect.","Many songs are about the passage of years in a single household, and the album's title captures that tension: days that feel endless but are counted. Passing Afternoon, which closes the record, is built from a handful of repeating images of seasons and children. Listen also for the slide guitar, used sparingly as a second voice, and for how often the songs fade before their final cadence rather than resolving it.","Reviews were very warm, with most critics hearing a clear step forward in craft while keeping the intimacy. A minority missed the mystery of the tape hiss. Naked as We Came became a fixture at weddings and funerals alike, and the record is now often named as the most perfectly realised of his early, quiet period, before the larger arrangements that came next.","Start with Naked as We Came, then Passing Afternoon, then play the album through. Notice how the second voice in the harmonies is Sarah Beam's rather than his own double-tracking. Compare with The Creek Drank the Cradle to hear what a studio adds, and with Simon and Garfunkel-style close harmony. Then try Fleet Foxes' first album, which absorbed a lot from this record."],"iron-and-wine-the-shepherds-dog":["Twelve songs in which the whispering folk singer becomes a bandleader. Layered percussion, shakers, hand drums, organ, piano and electric guitar weave dense, rolling textures under Sam Beam's still-soft voice. Pagan Angel and a Borrowed Car opens on a loping groove; Boy with a Coin rides hand claps and a looped rhythm; House by the Sea has a nearly African guitar feel; Flightless Bird, American Mouth closes the album slowly on a waltz that later became his most famous song.","Sub Pop released it in September 2007, again produced by Brian Deck. Beam had toured and recorded with the Tucson band Calexico on the 2005 EP In the Reins, which opened his ears to fuller arrangements, and he spoke of the album as shaped by the confusion he felt about American politics after the 2004 election. Flightless Bird, American Mouth reached a mass audience in 2008 through the soundtrack of the film Twilight.","The songs are full of people caught between faith and doubt, and between belonging and exile. Listen for how the dense percussion is often recorded close and dry while the voice floats far back in reverb, inverting the old balance. Many of the sound loops were built in the studio from small hand-played fragments.","Critics were largely enthusiastic, many calling it his most adventurous and best record, and it sold more than his earlier albums. Some early fans lamented the loss of the bare sound. The change of direction is now seen as decisive: it showed that the quiet indie folk of the early 2000s could absorb world-music rhythm and studio colour without losing its centre.","Start with Boy with a Coin to hear the new rhythmic engine, then Flightless Bird, American Mouth for the old tenderness. Listen to the percussion as its own story under the songs. Compare Paul Simon's rhythm-driven later records, then go back to The Creek Drank the Cradle to measure how far the sound travelled in five years. Bon Iver's second album took a similar step."],"laura-marling-i-speak-because-i-can":["Ten songs in which a twenty-year-old's voice has dropped into a grave, assured alto, over fingerpicked and strummed acoustic guitar driven hard by a small band of banjo, upright bass and drums, with strings and brass at the edges. Devil's Spoke opens it with a stomping, minor-key urgency; Rambling Man is a resolute anthem; Goodbye England (Covered in Snow) is tender and pastoral; Alpha Shallows and the title song close darkly. The sound is closer to English folk-rock than to London indie.","Marling grew up in Hampshire, left home young for London, and was at the centre of the West London nu-folk scene alongside Mumford & Sons and Noah and the Whale. Released by Virgin in March 2010 and produced by Ethan Johns, this second album was recorded largely live, with members of Mumford & Sons in the band. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and the following year she won the Brit Award for best British female solo artist.","Listen to how the songs are written about women's duties, guilt and the weight of being responsible for others, rather than simple romance. Many guitar parts are in open tunings that let low strings drone beneath the melody, a habit inherited from Bert Jansch and Nick Drake. Ethan Johns kept the room sound, including performers' breaths.","Critics greeted the album as a leap from her charming debut, praising the maturity of the writing and the deep new voice; it topped a few British year-end lists and the Mercury nomination confirmed it. A few heard the folk-revival trappings of her scene as fashionable. It now reads as the start of the run of records that made her one of the most respected British songwriters of her generation.","Start with Devil's Spoke and Rambling Man for drive, then Goodbye England for the gentle side. Listen to how the bass and drums push behind the beat. Compare Sandy Denny's solo records and Joni Mitchell's Blue for women writing seriously over acoustic guitar, and Bert Jansch for the tunings. Ethan Johns's natural production rewards a good stereo."],"laura-marling-once-i-was-an-eagle":["Sixteen songs over an hour, opening with a run of four songs that flow into each other without a break for some sixteen minutes, all in the same open guitar tuning and darkening as they go. Marling's voice and guitar dominate, shadowed by a cello and by hand drums that pulse rather than keep time. An instrumental Interlude splits the album in two; the second half, with songs like Master Hunter and Where Can I Go?, is lighter, more varied and freer in mood.","Released by Virgin in May 2013 and again produced by Ethan Johns, who also plays drums and percussion, the album was recorded quickly, largely live, with Marling playing guitar and singing simultaneously. She had recently ended a relationship and moved toward a period of travel and relocation to Los Angeles. It was her third Mercury Prize nomination and was widely hailed as her most ambitious work.","The long opening suite is held together by a single guitar tuning and a recurring melodic shape, so that the four songs work almost as movements. Listen for Indian and Middle Eastern inflections in the guitar and the hand drums, which recall the raga-touched guitar of the 1960s British folk baroque. The songs track a woman refusing to be cast as victim, ending far from where they begin.","Reviews were outstanding, with several British critics calling it the album of the year and comparing its scope to Joni Mitchell's Hejira. Some found its length and uniformity of mood demanding. It is now often regarded as Marling's central work and one of the best British folk albums of the century, though listeners split over whether it surpasses her later, sparer records.","Play the first four songs straight through without interruption; that is the heart of the album. Notice the cello acting as a second voice, then wait for Interlude and hear how the mood lifts. Compare Joni Mitchell's Hejira and Joanna Newsom's Have One on Me for long-form songwriting, and Bert Jansch's open-tuned guitar for the playing tradition behind it."],"laura-marling-song-for-our-daughter":["Ten songs addressed, broadly, to a daughter Marling did not yet have, about what a young woman should know about love, power and self-respect. The sound is warm and unhurried: fingerpicked guitar and light piano, softly layered harmony vocals, a string section arranged with gentle restraint, and a band that sounds like it is in the room. Alexandra opens brightly; Held Down is a quiet anthem; the title song and For You end the record in near-lullaby simplicity.","Marling brought the release forward to April 2020 because of the first wave of the pandemic, issuing it on Chrysalis and Partisan with little warning. She co-produced it with Ethan Johns and engineer Dom Monks. It was nominated for the Mercury Prize and for the Grammy Award for Best Folk Album, and led to a series of livestreamed concerts during lockdown; Marling later had a daughter of her own.","Alexandra is an answer song to Leonard Cohen's Alexandra Leaving, reimagining the woman in Cohen's song as someone with her own reasons rather than a figure of a man's loss. The string arrangements consciously echo the orchestrations of late-1960s and early-1970s singer-songwriter records. Listen to how the harmony vocals are multitracked Marling herself, building a small choir of one voice.","Critics welcomed it as her warmest and most direct album, and it featured in many 2020 year-end lists; its lockdown timing gave its calm an unexpected resonance. A few longtime admirers missed the darker edge of her earlier work. It is now generally ranked among her finest records and is often the album new listeners are pointed to first.","Start with Alexandra, then go to Leonard Cohen's Alexandra Leaving to hear the conversation. Then listen to Held Down and the title track and notice how little the strings do and how much it matters. Compare Joni Mitchell's Ladies of the Canyon and Nick Drake's Bryter Layter for the string-laced folk tradition the album consciously joins."],"rhiannon-giddens-tomorrow-is-my-turn":["Eleven songs, nearly all associated with women singers across a century of American and European music: Dolly Parton, Patsy Cline, Odetta, Nina Simone, Elizabeth Cotten, Sister Rosetta Tharpe, the blues singer Geeshie Wiley and others, plus one original. Giddens's operatically trained voice moves from country tears to gospel shouting to a cabaret whisper. The band, assembled by T Bone Burnett, is spacious and woody, with fiddle, banjo and an organ. Waterboy surges; Last Kind Words is haunted; the title song is French chanson filtered through Simone.","Giddens had trained in opera at Oberlin Conservatory before co-founding the Carolina Chocolate Drops, the Grammy-winning string band that restored Black musicians to the story of old-time music. A showstopping appearance at a 2013 New York concert celebrating the film Inside Llewyn Davis led T Bone Burnett to produce this, her first solo album, released by Nonesuch in February 2015.","Each choice is an argument: the album is a quiet history of women, Black and white, who carried American music, and of the way songs moved between gospel, country, blues and French cabaret. Last Kind Words comes from a 1930 recording by Geeshie Wiley, a singer about whom almost nothing is known. Angel City, the original, is her only song on the record and points toward the writer she would become.","Reviews were excellent, singling out the range and control of the voice, and the album launched her solo career. Some critics wished for more of her own writing and found the covers too reverent. It now reads as a manifesto for the career that followed, which brought her a MacArthur Fellowship in 2017 and later a Pulitzer Prize for the opera Omar, written with Michael Abels.","Start with Waterboy and then go back to Odetta's version for comparison; then hear Last Kind Words. Listen to how Giddens changes vocal colour completely from song to song. Pair it with Nina Simone's Pastel Blues and Elizabeth Cotten's Folksongs and Instrumentals with Guitar, both direct sources, and then the Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig."],"rhiannon-giddens-freedom-highway":["Twelve songs, mostly originals, that move through the history of Black Americans from slavery to police violence. Giddens plays minstrel-era banjo and fiddle and sings with rough-edged power; the band adds blues guitar, brass, gospel choir harmonies and, on one song, a rap verse. At the Purchaser's Option opens over a hypnotic banjo; Julie is a dialogue between an enslaved woman and her mistress; Birmingham Sunday is a sorrowful cover; the title song, from the Staple Singers, closes in jubilation.","Released by Nonesuch in February 2017 and co-produced by Giddens and the multi-instrumentalist Dirk Powell, largely in Powell's home studio in Breaux Bridge, Louisiana. Giddens had been reading slave narratives and collections of advertisements for enslaved people, and the record appeared during the national argument over police killings and the Black Lives Matter movement, which it addresses directly in one song.","At the Purchaser's Option was prompted by a real nineteenth-century advertisement offering an enslaved young woman for sale with her nine-month-old baby included at the buyer's choice. Birmingham Sunday, written by Richard Fariña, remembers the 1963 church bombing. Listen to the banjo itself: Giddens plays a gourd or fretless style that recalls the instrument's African ancestry, a history she has spent her career reasserting.","Critics welcomed it as her most ambitious and personal record, praising the songwriting and the courage of its subjects; some found the stylistic shifts, especially the hip-hop track, uneven. It sealed her reputation as a musician-historian, and it remains a key album in the conversation about Black roots music and who owns the banjo and the fiddle.","Start with At the Purchaser's Option and listen to the banjo pattern under the voice; then hear Julie as a short play for two characters. End with the title song and then play the Staple Singers' original. Pair with Nina Simone's protest songs and with Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig. Read the liner notes, which give each song's history."],"rhiannon-giddens-there-is-no-other":["A duo record with the Italian multi-instrumentalist Francesco Turrisi, who plays frame drums, accordion, piano and early-music instruments. Twelve songs drawn from many places: American spirituals and ballads, a southern Italian tarantella-style dance, a Gian Carlo Menotti aria, an Oscar Brown Jr. song and originals. The sound is bare, physical and drum-led. Ten Thousand Voices opens with a pulse of frame drum; Wayfaring Stranger is slowed to a trance; Pizzica di San Vito whirls; Black Swan is sung like an old ballad.","Giddens had moved to Ireland, and she and Turrisi recorded in Dublin in a few days with producer Joe Henry. Nonesuch released it in May 2019. The project grew from their shared conviction that the banjo and the frame drum trace routes from Africa through the Mediterranean and Europe to America, so that there is no pure folk music, only exchange.","The title is the thesis: that what we call one tradition is always another's in disguise. Listen for Turrisi, a musician with roots in early music as well as jazz, switching between frame drum, accordion and piano, quietly connecting older European practice to folk. Black Swan comes from Menotti's 1946 opera The Medium, sung here as if it were an Appalachian ballad, which is precisely the point. Many takes were captured live with both players in one room.","Critics praised the album's daring and intimacy, and it was widely admired as a model of cross-cultural collaboration. Some listeners found it more of a scholarly exercise than an emotional record. It began a lasting partnership; the pair's follow-up, They're Calling Me Home, came in 2021, and the duo has become a central part of Giddens's work.","Start with Ten Thousand Voices for the drum and voice, then Pizzica di San Vito for the Italian dance, then Wayfaring Stranger. Listen to how few instruments there are. Pair with Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances, another case of art music made from shared folk dance, and with Alan Lomax's Columbia World Library, which set out the same idea of connected traditions in the 1950s."],"anais-mitchell-young-man-in-america":["Eleven songs, many sung by Mitchell in the voices of men: sons, fathers, wanderers and a shepherd. Her high, slightly nasal voice is set against a full band of guitars, piano, strings and a deep, rolling rhythm section, with a darker, more cinematic sound than her earlier folk records. The title song is a slow, ominous portrait of a restless young man; Shepherd tells a rural tragedy over a long, patient build; He Did is a quiet elegy-like portrait of her father; Coming Down glows.","Released on Mitchell's own Wilderland label in February 2012 and produced by Todd Sickafoose, it was her first album after the concept record Hadestown of 2010, the folk opera that would years later become a Tony-winning Broadway musical. Mitchell grew up on a sheep farm in Vermont, and the album draws on that world and on her father's writing, using the stage-trained skill of singing through characters she had developed on Hadestown.","Shepherd is a retelling of a story from her father Don Mitchell's novel The Souls of Lambs, about a farmer whose wife dies in childbirth while he works in the fields. Listen for the way recurring images of land, inheritance and debt link the songs into a loose cycle about American manhood. The album closes on songs of forgiveness that soften its darker portraits.","Reviews were warm and often admiring, calling it her most fully realised album and noting the confident, filmic production. Some reviewers missed the intimacy of her earlier acoustic work, and the Hadestown project overshadowed it in public memory. Folk listeners still hold it as her finest solo record and the bridge between her songwriting and her theatre writing.","Start with the title song and then Shepherd; follow the story in Shepherd as you would a short film. Notice how often she sings as a man and how her voice changes to do it. Then hear the original Hadestown album for comparison, and Gillian Welch's The Harrow & the Harvest for another meditation on rural American life."],"anais-mitchell-anais-mitchell":["Nine songs of reflective, quietly autobiographical folk-pop, gently produced and warmly sung. Mitchell's voice is clearer and calmer than before, set against fingerpicked guitar, piano, soft drums and a pale wash of keyboards. Brooklyn Bridge opens with a song about nostalgia for an earlier New York life; Bright Star is a gentle love song; Little Big Girl looks at a young woman's ambition; Revenant and Real Life probe memory and ageing; Now You Know closes in a radiant, simple tune.","Released by BMG in January 2022, produced by her Bonny Light Horseman bandmate Josh Kaufman, and recorded with musicians from the Hudson Valley scene, the album was her first collection of new original songs in roughly a decade. In that time Hadestown had grown from a folk album into a Broadway musical that won eight Tony Awards in 2019; Mitchell then moved back toward Vermont during the pandemic and wrote these songs looking back on her years in New York.","Listen to the economy of the arrangements: after years writing for a full Broadway pit, Mitchell and Kaufman deliberately limited each song to a few textures, often a single guitar figure. Several songs, including Brooklyn Bridge and On Your Way (Felix Song), name real places and people from her past. The self-title signals a reset, an album of herself after years of writing in characters' voices.","Critics welcomed the album as graceful and generous, some finding it her most personal record and others thinking it understated after the scale of Hadestown. It placed on some year-end folk lists. It is generally heard as a satisfying coda to the Broadway years and a companion to the more traditional songs of Bonny Light Horseman.","Begin with Brooklyn Bridge, then Bright Star and Now You Know. Listen to the warmth of the production and the restraint of the band. Pair it with Joni Mitchell's Ladies of the Canyon for the comfortable, domestic folk-pop sound, and with Bonny Light Horseman's debut to hear the same players on older material."],"sam-amidon-all-is-well":["Traditional Appalachian ballads, hymns and children's songs, sung in a flat, unhurried, conversational voice over banjo or guitar, then wrapped in chamber arrangements of strings, brass and winds by the composer Nico Muhly. The old songs, including Saro and Wild Bill Jones, are slowed and pulled apart so the arrangements can drift around them like mist. The effect is half folk record and half contemporary chamber music, with long, still introductions and sudden swells.","Amidon grew up in Brattleboro, Vermont, the son of folk musicians who led community singing and taught traditional song, and he played fiddle from childhood. He recorded the album in Reykjavík for Valgeir Sigurðsson's label Bedroom Community, which also released music by Muhly and Ben Frost. It appeared in 2008 and placed him in an unlikely circle where American folk song met New York and Icelandic contemporary composition.","Muhly's arrangements often take a single phrase from the tune and turn it into a repeating pattern that keeps going after the voice stops, so minimalist technique emerges from the song itself. Amidon learned many of these songs from field recordings and from his parents rather than from records. Listen for the different speed of the singer and the ensemble: he often sings slightly behind the arranged pulse, keeping the old songs loose.","Reviews in indie and folk circles were warm, with critics admiring the strangeness of the pairing and the honesty of Amidon's singing. Some traditionalists found the arrangements too fussy; some art-music listeners found the voice too plain. The record is now valued as a pioneering meeting of folk song and contemporary composition, and as the start of Amidon's long reinvention of the American songbook.","Start with Saro and hear how long the ensemble takes to settle before the voice arrives. Listen to the arrangements as a separate piece of music. Compare Harry Smith's Anthology of American Folk Music for the kind of sources Amidon draws on, and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for a similar stillness. Then hear I See the Sign for the next step."],"sam-amidon-i-see-the-sign":["Old ballads, shape-note hymns, a spiritual and one pop cover, recast with livelier arrangements than on All Is Well. Amidon's flat, unfussy voice and banjo are joined by Nico Muhly's strings and brass, a rhythm section, and duets with the English singer Beth Orton. The title song is a jittery revival chant; Way Go Lily has a nursery-rhyme bounce; Relief, an R. Kelly song, is quietly turned into folk lament; Kedron is a shape-note hymn made eerie by its open harmonies.","Released by Bedroom Community in 2010 and recorded in Reykjavík with Valgeir Sigurðsson producing, it was the follow-up to All Is Well and widened the circle of collaborators. Muhly had meanwhile become one of the busiest young composers in New York. The record joined a growing current of folk musicians, from the Punch Brothers to Rhiannon Giddens, who treated American traditional music as a living repertoire rather than a museum.","Kedron comes from the Sacred Harp shape-note tradition, sung in rural Southern churches since the nineteenth century, and its bare fourths and fifths give it a medieval sound that Muhly's arrangement brings out. The R. Kelly cover is a deliberate test: by singing a contemporary R&B song with folk plainness, Amidon shows that the old tradition and pop belong to one continuum of song.","Critics liked the album's warmth and variety and many named it one of the most original folk records of 2010; some still found Amidon's deadpan delivery hard to love. It confirmed him as an influential, if cult, figure whose approach to traditional song inspired later experimental folk musicians, and is often the best introduction to his work.","Begin with the title track for energy, then Kedron for the shape-note eeriness, then Relief to hear an R&B song as an old ballad. Listen to Beth Orton's harmony on the duets. Compare Shirley Collins's Anthems in Eden, another bold dressing of old songs, and Sufjan Stevens's Illinois for related arrangements."],"the-weather-station-ignorance":["Ten songs in which Tamara Lindeman's soft, breathy, conversational voice floats over a band that pulses rather than strums: two drummers, a running bass, rippling piano and electric guitar, with flute, saxophone and strings drifting in and out. Robber opens with a nervous, propulsive groove; Atlantic is about watching the sea while thinking of catastrophe; Tried to Tell You is close to a pop song; Parking Lot and Heart are slower and more fragile. The sound recalls soft rock and jazz-inflected art pop more than acoustic folk.","Lindeman, a Toronto songwriter and former actor, had made four quieter folk albums as the Weather Station before this one, released by Fat Possum in February 2021 and co-produced with Marcus Paquin. She had become involved in climate activism, and the album was written out of grief and fear about the climate crisis and the struggle to feel and speak about it. It arrived during the pandemic's second winter and became her breakthrough.","The double drumming is the engine: two players lock into a light, steady grid, so the songs move like a nervous heartbeat beneath the calm vocal. Lindeman deliberately set personal, anxious lyrics against warm, almost seductive arrangements to mirror the way climate dread sits under ordinary life. Listen to the flute and saxophone as the record's weather, entering and receding without solos.","Critics greeted it as a major leap and among the best albums of 2021; it was shortlisted for the Polaris Music Prize in Canada. Some folk listeners missed the acoustic intimacy of her earlier work, and some found the climate theme stated too gently. It is now heard as one of the defining Canadian albums of its decade.","Begin with Robber and listen to the drums, then Atlantic. Notice how the voice never pushes, even when the band builds. Compare Joni Mitchell's The Hissing of Summer Lawns for jazzy, wide-eyed songwriting, and Radiohead's In Rainbows for anxious, beautiful grooves. Then go back to her earlier folk albums."],"lankum-the-livelong-day":["Traditional Irish and English songs and one or two originals, slowed to a crawl and stacked on enormous drones from uilleann pipes, harmonium, concertina and bowed strings, so that the music sounds closer to doom metal or drone music than to the pub session. Radie Peat's unadorned voice leads the slow ballads; the brothers Ian and Daragh Lynch take others. The Wild Rover, usually a drinking song, becomes a menacing dirge; Katie Cruel is stark; Hunting the Wren ends in a long, swirling instrumental.","Lankum, a Dublin quartet originally called Lynched, grew out of the city's squat and punk scenes as much as from traditional music. Produced by John Murphy and released on Rough Trade in October 2019, this third album was recorded in Dublin and drew wide acclaim in Ireland and Britain. It came as a young generation of Irish musicians, including Lisa O'Neill and Ye Vagabonds, were reclaiming traditional song in an Ireland transformed by boom and bust.","Listen to the drones themselves: the band layers pipes, harmonium and bowed instruments that are slightly out of tune with one another, so the overtones beat against each other and the sound seems to throb. Murphy recorded instruments in reverberant spaces and with close microphones together, so you hear breath and key clicks inside a vast sound. The Wild Rover, slowed and stripped of its chorus energy, reveals a song about poverty and regret.","Critics in Ireland and Britain hailed it as a radical reinvention of Irish folk, and it topped several lists. Some traditional players found the drones and darkness too heavy-handed. It prepared the ground for the wider breakthrough of False Lankum, and is now heard as a founding document of a darker, experimental Irish revival.","Start with The Wild Rover, ideally loud, and let the drone fill the room. Then listen to Katie Cruel. Compare Planxty's The Well Below the Valley for the classic Irish revival, then Swans' The Seer for the drone side. Notice how silence and decay are part of the music. Headphones help reveal the layers."],"lankum-false-lankum":["Over seventy minutes, traditional ballads and two originals stretched into slow, massive structures, separated by three short instrumental pieces called Fugue. Go Dig My Grave opens with Radie Peat's lone voice before drones, distortion and pounding percussion swallow it; The New York Trader is a ghostly sea ballad; Master Crowley's is a reel that disintegrates; The Turn is the band's own. The ending, The Turn, is huge, slow and cathartic. It is folk music played like experimental noise.","Released on Rough Trade in March 2023 and again produced by John Murphy in Dublin, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, bringing Lankum to international festival stages. The title plays on the Child ballad False Lankin, a song of murder and revenge, and on the band's own name. It appeared as Irish traditional music was attracting audiences who also followed doom, drone and ambient music.","The three Fugues are short studies in texture and processed sound rather than tunes, giving the ear somewhere to rest between the ballads. Listen to the long tails of reverb and decay, which Murphy treats as part of the arrangement rather than as ambience. Go Dig My Grave uses a ballad that also exists in American versions, linking Dublin to Appalachia across the Atlantic.","Reviews were rapturous, many calling it the year's best album in any genre; it reached audiences far beyond folk. A few listeners found the length and density daunting, and purists questioned how much tradition remained. It is now considered a landmark of twenty-first-century Irish music and of experimental folk anywhere.","Start with Go Dig My Grave and give it all of its length; the crescendo is the point. Then The New York Trader. Play loud or on good headphones. Compare Planxty's Cold Blow and the Rainy Night, a classic revival, with this, and Sunn O)))'s Monoliths and Dimensions for a cousin in drone. Then go back to The Livelong Day."],"sarah-jarosz-undercurrent":["Eleven songs, sparely arranged around Sarah Jarosz's clear alto and her guitar, octave mandolin or clawhammer banjo, with only bass and occasional percussion or a second voice behind her. The songs are written with folk economy and bluegrass precision, but the feel is intimate and reflective rather than virtuosic. House of Mercy rolls on a mandolin pattern; Green Lights is airy; Comin' Undone and Jacqueline are restless; Lost Dog closes in near silence. Nothing is busy; every note sounds chosen.","Jarosz grew up in Wimberley, Texas, a mandolin prodigy in the Austin bluegrass scene, signed to Sugar Hill while still in high school, and studied at the New England Conservatory. Released by Sugar Hill in June 2016 and produced with Gary Paczosa, this fourth album was her first after moving to New York and her first written largely alone. It won the Grammy Awards for Best Folk Album and, for House of Mercy, Best American Roots Performance.","Listen to the octave mandolin: it is tuned an octave below a standard mandolin, so it gives Jarosz a low, ringing voice somewhere between guitar and bouzouki. Several songs were co-written with friends from the Nashville and New York acoustic scenes but deliberately recorded with few players, the opposite of her more crowded early records. Her earlier album covered Joanna Newsom's Book of Right-On, a hint of her taste.","Critics praised the album's confident simplicity and her growth as a writer, and the Grammy wins brought her a larger audience. Some found it subdued compared with her bluegrass beginnings. It is now generally heard as the moment she became a songwriter first and an instrumentalist second, leading toward her trio I'm With Her and the Grammy-winning World on the Ground.","Start with House of Mercy and listen to the instrument pattern under the voice, then Green Lights and Lost Dog. Notice how much space there is. Compare Alison Krauss's So Long So Wrong for an earlier generation's precision, and Gillian Welch's Revival for duo spareness. Punch Brothers' records show where the instrumental side of her world goes."],"punch-brothers-punch":["The centrepiece is The Blind Leaving the Blind, a four-movement suite of about forty minutes for bluegrass quintet: mandolin, fiddle, banjo, guitar and bass, with Chris Thile singing lead. It moves through slow introductions, fast contrapuntal passages where each instrument has its own line, song sections and long instrumental developments, much closer to a string quartet than to a bluegrass band. A few shorter songs frame it. The sound is acoustic and dry, every instrument distinct, the playing ferociously exact.","Punch Brothers formed in 2006 around Chris Thile, the mandolin prodigy of Nickel Creek, with Gabe Witcher, Noam Pikelny, Chris Eldridge and Greg Garrison. Thile wrote the suite while coming through a divorce, and Nonesuch released the album in 2008. The band was openly aiming to bring chamber-music thinking into bluegrass, as Thile also recorded Bach on mandolin and later received a MacArthur Fellowship in 2012.","The suite uses recurring motifs: a phrase from the first movement returns, transformed, in later movements, the way themes work across a classical symphony. Listeners have long heard Bach's partitas and Bartók's string quartets behind it, and Thile has recorded Bach on mandolin. Listen to the banjo: Pikelny often plays sustained, melodic lines instead of rolling bluegrass patterns, so the instrument behaves more like a harp or a piano.","Critics and musicians were impressed, and the suite was widely discussed as a breakthrough for acoustic music, though some found it more admirable than lovable and wondered whether it was bluegrass at all. It is now seen as a founding document of progressive acoustic chamber music in America, and the launch pad for one of the most technically celebrated bands in any genre.","Hear the suite in one sitting, as you would a chamber piece. Follow one instrument, for example the fiddle, through a movement, then listen again following the banjo. Compare Bach's Violin Partita No. 2 and Bartók's String Quartet No. 4 for the models, and Bill Monroe's Blue Moon of Kentucky to measure how far bluegrass travelled."],"punch-brothers-the-phosphorescent-blues":["An album about how people connect in the age of the smartphone, built on the band's all-acoustic quintet but sounding as layered as an art-rock record. Familiarity opens with a ten-minute, multi-part song that swings from hushed choral harmony to driving rock; Julep and My Oh My are tuneful and wistful; two pieces are arrangements of piano works by Debussy and Scriabin; I Blew It Off is jaunty. Vocal harmonies are lush, close and often wordless, and the playing remains dazzlingly exact.","Released by Nonesuch in January 2015 and produced by T Bone Burnett, who had worked with members of the band on film soundtracks. By now the line-up had settled with Paul Kowert on bass. The band were touring large venues and their ambitions had widened from bluegrass to art rock, with Radiohead's influence openly acknowledged. Burnett's production gave them a warm, enveloping sound.","Passepied is an arrangement of a movement from Debussy's Suite bergamasque, the same suite that includes Clair de lune, for mandolin, fiddle, banjo, guitar and bass, and it shows how naturally impressionist harmony lies on bluegrass instruments. Familiarity's wordless chorale was built from stacked vocal lines in the studio. Listen for the banjo and mandolin playing short repeated cells in the minimalist manner.","Critics praised its ambition and beauty; it reached high on folk and Americana charts and many year-end lists, though some reviewers found its preoccupation with technology dated or heavy-handed. It is now widely heard as the band's most complete album and the clearest statement of what progressive acoustic music could be in the twenty-first century.","Start with Familiarity and stay for its whole ten minutes, then hear Passepied and play Debussy's Clair de lune afterwards for the source. Listen to the vocal harmonies in Julep. Compare Radiohead's In Rainbows for the art-rock side, and Steve Reich's Music for Pieces of Wood for the minimalist patterns. Punch is their earlier, more austere suite."],"jake-blount-spider-tales":["Old-time fiddle tunes, ballads, blues and spirituals, all drawn from the music of Black and Indigenous Americans, played with deep, driving rhythm on fiddle and banjo by Jake Blount and a small circle of guests. His voice is rich and unhurried; the fiddle saws with the insistent drive of a dance hall. Some tracks are stripped to fiddle and voice, others build to full string-band momentum. The playing is traditional in style but the selection makes a pointed, coherent statement.","Blount, a Black and queer banjo player and fiddler who studied ethnomusicology, released this debut on Free Dirt Records in May 2020, weeks before the George Floyd protests. He had spent years researching the forgotten Black roots of old-time music, a scene long presented as mainly white and rural. The title refers to the trickster spider Anansi of West African and Caribbean stories, who survives by wit, a figure for how this music survived erasure.","Each song was chosen from a documented Black or Indigenous source, many from field recordings by players whose names appear only in archives, so the album is also a work of research. Listen to the rhythm of the fiddle bow, which emphasises off-beats in a style that traces back to African-American dance fiddling and through it to West African string traditions. The liner notes give every song's origin.","Reviews were very strong in folk and roots circles and the album appeared on many 2020 year-end lists; critics praised both the playing and the argument. A few noted that the austere sound might limit its reach. It is now considered an important record in the reclamation of Black string-band music, in a line with the Carolina Chocolate Drops and Rhiannon Giddens.","Listen first for the fiddle's rhythm, then the voice. Pick the songs that are only fiddle and voice and hear how little is needed. Pair with the Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig and with Lead Belly's Last Sessions for the older sources. Read the liner notes alongside; they turn the album into a lesson in history."],"jake-blount-the-new-faith":["A concept album of spirituals and old religious songs, imagined as the hymns of survivors in a future ravaged by climate change. Banjo, fiddle and Blount's voice sit alongside drum machines, distorted bass, synthesizers and choral overdubs, with rap verses and guest singers. Some songs stay close to traditional performance; others are rebuilt as stark electronic soundscapes. The effect is of folk music heard through a broken radio from the future, both ancient and brand new.","Released by Smithsonian Folkways in September 2022, the label that holds much of the archive of American traditional music, this was a striking choice: a folk album framed as Afrofuturist speculation. Blount described a story in which displaced Black people in a climate-ravaged future preserve their faith through old spirituals. It came as Black musicians across genres were turning to science fiction to imagine survival.","Many songs were taken from early twentieth-century recordings of Black congregational singing, and Blount kept their call-and-response structure even when the sound is electronic. Listen for field-recording textures and sampled crowd noise used as a choir from the past. The project quietly argues that the spirituals were themselves a kind of future-oriented music, written by people imagining freedom they could not yet see.","Critics praised the concept's daring and the album received strong reviews and folk awards attention; a few traditionalists found the electronic treatment jarring, and some reviewers felt the narrative was hard to follow from the music alone. It is now seen as one of the most adventurous American folk records of its decade.","Start with the most traditional-sounding tracks and then move to the most electronic, listening for the old call and response beneath. Read the story Blount supplies with the record. Compare Sam Amidon's I See the Sign for another experimental take on hymns, and Pete Seeger's We Shall Overcome for spirituals as songs of struggle."],"bonny-light-horseman-bonny-light-horseman":["Ten traditional Anglo-Irish and American folk songs, rewritten and rearranged by a trio: Anaïs Mitchell and Eric D. Johnson share lead vocals and harmonies over Josh Kaufman's guitars and a soft, drum-led band. The title song, a ballad of a woman mourning a soldier lost in the Napoleonic wars, opens slowly and swells; Deep in Love is a lovely duet; The Roving and Magpie's Nest are lighter. The sound is warm, unhurried and close to 1970s folk rock, with hints of Fleet Foxes harmony.","The trio first performed together at the Eaux Claires festival run by Bon Iver's Justin Vernon, and the album was released on Vernon's associated label 37d03d in January 2020, partly recorded during a residency in Berlin. Mitchell had just seen Hadestown open on Broadway, and Johnson led the indie band Fruit Bats. The record was nominated for the Grammy Award for Best Folk Album.","Many of the songs were altered: the band added verses, changed melodies or combined fragments of different versions, so this is a new composition from old material, in the way folk singers have always worked. Listen to the title song's source, a ballad associated with Ireland's long tradition of songs about soldiers who did not return. Mitchell and Johnson's voices are balanced so neither dominates.","Critics welcomed it warmly as a graceful renewal of folk-rock and a fine showcase for Mitchell's voice outside theatre; a few found it too smooth or polite for songs about loss and violence. It has become a staple of the Americana-folk scene, and the band continued with further albums.","Start with the title song, then Deep in Love. Listen to how the two voices trade lines. Compare Fairport Convention's Liege & Lief and Planxty's The Well Below the Valley for the classic folk-rock treatment of old songs, and Fleet Foxes' first album for the harmonies. Then hear Anaïs Mitchell's 2022 solo record for the same players."],"adrianne-lenker-songs-and-instrumentals":["Two albums released together. songs is eleven tracks for voice and acoustic guitar alone, recorded in a wooden cabin so that rain, wind and birds can be heard around the music: Two Reverse, Anything, Zombie Girl, Ingydar and Forwards Beckon Rebound are full of lucid, tender detail. instrumentals is two long pieces, music for indigo and mostly chimes, the first a meandering guitar improvisation, the second built from wind chimes and the room. It is about as close and unadorned as recorded folk can be.","When the pandemic cancelled Big Thief's 2020 tour, Lenker retreated to a one-room cabin in western Massachusetts and recorded with the engineer Philip Weinrobe on analog tape, after the end of a relationship. 4AD released the double album in October 2020. It came after Big Thief's two 2019 albums and became her most celebrated solo work, deepening the mythology of her as a songwriter of rare openness.","Weinrobe recorded to tape with simple equipment and made no attempt to seal out the world beyond the walls. Listen to the outdoors: rain on the roof on some songs, wind chimes and birds on others, so the place is the album's other instrument. Some songs here were later reworked by Big Thief, showing how Lenker's solo writing feeds the band.","Reviews were rapturous, critics calling songs one of the year's best albums and praising its emotional honesty; some found the instrumentals self-indulgent. The album has come to symbolise the hushed, intimate recordings of the pandemic year, and many listeners regard it as the purest expression of Lenker's art.","Listen to songs late at night, quietly, in order. Start with Anything and Zombie Girl and listen for the room around them. Save instrumentals for a long walk or a still hour. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon for solitude and guitar, and Big Thief's U.F.O.F. for how her songs grow in the band. Bon Iver's For Emma, Forever Ago is another cabin record."],"adrianne-lenker-bright-future":["Twelve songs recorded live by Lenker with three friends, Nick Hakim, Mat Davidson and Josefin Runsteen, around a single room: guitars, an upright piano, fiddle and voices. Real House opens with a slow, bare piano and a memory of childhood; Sadness as a Gift is a gentle country song; Free Treasure and Vampire Empire are older songs reworked; Ruined and Donut Seam are tender; Evol plays with language. The playing sounds unrehearsed, with mistakes and laughter left in.","4AD released the album in March 2024. It was recorded at a studio deep in the woods, again with engineer Philip Weinrobe and onto analog tape, the four musicians playing together without headphones. Lenker made it as Big Thief's profile was growing, and the band's bassist Max Oleartchik left soon after; the solo record served as a quieter counterweight to the band's large, celebrated albums.","Vampire Empire had been played live by Big Thief for some time; here it is slowed and softened, so the same song shows two different characters. Listen for the background: the performers react to one another, a piano chair creaks, voices join late. Runsteen's fiddle often doubles the melody slightly ahead or behind, giving an old-time string-band roughness to otherwise intimate songs.","Critics praised its warmth and the communal feel; the album placed on many year-end lists, though some found it less striking than songs and instrumentals. It has been embraced as an affectionate, communal companion to her solitary earlier work, with Real House widely singled out as one of her finest songs.","Start with Real House and listen to how the piano and voice sit in the room. Then hear Sadness as a Gift and Vampire Empire. Notice the humanity of the performance, the breath and stray sounds. Compare songs and instrumentals for the solo version of her voice, and Gillian Welch's Time (The Revelator) for live, close-knit acoustic intimacy."],"nick-mulvey-first-mind":["Songs built on intricate fingerpicked guitar patterns that owe as much to West African and Cuban music as to English folk, sung in a soft, low voice. The guitar often plays repeating cross-rhythms in which melody and accompaniment interlock; light percussion and bass add pulse, and occasional synthesizer colours deepen the sound. Cucurucu is bright and hypnotic; Fever to the Form and Juramidam are warmer and more expansive. The record's calm, rippling surface hides complicated rhythmic design.","Mulvey, from Cambridge, studied ethnomusicology at SOAS in London, spent time in Havana, and was a founding member of the Mercury-nominated Portico Quartet, playing the hang drum. Leaving the group, he became a singer-songwriter, and this debut was released on Fiction Records in May 2014. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize and arrived amid a British folk scene that was looking beyond Anglo-American roots.","Listen for polyrhythm: many of his guitar patterns set three against two or four, a technique he learned from West African kora and guitar traditions, so the songs seem to move at two speeds at once. His years playing the hang drum, a melodic steel percussion instrument, show in the way the guitar patterns sound like bells. Juramidam's title refers to a Brazilian spiritual tradition.","Critics welcomed it warmly, praising the guitar writing and the global influences, and the Mercury nomination widened his audience; some reviewers found the songs too gentle and the lyrics vague. It is now remembered as one of the more musically curious British folk debuts of its decade.","Start with Cucurucu and try to tap the two rhythms in the guitar at once. Then hear Fever to the Form. Compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for interlocking patterns, and Nick Drake's Bryter Layter for English fingerpicking. Then seek out West African guitar records to hear the deeper roots."],"julia-jacklin-crushing":["Ten songs about the aftermath of a breakup and the right to one's own body and space, sung in a clear, steady voice that rarely raises itself. The arrangements are sparse indie-folk: chiming electric guitar, brushed or tight drums, and much empty space. Body opens over a single guitar line; Pressure to Party is the one fast, defiant rocker; Don't Know How to Keep Loving You and Comfort are quietly devastating ballads. The restraint is the force; nothing is overplayed.","Jacklin grew up in the Blue Mountains near Sydney and released her debut in 2016. This second album, released in February 2019 by Polyvinyl and Transgressive and produced by Burke Reid in Sydney, was written after two years of touring and the end of a long relationship. It arrived amid wider conversations about consent and personal boundaries, and several songs speak to them directly but quietly.","Listen to the drums: on several songs they enter only halfway through, changing the meaning of what came before. The vocal is placed very close and dry, as if the singer were standing her ground in the same room as the listener. Comfort, the last song, is sung to a former partner and turns the album's focus from her own hurt to care for someone else.","Reviews were excellent and the album appeared on many year-end lists, especially in Australia and Britain; critics praised its emotional precision. A few found the mood too uniform. It established Jacklin as one of the leading singer-songwriters of her generation, often grouped with Phoebe Bridgers and Big Thief.","Start with Body and Don't Know How to Keep Loving You, then hear Pressure to Party for contrast. Listen to the silences between lines. Compare Phoebe Bridgers's Stranger in the Alps and Big Thief's U.F.O.F. for the shared sound, and Joni Mitchell's Blue for the tradition of plain, unflinching confession."],"wynton-marsalis-black-codes-from-the-underground":["A quintet record built on the acoustic language of the mid-1960s, played with frightening precision. Wynton Marsalis's trumpet leads his brother Branford on tenor and soprano saxophone, with Kenny Kirkland on piano, Charnett Moffett on bass and Jeff \"Tain\" Watts on drums. The tunes, among them the title track, Phryzzinian Man and Chambers of Tain, lean on shifting meters and tempos that speed up and slow down by agreement, so the band seems to stretch and compress time while the soloists keep their footing.","In 1985 jazz on major labels meant fusion, smooth radio sounds or reissues of older acoustic records. Marsalis, a New Orleans prodigy who had come through Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers and was also recording Haydn and Hummel trumpet concertos for Columbia, argued loudly for swing, the blues and acoustic instruments as the core of the music. The title points to the laws passed across the South after the Civil War to restrict the freedom of Black Americans, a sign that he heard the music as history as well as style.","Listen to Watts and Kirkland more than to the horns. The rhythm section is quoting the elastic playing of the Miles Davis quintet of 1965 to 1968, where tempo and pulse are bargained over bar by bar, but Watts adds a heavier, more muscular backbeat of his own. On several tracks the time feel changes under a soloist without warning, and the soloist has to follow it while carrying on with the line.","The album won two Grammy awards in 1986 and made Marsalis the public face of a young-lions revival. Critics split over it then and still do: admirers heard real command and a corrective to commercial fusion, while detractors heard a gifted player re-enacting the Davis quintet rather than moving past it. Many now hear the record as the strongest work of that argument, and Kirkland and Watts as its unsung engine.","Start with the title track and keep your ear on the drums: count the moments the pulse seems to slip and then re-lock. Then play the second Davis quintet's mid-sixties records beside it and listen for what Branford's saxophone does differently from Wayne Shorter's. Any remaster of the original Columbia LP will do; the sound is clean, close and dry."],"wynton-marsalis-blood-on-the-fields":["An oratorio for big band and three singers, nearly three hours long and issued on three discs. It follows two Africans, Jesse and Leona, through capture, the Middle Passage and enslavement toward a hard-won freedom, with Cassandra Wilson as Leona, Miles Griffith as Jesse and Jon Hendricks as Juba, an older man who guides them. The writing moves through work songs, blues, marches, ballads, shout choruses and dense ensemble passages, each section built from a different strand of the Black musical past.","Marsalis wrote it for Jazz at Lincoln Center, the institution he had co-founded in 1987 to give jazz the repertory standing of an orchestra or opera house. It was first performed in 1994 and recorded the following year. In 1997 it became the first jazz composition to win the Pulitzer Prize for Music, a prize that had famously been refused to Duke Ellington in 1965. That history gave the award a weight well beyond this one piece.","Marsalis maps each stage of the story onto a historical idiom: the scenes on the ship and in the field lean on field hollers and call and response, while the later scenes borrow from swing-era ensemble writing and Ellington's sound for reeds. The journey of the characters is therefore also a walk through the history of the music itself, ending in the language of the big band Marsalis was leading.","The Pulitzer brought praise and resentment in equal measure. Supporters heard a serious extended work in the line of Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige; skeptics found it long, uneven and more summary than discovery, with a libretto some thought heavy-handed. It has been revived by the Lincoln Center orchestra but is rarely heard complete, and its reputation now rests as much on its symbolic place as on the score.","Do not try it in one sitting. Take one disc at a time, follow the story in the booklet, and listen for how Cassandra Wilson's dark, unhurried voice changes the temperature whenever she enters. Then play Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige to hear the model Marsalis was answering."],"cassandra-wilson-blue-light-til-dawn":["A quiet, acoustic record that sounds as if it were made on a porch at night. Cassandra Wilson sings low and slow over spare acoustic guitars, hand percussion, violin and the occasional cornet, with no piano and no drum kit swinging in the usual jazz sense. The songs come from everywhere: Robert Johnson's Hellhound on My Trail and Come On in My Kitchen, Joni Mitchell's Black Crow, Van Morrison's Tupelo Honey, Ann Peebles's I Can't Stand the Rain, and a handful of her own pieces.","Wilson had spent the 1980s in Brooklyn among the M-Base circle of Steve Coleman, recording angular, funk-driven records for the German label JMT. Signing to Blue Note in 1993, she worked with the producer Craig Street, who stripped the sound back to wood and strings. The guitarist Brandon Ross and the cornetist Olu Dara were among the players. The result reached listeners far beyond jazz and helped set a template for the roots-minded jazz singing of the next decade.","Listen to how slow everything is. Wilson often sings well behind the beat and lets phrases trail off into silence, so that the songs from the Delta, the 1970s singer-songwriter shelf and Memphis soul all end up sharing one tempo and one mood. The blues numbers are not treated as relics; the guitars play them in open, ringing tunings that point to Mississippi, where Wilson grew up.","Reviewers welcomed it at once, and it sold strongly for a jazz vocal record. Some jazz purists asked whether an album with so little swing and so much pop and blues repertoire belonged in the category at all. That debate now looks dated: the record is widely treated as one of the defining jazz vocal albums of its decade, and as the moment Wilson found her own sound.","Start with Hellhound on My Trail and then play the Robert Johnson original: notice how she keeps the menace but slows it to a crawl. Then try Black Crow beside Joni Mitchell's Hejira. Play the album late and quietly; it rewards a room with the lights down."],"cassandra-wilson-new-moon-daughter":["The second Wilson record with the producer Craig Street, darker and more varied than the first. It opens with Strange Fruit, sung almost without inflection over a bed of low drones, and moves through Son House's Death Letter, Hank Williams's I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry, Neil Young's Harvest Moon, U2's Love Is Blindness and even the Monkees' Last Train to Clarksville, alongside originals such as Solomon Sang. Pedal steel, acoustic guitar and cornet weave around her slow contralto.","After the surprise success of Blue Light 'til Dawn, Blue Note backed Wilson in carrying the approach further, and she treated rock and country songs as material as open to reinterpretation as anything by Gershwin. The guitarist Brandon Ross returned, with the cornetist Graham Haynes among the guests. The album won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Performance and confirmed her as the most widely heard jazz singer of the mid-1990s.","The Monkees song is the giveaway. Wilson slows a bright pop single into a brooding, almost menacing blues, showing how much of a song's meaning lives in tempo and arrangement rather than in the tune. Her Strange Fruit, by contrast, refuses the dramatic build most singers bring to it; the horror is carried by restraint, and by the sour drone that never resolves.","It was praised on release and won its Grammy, though some critics felt the formula of the first record was being repeated and that a few covers were more clever than deep. Over time its Strange Fruit and Death Letter have been singled out as among her finest recorded performances, and the album sits beside its predecessor as a pair.","Begin with Death Letter and then play the Son House recording: one man shouting over a slide guitar against a singer who barely raises her voice. Then hear Strange Fruit beside Billie Holiday's 1939 version and ask which unsettles you more. Listen on good speakers; the low end matters."],"joe-lovano-from-the-soul":["A quartet record on which an unusually broad, breathy tenor saxophone sound meets three very different partners: the French pianist Michel Petrucciani, the bassist Dave Holland and the New Orleans drummer Ed Blackwell. Lovano's tone is the first thing you notice, a big, furry, slightly husky sound that bends pitches and slides between notes, and his lines move freely between straight-ahead swing, open-ended improvising and tender ballad playing.","Lovano, from Cleveland, had spent years as a sideman with Woody Herman, Mel Lewis's big band and the trio of the drummer Paul Motian with Bill Frisell. Signed by Blue Note around 1990 in his late thirties, he became one of the label's flagship players of the decade. Blackwell, a long-time partner of Ornette Coleman, was gravely ill with kidney disease at the time, and this was among his last recordings before his death in 1992.","Listen to Blackwell. His drumming comes out of New Orleans parade rhythms, and he tunes his kit so that each drum sings a distinct pitch; you can follow his solos almost as melodies. Against him Petrucciani plays crisp, bright, classically trained piano, and Holland holds the middle. Lovano's tenor seems to borrow a little from each, which is why the record feels like a conversation rather than a saxophonist with accompaniment.","Critics welcomed it as proof that Lovano was a major voice rather than a well-schooled journeyman, and it helped launch a run of acclaimed Blue Note albums. It is sometimes overshadowed by his later, more ambitious projects, but players often name it as the cleanest portrait of his sound.","Start with any ballad and listen to the edges of Lovano's notes, the air and grain around them. Then play a track with Blackwell at the front and follow his drums as if they were a second horn. Compare with Ornette Coleman's quartet recordings to hear Blackwell in his original home."],"joe-lovano-rush-hour":["Lovano's tenor saxophone set inside chamber-orchestral writing by Gunther Schuller, who arranged and conducted. Some tracks are small and spare, others full ensemble pieces in which strings, woodwinds and brass weave independent lines around the saxophone rather than simply padding beneath it. Judi Silvano adds wordless vocal lines on some pieces. The repertoire mixes jazz-composer tunes with Schuller's own scores, and the overall mood moves between urban bustle and nocturnal calm.","Schuller had coined the phrase third stream in 1957 for music that joined jazz improvisation to classical composition, and by the 1990s he was a senior figure: composer, conductor, horn player, historian and teacher. Pairing him with Lovano was Blue Note's way of reviving that idea for a new generation. Lovano, the son of a Cleveland tenor player, had a lifelong interest in orchestral colour and welcomed the challenge of improvising inside fully written scores.","Listen to how often Schuller writes a counter-melody for a single instrument, a bassoon or a solo violin, that shadows Lovano's line a beat behind. The orchestra is not a cushion; it is a second improviser in disguise, and in places it is hard to tell which notes were written and which were made up on the spot.","Reviews were warm, and many critics named it among the year's best jazz records. Some felt the orchestral settings softened Lovano's edge; others thought it the most convincing third-stream album in decades precisely because neither side gives way. It is now heard as a high point of the 1990s attempts to reconcile jazz and concert music.","Choose a track with full ensemble and follow one orchestral instrument the whole way through rather than the saxophone. Then go back and hear how Lovano reacts to it. Compare Schuller's own third-stream work, or Miles Davis and Gil Evans's Miles Ahead, for an older version of the same marriage."],"dave-holland-extensions":["A quartet record without piano, in which Steve Coleman's alto saxophone and Kevin Eubanks's electric guitar trade lines over Holland's bass and the drums of Marvin \"Smitty\" Smith. The pieces run in long, lopsided cycles of beats and lock into grooves that feel like funk but refuse to settle into four. Holland's bass is the centre of gravity: a fat, woody sound that carries both the harmony and the hook.","Holland, born in Wolverhampton, England, had played in Miles Davis's band around 1968 to 1970 and in the free-improvising group Circle before making his name as a leader on ECM in the 1970s. By the late 1980s he was working with younger New York musicians from the circle around Coleman's M-Base collective, whose interest in odd meters and dense rhythmic cycles fed into his writing. Extensions was recorded at the end of that decade.","Count along to any tune and you will usually lose your place; Holland writes riffs that run in unusual lengths and lets the drums cut across them, so that the groove seems to tilt. Eubanks, later famous as a television bandleader, plays some of his most adventurous guitar here, using a clean, almost acoustic tone that blends with the alto rather than overpowering it.","It was widely praised on release and is often named among the best ECM jazz records of its period. It has sometimes been eclipsed by Holland's 1972 debut, Conference of the Birds, but many listeners prefer this record's tighter rhythmic language, which points ahead to his celebrated quintet of the late 1990s.","Put on the opening track and follow only the bass for a full minute, then switch your attention to the drums. Notice how the two seem to argue and still agree. Then play Holland's Prime Directive to hear where this approach led ten years later."],"dave-holland-prime-directive":["Holland's quintet at full strength: Chris Potter on saxophones, Robin Eubanks on trombone, Steve Nelson on vibraphone and marimba, Billy Kilson on drums, and Holland on bass. Instead of piano, Nelson's shimmering vibraphone fills in the harmony, which gives the group a lighter, ringing sound. The tunes run in odd meters, but the band plays them with such ease that they sound like dance music, building toward long collective climaxes in which saxophone and trombone trade riffs.","Holland formed the quintet in the late 1990s, and over the next several years it became one of the most admired working bands in jazz, winning polls and touring constantly. ECM, a label associated with spacious, reflective music, gave it room to record a series of albums. Potter, then in his late twenties, emerged from this group as one of the leading tenor saxophonists of his generation.","Listen to the backgrounds behind each soloist. Holland writes interlocking riffs for the other horns and the vibraphone that come in partway through a solo, so the music thickens as it goes rather than staying a solo with rhythm section. The meters are often in fives, sevens or longer cycles, but Kilson plays them with a backbeat you can nod to.","Critics greeted it with enthusiasm and the quintet was voted among the best groups of the period in jazz polls. Some found it polished to a fault, a model of ensemble craft rather than of risk. Musicians, however, still study these records as textbooks of how to write odd-meter tunes that groove.","Start with the title track and tap your foot; when you lose count, listen to the bass, which always shows you where the cycle begins. Then follow the vibraphone behind a saxophone solo. Afterwards compare Extensions from 1990 to hear the earlier, guitar-led version of Holland's rhythmic ideas."],"john-zorn-naked-city":["A record that cuts between surf twang, film-noir cool, country picking, lounge jazz and short bursts of hardcore noise, sometimes within seconds. Zorn on alto saxophone leads Bill Frisell on guitar, Wayne Horvitz on keyboards, Fred Frith on bass and Joey Baron on drums, with the Japanese vocalist Yamatsuka Eye screaming on some tracks. Alongside originals, the band plays film and television themes by Ennio Morricone, Henry Mancini and John Barry, and Ornette Coleman's Lonely Woman.","Zorn was the central figure of the New York downtown scene, a loose network of improvisers, rock musicians and composers gathered around Lower Manhattan clubs such as the Knitting Factory. He had been developing game pieces that directed improvisers with cue cards, and he loved cartoon music, Japanese hardcore and spaghetti westerns. The band took its name and its cover image from the crime photographer Weegee's book Naked City.","Listen to the edits. Many tracks change style at a single drum hit, and the band does it live, not by splicing tape; Zorn cues the cuts with hand signals in the manner of his game pieces. The short hardcore numbers, some under a minute, are the record's real experiment: noise played with the discipline of chamber music.","It confused many jazz critics, who heard parody and racket, and delighted others, who heard a new kind of composition built from the clutter of mass media. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now regarded as a landmark of the downtown aesthetic and of genre-collapsing music in general.","Start with the Morricone and Mancini covers to get your bearings, then try a run of the short noise pieces at volume. Hear the band's Lonely Woman beside Ornette Coleman's 1959 original and notice what survives the assault. Afterwards, play anything by the Boredoms to hear where Eye came from."],"john-zorn-masada-alef":["The first album by Masada, a piano-less acoustic quartet: Zorn on alto saxophone, Dave Douglas on trumpet, Greg Cohen on bass and Joey Baron on drums. The tunes are short, tightly written melodies in the scales of Jewish liturgical and klezmer music, with their characteristic augmented intervals, launched into fast, free-swinging improvisation. The sound is lean and urgent: two horns in unison or close harmony, then breaking apart as Baron drives the band forward.","In 1993 Zorn began writing a book of short tunes he called Masada, setting himself the task of creating a body of explicitly Jewish music in the 1990s, partly in response to what he and others in downtown New York called Radical Jewish Culture. The book eventually ran to hundreds of pieces. The quartet's first record was released by the Japanese label DIW, which was releasing many of Zorn's projects at the time.","The instrumentation is a deliberate tribute to Ornette Coleman's quartet of 1959, with Douglas in Don Cherry's role and Baron playing with Billy Higgins's lightness. Listen to how the two horns often end a tune on a long, unresolved interval rather than a cadence. The Jewish modes and the Ornette model fit together surprisingly well because both put melody before chord changes.","Masada was quickly recognised as one of Zorn's most accessible and durable projects, and the quartet recorded prolifically through the decade. Some listeners questioned the identity politics around it; others simply heard great tunes and one of the most exciting small bands of the 1990s. The songbook has since been played by dozens of other groups.","Start with any short tune and listen for the opening melody in unison; then follow how quickly the band unravels it. Play Ornette Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come next to hear the model, and Naftule Brandwein's klezmer clarinet to hear the other root."],"bill-frisell-have-a-little-faith":["A tour of American music played by a quintet of guitar, clarinet, accordion, bass and drums. Frisell takes on Aaron Copland's Billy the Kid, music by Charles Ives and John Philip Sousa, Stephen Foster, Muddy Waters, Sonny Rollins, Bob Dylan's Just Like a Woman, John Hiatt and Madonna's Live to Tell, treating all of it with the same gentle seriousness. His guitar, swelled and echoed by pedals, can sound like a pedal steel or a church organ.","Frisell, raised in Denver and trained on clarinet before guitar, had become a fixture of the New York downtown scene and of John Zorn's projects. With Nonesuch, a label that also issued contemporary classical music, he began to explore Americana in a broad sense. The band here included Don Byron on clarinet, Guy Klucevsek on accordion, Kermit Driscoll on bass and Joey Baron on drums.","Listen to how he treats Copland and Madonna with exactly the same affection, lingering on a melody's turns and letting the band hold chords longer than expected. The accordion and clarinet give the record an old-time, small-town sound, which makes Copland's cowboy ballet feel as if it were being played on a bandstand in a park.","Reviewers greeted it with delight and some puzzlement about its pop selections. It is now seen as the turning point at which Frisell moved from downtown experimentalist to the patient interpreter of American song he became, and as a forerunner of the broader jazz-Americana movement.","Play the Copland sequence first, then the Madonna, and notice how the band treats them as equals. Compare Copland's own orchestral Billy the Kid to hear what Frisell keeps and what he strips away, and listen for how the accordion takes over the strings' role. The original Nonesuch CD sounds warm and natural."],"bill-frisell-nashville":["Frisell's electric guitar among Nashville acoustic players, including the dobro master Jerry Douglas and the bassist Viktor Krauss. Drums are mostly absent, so the music floats on plucked strings: banjo rolls, mandolin chop and the slide of the dobro around Frisell's gentle, reverberant lines. The tunes, mostly his own with a few covers, are short, plain-spoken melodies that sound like old country songs heard in a dream.","After years of working in New York with improvisers, Frisell, who had moved to Seattle, went to Nashville to record with bluegrass and country session musicians who had no interest in jazz conventions. Nonesuch backed the project. It was part of a wider 1990s turn among American jazz musicians toward roots music, and Frisell treated it as a meeting of equals rather than a jazz player borrowing a flavour.","Listen for the moments when no one solos. The players trade small phrases and fills in the manner of a bluegrass jam, but Frisell slows the tempos so far that the fast picking style becomes a shimmering texture. His guitar often enters with the attack removed, the note swelling up from nothing, which makes him sound like a ghost among the acoustic players.","It was named album of the year in at least one major jazz critics' poll, which provoked debate about whether a record with almost no swing or jazz harmony belonged in the category. That argument has faded; it is now seen as a gentle classic and a key text of jazz Americana.","Put it on in the morning and do not wait for solos; listen to how the instruments fit together like a quilt. Then play Bill Monroe or the Stanley Brothers to hear the tradition Frisell is visiting, and Have a Little Faith to hear where the journey began."],"vijay-iyer-historicity":["A piano trio record that sounds less like a cocktail lounge than a machine room. Iyer, with Stephan Crump on bass and Marcus Gilmore on drums, plays tight, repeating figures that lock into grooves of unusual length and gradually shift. The repertoire mixes his own pieces with Stevie Wonder's Big Brother, M.I.A.'s Galang, Leonard Bernstein's Somewhere, Andrew Hill's Smoke Stack and Julius Hemphill's Dogon A.D., all reworked into the trio's dense rhythmic language.","Iyer, born in upstate New York to Indian Tamil parents, studied physics and wrote a doctoral thesis on music cognition and rhythm in the body before committing to music. He had worked with Steve Coleman and in the cooperative trio Fieldwork. This was the first album with this trio, issued by the German label ACT, and it earned a Grammy nomination and topped many year-end lists.","Listen to the hip-hop and pop covers closely: Iyer keeps their bass lines and rhythmic hooks rather than their melodies, turning a Stevie Wonder song or an M.I.A. track into a loop that the trio bends and stretches. Gilmore, grandson of the drummer Roy Haynes, plays the drums like a sampler, repeating tiny patterns with small changes each time.","The album was among the most praised jazz records of 2009 and established Iyer as a leading figure in a new generation. Some traditionalists found the trio too cerebral and short on lyrical melody; admirers heard a fresh approach to the oldest format in modern jazz, drawn from hip-hop and minimalism as much as from bebop.","Start with Galang or Big Brother, and listen to the bass and drums before the piano; notice how the loop slowly mutates. Then play Andrew Hill and Julius Hemphill to hear Iyer's jazz ancestors, and Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians to hear his minimalist cousin."],"vijay-iyer-accelerando":["The second record by Iyer's trio with Stephan Crump and Marcus Gilmore, built around the idea of music for dancing. The covers include Michael Jackson's Human Nature, Flying Lotus's MmmHmm, Henry Threadgill's Little Pocket Size Demons and Herbie Nichols's Wild Flower, alongside originals whose grooves gradually speed up, overlap or slip against each other. The sound is lighter and more springy than on Historicity, with more space between the three players.","Iyer described the album in terms of dance and the body, drawing on his research into how people feel rhythm physically. By 2012 he was a widely honoured figure, and the record swept major jazz critics' polls that year. His choice of material, from a Los Angeles beat producer to an AACM elder, mapped the wide range of what jazz musicians of his generation listened to.","The title is a classical tempo marking meaning getting faster, and several pieces use metric modulation, a technique in which a subdivision of the old beat becomes the new beat, so the music seems to accelerate without anyone rushing. Threadgill's tune is especially knotty; listen to how the trio keeps its odd, lopsided phrase lengths intact.","Critics praised it as warmer and more open than its predecessor, and it was widely named among the best jazz records of 2012. Some still found Iyer's music more interesting in concept than in feeling, a criticism he has long faced. The record is now seen as the peak of this trio's work.","Start with Human Nature and notice how close the trio stays to Michael Jackson's gentle groove. Then play Little Pocket Size Demons and compare Threadgill's own recordings, listening for the lopsided phrase lengths. Finish with the Flying Lotus original to hear how a beat track becomes trio music."],"jason-moran-modernistic":["A solo piano album that ranges across a century of music. Moran plays James P. Johnson's stride showpiece You've Got to Be Modernistic, a song from Robert Schumann's Liederkreis, Afrika Bambaataa's electro anthem Planet Rock and his own pieces, including entries in his ongoing Gangsterism series. He moves between thundering stride left hand, delicate classical voicings and percussive, hip-hop-style repetition, sometimes in a single piece.","Moran, from Houston, studied with the pianist Jaki Byard and later with Andrew Hill and Muhal Richard Abrams, and signed to Blue Note in his early twenties. By 2002 he had recorded with his trio Bandwagon and with the saxophonist Greg Osby. Making a solo record so early in a career was a statement; Moran wanted to show his whole lineage, from Harlem stride to German Romantic song to the South Bronx.","Listen to his Planet Rock. Moran keeps the robotic pulse of the Roland drum machine in his left hand while his right hand plays shards of melody, so the piece sounds like a stride pianist imitating a sampler. In the Schumann, he lets the song's original harmony sit almost untouched before nudging it into blues inflections.","Critics praised it highly, and some named it the best jazz record of its year. A few found it more a catalogue of influences than a coherent statement. In hindsight it reads as an early, clear map of the eclectic, history-conscious approach that defined Moran's career and that of many pianists after him.","Hear James P. Johnson's original You've Got to Be Modernistic first, then Moran's version, and notice how much faster and harder his left hand hits. Then put the Schumann beside a recording of the Liederkreis with a singer. Finish with Planet Rock and imagine a dance floor."],"jason-moran-ten":["A record celebrating ten years of The Bandwagon, Moran's trio with Tarus Mateen on bass and Nasheet Waits on drums. It ranges from Thelonious Monk's Crepuscule with Nellie to Conlon Nancarrow's Study No. 6, originally for player piano, and Leonard Bernstein's Big Stuff, alongside Moran's own pieces. The trio can sound like a single body: they speed up, drop out and re-enter together, and Waits's drumming is as melodic as the piano.","Moran had formed The Bandwagon around 2000, and by 2010 it was one of the most admired working piano trios in jazz. That year he also received a MacArthur Fellowship, unusual for a jazz musician. His interests had widened to collaborations with visual artists and art galleries, and Ten shows that range in its choice of material from player-piano studies to Broadway.","The Nancarrow piece is the giveaway. Nancarrow punched his studies into player-piano rolls precisely because no human could play their layered tempos; Moran's trio plays a version by hand, letting the bass and drums take parts of the texture. In Big Stuff, a song Bernstein wrote for his ballet Fancy Free and Billie Holiday recorded, listen to how slowly the trio lets it unfold.","Many critics named it among the best jazz albums of 2010, and it was voted top album in at least one major poll. A few found its range of sources restless. It is now widely considered the definitive Bandwagon record, the place where the trio's long-developed group language is at its clearest.","Start with Crepuscule with Nellie, then Study No. 6, and compare Nancarrow's own player-piano version to hear what human hands change. Listen for the moments when the three players speed up or stop together without any obvious cue; that shared instinct is the record's real subject."],"mary-halvorson-away-with-you":["A record for octet in which Halvorson's guitar, with its warped, pitch-bending lines, sits beside the pedal steel guitar of Susan Alcorn. Jonathan Finlayson on trumpet, Jon Irabagon and Ingrid Laubrock on saxophones, Jacob Garchik on trombone, John Hébert on bass and Ches Smith on drums complete the band. The pieces are intricate, long-form compositions that move between brass-band fanfares, grinding dissonance and lyrical passages, with Alcorn's steel adding a weeping, floating colour that no jazz horn section usually has.","Halvorson studied with Anthony Braxton at Wesleyan and became one of the leading guitarists of the New York avant-garde in the 2000s. Her groups grew step by step from trio to quintet to septet; for this record she added the pedal steel, an instrument tied to country music and almost never heard in jazz. The album was issued by Firehouse 12, a New Haven label and venue that documented much of the new improvised music of the period.","Listen to how often Halvorson's guitar dips in pitch at the end of a phrase, an effect she makes with a delay pedal pushed past its normal setting. It is her signature, and Alcorn answers it with the pedal steel, which slides between notes by its nature. In places their lines merge so closely that you cannot tell which guitar is playing.","Critics praised it widely, and it was a fixture on year-end jazz lists. Some listeners find her writing knotty and dry, with too much plotted detail; others find it exhilarating precisely for that density. It is often cited as the moment her ensemble writing caught up with her reputation as a guitarist, and as proof that Braxton's teaching had found a distinctive new voice.","Start with the title track and follow the pedal steel all the way through; then play it again and follow the guitar. Notice how the horns often enter as a block, like a small brass band. Afterwards hear Anthony Braxton's Creative Orchestra Music 1976 to meet her teacher's way with a large ensemble."],"mary-halvorson-code-girl":["A double album of songs, the first for which Halvorson wrote her own words. Amirtha Kidambi sings, with Ambrose Akinmusire on trumpet, Michael Formanek on bass and Tomas Fujiwara on drums. The songs move between delicate art-song passages, jagged rock riffs and long instrumental stretches; Kidambi's voice is clear and exact, sometimes doubled by Halvorson's guitar note for note. The texts are oblique, closer to poetry than storytelling, and the whole has the slow-burning shape of a song cycle.","Halvorson had been known mainly as a guitarist and instrumental composer. Writing words was a new direction, prompted partly by her love of singer-songwriters and art rock. Kidambi, a singer with roots in Carnatic music and experimental improvisation, and Akinmusire, one of the most admired trumpeters of his generation, gave the project its unusual palette. Formanek and Fujiwara had played with her in other groups, so the band already shared a language.","Listen to Akinmusire's solos. He plays long, slowly unfolding lines in which notes crack, smear and whisper, very different from Halvorson's angular guitar, and the contrast gives the songs their emotional range. Listen also for how often voice and guitar share a melody exactly, so that the song seems to come from a single strange instrument.","It was praised as a bold new direction, though some reviewers found the lyrics opaque and the singing cool to the point of distance. It led to a second Code Girl album with a changed lineup and to much wider recognition, crowned by Halvorson's MacArthur Fellowship in 2019. Debate continues over whether the project is jazz at all, which is partly the point.","Pick a song where Akinmusire solos and listen once for the voice alone, then once for the guitar alone. Then go to Akinmusire's own When the Heart Emerges Glistening to hear him lead. For a kindred cross of art song and rock, try Kate Bush's Hounds of Love afterward."],"ambrose-akinmusire-when-the-heart-emerges-glistening":["Akinmusire's Blue Note debut, a quintet record with Walter Smith III on tenor saxophone, Gerald Clayton on piano, Harish Raghavan on bass and Justin Brown on drums. The trumpet sound is the point: breathy, cracked, sometimes barely voiced, then suddenly bright and piercing. Pieces unfold slowly, with long passages of quiet, inward group playing and few conventional head-solo-head shapes; one track, My Name Is Oscar, is a spoken elegy delivered over drums alone.","Akinmusire, from Oakland, California, won the Thelonious Monk International Jazz Competition in 2007 and signed to Blue Note soon after. Jason Moran produced the album. My Name Is Oscar refers to Oscar Grant, a young man shot dead by a transit police officer at an Oakland station on New Year's Day 2009, an event that marked the Bay Area deeply and which Akinmusire chose to address directly on a major-label debut.","Listen to the gaps. Akinmusire often lets a phrase fade almost to silence before the band moves on, and Brown's drums fill the space with small, crisp figures rather than steady time. The cracked notes are deliberate: he uses unstable sounds as expression, the way a singer uses a break in the voice, and he often holds back the trumpet's bright upper register until late in a piece.","It was widely praised as one of the strongest jazz debuts in years and established Akinmusire as a leading trumpeter of his generation. Some listeners found it austere and slow to reward. In hindsight many hear it as a turn in jazz trumpet away from the polished brilliance of the 1980s toward something more fragile and searching.","Start with My Name Is Oscar, then move to one of the long quintet pieces and follow the trumpet's tone rather than its notes. Then put on Wynton Marsalis's Black Codes to hear two generations' ideas of what a jazz trumpet should sound like, and decide which moves you more."],"ambrose-akinmusire-the-imagined-savior-is-far-easier-to-paint":["A long, ambitious record in which Akinmusire's quintet is joined by a string quartet and guest singers, among them Becca Stevens, Theo Bleckmann and Cold Specks. The music moves from chamber-like string passages to art-song ballads to extended group improvisations. Rollcall for Those Absent, in which a child recites names of people killed by police and in other violence, is one of the most direct statements on any jazz record of the decade, all the more powerful for its plainness.","By 2014 Akinmusire had toured widely with his quintet, which now included guitar, and he wanted a broader canvas. The strings and singers brought him close to contemporary classical music and to indie rock. The album appeared as public argument over police violence in the United States was intensifying, and Akinmusire, like several jazz musicians of his generation, placed that argument inside the music rather than in a liner note.","Listen to how the string quartet often plays sustained harmonies that shift very slowly under the trumpet, a texture closer to Morton Feldman than to the lush jazz-with-strings albums of the 1950s. And listen to Akinmusire's own solos for how often he chooses a whisper over a shout, letting the singers carry the climaxes.","It was praised for ambition and moral courage, and appeared on many year-end lists. Some critics found it overlong and uneven, with the guest songs sitting awkwardly beside the instrumental pieces. It is now often cited as a key jazz album of the years when the Black Lives Matter movement took shape, and as a forerunner of the protest-minded jazz records that followed.","Start with Rollcall for Those Absent, then a song with Becca Stevens, and notice how differently the trumpet behaves around a voice. Then play Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly, released the following year with many jazz players aboard, to hear the same moment from the other side."],"esperanza-spalding-radio-music-society":["A record of songs meant for the radio, built on Spalding's bass and voice with horns, keyboards, drums and guests including Joe Lovano, Jack DeJohnette, Lalah Hathaway and Gretchen Parlato. It covers Wayne Shorter's Endangered Species and Michael Jackson's I Can't Help It, a Stevie Wonder song, alongside originals such as Black Gold. The sound mixes soul, funk, pop and jazz, with brass arrangements, a big-band feel on some tracks, and Spalding's agile, high, bright voice on top.","Spalding, from Portland, Oregon, had won the 2011 Grammy for Best New Artist, a surprise victory over pop stars that made her famous outside jazz. This album was conceived as a companion to Chamber Music Society, its more intimate predecessor, and aimed squarely at a broader audience. Q-Tip of A Tribe Called Quest co-produced some tracks. It went on to win the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album.","Listen to the bass lines. Spalding sings and plays at once, often with lines that move against the vocal melody in rhythm and direction, a feat very few musicians attempt. The Shorter cover keeps his twisting harmony but adds words, turning an instrumental piece from the jazz canon into a song with a message.","It was warmly received and won its Grammy, though some critics found it too polished and radio-ready, and others missed the chamber intimacy of its predecessor. Over time it has come to be heard as a careful, generous attempt to reconnect jazz with soul and R&B listeners, a project Robert Glasper was pursuing from the other direction the same year.","Start with Black Gold, then Endangered Species, and compare Wayne Shorter's original instrumental. Notice how often the bass line is as catchy as the vocal. Then play Chamber Music Society to hear the paired record, and Robert Glasper's Black Radio for a neighbour from the same year."],"esperanza-spalding-emilys-d-evolution":["A loud, theatrical, guitar-heavy record built around a character named Emily, Spalding's middle name and childhood alter ego. Matthew Stevens's electric guitar and Karriem Riggins's drums push the music toward progressive rock and funk, with Spalding's voice and electric bass at the front. The songs are knotty and quick-changing, layered with stacked backing vocals, and a cover of Pure Imagination from the film Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory sits among the originals.","Spalding co-produced the album with Tony Visconti, best known for his work with David Bowie, and presented it on a staged tour with choreography, costumes and spoken interludes. The move away from acoustic jazz was deliberate, part of her wish to escape the image of a polite jazz prodigy she had acquired after her Grammy win. The record was released by Concord, which had absorbed her earlier label.","Listen to the bass. Spalding plays electric bass with the agility of her upright playing but in rock-style riffs, often in uneven meters, and the guitar locks into those patterns in a way that recalls 1970s progressive rock more than jazz. The vocal harmonies are tracked in many layers, a studio technique foreign to most jazz records.","Some longtime fans were bewildered by the volume and the rock trappings; others were thrilled. Critics generally praised her daring while differing on the songs themselves. The record now looks like the hinge of her career, the point at which she claimed the freedom that produced the experimental 12 Little Spells and her later work with Wayne Shorter on the opera Iphigenia.","Start with a fast track at volume and follow the bass rather than the voice. Then play Joni Mitchell's Hejira, which Spalding has often named as a touchstone, and Jaco Pastorius's debut for a fretless bass ancestor. Finally go back to Radio Music Society to hear how far she moved in four years."],"cecile-mclorin-salvant-dreams-and-daggers":["A double album recorded mostly live at the Village Vanguard in New York with Aaron Diehl on piano, Paul Sikivie on bass and Lawrence Leathers on drums, framed by studio tracks with the Catalyst Quartet. Salvant sings songs from the Bessie Smith repertoire, Broadway and Tin Pan Alley, including Noël Coward's Mad About the Boy, alongside her own pieces. Her voice ranges from a deep, growling low register to a light, almost operatic top, and her phrasing is theatrical, witty and exact.","Salvant, born in Miami to a Haitian father and a French mother, studied classical voice and Baroque singing in Aix-en-Provence before turning to jazz, and won the Thelonious Monk competition in 2010. By the time of this record she had a long-standing partnership with Diehl's trio, honed on the road. The Vanguard, the most storied jazz room in New York, gave the album its intimate, close-up sound.","Listen to how she treats old songs about women. Salvant often chooses material whose attitudes now sound uncomfortable and sings it straight-faced but with a slight edge, so the listener hears the song's assumptions rather than simply enjoying them. The string quartet interludes are her own arrangements and frame the live set like chapters in a book.","It was widely praised and won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album, her second win in the category. A few listeners find her mannered, every phrase a performance; most hear the most gifted jazz singer of her generation, regularly compared to Sarah Vaughan and Betty Carter for range and to Abbey Lincoln for seriousness. The album is often named the fullest portrait of her live art.","Start with Mad About the Boy, then pick one of the Bessie Smith songs and play Bessie's original 1920s recording beside it. Listen for the moments when Salvant drops suddenly from her light top voice to her low growl, and for how Diehl's trio answers."],"cecile-mclorin-salvant-the-window":["A duo record with the pianist Sullivan Fortner, partly recorded live at the Village Vanguard. The songs range from Broadway and the Great American Songbook to Stevie Wonder and French chanson. With no bass or drums, the music is intimate and loose; Fortner follows Salvant closely, stretching and compressing time, adding sudden harmonies and stride flourishes, and the two improvise together as equals. The mood moves between hushed ballads, comic numbers and moments of real darkness.","The album followed Dreams and Daggers and won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album, Salvant's third. Fortner, from New Orleans, had become one of the most sought-after pianists of his generation, partly through his work with the trumpeter Roy Hargrove. The voice-and-piano duo, rare in recent jazz singing, recalls Ella Fitzgerald's and Sarah Vaughan's recordings with a single accompanist and the cabaret tradition.","Listen to the silences. Without a rhythm section, Salvant and Fortner can pause, slow down or rush at will, and those tempo changes carry much of the drama; a phrase may hang in the air for a long moment before the piano catches it. Fortner often answers her lines with tiny, unexpected harmonies that change the meaning of the words she has just sung.","Critics praised it as her most intimate record and among her best, and it took the Grammy. Some missed the swing of a full band. It is now often cited as a model of the voice-piano duo, and as evidence that Salvant's art depends as much on her choice of partner as on her voice.","Start with a slow ballad and listen once only to the piano, then once only to the voice. Then compare Ella Fitzgerald's duo work, or Bill Evans's Peace Piece for the patience of a pianist alone. Play it late, quietly, as if in a small club."],"makaya-mccraven-in-the-moment":["An album edited from live improvisations by McCraven's bands during a long weekly residency at a small Chicago venue. McCraven recorded the shows, then cut, looped and layered the best moments into short tracks, much as a hip-hop producer builds beats from samples. The result sounds like a beat tape made from live jazz: tight drum grooves, looping bass figures, fragments of horn and guitar solos, with McCraven's crisp drumming at the centre of nearly every track.","McCraven, born in Paris and raised in western Massachusetts, settled in Chicago and became central to its new jazz scene. The album was released by International Anthem, a small Chicago label that grew into a hub of that scene. The approach, which McCraven called organic beat music, drew on the production of J Dilla and Madlib as much as on jazz, and on cheap digital editing that let one musician shape hours of tape at home.","Listen for the edits. Many tracks repeat a short passage of live playing, with small variations, as if a sampler were looping it, and you can hear the seams if you listen for them. The crowd and room noise are often audible, a reminder that the source was a bar on an ordinary weeknight rather than a studio.","It was praised as a genuinely new way to make jazz records and became a cult favourite, launching McCraven's wider career. Some purists questioned whether editing improvisation honours it or betrays it, since the finished tracks never happened in that order. Teo Macero's edits for Miles Davis are the usual counter-argument, and the debate has since become part of the record's story.","Start anywhere and listen as you would to a beat tape, letting the loops settle rather than waiting for solos. Then play J Dilla's Donuts to hear the model, and Miles Davis's In a Silent Way, whose tape edits did something similar forty-six years earlier."],"makaya-mccraven-universal-beings":["A double album recorded with different bands in four cities: New York, Chicago, London and Los Angeles, each given its own side and mood. Players include the harpist Brandee Younger, the saxophonists Shabaka Hutchings and Nubya Garcia, the tuba player Theon Cross, the cellist Tomeka Reid, the vibraphonist Joel Ross and the guitarist Jeff Parker. As before, McCraven recorded live sessions and edited the results, but here the playing itself comes further forward and the tracks breathe longer.","By 2018 several young jazz scenes were in conversation: London's players around Hutchings and Garcia, Chicago's International Anthem circle and the beat-influenced jazz of Los Angeles. McCraven connected them in a single project, travelling to record with local musicians in each city. The album was among the most praised jazz releases of its year and was followed by a documentary film of the same name.","Listen to how each city's side has its own character: the London tracks drive hard toward the dance floor, with tuba carrying the bass, while the Los Angeles side is spacious and hazy. The edits often loop a single moment of collective improvisation into a groove, so that a few seconds of spontaneity become the foundation of a whole track.","It was hailed as a landmark of the new international jazz moment and appeared at or near the top of many year-end lists. Some critics wanted longer, uninterrupted performances and felt the editing flattened the players' arcs. Most heard it as proof that the producer's chair could be a jazz musician's instrument.","Play the London side first, then the Los Angeles side, and compare how the same drummer sounds in each. Listen for the harp on the New York side. Then hear Kamasi Washington's The Epic, its west-coast cousin, and Flying Lotus's Cosmogramma for the beat-music side of the family."],"tyshawn-sorey-verisimilitude":["A trio record with Cory Smythe on piano and Chris Tordini on bass. The pieces are long, slow and spacious, unfolding over many minutes through quiet sustained sounds, silences and gradual swells. Sorey's drums are often played softly, with mallets, bowed cymbals and hand percussion, creating textures rather than keeping time. At moments the trio erupts into dense, loud playing, but the prevailing atmosphere is closer to contemporary classical chamber music than to a conventional jazz piano trio.","Sorey, from Newark, New Jersey, is a drummer, trombonist, pianist and composer who studied with Anthony Braxton at Wesleyan and later at Columbia. He works equally in jazz and in concert music, and had recorded with this trio before. Pi Recordings, a New York label devoted to adventurous jazz, released the album in the year Sorey received a MacArthur Fellowship, and it appeared on many critics' year-end lists.","Listen to how long each sound is allowed to last. Sorey writes and improvises with extreme patience, so that a single chord or cymbal roll may hold for close to a minute, and the structure becomes clear only over time. Ideas return much later in altered form, which is why the record rewards listening straight through.","Critics praised it as a major statement from a composer-performer coming into his own, though some found its slowness and quiet demanding to the point of frustration. It helped establish Sorey's reputation beyond jazz circles, paving the way for commissions from classical ensembles and, eventually, a Pulitzer Prize.","Listen in a quiet room without distraction, and resist skipping ahead when nothing seems to happen; the stillness is the material. Then play Morton Feldman's Triadic Memories or Rothko Chapel to hear a kindred patience from the concert world, and Anthony Braxton for Sorey's teacher."],"tyshawn-sorey-pillars":["A three-disc work close to four hours long for a large ensemble of improvisers, with Sorey on drums, trombone and other instruments. The music unfolds as slow sustained drones, quiet scraping textures, sudden eruptions and long stretches near silence, with several double basses giving it a dark low floor. There are no tunes and almost no steady beat. It feels more like an installation or a ritual than a jazz record, a single span to be lived inside rather than followed.","Sorey conceived the work as one long composition for musicians he trusted, mixing written material, cues and open improvisation. It was released by Firehouse 12, the New Haven label that also issued Mary Halvorson's records. It came the year after his MacArthur Fellowship, when he was receiving growing attention as a composer, and it showed how far he was willing to take duration as a musical material.","Listen to how the ensemble moves from near-silence to loud, dense passages and back over spans of many minutes, often so gradually that you notice the change only after it has happened. The bowed basses and sustained brass blur together into a single shifting sound, so individual players are hard to pick out.","Critics praised it as a major, uncompromising statement and it appeared on year-end lists; others found its length forbidding and wondered how many listeners would ever hear it whole. It is now discussed alongside the long late works of Morton Feldman as a test of what duration can do to attention.","Take one disc at a time, in a quiet room, ideally at night, and do not multitask. Notice how your sense of time shifts after twenty minutes. Afterwards, Feldman's String Quartet No. 2 makes a natural companion, and Coltrane's Ascension shows a far louder idea of collective sound."],"henry-threadgill-in-for-a-penny-in-for-a-pound":["A double album by Threadgill's group Zooid: Threadgill on alto saxophone and flutes, Liberty Ellman on guitar, Jose Davila on tuba and trombone, Christopher Hoffman on cello and Elliot Humberto Kavee on drums. The music is intricate, with lines weaving around one another like counterpoint while the drums keep a light, springy pulse. Guitar, cello and tuba make an unusual middle and low register, and Threadgill's sour, keening alto cuts through on top.","Threadgill, a Chicago-born veteran of the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians, had led Zooid since the early 2000s and was over seventy when this record appeared. It won the 2016 Pulitzer Prize for Music, placing him beside Wynton Marsalis and Ornette Coleman among the jazz musicians so honoured. Pi Recordings had become his regular home and issued the set as a two-disc release.","The players are not improvising over chords. Threadgill developed a system in which each musician is given a set of intervals to work within, so the lines move independently yet always fit together. That is why the music sounds both free and strangely ordered, like several conversations that keep agreeing without meeting.","Critics praised it highly, and the Pulitzer brought Threadgill late recognition well beyond the jazz world. Some listeners find the music cerebral and hard to grasp on first hearing; others treat it as the culmination of fifty years of searching. It has since become one of the most discussed jazz composition records of its decade.","Start anywhere and follow one instrument, the tuba, say, for a full piece, then go back and follow the cello. Notice how each line is complete in itself. Then hear Muhal Richard Abrams and the Art Ensemble of Chicago to meet the AACM world Threadgill came from."],"henry-threadgill-old-locks-and-irregular-verbs":["A four-part suite for Threadgill's Ensemble Double Up, in which he composes and directs but does not play. The band pairs two pianos, played by Jason Moran and David Virelles, with two alto saxophones, cello, tuba and drums. The paired instruments shadow, echo and contradict each other, creating a doubled, mirrored texture. The suite moves through spacious, almost mournful passages and dense, knotted ensemble writing, with the pianos often taking the lead in turn.","The suite is dedicated to Lawrence \"Butch\" Morris, the cornetist and conductor whose system of conducted improvisation, which he called conduction, influenced many New York musicians; Morris died in 2013. Threadgill had known him since the 1970s. The album appeared on Pi Recordings shortly after Threadgill's Pulitzer Prize was announced, which brought it more attention than his records usually received.","The doubling of pianos and altos is the key. Listen for moments when the two pianists play nearly the same thing slightly apart, producing a blurred echo, and others when they split into opposing lines. The tuba and cello beneath them often carry the true melody while the doubled instruments decorate it.","Critics welcomed it as a moving tribute and a strong companion to the Zooid records, often praising Moran's and Virelles's interplay. Some found it austere and short on Threadgill's own alto. It has come to be heard as one of his most personal recent works, a memorial written in his most complex language.","Hear it straight through, about forty minutes, and try to keep the two pianos apart in your ear; on headphones they are usually set left and right. Then play Jason Moran's Ten to hear one of the pianists in his own trio, and In for a Penny, In for a Pound for the Zooid sound."],"garbarek-witchi-tai-to":["Five long performances by the quartet Jan Garbarek co-led with the Swedish pianist Bobo Stenson, with Palle Danielsson on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. Almost nothing here is by the band: there is a Carla Bley tune, a Cuban song in praise of Che Guevara, Jim Pepper's chant-like title piece and, to close, Don Cherry's \"Desireless\", stretched to around twenty minutes. Garbarek's soprano is keening and bright, the rhythm section loose and breathing, the mood ecstatic rather than cool.","Recorded in Oslo in late 1973 for Manfred Eicher's young Munich label ECM, this was the point where Garbarek moved past his early fiery, Coltrane-and-Ayler-inspired records toward a more open, song-led manner. The quartet was a pan-Scandinavian band of the kind ECM was quietly building, and the choice of material shows how widely these players listened: American free jazz, Latin American protest song and a Native American saxophonist's hit tune all sit together.","Jim Pepper, who wrote the title piece, was a Native American saxophonist who built it on a chant he had learned from his grandfather; Garbarek turns that repeated, almost ceremonial phrase into a slow-burning hymn. Listen to how Christensen keeps time mostly on cymbals and toms, never quite settling into swing, so the music floats. \"Desireless\" builds from near-silence into a long, hypnotic ostinato that anticipates much later Nordic jazz.","The album was well received on release and has grown steadily in reputation, often named as the best of Garbarek's early ECM records and a founding document of the Nordic sound. Some admirers of his later, sparer work find it surprisingly hot-blooded; others think that warmth is exactly what his later records lost. Its reissue in ECM's Touchstones series introduced it to a new generation of listeners.","Start with the title track and wait for the moment the melody returns after the solos, now carried with full force. Then give \"Desireless\" its full length on headphones and notice how little changes and how much it grows. Compare with Keith Jarrett's Belonging, which uses almost the same band, and with Don Cherry's own records."],"garbarek-dis":["A duo record for Jan Garbarek's saxophones and wooden flute and Ralph Towner's twelve-string and classical guitars, joined on some tracks by a wind harp, an instrument played only by the air moving across its strings. The pieces are slow, modal and spacious: long held saxophone notes over ringing guitar patterns, or over the harp's eerie, unpredictable drone. There is almost no swing and almost no harmony moving in the usual jazz way. It sounds like weather and distance more than a club.","By the mid-1970s ECM had become a home for a new kind of chamber jazz, and Manfred Eicher liked to pair musicians who had not played together as a regular unit. Towner, an American from the group Oregon, brought folk and classical guitar technique; Garbarek was turning more and more toward Norwegian folk song. The title is an old Norwegian word for mist or haze, and the cover photograph of grey sea set the visual style many listeners still associate with the label.","The wind harp was recorded outdoors on the Norwegian coast, so on those tracks the composer of the drone is literally the wind: the overtones that bloom and fade under Garbarek's line were not played by anyone. Listen for the way he shapes his notes to match them rather than the other way round. Towner's twelve-string patterns are often in odd groupings that make the pulse feel slightly unsteady.","Dis became one of Garbarek's best-known albums and, for many, the record that fixed the cliché of the ECM sound as fjords and fog. Jazz traditionalists asked whether it was jazz at all, a question that would follow Garbarek for decades. Others heard in it a serious attempt to make improvised music from northern landscape and folk melody, and it has long been admired by ambient and new-age musicians.","Listen in a quiet room, not as background. Start with the first track and follow the single saxophone line as if it were a singer. Notice how Towner never fills space, then wait for the wind harp and listen to its tones drifting in and out. Afterwards try Brian Eno's ambient records, then Garbarek's later Officium."],"garbarek-officium":["Jan Garbarek improvises on soprano and tenor saxophone around the Hilliard Ensemble, four male voices singing Gregorian chant and polyphony from the medieval and Renaissance church. The voices sing the old music as written; the saxophone weaves above and between them, sometimes doubling a line, sometimes floating free, sometimes answering a phrase like a fifth singer. The acoustic is huge and resonant. The whole album moves at the pace of a slow procession, with silence ringing after every cadence.","The project was Manfred Eicher's idea, made for ECM's New Series, which he had founded to record composers such as Arvo Pärt alongside the label's jazz. The sessions took place in 1993 in the Austrian monastery of St Gerold, chosen for its echo. The early 1990s were the years of chant on the pop charts and of Górecki's Third Symphony as a bestseller, and Officium arrived in the same climate of hunger for slow, sacred-sounding music.","Most of the repertoire is real early music: Cristóbal de Morales's \"Parce mihi Domine\", from his Office of the Dead, returns more than once, and Pérotin's \"Beata viscera\", a song from Notre-Dame around 1200, also appears. Listen for how Garbarek usually enters after the voices have established the mode, then stays inside it. Where the saxophone is silent, the singers are performing straight; the improvisation is added, never written into the parts.","Officium became one of the biggest sellers in ECM's history and charted in several countries in both classical and pop listings. Early-music purists objected that the saxophone turned liturgy into mood music; some jazz critics found the improvising too polite. Yet many listeners came to chant and Renaissance polyphony through it, and the team made two sequels, Mnemosyne and Officium Novum. Its long success has made it a touchstone in arguments about crossover.","Start with \"Parce mihi Domine\" and listen first only to the voices, then play it again and follow the saxophone. Notice the long reverberation and how the players let each phrase die away before moving. Then hear Pérotin's music sung plainly by a choir, and Arvo Pärt's Summa, to judge what the saxophone adds and what it changes."],"stanko-balladyna":["A quartet without piano: Tomasz Stańko's trumpet and Tomasz Szukalski's saxophones over the American bassist Dave Holland and the Finnish drummer Edward Vesala. The tunes are dark, slow-moving melodies that open into free, smeared, often wailing improvisation, then return. Stańko's tone is the recognisable thing: breathy, cracked at the edges, bending notes downward as if sighing. The absence of a chord instrument gives the music a raw, open feel, closer to Ornette Coleman's quartet than to cool jazz.","Stańko was the leading trumpeter of the Polish avant-garde and had played in Krzysztof Komeda's groups in the 1960s. In communist Poland jazz had been tolerated and sometimes celebrated, but recording in the West remained a privilege. This 1975 Oslo session, made for ECM, was his first for the label and his first wide exposure outside Eastern Europe. The title comes from a nineteenth-century Polish verse drama by Juliusz Słowacki about ambition and murder.","Listen to how often Holland plays a melody against the horns rather than a walking bass line; with no piano he becomes a second voice. Vesala's drumming is full of rattles and washes, closer to free jazz than to swing, but he keeps an undertow of pulse. Stańko frequently plays two or three notes and then lets them decay, using silence as part of the phrase in a way that would become his signature.","Balladyna was respected but not widely noticed at first. Its standing rose after Stańko's return to ECM in the 1990s made him a major international name, when critics went back and heard in it the seeds of his late style. Some prefer its harder, freer edges to the lyrical later records. It remains a key document of how Eastern European jazz musicians absorbed American free jazz and then made something darker of it.","Start with the title track and listen to Stańko's very first phrases: notice how he bends and frays the notes. Then follow Holland's bass as an equal voice. Compare with Ornette Coleman's piano-less quartet of 1959, and then jump forward to Stańko's Soul of Things to hear how the same tone sounds in a gentler setting."],"stanko-litania":["Tomasz Stańko leads a septet through the music of Krzysztof Komeda, the Polish pianist and film composer who had been his mentor. The band is Scandinavian and Swedish-heavy: Bobo Stenson on piano, Palle Danielsson on bass, Jon Christensen on drums, the tenor saxophonists Bernt Rosengren and Joakim Milder, and Terje Rypdal's guitar on some tracks. The music is slow, melancholy and lyrical, with brooding melodies, hushed ensemble passages and Stańko's breathy, grieving trumpet always at the centre.","Komeda had died in 1969, aged thirty-seven, after an accident in Los Angeles, at the height of a career that included the scores for Roman Polański's films, among them Knife in the Water and Rosemary's Baby. Stańko had played in Komeda's quintet as a young man, and Rosengren too had played with him. Recorded in Oslo in 1997, the album was both a tribute and a statement that Komeda's music belonged in the international jazz canon.","Listen for the lullaby from Rosemary's Baby, \"Sleep Safe and Warm\", which appears in more than one version: what was a sinister nursery tune in the film becomes, slowed down, a tender ballad. Komeda's themes often move in modal patterns that recall Polish folk music, and Stenson's piano voicings hint at Chopin. Rosengren's presence links the recording to Komeda's own 1960s band.","Litania was greeted as one of the finest ECM records of the 1990s and helped spark a revival of interest in Komeda, whose own records had been hard to find outside Poland. Critics praised Stańko for honouring the composer without nostalgia. Some felt the ECM production smoothed away the urgency of the originals. It is now regarded as a central work of Polish jazz and of Stańko's late career.","Start with \"Sleep Safe and Warm\", then watch the opening of Rosemary's Baby to hear the original. Notice how slowly the band lets the melody unfold, and how the two tenors blend almost into one voice. Then seek out Komeda's own album Astigmatic, with the young Stańko on trumpet, to hear where this all began."],"stanko-soul-of-things":["Thirteen pieces, all called simply \"Soul of Things\" and numbered I to XIII, played by Tomasz Stańko with a trio of young Polish musicians: Marcin Wasilewski on piano, Sławomir Kurkiewicz on bass and Michał Miśkiewicz on drums. The music is mostly slow and lyrical, with ballads and gently swinging pieces in which the trumpet sings in long, smoky phrases. The trio plays with real delicacy and fluidity, and the whole record has the shape of one long, continuous meditation.","Stańko, by now in his late fifties, chose to work with a trio of musicians in their twenties who had grown up admiring him. He had heard them as a group from Koszalin and adopted them as his band. Recorded in Oslo in 2001 for ECM, this was their first album together; they went on to make Suspended Night and Lontano with him before becoming famous in their own right as the Marcin Wasilewski Trio.","The thirteen pieces are separate compositions, but several share melodic cells and moods, so the numbering is more than a gimmick: it invites you to hear one suite. Listen to how Wasilewski often lays out entirely during Stańko's solos, leaving bass and drums alone with the trumpet, then re-enters softly. Miśkiewicz plays mostly with brushes and cymbals, keeping a pulse that never insists.","Soul of Things was widely praised as one of Stańko's finest records and among the most beautiful jazz albums of its decade. Critics noted a new serenity in his playing after years of rawer, freer work. A few missed the edge of his earlier music. The album also launched the young trio's international career, and their later records on ECM have kept its reputation alive.","Play it from the beginning and let it run; the album works best whole. Notice how Stańko rarely plays fast, and how much feeling he gets into single sustained notes. Then compare with Miles Davis's ballad playing on Kind of Blue, and with the Bill Evans trio's interplay, to hear the tradition this record carries forward."],"kenny-wheeler-gnu-high":["Three long pieces, \"Heyoke\", \"Smatter\" and \"Gnu Suite\", for Kenny Wheeler's flugelhorn with Keith Jarrett on piano, Dave Holland on bass and Jack DeJohnette on drums. Wheeler writes melodies that leap in wide intervals and then fall back with a wistful, almost Romantic ache; his tone is warm and dark, his solos full of sudden flights into the high register. The rhythm section is a dream team, and Jarrett, unusually, plays strictly as a sideman, supporting another composer's music.","Wheeler, born in Canada, had lived in London since the early 1950s and was a central figure in the British free-improvisation and big-band scenes, yet he was barely known in America. This 1975 New York session for ECM was his first album for the label and gave him an international profile. It was also one of the very few times Jarrett, by then a major star, recorded as a sideman after the early 1970s.","\"Heyoke\" lasts more than twenty minutes and is built in sections that return like a rondo; listen for the opening melody coming back in altered form near the end. Wheeler's characteristic leaps of an octave or more are hard to play on the flugelhorn, and he lands them almost casually. Listen also to how Jarrett holds back his trademark long solos and comps with real restraint.","Gnu High was welcomed as a fine record at the time and has since become a cult favourite, often named as one of the best ECM albums of the 1970s and as Wheeler's finest small-group statement. Musicians prize Wheeler's compositions, which are now studied in jazz schools. The record is also cherished for showing a side of Jarrett rarely heard, as a sympathetic accompanist.","Start with \"Heyoke\" and simply follow Wheeler's melody, noticing its leaps and sighs. Then listen again to Jarrett behind him. Compare with Jarrett's own Facing You to hear the pianist in charge, and with Wheeler's Angel Song to hear his writing in a very different, drumless setting."],"kenny-wheeler-angel-song":["A quartet with no drums and no piano: Kenny Wheeler on flugelhorn, Lee Konitz on alto saxophone, Bill Frisell on guitar and Dave Holland on bass. The music is quiet chamber jazz, almost all slow or medium tempo, with Wheeler's melodies passed between horn and saxophone while Frisell's guitar adds soft clouds of harmony and Holland holds everything together. Without a drummer the pulse is implied rather than stated, and the record has an intimate, conversational, searching feel throughout.","Recorded in New York in 1996 and released by ECM the following year, Angel Song brought together four musicians from different generations and traditions: Konitz from the cool jazz of the late 1940s, Holland from Miles Davis's electric bands, Frisell from the downtown New York scene and Americana. Wheeler, by now in his late sixties, wrote all the music, and the group's line-up had never played together as a band before the session.","Konitz is a famously unpredictable improviser who rarely plays a phrase the same way twice, and his lines often run against Wheeler's long arcs in a quiet counterpoint. Listen to how Frisell uses volume swells so that his chords seem to appear from nowhere, with no attack. The absence of drums means every player must keep time inside their own phrasing, and you can hear them listening intently.","Critics praised it as one of the most beautiful records of the decade and a model of chamber jazz; the British press in particular embraced it as a crowning statement of Wheeler's writing. Some found it too subdued. It is now widely considered among his finest albums and is often recommended to listeners who come to jazz from classical chamber music.","Listen at low volume, ideally late at night. Start with the title track and follow the interplay between flugelhorn and alto: which one leads, and when? Then notice Frisell's guitar entering underneath. Compare with Lee Konitz's early records, and with Gnu High to hear Wheeler's tunes with a full rhythm section."],"surman-the-amazing-adventures-of-simon-simon":["A duo between John Surman, playing soprano and baritone saxophones, bass clarinet and synthesisers, and Jack DeJohnette on drums, congas and keyboards. The album is laid out as a loose story in short episodes, each with an evocative title. Surman often builds a repeating synthesiser pattern first, then plays long, folk-like melodies over it on baritone or soprano, while DeJohnette moves between crisp jazz drumming and rolling, tribal percussion. The sound is bright, rhythmic and unusually lyrical.","Surman, from Devon, had been a leading figure in British jazz since the late 1960s, famous for making the baritone saxophone fly. During the 1970s he became fascinated by synthesisers and overdubbing and began working regularly with ECM. DeJohnette had played with Miles Davis and Keith Jarrett and led his own ECM groups. The two had played together before, and this 1981 Oslo session was a chance to combine Surman's electronic experiments with a great drummer.","Many of the synthesiser patterns are simple repeating figures, close to the early minimalism of Terry Riley and Steve Reich, and Surman plays against them in English modal melodies that sound like folk song or church music. Listen to how DeJohnette shifts the pulse under a pattern so that the same loop suddenly feels like a different metre. The story behind the titles is never spelled out: it is left to your imagination.","The album was warmly received and has remained one of Surman's most popular records; it was reissued in ECM's Touchstones series. Some purists disliked the synthesisers, which date the sound to the early 1980s. Others hear it as a forerunner of later fusions of jazz, minimalism and electronics. It is often cited as the best introduction to Surman's work.","Start at the beginning and listen for the moment the baritone first enters over the synthesiser loop: it is deep yet light on its feet. Follow DeJohnette as he changes the feel beneath a repeating pattern. Then try Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and Brian Eno's Another Green World for kindred ideas."],"surman-road-to-saint-ives":["A solo album on which John Surman plays everything himself, layering soprano and baritone saxophones, bass clarinet, keyboards and percussion through overdubbing. The tracks are named after places in Cornwall, from Bodmin Moor to Marazion, and each is a short sound-picture: a drone or keyboard pattern sets the scene, then a melody arrives on reed or saxophone, sometimes doubled, sometimes answered by another of Surman's instruments. The music is pastoral, misty and melodic, with an English folk accent.","Surman had been making overdubbed solo records since the early 1970s, and he returned to the format for ECM at the end of the 1980s. He was born in Tavistock, close to the Cornish border, and the album is a portrait of the landscape of his youth. By this time he was living largely in Norway, and the record has the distance of a memory: Cornwall imagined from far away.","Because Surman plays every part, listen for how the instruments are voiced like a small church choir, with the bass clarinet often taking the bass line and soprano saxophones singing the top. Several melodies use old English modes, the scales of folk song and Tudor church music, and some phrases sound like hymn tunes. The keyboard patterns repeat with tiny changes, a technique closer to minimalism than to jazz.","The record was praised for its atmosphere and has a devoted following, especially in Britain. Some critics thought it closer to ambient music than to jazz, with too little improvisation in the obvious sense. Others value it precisely as a portrait in sound, comparing it to English pastoral composers such as Vaughan Williams. It remains one of the most personal records in Surman's large catalogue.","Read the track titles as a map and imagine the road west. Start with a single piece and listen for the layering: how many Surmans can you count? Notice the folk-like turns in the melodies. Then try Shirley Collins's English folk singing, and Steve Reich's Electric Counterpoint, another work built from one player multiplied."],"eberhard-weber-the-colours-of-chloe":["Four long pieces led by Eberhard Weber's bass, with Rainer Brüninghaus on piano and synthesiser, Ack van Rooyen on flugelhorn, Peter Giger on drums and a section of orchestral cellos. The music moves in slow, shimmering layers: repeated piano figures, a singing bass melody, swelling strings and soft percussion. It sounds closer to film music or impressionist chamber music than to bebop, and Weber's bass tone, high, sustained and glowing, is unlike any jazz bass heard before.","Weber, from Stuttgart, had played in the German free-jazz and rock scenes and with the pianist Wolfgang Dauner. In the early 1970s he designed a modified electric upright bass with a slim body and an extra string, which gave him a sustained, cello-like sound. This, his first album as leader, recorded near Stuttgart in late 1973, was one of the early ECM records that defined the label's polished, spacious production. The cellists came from the local radio symphony orchestra.","Listen to how often the piano and synthesiser play short repeating patterns, very much like the minimalism Steve Reich was then developing in America; Weber was one of the first jazz musicians to build pieces this way. The cellos sometimes play long chords that sound like a second, deeper bass. Notice too how Weber uses his bass to play melodies in a high register usually reserved for horns.","The album was a surprise success, establishing Weber as an important new voice. Some critics heard it as soft, more like soundtrack than jazz. Over time it has been recognised as a model of European jazz composition, and many bassists cite Weber's tone as an inspiration. It also helped define ECM's identity in its first years.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the bass melody emerges above the shimmering piano. Notice the cellos entering beneath. Then compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, with Pat Metheny's Bright Size Life, and with Weber's next album Yellow Fields, which puts the same ideas into a tighter quartet."],"eberhard-weber-yellow-fields":["Four pieces by a quartet: Eberhard Weber on his custom electric bass, Charlie Mariano on soprano saxophone and the South Indian nagaswaram and shehnai, Rainer Brüninghaus on piano and synthesiser, and Jon Christensen on drums. The music is driving and bright, with repeating keyboard patterns, long rising melodies and a pulse that builds steadily. Mariano's Indian reeds give some passages a piercing, nasal colour, and Weber's bass alternates between deep grooves and high, singing lines.","After the success of The Colours of Chloë, Weber formed a working band, later called Colours, to play his music live. Mariano, an American saxophonist who had played with Charles Mingus, had studied Indian music and brought instruments from that tradition. Recorded in 1975 and released in 1976 by ECM, the album came out at a time when jazz-rock fusion was at its height, but Weber's music took a cooler, more architectural direction.","Listen to the title track for the way the keyboards repeat a pattern while the harmony underneath shifts slowly, so the same figure changes colour; it is a technique taken from minimalism. Christensen plays with a light touch, often around the beat rather than on it. Mariano's nagaswaram, a long double-reed instrument from South Indian temple music, has a sound you will not hear on many other jazz records.","Yellow Fields was well received and is often considered the best record of Weber's Colours band. Some critics thought the music too polished, but many have praised its compositional strength and the unique blend of jazz, Indian and minimalist influences. It remains a favourite among fans of ECM's 1970s catalogue.","Start with the title track and follow how the energy builds over its length. Listen for Mariano's switch to the Indian reed, and notice the difference in colour. Then compare with Shakti's fusion of jazz and Indian music, and with Weather Report's Heavy Weather for a contemporary American take on electric jazz."],"stenson-war-orphans":["A piano trio record by Bobo Stenson with Anders Jormin on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. The material ranges widely: Ornette Coleman's title piece, a song by the Cuban singer-songwriter Silvio Rodríguez, Jormin's own compositions and free, open pieces. The trio plays with great patience, letting melodies unfold slowly, then dissolving them into conversational improvisation where piano, bass and drums are equal voices. The sound is clear and resonant, the mood often melancholy and searching.","Stenson had been a central figure in Scandinavian jazz since the late 1960s, playing with Jan Garbarek, Charles Lloyd and Tomasz Stańko. His trio with Jormin and Christensen began in the late 1980s and became one of the most respected in Europe. This was their second ECM album, following Reflections, and it came after a long period in which Stenson was better known as a sideman than as a leader.","Ornette Coleman's \"War Orphans\" is one of his darkest pieces, a lament; listen to how the trio slows it down further and leaves silences in place of the expected harmony. Christensen rarely plays a steady beat, instead using cymbal colours and short bursts; the pulse lives in Jormin's bass. The Silvio Rodríguez song connects the record to Latin American protest music, a recurring interest in Nordic jazz going back to the Garbarek–Stenson quartet's Witchi-Tai-To.","War Orphans was widely praised and helped establish Stenson as one of the great European piano trio leaders. Critics compared the trio's freedom and interplay to that of Bill Evans and Paul Bley. Some found the free sections hard going. The trio's later records, Serenity and Goodbye, have built on its reputation.","Start with the title track and listen to the space between notes. Follow the bass as the anchor while the drums float. Then hear Ornette Coleman's own records to compare, and Keith Jarrett's Belonging to hear Stenson's Nordic colleagues in a quartet setting from twenty years before."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-from-gagarins-point-of-view":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio, Svensson on piano, Dan Berglund on double bass and Magnus Öström on drums, plays original tunes that sound as much like pop songs as jazz: simple, singable melodies, clear verse-and-chorus shapes and grooves that borrow from rock. The title track opens with a hushed, floating theme that slowly builds; elsewhere there are bluesy, driving pieces and tender ballads. The piano trio format is traditional, but the sound is clean, modern and emotional.","The trio had formed in Stockholm in the early 1990s and already had several albums out in Sweden. This was the record that first reached audiences across Europe through the German label ACT, which had begun to promote a new, rock-influenced European jazz. The title nods to the Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin, the first man in space, and the record's mood of wonder and distance matches it.","Listen to how often Berglund doubles the piano melody on bass, a pop arranging technique rarely used in jazz trios. Öström's drumming often follows rock patterns, with a backbeat, but he plays them with jazz brushes and touch. Svensson sometimes plays the same simple phrase over and over, building intensity through repetition rather than through fast runs, a sign of his love of pop and minimalism.","The album marked the start of the trio's international breakthrough. Critics praised its freshness and accessibility, and younger listeners who had never bought jazz took to it. Some purists complained that it was too close to pop. Over time it has been seen as the beginning of a European piano-trio movement that includes bands like GoGo Penguin.","Start with the title track and notice how simply the melody is stated, then how much the trio builds from it. Listen to the bass doubling the piano. Then compare with Bill Evans's trio to hear the tradition, and with Radiohead's OK Computer for the rock mood the trio admired."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-seven-days-of-falling":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio at full strength: piano, double bass and drums, now coloured by electronic effects, distorted bowed bass and studio processing. Some tracks, like \"Ballad for the Unborn\", are slow, aching songs; others, such as the title track and \"Mingle in the Mincing-Machine\", build to rock-like climaxes with crashing drums and repeated piano riffs. The album moves between delicacy and noise, and its emotional arc is clear even to listeners who know little about jazz.","By 2003 e.s.t. had become one of the most popular jazz groups in Europe, playing rock venues and festivals as well as jazz clubs. Released by ACT, Seven Days of Falling was a major commercial success, especially in Sweden and Germany. The early 2000s were the era of Radiohead's Kid A, and many young jazz musicians were drawn to electronics, atmosphere and song form; e.s.t. led the way among European piano trios.","Listen to Berglund's bowed bass on some tracks: he runs it through distortion and effects pedals so that it sounds like an electric guitar. Svensson sometimes plays inside the piano, damping the strings. The trio recorded largely live in the studio, then added layers and processing afterwards, so the album is a blend of real-time playing and studio construction, more like a rock record than a traditional jazz date.","Seven Days of Falling was a critical and commercial triumph and is often named as e.s.t.'s finest album. It drew a young audience to jazz and filled concert halls across Europe. Traditionalists criticised the use of effects and the pop-like structures; many younger critics saw it as the future of the piano trio. It is now regarded as a classic of 2000s European jazz.","Start with \"Ballad for the Unborn\" for the melody, then play the title track loud. Listen for the distorted bass and how the trio builds a climax. Then compare with Radiohead's Kid A, and with GoGo Penguin's v2.0, which carries this approach further into electronica."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-leucocyte":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio's last studio album, and its most experimental. Alongside a few melodic, song-like pieces are long passages of free improvisation, electronic noise, processed piano and distorted bass. The four-part title suite at the end stretches into near-ambient soundscapes, with drones and textures that barely resemble a piano trio. The mood is darker and more restless than on earlier albums, as if the band were deliberately breaking apart its own style.","The music was recorded in Sydney in January 2007 during a tour of Australia, in a few days of largely improvised sessions. Svensson died in June 2008 in a diving accident near Stockholm, at the age of forty-four, and Leucocyte was released a few months later. The album therefore became a farewell, though the band had intended it as a new beginning. Further music from the same sessions was released as the album 301 in 2012.","Listen to how much of the album was improvised in the studio rather than composed in advance: the trio would start with a simple idea or sound and let it grow. Some passages use the piano as a source for electronic processing, so that the notes are smeared and stretched. The title, the name of a white blood cell, reflects the band's interest in organic, cell-like growth in music.","Leucocyte's release so soon after Svensson's death made calm judgment difficult. Many critics praised it as a bold step forward; some found the electronic passages self-indulgent. With time it has come to be heard as evidence of where e.s.t. might have gone, and it is often discussed alongside 301. Svensson's death left a gap in European jazz that many musicians have spoken about.","Start with one of the melodic pieces, then listen to the title suite in full, preferably on headphones. Notice how the piano sound is changed by electronics. Then compare with Seven Days of Falling to hear how far the band had travelled, and with Aphex Twin's ambient work for the textures they were drawn to."],"molvaer-khmer":["Nils Petter Molvær's trumpet floats over electronic beats, samples, ambient textures and guitars. Some tracks are slow and spacious, with long, echoing trumpet notes over drones; others lock into drum-and-bass and trip-hop rhythms, with layered guitar noise and pulsing programmed drums. Molvær's tone is breathy and melancholy, often processed with effects. The album sounds like a cross between Miles Davis's electric period, the ECM aesthetic and the club music of the 1990s.","Molvær had played in the Norwegian group Masqualero and as a sideman for ECM before this, his debut as a leader. It was a surprising release for the label, which was known for acoustic chamber jazz. The late 1990s saw drum-and-bass, trip-hop and electronic music dominate European club culture, and a new generation of Norwegian musicians, sometimes called the Nu jazz scene, set out to combine improvisation with electronics.","Listen to how many sounds come from samples and loops rather than live instruments; Molvær treats the studio as a band member. The guitar of Eivind Aarset adds textures that sound more like synthesisers than a guitar. Notice too that the trumpet often plays very little, a few notes repeated or sustained, leaving room for the electronic backdrop. The title refers to the Khmer people of Cambodia, though the music makes no direct reference to their tradition.","Khmer was a major success and is considered one of the founding albums of Nu jazz. It was widely praised for its fresh approach and sold far beyond the usual audience for an ECM jazz record. Some jazz purists objected to the electronics and simple grooves. It influenced a wave of Nordic musicians who combined jazz and electronica, and it is often named as one of the most important European jazz albums of the 1990s.","Start with the title track and listen to the trumpet floating over the electronic pulse. Then try a faster, beat-driven track. Notice the guitar textures. Then compare with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew and In a Silent Way, the electric records it grows from, and with Massive Attack's Mezzanine for its trip-hop cousins."],"molvaer-solid-ether":["Nils Petter Molvær's second album for ECM pushes further into electronic music. The beats are harder and faster, often drawing on drum-and-bass, while the trumpet weaves above in long, cool, echoing lines. Distorted guitar, samples and turntables add layers of noise and texture. The Norwegian singer Sidsel Endresen appears on some tracks, adding a wordless, strange vocal colour. The album moves between dense, driving pieces and quieter, ambient passages.","After Khmer's success, Molvær toured widely with his band and played jazz festivals and club venues alike. The turn of the millennium was a fertile time for Norwegian music, with labels and musicians exploring the boundaries between jazz, electronica and ambient. Solid Ether built on the first album's ideas, with a band that had grown together on stage, and was again released by ECM, which by now accepted that its catalogue could include electronics.","Listen to how the drum programming and live drumming are mixed, so that you often cannot tell which is which. Molvær processes his trumpet heavily on some tracks, adding delay and filtering until it sounds almost like a synthesiser. The vocal pieces use Endresen's improvised, wordless singing, an approach she developed in her own work. The title is a paradox: something that should be weightless made solid.","Solid Ether was well received, though some critics felt it repeated Khmer's formula. Others praised its harder edge and stronger beats. It confirmed Molvær as the leading figure of Nordic Nu jazz and was popular in clubs as well as jazz venues. Today it is often seen as the second panel of a pair with Khmer.","Start with one of the fast tracks and listen for the trumpet riding over the drum-and-bass rhythm. Then try a slower, ambient piece. Notice how the electronics and live instruments blend. Compare with Roni Size's New Forms and Squarepusher's Hard Normal Daddy for the electronic side, and with Khmer for the starting point."],"rava-the-pilgrim-and-the-stars":["Enrico Rava's trumpet leads a quartet with John Abercrombie on guitar, Palle Danielsson on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. The pieces are Rava's own: lyrical, often melancholy melodies that open into free, spacious improvisation. Rava's tone is warm and singing, with a hint of Italian opera in his phrasing, and Abercrombie's guitar adds a cloud of chords and fast, fluid lines. The music moves between gentle ballads and passages of loose, open energy.","Rava, born in Trieste and raised in Turin, had played with Steve Lacy and Gato Barbieri and spent years in New York and Buenos Aires before this, his first record for ECM. It was recorded in 1975 in Germany. ECM was then building a catalogue of European and American musicians playing a new, open style of jazz, and Rava brought an Italian lyricism to it that would become his trademark.","Listen to how Rava often plays short, simple melodic fragments, then repeats them with small variations, a technique that recalls Miles Davis but has a different, more operatic warmth. The Nordic rhythm section is the same pair, Danielsson and Christensen, who played with Keith Jarrett's European Quartet, giving the record a family resemblance to Belonging. Abercrombie's guitar often fills the role of a piano, but with a lighter, more transparent sound.","The album was well received and established Rava as a European voice to watch. It has remained a favourite among fans of 1970s ECM, though it has been somewhat overshadowed by his later, more famous records. Critics have praised its blend of Italian lyricism and Nordic space.","Start with the title track and listen to Rava's tone, warm and singing. Notice Abercrombie's guitar behind him. Then compare with Miles Davis's Milestones to hear Rava's model, and with Stańko's Balladyna to hear another European trumpeter on ECM in the same year."],"petrucciani-pianism":["Michel Petrucciani leads a piano trio with Palle Danielsson on bass and Eliot Zigmund on drums. The album mixes his own compositions with standards, among them Cole Porter's \"Night and Day\". Petrucciani's playing is bright, powerful and lyrical, with crystal-clear runs, strong left-hand chords and a sense of joy in every phrase. The trio swings with ease, and the ballads have a tender, singing quality that recalls Bill Evans, the pianist he most admired.","Petrucciani, born in France with a rare bone disease that limited his height to about one metre, moved to the United States in the early 1980s, where he joined Charles Lloyd's band. In 1985 he signed with the newly revived Blue Note Records, often described as its first European artist, and Pianism was his first album for the label. He was then in his early twenties and already considered one of the most gifted pianists of his generation.","Listen to his left hand: despite his small frame, Petrucciani produced a full, powerful sound and played wide chords, using a mechanical extension device to reach the pedals. Danielsson, a Swede, had played with Keith Jarrett's European Quartet, so the trio has a hint of the ECM sound. Notice how Petrucciani often restates a melody after his solo with fresh harmonies, a habit learned from Bill Evans.","Pianism was warmly received and helped establish Petrucciani as an international star. Critics praised his technique and lyricism, though some felt he leaned too heavily on Evans. Over time his originality has been more widely recognised, and the album remains a favourite among his Blue Note records.","Start with \"Night and Day\" and listen for how Petrucciani reshapes the familiar melody. Then try one of his own ballads. Notice the strength of his left hand. Then compare with Bill Evans's Sunday at the Village Vanguard to hear his model, and with Keith Jarrett's Standards, Vol. 1."],"petrucciani-power-of-three":["A live trio of piano, guitar and saxophone with no bass or drums: Michel Petrucciani with the guitarist Jim Hall and, on some pieces, the saxophonist Wayne Shorter. The music is intimate and conversational, with standards like \"In a Sentimental Mood\" alongside originals by the players, including Shorter's \"Limbo\". Petrucciani supplies bass lines and rhythm with his left hand, Hall adds soft, glowing chords and melodic lines, and Shorter's saxophone floats above, enigmatic and lyrical.","The concert took place at the Montreux Jazz Festival in July 1986, and the album was released by Blue Note the following year. Hall was a veteran guitarist who had played with Sonny Rollins and Bill Evans; Shorter was a founder of Weather Report and a former member of Miles Davis's quintet. Petrucciani, then in his mid-twenties, was the youngest player on stage, and the concert showed how highly his elders regarded him.","Without a bassist, listen to how Petrucciani and Hall share the role: sometimes the piano plays the bass line, sometimes the guitar. Shorter rarely played acoustic jazz in this period, as Weather Report was electric, so his presence here is a rare treat. Notice how the three musicians leave space for each other, with almost no moments when all three play at full volume.","Power of Three was praised for its intimacy and the rare meeting of these three musicians. Some critics found the lack of a rhythm section limiting; others thought it gave the music unusual clarity. It remains a favourite among fans of Petrucciani and Shorter and is often recommended as an example of chamber jazz in a live setting.","Start with \"In a Sentimental Mood\" and listen to how the three instruments share the melody. Then try \"Limbo\" for Shorter's writing. Notice the absence of drums and how the pulse is kept. Then compare with Jim Hall's playing on Sonny Rollins's The Bridge, and with Weather Report's Heavy Weather to hear Shorter electric."],"grappelli-jalousie":["Stéphane Grappelli and the classical violinist Yehudi Menuhin play popular songs of the 1930s, by composers such as George Gershwin, Cole Porter and Jerome Kern, along with Jacob Gade's tango \"Jalousie\". Grappelli improvises freely with his light, swinging tone; Menuhin, less at home in jazz, mostly plays written parts with his rich classical sound. The contrast between the two violinists is the point: one dances, the other sings, and both enjoy the meeting.","Menuhin, one of the most famous classical violinists of the century, had first played with Grappelli on British television and was charmed by his improvising. Their collaboration led to a series of records for EMI through the 1970s and early 1980s, and this was the first. Grappelli, then in his mid-sixties, was enjoying a late revival of his career in Britain, where he had spent the war years, and these records brought him a wider audience.","Listen to how the two violinists divide the work: Menuhin often takes the melody straight while Grappelli weaves around it, and when Menuhin attempts something like an improvised line, you can hear how different his phrasing is. The arrangements were written for Menuhin, who could not improvise, so his parts are carefully notated jazz phrases. Grappelli's violin has a lighter, faster vibrato, an immediate clue to which is which.","The album was a popular success and introduced Grappelli to many classical listeners. Jazz critics were sometimes dismissive of Menuhin's stiff swing, while classical critics found the music lightweight. Yet the warmth of the meeting has kept these records popular, and they are often cited as early examples of classical–jazz crossover.","Start with \"Jalousie\" and listen for the two different violin tones. Notice how Grappelli plays around the beat while Menuhin stays on it. Then compare with Grappelli and Django Reinhardt's swing version of Bach's double concerto, and with the original Bach for two violins."],"grappelli-young-django":["Stéphane Grappelli leads a string quartet of jazz: his violin, the guitars of Philip Catherine and Larry Coryell, and the double bass of Niels-Henning Ørsted Pedersen. The material includes tunes associated with Django Reinhardt and new pieces in his spirit. The two guitarists play with fire and speed, sometimes trading phrases, while Grappelli's violin glides on top with elegant, effortless swing. The sound is the Quintette du Hot Club updated for the 1970s.","Grappelli had co-founded the Quintette du Hot Club de France with Django Reinhardt in Paris in 1934, and after Reinhardt's death in 1953 he carried their music for decades. By the late 1970s he was touring the world in his seventies. This album, made for the German label MPS, paired him with younger guitarists who had grown up with jazz-rock: Coryell from America and Catherine, a Belgian, both deeply admirers of Reinhardt.","Listen to how Catherine and Coryell divide the role Reinhardt once played alone: one keeps the rhythm, the other solos, then they swap, and sometimes both solo at once. Ørsted Pedersen, a Danish bassist, was a virtuoso who could play melodic lines as fast as a guitarist. Notice how Grappelli's phrasing, after more than forty years, still carries the same light swing as on his records with Reinhardt.","The album was well received and is often cited as one of the best of Grappelli's late career. Critics praised the energy of the guitarists and Grappelli's undimmed playing. Some found the more modern guitar styles out of place, but most heard it as a lively tribute that kept Reinhardt's music alive.","Start with one of the up-tempo tunes and listen to the guitars trading phrases. Then notice Grappelli's violin. Compare with the original Quintette du Hot Club records, Minor Swing and Djangology, to hear how the music has changed and stayed the same."],"joachim-kuhn-colors-live-from-leipzig":["A duo concert by Joachim Kühn on piano and Ornette Coleman on alto saxophone. The music is free and energetic, with Coleman's original tunes as a starting point for wide-ranging improvisation. Kühn's piano moves between dense clusters, flowing runs and passages of classical clarity, while Coleman plays his distinctive bluesy, crying alto lines. The two players listen intently, building and releasing tension together, and the concert has the feel of a long, intense conversation.","Coleman had rarely worked with pianists since the late 1950s, when he abandoned the instrument to free his music from fixed harmony. Kühn, born in Leipzig in 1944, had left communist East Germany in the 1960s and become a leading figure in European free jazz. The concert took place in Leipzig in 1996, Kühn's home city, a few years after German reunification, and was released by Coleman's own Harmolodic label through Verve.","Listen to how Kühn avoids playing standard chords under Coleman: he responds with lines and clusters instead, matching Coleman's harmolodic theory, in which melody, harmony and rhythm are all equal. Kühn had studied classical piano in Leipzig, and some of his runs recall Bach. Notice too the symbolism of the concert itself: an exile returning home with one of the founders of free jazz.","The album was praised as a rare and successful meeting of Coleman with a pianist, and it is often considered one of Kühn's finest recordings. Some critics felt the music lacked the earthiness of Coleman's own bands. Others found it a fascinating glimpse of how Coleman's music could work with a European, classically trained partner.","Start at the beginning and follow how the two players react to each other. Notice Kühn's clusters and Coleman's crying alto. Then compare with Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come and his Dancing in Your Head, and with Cecil Taylor's Unit Structures for another approach to free piano."],"shabaka-hutchings-wisdom-of-elders":["Shabaka Hutchings's tenor saxophone with a band of South African musicians, billed as Shabaka and the Ancestors. The music is spiritual jazz: long, chant-like melodies over rolling, polyrhythmic drums and percussion, with the voice of Siyabonga Mthembu adding sung prayers and invocations. The tracks build slowly, the saxophone and alto interweaving like two singers, and the whole album has the feeling of a ceremony, both joyful and solemn.","Hutchings, born in London and raised partly in Barbados, had been visiting South Africa and playing with musicians in Johannesburg's jazz scene. The album was recorded there in a single day in 2015 and released by Gilles Peterson's Brownswood label. It came at the start of a boom in London jazz, but it looked beyond London, connecting British jazz with South Africa's long tradition, from Abdullah Ibrahim to the exiled Blue Notes.","Listen to how the melodies often move in short, repeating cycles, similar to South African church and protest music. Tumi Mogorosi's drumming draws on both jazz and traditional South African rhythms. Notice too that the vocals are not lyrics in the pop sense but chants and invocations, giving the album a ritual feel. The title points to the idea of learning from ancestors, a theme in both African spiritual tradition and jazz.","The album was widely praised and helped make Hutchings one of the most talked-about figures in jazz. Critics compared it to the spiritual jazz of Pharoah Sanders and Alice Coltrane and to South African jazz of the 1960s and 70s. It was seen as a bridge between the London scene and the African diaspora, and its success led to a follow-up, We Are Sent Here by History.","Start at the beginning and let the album build. Listen for the voice and the saxophone weaving together. Notice the drums. Then compare with Pharoah Sanders's Karma and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda, and with Abdullah Ibrahim's Mannenberg for the South African roots."],"shabaka-hutchings-perceive-its-beauty-acknowledge-its-grace":["Shabaka Hutchings's first album under his own name, and a striking change of direction: he plays mostly flutes, including the Japanese bamboo shakuhachi, instead of saxophone. The music is quiet, meditative and spacious, with soft harps, piano, gentle percussion and occasional voices. Guests include the harpist Brandee Younger, the bassist and singer Esperanza Spalding, the electronic producer Floating Points and André 3000 on flute. The mood is calm and contemplative, closer to ambient music than to his earlier, high-energy bands.","Released by Impulse! in 2024, the album followed Hutchings's announcement that he would stop playing saxophone in public at the end of 2023, after more than a decade as the leading saxophonist of the London jazz scene. He had been studying flutes for some years, drawn to their breath and stillness. The album was recorded partly in Los Angeles with musicians from the city's spiritual and ambient scene, including the percussionist and producer Carlos Niño.","Listen to the breath in the flute playing: Hutchings lets you hear air and noise, part of the shakuhachi's tradition, where the breath is as important as the note. The album's slow pace and spare textures are a deliberate contrast to his work with Sons of Kemet and The Comet Is Coming. Notice too how André 3000's appearance links the record to his own flute album, New Blue Sun.","The album was widely praised as a brave and beautiful reinvention, though some fans missed his saxophone and the energy of his earlier bands. Critics placed it in a lineage of spiritual jazz and ambient music, from Alice Coltrane to Harold Budd. It confirmed that Hutchings's search was as much spiritual as musical.","Listen in a quiet space, from beginning to end. Notice the breath in the flute and the spaces between notes. Then compare with Alice Coltrane's Universal Consciousness and Floating Points's Promises with Pharoah Sanders, and with Sons of Kemet's Your Queen Is a Reptile to hear how far he has travelled."],"sons-of-kemet-burn":["Sons of Kemet's first album, with an unusual line-up: Shabaka Hutchings on tenor saxophone and clarinet, Oren Marshall on tuba, and two drummers, Tom Skinner and Seb Rochford. There is no piano or guitar. The tuba plays bass lines and riffs, the two drummers create dense, interlocking rhythms drawn from Caribbean, African and New Orleans traditions, and the saxophone or clarinet soars above. The music is loud, danceable and full of energy, closer to a carnival band than to a jazz club.","Hutchings formed Sons of Kemet in London in 2011, drawing on his Caribbean roots, his experience in London's club and jazz scenes, and his interest in African music. Kemet is an ancient name for Egypt. Released by Naim in 2013, the album was an early landmark in what became a London jazz revival, a scene of young musicians mixing jazz with grime, Afrobeat, calypso and dub. The band won a MOBO award for best jazz act that year.","Listen to how the two drummers play complementary parts rather than doubling each other: one may play a straight pulse while the other plays across it. The tuba's role recalls New Orleans brass bands, where the sousaphone plays bass. Hutchings's clarinet playing reflects his classical training. Notice too the Caribbean rhythms underlying many tracks, from calypso and other Barbadian traditions he grew up with.","Burn was praised for its energy and originality and helped put the new London jazz scene on the map. Critics compared it to Fela Kuti, to New Orleans brass bands and to the Art Ensemble of Chicago. Some found the music repetitive, but many saw it as a bold new direction for British jazz.","Play it loud, and listen for the two drummers. Notice the tuba playing bass lines and riffs. Then compare with Fela Kuti's Zombie for the Afrobeat energy, with the Art Ensemble of Chicago's Fanfare for the Warriors, and with Sons of Kemet's later Your Queen Is a Reptile."],"sons-of-kemet-your-queen-is-a-reptile":["Sons of Kemet's third album: Shabaka Hutchings on tenor saxophone, Theon Cross on tuba, and two drummers, Tom Skinner and Eddie Hick. Each track is named after a woman of African heritage the band honours, among them Harriet Tubman, Angela Davis and Doreen Lawrence, offered as alternatives to the British monarchy. The music is rhythmic, urgent and danceable, with tuba riffs, interlocking drums and soaring saxophone. The poet Joshua Idehen adds spoken words on some tracks.","Released by Impulse!, the historic American jazz label, the album came at the height of the London jazz revival and of debates about race, Britishness and empire after the Brexit vote and the Windrush scandal. Hutchings framed the album as a challenge to the monarchy and a celebration of Black women. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2018, bringing the band to a wide audience.","Listen to the track titles as a list of heroes: each was chosen for a reason, from the Jamaican Maroon leader Nanny of the Maroons to the South African activist Albertina Sisulu. The rhythms draw on Caribbean, African and British club music, with grime and dub echoes. Notice how the tuba often plays the role of a bass guitar in a dancehall band. Hutchings has said the title refers to the unelected power of the monarchy.","The album was widely praised as one of the best jazz records of the decade and was a Mercury Prize nominee. Critics hailed its political force and musical energy. Some found the political framing heavy-handed; others thought it was exactly the point. It confirmed Sons of Kemet as one of the most important bands in British jazz.","Start with the first track and listen for the tuba and drums locking together. Read the track titles and look up the women named. Then compare with Fela Kuti's Coffin for Head of State for another political jazz-adjacent record, and with Little Simz's GREY Area for London's hip-hop of the same moment."],"nubya-garcia-source":["Nubya Garcia's debut full-length album, led by her tenor saxophone with Joe Armon-Jones on keyboards, Daniel Casimir on bass and Sam Jones on drums. The music mixes jazz with dub, cumbia and Caribbean rhythms. The title track is long and dub-influenced, with echoing bass and drums; elsewhere there are ballads and driving, spiritual pieces. Garcia's tenor tone is warm and full, her phrases long and flowing. The Colombian group La Perla appears on one track, adding cumbia rhythms and voices.","Garcia, born in London to a Guyanese mother and a Trinidadian father, grew up in the city's youth jazz programmes, including Tomorrow's Warriors, which nurtured many of the London scene's stars. She had played with Maisha, Nérija and Ezra Collective, and released an EP before this, her debut album, released by Concord Jazz in 2020. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and its release during the pandemic meant it reached audiences mostly through streaming.","Listen to the title track's dub influences: the echoing drums and deep bass recall Jamaican sound systems, which were central to Garcia's London upbringing. The cumbia track with La Perla reflects her interest in Latin American music and connections across the Caribbean. Notice too how Garcia uses space in her solos, letting phrases breathe before building to climaxes.","Source was widely praised and shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, confirming Garcia as one of the leading voices of the London jazz scene. Critics praised her tone and her ability to blend jazz with Caribbean and Latin influences. Some found the album long, but most heard it as a confident and personal debut.","Start with the title track and listen for the dub echoes. Then try the cumbia track with La Perla. Notice Garcia's tone. Then compare with Sonny Rollins's Saxophone Colossus for the tenor tradition, and with Sons of Kemet's Your Queen Is a Reptile and Massive Attack's Blue Lines for London and Bristol's sound-system culture."],"nubya-garcia-odyssey":["Nubya Garcia's second album pairs her tenor saxophone and band with orchestral strings she arranged herself. The music ranges from lush, cinematic ballads to driving grooves rooted in jazz, Caribbean rhythms and R&B. Guest vocalists include the bassist and singer Esperanza Spalding on the title track. Garcia's tenor sound is full and warm as ever, and the strings add depth and drama. The album moves like a journey, from contemplative opening to energetic peaks and reflective close.","Released by Concord Jazz in 2024, four years after Source, Odyssey reflected Garcia's growth as a composer and arranger. She had spent the intervening years touring internationally and widening her ambitions as a writer. The album's string writing places her in a lineage of jazz musicians who have embraced orchestral colour, from Charlie Parker with Strings to Alice Coltrane, and it confirmed the London scene's ambitions beyond the club.","Listen to how the strings are integrated rather than simply added: they often play counter-melodies against Garcia's saxophone, or take over the groove themselves. The arrangements are Garcia's own, and they show a composer thinking in orchestral colours. Notice the Caribbean rhythms underlying some tracks, a continuation of Source's themes. Spalding's vocal on the title track blends with the saxophone in a way that recalls her own chamber-jazz albums.","Odyssey was praised for its ambition and Garcia's growth as a composer and arranger. Critics welcomed the strings and the album's cinematic sweep, though some missed the rawer energy of Source. It was widely seen as a major statement from one of the leading jazz musicians of her generation.","Start with the title track and listen for the strings and Spalding's voice. Notice how the saxophone and strings interact. Then compare with Alice Coltrane's Universal Consciousness for jazz with strings, with Esperanza Spalding's Chamber Music Society, and with Garcia's own Source to hear how her sound has widened."],"jimmy-smith-the-sermon":["The whole first side is one blues, the title track, lasting about twenty minutes at a slow, lazy strut. Jimmy Smith lays down a walking bass line with his feet and left hand while a parade of soloists take long turns: Kenny Burrell on guitar, Tina Brooks on tenor, Lee Morgan on trumpet, Lou Donaldson on alto, and Smith himself, who builds from murmured chords to a full-drawbar roar. Art Blakey keeps a shuffle underneath. The second side holds two shorter, faster tracks with smaller groups.","Smith had arrived in New York only in 1956 and was recording for Blue Note at an astonishing pace, sometimes cutting enough material in one session for several albums. These tracks come from sessions in 1957 and 1958 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Hackensack, and the label released them as albums when it saw fit. Blue Note's founder Alfred Lion encouraged long jam-session takes; the long-playing record had only recently made a twenty-minute blues commercially possible.","The title is not decoration. The tempo is that of a slow church march, and the soloists behave like successive preachers taking the pulpit, each beginning quietly and working towards a shout. Listen to how Smith comps behind the horns with short, stabbing organ chords like a congregation answering. The organ's Leslie speaker, a rotating horn inside a wooden cabinet, is audible as a slow wobble on sustained chords; Smith speeds it up at climaxes.","The album was received as a solid blowing session rather than an event, and some critics found the length self-indulgent. Its reputation grew steadily, partly because its lineup now reads like a roll call of Blue Note's golden age, partly because it codified the slow organ blues as a form. It is now often named as the best entry point to Smith's huge Blue Note catalogue, and to the organ-jazz tradition generally.","Give the title track your full twenty minutes, without skipping. Notice that nothing changes harmonically; the drama is all in tone, rhythm and volume. Follow each soloist's arc, then listen for the moment Smith pulls out all the stops. Afterwards play Horace Silver's The Preacher to hear the same churchgoing impulse in a quintet."],"jimmy-smith-back-at-the-chicken-shack":["A quartet record of medium-tempo grooves: Jimmy Smith on organ, Stanley Turrentine on tenor saxophone, Kenny Burrell on guitar and Donald Bailey on drums. The title track is a loping blues with a riff anyone could hum, stated by organ and tenor together. Elsewhere there is a ballad, a standard and a slow-burning minor blues. With no bass player, Smith supplies the bass on the pedals, and the sound is compact, warm and conversational rather than fiery.","The album was recorded in 1960 but held back by Blue Note until 1963, by which time Smith had left the label for Verve and big-band records. The music therefore belongs to the moment when organ combos were the soundtrack of Black neighbourhood bars across Newark, Philadelphia and Harlem, where a Hammond was often a fixture of the room. Turrentine, newly married to the organist Shirley Scott, was the ideal partner for this kind of bar-room soul.","The title names a kind of place, the roadside chicken joint with an organ in the corner, and the music is tailored to it: tempos that encourage dancing and drinking rather than concentration. Listen for how Smith and Turrentine trade short phrases near the end of the title track, finishing each other's sentences. Burrell rarely solos at length; his chunky rhythm chords hold the groove where a bass player would normally sit.","Released after Smith's departure, it might have been a contractual leftover, but it became one of his most loved records and one of Blue Note's steadiest sellers. Critics now generally rank it alongside The Sermon! as his best work for the label. Turrentine's playing here is often cited as the definitive soul jazz tenor sound, and the album is a common recommendation for listeners new to the organ combo.","Start with the title track and hum the riff after one chorus; that is the point. Then notice the absence of a bass player and listen to Smith's feet doing the work. Compare Turrentine here with his much more lavish CTI album Sugar a decade later to hear how the same voice was dressed up for the 1970s."],"jimmy-smith-the-cat":["Hammond organ against a blaring big band, arranged and conducted by Lalo Schifrin. The title track is a prowling minor-key strut where trumpets and trombones stab out riffs and Smith's organ answers with snarling runs. Elsewhere the album draws on film music, including the theme from the French thriller Joy House, which Schifrin had scored. Tracks are short, tightly arranged and loud, more like soundtrack cues than jam sessions. The organ is always in front, the brass a wall behind.","After moving to Verve, Smith was produced by Creed Taylor, who would later found CTI, and paired with orchestral arrangers to reach a wider market. Schifrin, an Argentine pianist who had worked with Dizzy Gillespie, was beginning a Hollywood career and would soon write the Mission: Impossible theme. The album was a commercial success, and the cinematic, spy-movie atmosphere matched the James Bond boom of 1964.","Listen to how Schifrin voices the brass: rather than swinging section riffs in the Count Basie manner, he uses tight, dissonant chords and sudden silences, the language of film suspense. Smith in turn plays like a lead horn, bending notes with the organ's percussion setting and swells. The album is a direct ancestor of Schifrin's later television and film themes, and a sketch for Taylor's CTI production style.","Jazz critics were cool on Smith's Verve big-band albums, finding them commercial and overarranged compared with his Blue Note sessions. Listeners disagreed, and The Cat sold strongly. Opinion has since softened considerably; the record is admired as a piece of orchestration, and its title track became a standard for organists and a favourite with DJs who prize its punch and brevity.","Play the title track loud, and listen for the call and response between organ and brass. Then put on Schifrin's Mission: Impossible theme and hear the same composer's taste for tension. For contrast, return to The Sermon! and notice how much freedom Smith gave up, and what he gained, by working inside an arrangement."],"jimmy-smith-root-down":["A live set from a Los Angeles club in which Jimmy Smith fronts a young funk rhythm section with electric bass, guitar, drums and congas. The grooves are long and hard: the title track rides a churning bass riff for minutes at a time while Smith digs in with clipped, percussive organ phrases. Covers of soul hits of the day, including Al Green's Let's Stay Together, sit beside blues jams. The sound is raw and close, with audience noise and a room you can feel.","By 1972 the organ-trio circuit that had made Smith a star was shrinking, and soul and funk dominated Black radio. Verve recorded him at the Bombay Bicycle Club in Los Angeles with a band of local session players, among them the bassist Wilton Felder of the Crusaders, aiming to put the organ back into the funk conversation. It was one of Smith's last important records for the label.","The Beastie Boys sampled the title track, Root Down (And Get It), for their own 1994 song Root Down, and the album was a staple of the crate-digging DJs who built early hip-hop beats. Listen to how the electric bass frees Smith's feet: for once he is not playing bass, so his left hand comps chords like a funk guitarist and the organ sounds lighter and more stabbing than on his Blue Note records.","It received little critical attention on release, seen as one more live date from a veteran past his prime. Its afterlife came through sampling and the rare-groove scene of the 1980s and 1990s, when it was reappraised as one of the hardest funk records any jazz organist made. It is now often ranked among Smith's essential recordings, though some jazz writers still prefer his earlier work.","Begin with the title track, and listen for the bass riff and the space between Smith's phrases. Then hear Let's Stay Together and compare it with Al Green's original. If you know the Beastie Boys' Check Your Head and its organ-funk instrumentals, you will hear exactly where they learned the sound."],"grant-green-idle-moments":["Four long tracks played by a sextet: Grant Green on guitar, Joe Henderson on tenor saxophone, Bobby Hutcherson on vibraphone, Duke Pearson on piano, Bob Cranshaw on bass and Al Harewood on drums. The title track, Pearson's slow, hushed blues ballad, lasts about fifteen minutes and sets the mood of the whole record: late-night, unhurried, almost whispered. There is also a version of John Lewis's Django and a gentle Latin-tinged piece. Nothing is loud; everything breathes.","Recorded in November 1963 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1965, the album caught Green at the height of his Blue Note period, when he was playing on dozens of other leaders' sessions. Pearson, a pianist and arranger, was becoming central to the label's sound. The record sits between soul jazz and the more reflective post-bop of Henderson and Hutcherson, both then rising stars at Blue Note.","The title track ran so long because the players misread its structure and took double-length choruses; producer Alfred Lion liked the result, so the other tunes were re-recorded at greater length to fit the LP's sides. Later CD issues include the original shorter takes, which let you hear the change. Listen to Green's solo on the title track: he repeats tiny phrases almost obsessively, a habit that becomes hypnotic at this slow tempo.","The album was not a hit, but it became one of the most cherished records in the Blue Note catalogue, often called Green's finest and one of the great late-night jazz albums. The vibraphone and guitar blend has been widely admired. Debate, where it exists, concerns whether the short or long takes of the shorter tunes are better; the long versions remain the canonical album.","Play the title track at night, quietly, and resist doing anything else. Listen for how the vibraphone and guitar share the same soft register without crowding each other. Then compare Henderson's dry, searching tenor with Green's round tone. For the next step, try Hutcherson's own records or Green's livelier Alive!"],"grant-green-alive":["A live club recording of hard, extended funk. Grant Green leads a band with organ, saxophone, conga and Idris Muhammad's drums through long workouts on soul and pop material of the day, including Kool & the Gang's Let the Music Take Your Mind and Don Covay's Sookie Sookie. Green's guitar is the centre: a dry, biting tone repeating short riffs dozens of times, locked to the drums. The crowd is audible and the band responds to it.","Recorded in 1970 at the Cliché Lounge in Newark, New Jersey, a working club on the organ-bar circuit, during Green's return to Blue Note after a period away from recording. By then soul jazz had turned towards funk, and Green chose current hits by James Brown-era bands rather than standards. Blue Note itself had been sold and was chasing a younger Black audience with more danceable records.","A Tribe Called Quest built Vibes and Stuff on The Low End Theory partly from Green's Down Here on the Ground, the record's slow soul ballad. Listen closely to how Green solos: rather than running scales, he picks one short phrase and repeats it with tiny rhythmic shifts until the band locks to him, a technique closer to a funk guitarist's than a bebop player's. Idris Muhammad's drumming is a lesson in tight, swinging funk.","Jazz critics of the time largely dismissed Green's funk period as commercial. Collectors and DJs in the 1980s and 1990s reversed the judgment, and the album is now valued as one of the most physically exciting live jazz-funk records. Some still argue that Green's earlier Blue Note work is deeper, but few dispute that his rhythmic drive was never stronger than here.","Start with the opening Kool & the Gang cover and let it run; the length is the pleasure. Listen for the repeated guitar figure and the moment the crowd starts shouting. Then hear Down Here on the Ground and put on A Tribe Called Quest's Low End Theory afterwards to catch where it went."],"lou-donaldson-alligator-bogaloo":["A party record of the boogaloo era. The title track is a strutting, almost comic groove built on a riff any listener can remember, with Donaldson's alto bright and tart over Lonnie Smith's organ and a crisp backbeat from the drums. George Benson, then a young organ-band guitarist, plays chunky rhythm and quick, clean solos. The rest of the album mixes more grooves with a ballad and a bebop flourish, all compact and good-humoured.","Recorded in 1967, at the height of the boogaloo dance craze, the album was Donaldson's biggest seller and one of Blue Note's. The label had learned from Lee Morgan's The Sidewinder that a catchy groove at the start of an album could reach jukeboxes and soul radio. Donaldson, a bebop veteran, embraced the move wholeheartedly, and organ combos on the East Coast bar circuit were his natural setting.","The drummer is credited as Leo Morris, the name Idris Muhammad used before his conversion to Islam; his New Orleans training is why the backbeat feels so loose and springy. Listen for Benson's guitar comping on the title track, already precise and rhythmically sure, years before he became a star. Donaldson quotes snatches of pop tunes in his solos, a habit that marks his whole style.","Purists treated the record as evidence of jazz selling out to dance music, and it was rarely discussed seriously at the time. It sold, however, and its reputation has grown among DJs and collectors who value groove records of the period. It is now regarded as a classic of boogaloo jazz, and a document of the early careers of Benson and Lonnie Smith.","Play the title track and move to it; that is its whole purpose. Then listen again for the drums, which are doing far more than they seem. Pair it with Lee Morgan's The Sidewinder and Cannonball Adderley's Mercy, Mercy, Mercy to hear how jazz chased the jukebox in the mid-1960s."],"ramsey-lewis-the-in-crowd":["A piano trio recorded live in a club, with an audience that claps, shouts and laughs throughout. The title track turns a current pop hit into a gospel-blues stomp: Ramsey Lewis plays the tune in big block chords, Eldee Young's bass walks hard and Redd Holt hits a backbeat. The rest of the set mixes standards, blues and pop tunes, each treated the same way, with churchy chords and a light, bouncing swing. It sounds like a very good Friday night.","Recorded in May 1965 at the Bohemian Caverns, a basement club in Washington, D.C., and released on Chess Records' jazz imprint Argo. The trio had been together for a decade and was popular in Chicago but not nationally known. The title song had been a recent hit for the soul singer Dobie Gray, and the trio's version, issued as a single, became a crossover hit and won a Grammy.","The audience is central: its clapping on two and four is effectively a fourth instrument, and Lewis plays to it, leaving gaps for shouts. Listen for the moments in the title track where Lewis thins the texture to a few repeated chords and lets the room fill the gap. The pop song is barely changed; the transformation is entirely rhythmic and harmonic.","It was a commercial phenomenon, a jazz trio album selling to a mass audience, and that made some critics suspicious. Over time it has been understood as part of the broader soul jazz moment alongside Mercy, Mercy, Mercy, and as a model for the live, audience-driven jazz record. Debate continues over whether it counts as jazz at all; Lewis himself shrugged at the question.","Play the title track first and listen for the audience's clapping, then the block chords. Notice how little Lewis decorates the melody. Then hear Ahmad Jamal's Poinciana, an earlier live trio hit, to compare two very different ideas of how a club audience can shape a record."],"ramsey-lewis-sun-goddess":["Ramsey Lewis plugs in. The title track is a long, sunny groove of Fender Rhodes electric piano, chattering percussion, wah-wah guitar and wordless voices chanting in the background, built over a light funk beat. Other tracks move between soft-focus ballads, Latin rhythms and harder funk. Lewis plays acoustic piano too, but the record's identity is the electric keyboard and the warm, communal sound of a band in which everyone is a percussionist as well as a melody player.","By 1974 the trio format that had made Lewis famous was out of step with the times. Maurice White of Earth, Wind & Fire, who had played drums in Lewis's trio in the 1960s, produced the title track and brought members of his band to the session. The record was released on Columbia and became one of the best-selling jazz albums of the year, reaching soul radio and a young audience.","White's presence changes everything: the title track sounds like an Earth, Wind & Fire instrumental, with the same kalimba-like percussion textures and chanted vocals, and Lewis becomes a soloist inside a band sound rather than its centre. Listen to how the electric piano's tremolo blurs into the voices. The record is a hinge between the 1960s soul jazz trio and the late-1970s jazz-funk that would dominate radio.","Jazz critics were mostly dismissive, seeing a fine pianist diluted into background funk. Commercially it was a triumph, and it has since been reassessed by listeners who value the sunny, unforced mood of 1970s jazz-funk. It remains contested ground, a record loved by its audience and still sometimes used as a shorthand for the dilution of jazz in the 1970s.","Begin with the title track and let it unfold; it is about atmosphere, not development. Listen for the voices behind the electric piano. Then play Earth, Wind & Fire's That's the Way of the World to hear the shared sound, and go back to The In Crowd to hear how far Lewis travelled in nine years."],"les-mccann-swiss-movement":["A live festival set by a quintet co-led by pianist and singer Les McCann and tenor saxophonist Eddie Harris, with Benny Bailey on trumpet. The opening track, Compared to What, is a churning protest song over a relentless minor-key vamp, with McCann's raw voice and stabbing piano driving it forward. The remaining tracks are long instrumentals, gospel-tinged blues and grooves where Harris's light tenor and Bailey's trumpet take extended solos over a band that plays as if lives depended on it.","Recorded at the Montreux Jazz Festival in Switzerland in June 1969. McCann and Harris had not played together before; they were thrown together for the festival and barely rehearsed, with Bailey, an American expatriate in Europe, joining at short notice. Compared to What was written by Gene McDaniels and attacks the Vietnam War and the hypocrisy of American life. The album was released by Atlantic and sold extremely well.","The excitement is partly nerves: the band was improvising arrangements on the spot, and you can hear moments where players work out who will solo next. Listen to Bailey, a musician little known in America, whose solos are among the highlights. The title is a pun on the precision of Swiss watches, ironic for a set held together by instinct and goodwill.","It was one of the best-selling jazz albums of its time. Compared to What became a soul jazz standard and a protest anthem covered by many artists. Critics have since praised it as a rare example of a live recording that captures the danger and joy of improvisation at full stretch, while some regard its long instrumental tracks as uneven.","Start with Compared to What and listen to how the band keeps the vamp going while McCann pushes against it. Then listen for Bailey's trumpet. Pair it with Nina Simone's protest songs of the same period, and with Eddie Harris's own The Electrifying Eddie Harris, for the saxophonist's studio sound."],"eddie-harris-the-electrifying-eddie-harris":["Eddie Harris plays tenor saxophone through the Varitone, an electronic attachment that could add an octave below the note, echo and tremolo, giving his horn a thick, doubled, slightly eerie sound. The opener, Listen Here, is a funky riff over a loping groove that stretches for several minutes and became his biggest hit. The rest of the album mixes more riff tunes with ballads and fast pieces, often with a brass section punching behind him.","Harris was signed to Atlantic, which was eager to reach soul radio, and the label's producer Joel Dorn encouraged him to experiment. The Varitone, made by the Selmer company and introduced in the mid-1960s, was one of the first electronic devices for wind instruments. Harris was among its earliest and most committed users. Listen Here was released as a single and crossed over to the rhythm and blues charts.","The octave effect makes a single saxophone sound like two playing in unison, and Harris exploits this for riffs that seem to come from a section rather than a soloist. Listen for the moments where the electronic doubling slips slightly, producing a wobbling, phasing tone. Harris was also a technically gifted player in the high register, and his fast, light runs on the ballads show he did not need electronics to dazzle.","Listen Here was a genuine hit and the album sold strongly. Critics were divided: some heard novelty, others real innovation. The Varitone has largely disappeared, but Harris's experiments are now credited as an important step towards the electric jazz of the following years, alongside Miles Davis's turn to electric instruments. The album remains a favourite of DJs and soul jazz collectors.","Start with Listen Here and focus on the saxophone's doubled sound. Notice how the groove barely changes; the interest is in the timbre and the riff. Then hear Swiss Movement to compare Harris's acoustic tenor on stage, and Miles Davis's In a Silent Way for where the electric idea went next."],"donald-byrd-a-new-perspective":["A jazz septet joined by a wordless gospel choir. Donald Byrd's trumpet, Hank Mobley's tenor saxophone, Herbie Hancock's piano and Kenny Burrell's guitar play blues and spirituals while the voices hum, chant and swell in arrangements by Duke Pearson. The best-known piece, Pearson's Cristo Redentor, is slow and solemn, the choir carrying a mournful melody while the trumpet floats above. Elsewhere the music is joyful and swinging, a jazz band in church.","Recorded in January 1963 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, the album reflected Byrd's interest in the roots of Black American music; he was studying composition and would later teach at Howard University. The use of a choir in a small-group jazz record was unusual. The civil rights movement was at its height, and gospel and spirituals carried political as well as spiritual weight.","Cristo Redentor, named after the statue above Rio de Janeiro, is by Pearson, and its slow, swaying melody owes as much to a hymn as to bossa nova. Listen to Hancock, then a young sideman before his own fame, who plays spare, churchy chords. The choir never sings words, which keeps the record from sounding like a concert spiritual and makes the voices another section of the band.","The album was well received and Cristo Redentor became a minor hit, covered by Harvey Mandel and others. Some critics found the choir sentimental. Its standing has grown with time, and it is now seen as an important precursor of the spiritual and gospel-inflected jazz of later decades, and of Byrd's own move towards voices in his 1970s records.","Start with Cristo Redentor and listen to how the choir and trumpet interact. Then hear the faster tracks for Hancock and Mobley. Compare it with Mahalia Jackson's recordings to hear the gospel tradition it draws on, and with Black Byrd to hear Byrd's later use of voices."],"donald-byrd-black-byrd":["Smooth, insistent jazz-funk produced by Larry Mizell. Donald Byrd's trumpet, often treated with echo, floats over electric bass lines, clavinet, synthesiser and soft group vocals that chant short phrases rather than sing songs. The tracks are compact, built on repeating grooves rather than chord changes, and the mood ranges from the brooding title track to the airy Flight Time and the laid-back Mr. Thomas. It sounds more like a soul record than anything Blue Note had made.","Byrd was teaching at Howard University and looking for a new direction when he began working with Larry and Fonce Mizell, brothers with Motown experience who ran a production company. Their combination of jazz soloing, soul production and catchy vocal hooks was entirely new for Blue Note. Black Byrd became the best-selling album in the label's history up to that point, reaching soul and pop audiences.","DJ Premier sampled Flight Time for Nas's N.Y. State of Mind, and the album was a deep well for hip-hop producers generally. Listen to how little Byrd actually plays: the trumpet is one colour among many, entering briefly, and much of the record's identity comes from the bass and the vocal hooks. The Mizells' Motown background shows in the tight, layered rhythm arrangements.","Jazz critics were scathing, and Byrd was accused of abandoning jazz for commerce. The public disagreed, and the album sold in huge numbers. Its reputation was transformed by hip-hop sampling and the rare-groove scene, and it is now seen as a founding record of jazz-funk. The argument over whether it is jazz at all has never fully gone away.","Start with Flight Time and listen to the bass and vocal hooks, then put on Nas's N.Y. State of Mind. Then hear the title track. Compare it with A New Perspective to hear Byrd's career in two records, and with Herbie Hancock's Head Hunters, from the same year."],"donald-byrd-places-and-spaces":["The Mizell production style at its most lush. Strings swirl over soft disco-era beats, voices sing short, airy hooks, and Donald Byrd's trumpet floats in and out like a guest at his own party. The opener, Change (Makes You Want to Hustle), is a gliding dance track; the title piece is slower and more dreamlike; Wind Parade is light and buoyant. Everything is polished, warm and designed for movement rather than close listening.","By 1975 the Mizell brothers were among the most successful producers in jazz and soul, working with Byrd, Bobbi Humphrey, Johnny Hammond and others. Disco was rising, and the hustle was the dance of the moment. Byrd was also guiding his Howard University students in the Blackbyrds, a group whose records sold heavily. Places and Spaces was his most sophisticated collaboration with the Mizells.","Listen for how the strings are used: not as a romantic wash but as rhythmic punctuation, short and crisp, borrowed from Philadelphia soul production. The record's sense of space comes from echo applied to voices and trumpet, a studio technique that makes the sound seem to float. Byrd plays fewer notes than on any earlier record, and his solos are almost melodic decorations.","Jazz critics largely ignored or dismissed it. It was a solid seller and has become one of the most loved records of the Mizell sound, reappraised through DJ culture and sampling. Many now regard it as the high point of Byrd's 1970s work. Its sleekness divides listeners: some hear a perfect mood record, others the beginning of jazz dissolving into background music.","Play Change (Makes You Want to Hustle) first and notice the crisp strings and the light beat. Then hear the title track for its dreamlike atmosphere. Compare it with Black Byrd to hear how the Mizell sound smoothed out in two years, and with Roy Ayers's Everybody Loves the Sunshine for kindred warmth."],"bobbi-humphrey-blacks-and-blues":["Bobbi Humphrey's flute floats over grooves produced by Larry Mizell: electric bass lines, chiming electric keyboards, light percussion and soft group vocals singing short hooks. The album is compact, six tracks, and the mood is warm, airy and unhurried. Harlem River Drive is the best known, a gliding groove with a vocal refrain, but every track works the same way, with the flute as the singing voice and the rhythm section never letting the groove drop.","Humphrey had been signed to Blue Note in 1971 and her first records were conventional soul jazz. Producer Larry Mizell, fresh from Donald Byrd's Black Byrd, gave her the same treatment, and the result sold well. Female instrumentalists were rare at Blue Note and in jazz generally, and Humphrey's success opened doors. The album came out in the same year as Byrd's record and shares much of its sound.","Listen to how the flute is recorded: close, breathy and slightly echoed, so that it sounds almost like a voice. The Mizells write the melodies to suit the flute's range rather than adapting horn lines. Harlem River Drive names an actual Manhattan highway, and the music evokes cruising rather than any specific story. The vocal hooks are sung by a small group, not soloists.","It was a solid seller and helped establish the Mizell sound. Critics paid it little attention. In the 1980s and 1990s it became a favourite of British jazz-funk and rare-groove DJs, and its reputation has grown. It is now seen as one of the defining records of 1970s Blue Note, though still rarely discussed in mainstream jazz histories.","Begin with Harlem River Drive and listen for the flute's breathy, singing tone. Then play the title track. Compare it with Donald Byrd's Black Byrd to hear the shared production, and with Lonnie Liston Smith's Expansions for another take on gentle, danceable jazz."],"george-benson-breezin":["Six tracks of polished, relaxed guitar music with string arrangements by Claus Ogerman. The title track, by Bobby Womack, is a gliding instrumental where George Benson's clean, round guitar plays the melody over a light groove. The one vocal, This Masquerade by Leon Russell, has Benson singing in a warm, supple voice and scatting in unison with his own guitar solo. Everything is smooth, tasteful and carefully produced, with space for long, fluent guitar improvisations.","Benson had moved from CTI to Warner Bros., where producer Tommy LiPuma aimed to make a record that could reach pop radio without losing the jazz soloing. The sessions took place in Los Angeles. The album became the first jazz record to reach the top of the Billboard album chart and went multi-platinum. This Masquerade won the Grammy for Record of the Year.","The unison singing and guitar is the trick: Benson sings exactly the notes he plays, a technique he had practised for years, and the combination creates a timbre that sounds like a new instrument. Listen to the solos, which are genuinely complex jazz improvisations, fast and harmonically sophisticated, even when the setting is soft. The string arrangements are subtle, placed far back in the mix.","It was a massive commercial success and earned several Grammys. Jazz critics were divided; some saw a great guitarist selling out, others noted the quality of the playing. It is often cited as a founding record of smooth jazz, which colours its reputation. Many guitarists regard the soloing as among Benson's finest, and the album remains a touchstone of 1970s production.","Start with This Masquerade and listen for the moment the voice and guitar become one. Then hear the title track for Benson's tone. Compare with Wes Montgomery, Benson's model, and with Benson's earlier organ-band work on Lou Donaldson's Alligator Bogaloo to hear how far he came."],"grover-washington-jr-mister-magic":["Four long tracks of warm, sensual jazz-funk. The title track is a slow, sultry groove that stretches over nine minutes, with Grover Washington Jr.'s soprano saxophone singing a simple, insistent melody over electric piano, bass and percussion. Bob James's arrangements add strings and brass in soft layers. The other tracks are similar: relaxed tempos, long solos, and a mood that is intimate rather than energetic, built for late-night listening.","The album was released on Kudu, Creed Taylor's label for soul-oriented records, an offshoot of CTI. Bob James, Taylor's chief arranger, was at the height of his influence. The title track, written by Ralph MacDonald and William Salter, became a crossover hit and the album crossed from jazz to soul and pop audiences. It was Washington's commercial breakthrough. The CTI family of labels gave its records matching gatefold sleeves and glossy photography, so the look sold the mood before a note was heard.","Listen to the percussion on the title track: the congas and shakers give the groove its gentle propulsion, more important than the drums. Washington's soprano tone is round and vocal, without the nasal edge many soprano players have. The title track's length is deliberate; the melody returns again and again, each time slightly varied, and the effect is hypnotic.","It was a major hit and established Washington as a star. Jazz critics were mixed, though many admired his tone. It is often cited as a key record in the development of smooth jazz, and the title track has been widely covered. Its reputation has held up better than much of the genre it helped create.","Start with the title track and let it run; the length is part of the effect. Listen for the percussion and the soprano's singing tone. Then hear Winelight, from five years later, and Bob James's One, to hear the arranger's hand."],"grover-washington-jr-winelight":["Six tracks of polished, intimate music. The title track is a slow, glowing groove for soprano saxophone. The best known, Just the Two of Us, is a love song sung by Bill Withers, with Grover Washington Jr.'s saxophone weaving around the voice and taking a long solo. Marcus Miller plays bass, Steve Gadd drums, and the sound is soft, warm and impeccably recorded. Nothing is rushed; every note sits in place.","Washington had moved to Elektra, and the album was produced with Ralph MacDonald and William Salter, who co-wrote Just the Two of Us with Withers. The song became a huge crossover hit and won the Grammy for Best R&B Song; the album won for Best Jazz Fusion Performance. It defined the sound of what would soon be called quiet storm and smooth jazz radio.","Listen for Marcus Miller's bass, which is unusually melodic and rhythmically playful, foreshadowing his later work with Miles Davis. Withers's vocal is relaxed and conversational, recorded close, and Washington's saxophone answers him like a second singer; the light, Latin-tinged percussion keeps the song moving so it never sags into a slow ballad.","It was a huge commercial success and won Grammys. Jazz critics were mostly dismissive, seeing it as background music. Over time it has become a touchstone of smooth jazz and quiet storm radio, loved by its audience and sometimes mocked by jazz purists. Just the Two of Us has been covered and sampled widely, and remains one of the most recognisable songs of its era.","Start with Just the Two of Us and listen for how the saxophone and voice converse. Then hear the title track for Washington's soprano tone. Compare with Mister Magic to hear how his sound smoothed further, and with George Benson's Breezin' for a parallel crossover."],"roy-ayers-everybody-loves-the-sunshine":["Four minutes of slow, shimmering heat. Roy Ayers's vibraphone and synthesisers lay down a hazy, swaying groove; voices, his own among them, sing a gentle, repeated refrain about the pleasures of a summer day. The tempo is languid, the bass round and slow, and the sound feels like warm air moving. There is no solo to speak of; the song is all mood, a single texture held for its whole length.","Ayers recorded the song with Roy Ayers Ubiquity for an album of the same name on Polydor. He had moved from hard bop vibraphone to soul, funk and early disco, and was working in New York with a rotating band. The song was not a major hit on release but became a staple of DJs and of the community block parties that would shape hip-hop.","Mary J. Blige's 1994 song My Life is built on it, and producers from many genres have sampled or replayed its chords and refrain. Listen closely to the vibraphone: Ayers uses it for sustained, shimmering chords rather than fast runs, blending it with synthesiser so the two instruments become one hazy sound. The song's simplicity is its strength; it barely changes, and that is the point.","It was a modest success on release. Its reputation has grown enormously through sampling and DJ culture, and it is now regarded as one of the defining soul-jazz songs of the 1970s, a symbol of summer for several generations. Some jazz critics still see Ayers's 1970s work as a loss, but few dispute the song's influence.","Play it on a warm day and do nothing else. Listen for the blend of vibraphone and synthesiser, then for the slow bass. Then hear Mary J. Blige's My Life to hear how it was reused, and Donald Byrd's Places and Spaces for kindred warmth."],"lonnie-liston-smith-expansions":["Lonnie Liston Smith and the Cosmic Echoes play gentle, spacious jazz-funk with a spiritual undertow. The title track rides a buoyant, endlessly circling bass line under washes of electric piano and synthesiser, with a soft vocal from Donald Smith, the leader's brother, delivering a message of consciousness and unity. Other tracks lean towards Latin percussion or slower, meditative drift. The textures are soft, the grooves insistent, the mood hopeful and calm.","Smith had played with Pharoah Sanders, whose spiritual jazz albums used drones and devotional moods, and with Miles Davis's electric band of 1972 and 1973. On Bob Thiele's Flying Dutchman label he combined both influences with funk rhythms. Expansions sold well and found an audience well beyond jazz, especially in British clubs where it became an anthem of the jazz-funk scene.","Listen to the bass line on the title track: it never stops, a single circular figure that makes the track as much a trance record as a funk one. The electric piano chords are held and allowed to decay, creating a floating sense of space. The vocal is spoken-sung rather than belted, which keeps the record meditative. It is the Pharoah Sanders influence carried onto the dance floor.","It was a commercial success and became a dance-floor classic, particularly in Britain. Jazz critics were mostly uninterested at the time, but its reputation has grown through DJ culture, and it is now seen as a crucial bridge between spiritual jazz and later dance music. It is often cited by house and broken-beat producers as an influence.","Start with the title track and listen to the bass line, then the electric piano washes. Then hear Pharoah Sanders's Karma, to which Smith contributed, to hear where the spiritual mood comes from. Compare with Roy Ayers's Everybody Loves the Sunshine for another warm, communal groove."],"freddie-hubbard-red-clay":["A quintet record that blends hard bop with funk and electric sound. The title track rides a loping, insistent bass line and a funky drum groove, with Freddie Hubbard's brassy trumpet and Joe Henderson's tenor stating a punchy theme before long, fiery solos. Herbie Hancock plays electric piano, Ron Carter bass and Lenny White drums. The album has four long tracks, all adventurous but grounded in groove, with room for real improvisation.","Recorded in January 1970 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, it was one of the first releases on Creed Taylor's new CTI label. Taylor wanted jazz with high production values and wider appeal, and he gathered the best players of the day. Hubbard had been a Blue Note star; here he was moving towards a more electric, funk-influenced sound, in step with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew of the same period.","Listen to Hancock's electric piano, which he was just beginning to explore; its slightly distorted, bell-like tone shapes the record's sound. The title track's bass line is a simple repeated figure, but Carter varies it subtly throughout. The record balances CTI's polish with the raw energy of a blowing session; there are no strings or overdubs, just a quintet in a room.","It was well received and became one of CTI's most admired records, often cited as Hubbard's finest album. Critics generally praised its balance of groove and improvisation. It has been less controversial than later CTI albums with strings, and is regarded as a model of early-1970s jazz that embraced electric sound without losing its edge.","Start with the title track and listen to the bass line, then follow Hubbard's solo, which builds to dazzling high notes. Compare with Herbie Hancock's Maiden Voyage, on which Hubbard and Carter also play, and with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew for a more radical take on electric jazz."],"freddie-hubbard-first-light":["Freddie Hubbard's trumpet set against lush orchestral arrangements by Don Sebesky. The title track, Hubbard's own, is a long, slowly building piece where strings and woodwinds lay down a soft, shimmering backdrop and the trumpet rises above in long, lyrical lines. The album also includes a version of a Paul McCartney song and a film theme by Henry Mancini. The mood is warm, cinematic and polished, with the trumpet always in front.","Creed Taylor had built CTI on lush production, and Sebesky was his signature arranger. The album was a commercial success and won a Grammy. Hubbard, a fiery hard bop player, was here presented as a romantic soloist, part of Taylor's strategy to reach a wider audience. The sessions used top New York studio players of the CTI circle. The album was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, as nearly all CTI records were.","Listen to how Sebesky voices the strings: not as a continuous wash but in short, shifting chords that respond to Hubbard's phrases. Hubbard's tone on the title track is unusually soft and rounded, closer to a flugelhorn than his usual brassy sound. The album shows a different side of a player usually associated with speed and power, and that restraint was a deliberate choice.","It won a Grammy and sold well. Critics were divided: some praised Hubbard's lyricism, others found the strings sentimental. It has come to represent the polished CTI sound, admired and criticised in equal measure. Many listeners regard the title track as one of Hubbard's most beautiful recordings, while purists prefer Red Clay.","Start with the title track and listen to how the trumpet floats above the strings. Then compare with Red Clay to hear Hubbard without an orchestra. For context, hear Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain, an earlier and more austere model of trumpet with orchestra."],"bob-james-one":["Bob James's first CTI album mixes funk grooves, orchestral arrangements and classical pieces. It includes a funk version of Mussorgsky's Night on Bald Mountain, with strings and brass rising over a hard backbeat, and In the Garden, built on Pachelbel's Canon. There is also a version of Roberta Flack's Feel Like Making Love and the brooding closing track Nautilus, a slow groove with a chiming Fender Rhodes and a stark drumbeat.","James was Creed Taylor's chief arranger, and this album was his debut as a leader for the label. CTI's records were known for their lush production and crossover ambitions, and James combined jazz, funk, classical and pop in a single package. The album sold well and established him as a leading figure in the emerging smooth jazz and jazz-funk scenes.","Nautilus became one of the most sampled tracks in hip-hop; Ghostface Killah's Daytona 500 is one of many records built on it. Listen to the track's bare opening, just Rhodes and drums, which is what producers wanted. In the Mussorgsky, notice how the famous swirling string figures are given to synthesiser and electric piano, while the brass keeps the original's menace over the backbeat.","Jazz critics were sceptical of the classical adaptations, seeing them as gimmicks. The album sold well and James's career flourished. Its reputation was transformed by hip-hop, which made Nautilus a foundational sample. It is now valued both as a CTI artefact and as a source text for a later genre, though debate over the classical adaptations continues.","Start with Nautilus and listen to the opening; then imagine it looped under a rapper's voice. Then hear Night on Bald Mountain and compare it with Mussorgsky's original. Follow with Two, his next album, which has the Bizet adaptation and Take Me to the Mardi Gras."],"bob-james-two":["Bob James's second CTI album continues the mix of funk, orchestral arrangements and classical adaptation. It opens with Take Me to the Mardi Gras, Paul Simon's song, which starts with a few bars of solo bells and drums before a gentle groove takes over. Farandole is a funk version of Bizet's L'Arlésienne, with the march tune carried by brass over a hard backbeat. The rest of the album is smooth, tuneful and polished, with James's Fender Rhodes at the centre.","The album followed the success of One and used the same team of top New York studio players. CTI was at the height of its influence, though Creed Taylor's empire would soon run into financial trouble. James was arranging for many CTI artists, and his own records served as showcases for his production style, blending jazz soloing with pop melody and classical grandeur.","The opening bells and drums of Take Me to the Mardi Gras became one of the most famous breaks in hip-hop: Run-DMC built Peter Piper, on Raising Hell, on them. Listen to how the bells ring alone before the band enters; that brief isolation is what made the passage usable by DJs. In Farandole, listen for how the stately march tune keeps its dignity even over a drum kit.","It sold well, and like One received mixed reviews from jazz critics. Its afterlife in hip-hop has given it a new reputation, and the Mardi Gras break is among the most widely used samples in rap history. The Bizet adaptation remains divisive: some hear an imaginative crossover, others a cheapening of the classical source.","Play the opening of Take Me to the Mardi Gras and listen for the bells alone, then put on Run-DMC's Peter Piper. Then hear Farandole beside Bizet's original. Compare with One for the Mussorgsky adaptation and with Deodato's Also sprach Zarathustra for another CTI take on a classical source."],"bob-james-angela":["A wistful, gently swinging melody played on Fender Rhodes electric piano, with soft synthesiser and a light rhythm section. The tune moves in a lilting rhythm with a hint of melancholy, rising and falling over a patient bass line. In its full-length version on James's album Touchdown it stretches out with room for improvisation; on television the opening was trimmed to under a minute. It is instantly recognisable from its first phrase.","James wrote it for the American sitcom Taxi, which premiered in 1978 and ran until 1983. The show followed a group of New York cab drivers, and the music, played over the opening shots of a cab crossing the Queensboro Bridge, set a tone of quiet loneliness that the comedy played against. James had started his own label, Tappan Zee, distributed by Columbia, after leaving CTI.","The theme does not hurry: it lets a single phrase hang, then answers it a step lower, a shape that suits the show's bittersweet character better than any upbeat sitcom jingle would. Listen to the Rhodes tone: James uses the instrument's natural bell-like attack and slow decay to give the melody a slightly blurred, nostalgic quality. The rhythm section stays very light, almost brushed, so the melody can float.","It became one of the most famous television themes ever written and introduced James's music to millions who never heard his albums. Critics have praised its economy of mood, and it is often cited as a model of how a short theme can define a show. Its success also tied James firmly to the smooth jazz label, for better or worse.","Listen to the full version on Touchdown and follow the melody through its gentle variations. Notice the Rhodes tone. Compare with Henry Mancini's Peter Gunn, another jazz-inflected television theme, and with Bernard Herrmann's Taxi Driver, a darker vision of the same city in the same decade."],"deodato-also-sprach-zarathustra":["A funk version of the opening of Richard Strauss's tone poem. The famous rising trumpet motif is stated over a hard, Latin-tinged groove of electric bass, conga and drums, then the piece opens out into long, jazzy improvisations on electric piano and guitar. The original's grand fanfare becomes a vamp, repeated and varied. Brass and strings punctuate the groove. The track runs about nine minutes on the album, much shorter as a single.","Deodato, a Brazilian arranger working in New York, recorded it for his CTI album Prelude. Stanley Kubrick's film 2001: A Space Odyssey, released in 1968, had made Strauss's opening world famous, and the track's parenthetical title acknowledged the film. Released as a single, it became a major pop hit and won a Grammy. It was one of the biggest hits CTI ever had.","Listen to how Deodato handles Strauss's famous opening: he keeps the rising three-note motif but replaces the original's organ pedal and timpani with a funk groove, so the grandeur becomes a dance. The Brazilian rhythmic feel, a light, syncopated lilt, is subtle but distinctive. After the opening statement, the track becomes essentially a jazz jam, with Strauss abandoned.","It was a huge commercial success. Classical listeners were mostly bemused, jazz critics mostly dismissive. Over time it has been seen as a defining example of 1970s crossover, alongside other jazz-funk adaptations of classical music. Its kitsch value has grown, but so has appreciation of its groove and arrangement. It also opened the door for many disco-era classical remakes.","Listen first to Strauss's original opening, then to Deodato's version, noticing what is kept and what is changed. Then follow the improvisations after the motif. Compare with Bob James's adaptations of Mussorgsky and Bizet for other CTI takes on classical themes."],"stanley-turrentine-sugar":["Three long tracks led by Stanley Turrentine's big, bluesy tenor. The title track is a slow, sultry minor-key groove where Turrentine's smoky tone stretches out over electric piano, organ and a patient bass line, followed by solos from Freddie Hubbard on trumpet and George Benson on guitar. The album also includes a version of John Coltrane's Impressions, played fast and hard. The sound is CTI-polished but rooted in soul jazz and the blues.","Turrentine had moved from Blue Note to Creed Taylor's CTI, and this was his first album for the label. Taylor surrounded him with stars, including Ron Carter on bass, and the result sold strongly. The title track became Turrentine's signature tune. It was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, whose clean, warm sound defined both Blue Note and CTI. The label's photographic sleeves made it look like a luxury product.","Listen to how Turrentine sits just behind the beat on the title track, stretching each phrase so it seems to lag and then catch up. The organ and electric piano are both present, giving the groove a doubled keyboard texture. The Coltrane cover shows that Turrentine, often thought of as a soul player, could handle modal jazz at speed.","It was well received and sold well, becoming one of CTI's best-loved records. Critics praised Turrentine's tone and the quality of the supporting players. It has been less criticised than later, more heavily produced CTI albums, and is seen as a model of soul jazz updated for the 1970s. The title tune has since become a jam-session standard.","Start with the title track and listen to Turrentine's tone, then the solos by Hubbard and Benson. Then hear the Coltrane cover. Compare with Back at the Chicken Shack, where Turrentine plays with Jimmy Smith a decade earlier. The voice is the same; only the setting changed."],"hubert-laws-the-rite-of-spring":["Hubert Laws's flute plays classical pieces in jazz arrangements by Don Sebesky. The title track condenses parts of Stravinsky's ballet into a groove, with Laws's flute carrying melodies over electric bass and percussion. There are also versions of Fauré's Pavane, Debussy's Syrinx, the solo flute piece, and a movement from Bach's Third Brandenburg Concerto. The arrangements are lush, the flute tone pure, and the mood moves between meditative and propulsive.","Laws was a classically trained flautist who also played in symphony orchestras. Creed Taylor's CTI label was known for polished crossover records, and this album was one of the most ambitious, applying jazz techniques to a classical repertoire. The late 1960s and early 1970s saw a wave of such crossover projects, among them Wendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach of 1968, which had turned Bach into a synthesiser best-seller.","Listen to the Syrinx, originally a piece for one unaccompanied flute, and notice how Laws's pure, vibrato-light classical tone survives the jazz setting. The Stravinsky arrangement keeps fragments of the original's rhythms but turns its violence into a groove. The Bach is played almost straight, with a jazz rhythm section adding a light swing. The album's interest lies in these contrasts.","It received mixed reviews: some praised Laws's skill and Sebesky's arrangements, others found the classical adaptations superficial. It has become a cult item, valued by flautists and collectors of CTI. Debate over its classical adaptations continues; some see it as a bridge between traditions, others as a gimmick. It remains an unusual listening test of how far a groove can carry a score.","Start with Syrinx and compare it with a classical recording. Then hear the title track beside Stravinsky's original, noticing which rhythms survive. Compare with Bob James's adaptations of Mussorgsky and Bizet, and with Deodato's Strauss, for other CTI takes on the classics."],"larry-young-unity":["An organ quartet that sounds nothing like the soul jazz organ combos. Larry Young plays Hammond organ with long, flowing lines and sustained chords, not churchy blues, while Woody Shaw on trumpet, Joe Henderson on tenor saxophone and Elvin Jones on drums push through complex, modal compositions. The opener, Shaw's Zoltan, begins with a march-like theme before exploding into fast swing. There is also a version of Monk's Dream and a ballad. The energy is relentless.","Recorded in November 1965 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1966. Young had begun as a soul jazz organist but was moving towards the modal jazz of John Coltrane, whose drummer Elvin Jones plays here. Shaw, then twenty, contributed three compositions. Blue Note was recording a wide range of styles, from boogaloo to the avant-garde. Van Gelder's close miking puts the organ and drums right in your face.","Zoltan, named for the composer Zoltán Kodály, borrows a march theme from his Háry János before turning into modal jazz. Listen to Young's left hand: rather than walking a bass line in the usual organ way, he often plays sustained chords, giving the music a floating, open feel. Jones's drumming is dense and polyrhythmic, as with Coltrane, and Young matches him.","It was not a commercial success but has become one of the most admired organ jazz records ever made, often cited as the moment the organ entered modern jazz. Critics praise its originality and the quality of Shaw's compositions. It is regularly included in lists of essential Blue Note records and is a touchstone for organists who want to move beyond soul jazz.","Start with Zoltan and listen for the march theme, then the explosion into swing. Notice how Young's organ sounds unlike Jimmy Smith's. Then hear Coltrane's A Love Supreme for the modal influence, and The Sermon! for the organ tradition Young was leaving."],"richard-groove-holmes-misty":["Erroll Garner's ballad played by an organ trio at a slow, swinging medium tempo. Richard Holmes states the melody with big, swelling organ chords, then walks a deep bass line under his own improvisations, building gradually without ever losing the tune. Gene Edwards's guitar comps softly, and the drums keep a light, patient swing. The result is warm, relaxed and gently grooving, a ballad turned into something you could dance slowly to.","Holmes recorded it for the album Soul Message on Prestige, which specialised in organ combos for the Black jukebox and bar market. The track was released as a single and became a surprise crossover hit, reaching pop and rhythm and blues audiences. Garner had written the tune in 1954 and it had become a standard through vocal versions, especially Johnny Mathis's.","Listen to Holmes's bass line: he plays it with his left hand and feet together, giving it a weight and punch that few organists matched, and that is the reason for his nickname. The tempo is faster than most versions of the ballad, which keeps it from becoming sentimental. The organ's swell pedal is used for dramatic crescendos at the ends of phrases.","It was a hit and made Holmes a star of the organ-jazz circuit. Critics generally praised it as a fine example of the organ trio's ability to transform a standard. It remains his best-known recording and a staple of organ-jazz collections, though some prefer his harder-swinging work. It showed that an organ trio could sell a ballad, not only a blues.","Listen first to the bass line, then the chords. Compare with Erroll Garner's own piano version to hear how the organ changes the tune. Then hear Jimmy Smith's The Sermon! for the organ trio's other side, the long, slow blues."],"the-kinks-face-to-face":["Fourteen short songs, every one by Ray Davies, and the first Kinks album that sounds like a single sensibility rather than a run of singles with filler. The guitars that made 'You Really Got Me' are still there, but the record is dominated by music-hall bounce, harpsichord, and small character sketches: a session musician, a dandy, a family selling up its grand house, a holidaymaker in a fake Hawaii. It closes its first run with the lazy, descending 'Sunny Afternoon'.","Made at Pye in London across 1966, a year in which Ray Davies had suffered a collapse from exhaustion and the band had been banned from touring America after a union dispute. Cut off from the US market that every British group was chasing, Davies turned inward and toward England: its class system, its suburbs, its taxman. The album came out in the autumn, a few months after 'Sunny Afternoon' had topped the British chart, and in the same season as Revolver.","Davies wanted sound effects linking every track, a kind of radio play, and Pye cut most of them; the survivors, a ringing telephone at the start, thunder in 'Rainy Day in June' and surf in 'Holiday in Waikiki', are the fossil of that plan. Listen to 'Fancy', where the guitar holds a single droning note under the voice in the manner of an Indian drone, a quieter cousin of the Byrds' raga experiments the same year.","Reviews were warm but sales were modest, and the album was overshadowed by the louder events of 1966. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now often named as one of the first rock albums organised around a point of view rather than a sequence of hits. Arguments continue over whether Pye's removal of the linking effects spoiled a concept album or saved a set of songs from a gimmick.","Begin with 'Session Man', where the harpsichord and the joke about hired-hand musicians are the same gesture, then 'Sunny Afternoon' for Davies's voice at its most deadpan. Listen to how often the tunes come from the music hall rather than the blues. Follow it with Village Green and Waterloo Sunset to hear the idea mature, and with Revolver to hear what else was possible that year."],"the-kinks-arthur":["Twelve songs following one ordinary Englishman, a carpet-layer named Arthur, through the twentieth century: Victorian childhood, two world wars, a suburban semi named Shangri-La, and a son who emigrates to Australia. The music is fuller and louder than Village Green, with brass and strings over a tough rhythm section, and two long pieces, 'Shangri-La' and 'Australia', that stretch out past five minutes. 'Victoria' opens it with a fanfare-like riff that is half celebration, half sneer.","Ray Davies wrote the songs as the score for a Granada television play he devised with the novelist Julian Mitchell; the play was never made, and the album went out alone in October 1969. Arthur was modelled on Davies's brother-in-law, who had emigrated to Australia with Davies's elder sister. It was the first album with John Dalton on bass after Pete Quaife left, and it arrived a few months after the Who's Tommy, which took the attention and the label 'rock opera'.","Listen to how 'Shangri-La' changes speed and mood in sections, from a hushed, almost hymn-like opening to a jaunty music-hall middle and a massive orchestral climax; the structure mirrors a man's pride in his little house curdling into the realisation that it is a trap. 'Some Mother's Son', about a soldier killed in the First World War, sets that scene to the gentlest tune on the record, and the gentleness is the point.","American critics in particular championed it at the time, some preferring it to Tommy, but it sold modestly. Its standing has risen since, largely on the strength of 'Shangri-La' and 'Victoria', and it is now seen as the end of a run of four Kinks albums about Englishness that few British bands matched. The debate is whether Davies is mocking Arthur or mourning him; most listeners now hear both at once.","Start with 'Shangri-La' and follow its sections like movements of a short symphony, then hear 'Victoria' and 'Some Mother's Son' back to back for the record's range. Read about the unmade television play first; it makes the running order make sense. Compare it with Tommy, released the same year, to hear two very different British ideas of a story told in songs."],"the-who-i-cant-explain":["Two minutes of chopped power chords, a lead vocal from Roger Daltrey that sounds tense rather than tough, and a song about a young man who cannot find the words for what he feels about a girl. Pete Townshend's guitar riff, a few ringing chords hit hard and then cut off, does most of the work; Keith Moon's drumming is already busier than anyone else's in British pop, filling every gap with rolls; John Entwistle's bass holds the floor still underneath.","The Who's first single under that name, recorded in London with the American producer Shel Talmy, who had just made the Kinks' early hits. It was released early in 1965 and slowly climbed into the British top ten, helped by a television appearance on Ready Steady Go!. The band were the house group of the London mod scene, playing for crowds of sharp-dressed teenagers on scooters, and their managers sold them as the sound of that tribe.","Townshend has said openly that he wrote it as an imitation of the Kinks' 'You Really Got Me', and once you know, the family likeness in the riff is obvious. Listen to the backing vocals: they are not the band but a hired vocal group, the Ivy League, brought in because Talmy wanted a smoother pop sheen. The tension between that polish and Moon's drums is the sound of the Who being tidied up for radio and refusing to stay tidy.","It was received as a promising mod single rather than an event, and the band's bigger statement came with 'My Generation' later that year. Over time it has come to be seen as the template for power pop and for a whole line of short, frustrated guitar songs, from the Jam to Britpop. It remained in the Who's live set for decades, usually as an opener, which says something about how well the riff wears.","Play it loud and listen only to Moon on the first pass, then only to the guitar. Notice how short it is and how little happens, and how that brevity feels like the song's subject: a boy who cannot get the words out. Then play 'You Really Got Me' and 'My Generation' around it to hear the line from one to the other."],"the-who-a-quick-one":["The Who's second album, a deliberately mixed bag: songs by every member, a cover of Martha and the Vandellas' 'Heat Wave', and a closing nine-minute piece, 'A Quick One, While He's Away', that strings six short sections into a story about a woman whose lover is away for a year, a seduction, a confession and a forgiveness. John Entwistle's 'Boris the Spider', with its growling bass voice, sits alongside Townshend's tender 'So Sad About Us'. The horns heard here are Entwistle's own.","Released at the end of 1966 on the small Reaction label; in America it appeared as Happy Jack, named after the single. The band's publishing deal required each member to supply songs, which explains Keith Moon's instrumental and Roger Daltrey's single contribution. When they ran short of material, their manager Kit Lambert, son of the composer Constant Lambert, pushed Townshend to write something longer and joined together, and the mini-opera was the result.","The mini-opera's final section, the forgiveness, builds a chorus of overlapping voices over a simple repeated chord pattern until it becomes almost hymn-like, and that build is the direct ancestor of the climax of Tommy three years later. Listen also to the cello-like sound in the third section: the band could not afford strings, so they sang the word for them instead, in imitation of bowing.","The album was reviewed as uneven, which it is by design, and the band themselves treated it as a stopgap. Its reputation rests on the title piece, which the Who performed with ferocious energy at the Rolling Stones' Rock and Roll Circus in December 1968, a performance often said to have outplayed the hosts. Historians of the concept album now point to it as the hinge between the pop single and Tommy.","Go straight to 'A Quick One, While He's Away' and treat it as six tiny songs; notice how each section changes tempo and voice. Then hear 'So Sad About Us' and 'Boris the Spider' for the band's other faces. If you can find the Rock and Roll Circus footage, watch it: the live version shows what the studio take was reaching for."],"the-who-live-at-leeds":["Six tracks on the original LP: three rock-and-roll and rhythm-and-blues covers (Mose Allison's 'Young Man Blues', Eddie Cochran's 'Summertime Blues', Johnny Kidd's 'Shakin' All Over'), the band's own 'Substitute' and 'Magic Bus', and a fifteen-minute 'My Generation' that turns into a medley and a long improvisation. It is four players at maximum volume, with no overdubbed rhythm guitar, so Townshend's chords and Entwistle's lead-like bass carry all the harmony while Moon plays continuously around them.","Recorded at the University of Leeds refectory in February 1970, when the Who had been touring Tommy for nearly a year and were as tight as they would ever be. The band wanted a live record that sounded like the real thing rather than a cleaned-up studio product. The original sleeve imitated a bootleg: plain brown card with the title stamped on it, and inside, a folder of facsimile contracts, posters and receipts from the band's early years.","The original label carried an instruction to engineers not to remove the crackles, because the tapes had audible surface noise and the band preferred it to a fix. Listen in 'Young Man Blues' to the moments where everyone stops dead and starts again on a single beat; those breaks are rehearsed but sound reckless. Expanded reissues restored the whole show, including the complete Tommy, and revealed how much the LP left out.","It was received at once as one of the best live rock albums ever issued, and that verdict has barely moved. It is often named as a blueprint for hard rock and heavy metal performance, and a model for how a live record can stand as a work in itself. The only real argument concerns which version to hear: the tight six-track LP or the sprawling complete concert.","Start with 'Young Man Blues' and focus on Entwistle, whose bass is doing what a lead guitar would do elsewhere. Then 'Summertime Blues' alongside Eddie Cochran's original, to hear what volume and Moon did to a 1958 song. Hear the original six-track LP first; come to the expanded editions later."],"the-zombies-shes-not-there":["A two-and-a-half-minute minor-key single carried by Rod Argent's electric piano rather than guitar, Colin Blunstone's breathy, almost whispered lead vocal, and a chorus where the band's voices crowd in close. The song is about a man who was warned about a girl too late. The middle features a jazz-tinged electric piano solo, and the drums play with a light, swinging touch that sets it apart from the guitar bands of 1964.","The Zombies were schoolboys from St Albans, near London, who won a local beat-group competition and with it a contract with Decca. Argent wrote the song for their first session, and it was released in the summer of 1964, when the British Invasion was at its height. It was a modest hit at home and a much bigger one in America, which led the band to tour the United States while barely known in Britain.","Listen to the harmony: instead of the blues-derived chords of most British beat records, Argent uses the kind of shifting minor chords found in modal jazz and in the church music he had sung as a chorister. Blunstone's quiet delivery was unusual for a 1964 beat record, and it makes the song feel confessional rather than performed.","It was admired from the start as an unusually sophisticated pop single, and its standing has never really dipped. Musicians cite it as an early example of jazz harmony inside British pop, and it has been covered many times, notably by Santana in 1977. Critics sometimes wonder whether the band's later failure was a matter of being too subtle for their moment; this single suggests why.","Listen first to the electric piano, which plays the role a lead guitar would in most bands of the time, then to Blunstone's breath-heavy phrasing. Compare it with 'You Really Got Me' from the same year, to hear two opposite ideas of British pop. Then go to Odessey and Oracle to hear where the same ears ended up."],"the-yardbirds-shapes-of-things":["A tightly marching two and a half minutes, with Keith Relf's wary vocal over a pounding bass line, a song musing on war, progress and the future. It builds toward a guitar solo by Jeff Beck that does not sound like a guitar solo at all: a sustained, bending, feedback-laced line with an Indian or Middle Eastern cast, held in place with the controlled howl of an amplifier pushed past its limits.","Released early in 1966, written by Relf, the drummer Jim McCarty and the bassist Paul Samwell-Smith. Beck had replaced Eric Clapton in 1965, after Clapton left in protest at the band's turn toward pop. The Yardbirds were then the most adventurous of the London rhythm-and-blues groups, and they recorded parts of their work in America, including at Chess Studios in Chicago, the home of the blues records they had started out copying.","Beck's solo is built from feedback that he is controlling with his body, moving toward and away from the amplifier to bring the howl in and out. That technique, here only a few seconds long, would become a basic vocabulary of rock guitar within two years, through Hendrix and others. Beck liked the song enough to re-record it in 1968 on his album Truth, with Rod Stewart singing.","It was a British top-ten hit and was soon described as one of the first psychedelic singles. Critics now treat it as a key moment in the history of the electric guitar, the point where feedback moved from accident to instrument. Its status is complicated only by the fact that the Yardbirds have often been remembered for whose guitarists they launched rather than for their own records.","Wait for the solo, then play it again and listen only to the sound of the guitar: the sustain, the bending, the moment the amplifier seems to sing back. Notice how the verses tighten the tension before it. Then hear 'Eight Miles High' and Hendrix's Are You Experienced to follow where that sound went."],"the-yardbirds-roger-the-engineer":["Twelve original songs, mostly short, with Jeff Beck as the sole lead guitarist: blues shuffles sit beside stranger things: 'Hot House of Omagararshid' and 'Over Under Sideways Down' bring in drones, chanting and modal guitar lines. 'Jeff's Boogie' is a jaunty instrumental showcase, and 'Lost Woman' opens the album on a hypnotic bass riff. The sound is raw, live in the room, with Beck's guitar switching between fuzz, feedback and clean blues picking.","Recorded in London in 1966 and produced by Paul Samwell-Smith and the band's manager Simon Napier-Bell; in America it came out on Epic as Over Under Sideways Down. It was the only Yardbirds studio album made up entirely of their own songs. Samwell-Smith left soon after, and Jimmy Page joined first on bass and then as a second lead guitarist beside Beck, a line-up that lasted only months and made few records.","The title and the cover drawing come from the rhythm guitarist Chris Dreja's caricature of the studio's engineer, Roger Cameron, an affectionate joke that became the name the album is now known by. Listen to 'Over Under Sideways Down': its guitar line imitates a sitar's twang and slides, and the chanting, humming backing vocals owe more to Eastern devotional music than to Chicago blues.","It sold respectably in Britain but was soon overshadowed by bigger statements later that year. Its reputation has grown among guitarists, who treat it as the best document of Beck within a band, and among historians of psychedelia, who hear it as a bridge from British blues to the studio experiments of 1967. Some still find it too uneven to rank with the albums it anticipates.","Start with 'Over Under Sideways Down' and 'Hot House of Omagararshid' for the band's strangest side, then 'Lost Woman' for its blues roots. Follow Beck's tone from song to song; he rarely plays the same way twice. Then hear Cream's Fresh Cream and Hendrix's debut, both made within the year, to hear what the Yardbirds set in motion."],"small-faces-itchycoo-park":["A gentle, sing-along song about skipping school or work to spend a sunny day in a park, sung by Steve Marriott and Ronnie Lane in turn. The verses are light and acoustic, almost nursery-rhyme simple; the chorus swells with harmonies and Ian McLagan's organ. Then, in the instrumental break, the whole sound seems to tilt and whoosh, the drums sweeping like a jet engine passing overhead. That whooshing passage is what everyone remembers.","Released in the summer of 1967 on Immediate, the independent label run by Andrew Loog Oldham, after the band left Decca. The Small Faces were an East London mod band who had built their name on hard soul-influenced singles; 'Itchycoo Park' was their move into the psychedelic summer, with a gentler, pastoral sound. It became a top-ten hit in Britain and their only big American success during the band's lifetime.","The whooshing effect is phasing, made in the studio by running two copies of the same recording slightly out of step so that the sound sweeps against itself; the Olympic Studios engineer George Chkiantz is usually credited with the technique. Few British singles had used it so prominently, and its presence on a top-ten record helped spread the technique. The song's innocent tone carries a coy double meaning, which the band enjoyed and broadcasters mostly missed.","It was a hit and has stayed one of the most played songs of 1967 on British radio, but the band later felt it misrepresented their harder, soul-based sound. Critics sometimes dismiss it as lightweight, but the phasing alone gives it a place in studio history. Its cheerful mood is now often heard as the essence of a particular English moment.","Wait for the instrumental break and listen on headphones as the phasing sweeps across the drums. Then compare Marriott's lighter singing here with his full-throated soul voice elsewhere. Follow it with Ogdens' Nut Gone Flake to hear the same band at full stretch, and with 'Waterloo Sunset' for another English pastoral from 1967."],"small-faces-ogdens-nut-gone-flake":["Two sides that could hardly be more different. Side one is six separate songs: a heavy organ-led title instrumental, the soul ballad 'Afterglow', and the cockney music-hall singalong 'Lazy Sunday'. Side two is a fairy-tale suite about Happiness Stan, a young man searching for the missing half of the moon, with songs linked by the comedian Stanley Unwin narrating in his invented, tumbling gobbledygook language. Hard rock, soul, music hall and nonsense sit side by side.","Released by Immediate in 1968 and the Small Faces' most ambitious record, made at Olympic Studios in London with Glyn Johns engineering. The title parodied a well-known brand of pipe tobacco, and the original sleeve was round, folding out like the lid of a tobacco tin. It went to the top of the British album chart, but the band found it impossible to perform live, and within a year Steve Marriott had left to form Humble Pie.","Stanley Unwin was a British radio comedian famous for 'Unwinese', a speech in which real words bend and break into new ones; the band wrote his narration as a framing device for the whole side. Listen to 'Afterglow', where Marriott's voice moves from a hush to a full soul shout in a single verse, a reminder that these were mods who learned to sing from American soul records.","It was a commercial peak and a critical success, but its very richness ended the band; the Small Faces only ever performed it once in public, on a BBC television programme. It is now regarded as one of the key British psychedelic albums and an early concept record, admired for its mix of humour and ambition. Some critics still prefer the plainer first side.","Hear 'Lazy Sunday' and 'Afterglow' back to back to grasp the band's range, then put on side two straight through, letting Unwin's narration carry you between songs. Notice the music-hall accent and piano rhythms. Compare it with the Kinks' Arthur and the Who's Tommy, both of which tried in different ways to make an album tell a story."],"the-byrds-younger-than-yesterday":["Eleven songs in under half an hour, opening with 'So You Want to Be a Rock 'n' Roll Star', a sardonic piece about the pop machine, set against a screaming audience and a trumpet. Roger McGuinn's jangling twelve-string guitar is there, but the album spreads wider: David Crosby's jazz-tinged 'Everybody's Been Burned', Chris Hillman's country-leaning songs, the space-themed 'C.T.A.-102', and a cover of Bob Dylan's 'My Back Pages' that became one of their best known recordings.","Recorded in Hollywood late in 1966 and released by Columbia early in 1967, produced by Gary Usher. The Byrds had lost their main songwriter, Gene Clark, the year before, and the space he left was filled by Hillman, who suddenly emerged as a writer, and by Crosby. The band was moving between Los Angeles folk rock and the new San Francisco sounds, and between pop success and a more experimental studio approach.","The trumpet on the opening track is played by the South African musician Hugh Masekela, then living in America. On 'Time Between' and elsewhere, the guitar of the bluegrass and country player Clarence White appears, a quiet hint of the country-rock turn the Byrds would take on Sweetheart of the Rodeo the next year. Listen to the backwards guitar on 'Mind Gardens', a sign that the band were listening hard to the Beatles' Revolver.","It sold less than earlier Byrds records and was received as a curiosity at first, but its reputation rose steadily, and many critics now rate it as the band's most consistent album. It is valued as a bridge between 1965's folk rock and the country rock of 1968, and as the record on which Hillman and Crosby became writers. Crosby's contributions remain the most debated.","Start with 'So You Want to Be a Rock 'n' Roll Star' and 'My Back Pages', then try Hillman's 'Have You Seen Her Face' and 'Time Between' to hear country edging in. Listen for how short and economical everything is. Follow it with Sweetheart of the Rodeo and Gram Parsons's records to hear where the country thread led."],"the-byrds-notorious-byrd-brothers":["Eleven songs that flow into one another with crossfades, making the album feel like a single drifting suite. There are two songs by Gerry Goffin and Carole King, 'Goin' Back' and 'Wasn't Born to Follow'; there is a hushed anti-war song, 'Draft Morning'; there is a cheerful country song, 'Old John Robertson', with a string-quartet interlude. The record ends with 'Space Odyssey', in which the electronic tones of a Moog synthesizer replace the familiar twelve-string jangle.","Made in Hollywood across the second half of 1967 with producer Gary Usher, during a period of turmoil: David Crosby was sacked from the band in the middle of the sessions, and the drummer Michael Clarke also departed. The remaining Byrds, Roger McGuinn and Chris Hillman, finished the album with session players. It was released early in 1968 by Columbia and sold poorly compared to the band's earlier work.","On the cover four windows in an old stable frame the band's faces, but only three Byrds appear; a horse looks out of the fourth. Fans long took this as a joke at Crosby's expense; band members have given differing accounts. Crosby's song 'Triad', which the others refused to release, ended up with Jefferson Airplane. Listen for the Moog throughout; it was among the first rock records to use the instrument.","Sales were weak and reviews were warm but confused, but critical opinion has turned firmly in its favour, and it is now often rated the Byrds' finest album, admired for its seamless flow and studio adventurousness. It is cited as a forerunner of the album as a continuous listening experience. Arguments continue over how much Crosby contributed before his departure.","Play it straight through without stopping, as a single half-hour piece, and notice how the songs fade into each other. Pay attention to 'Goin' Back' and 'Draft Morning' for their quiet beauty and the moment the Moog enters. Compare it with the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds and the Zombies' Odessey and Oracle for other studio albums of this moment."],"buffalo-springfield-for-what-its-worth":["A slow, uneasy song built on a cool, restrained groove, about young people and police facing off in the street. Stephen Stills sings in a calm, almost detached voice, and the chorus asks everyone to stop and listen. The defining sound is a single guitar figure from Neil Young: two high harmonic notes, chiming like a bell or a warning signal, repeated throughout. The understatement is the point; it sounds like tension rather than rage.","Stills wrote it in November 1966 after the Sunset Strip curfew riots in Los Angeles, when young people protested against a crackdown on clubs and loitering on the Strip. The band recorded it in December and Atco released it as a single within weeks; it became a top-ten hit early in 1967 and was added to later pressings of the band's debut album. Its message about the street soon attached itself to Vietnam protests.","The song is not about Vietnam, though it has become the soundtrack to countless documentaries about that war; it is about a local dispute over a nightclub strip. Listen to how little the drums and bass do, keeping a lazy, steady beat, and how Young's harmonics sit on top like an alarm. The record was made fast, and its looseness is part of its watchful mood.","It was the band's only big hit, and it has become one of the most used protest songs of the sixties, sometimes to the irritation of those who know its origins. Critics praise it for its restraint and its feeling of watchfulness. Its success arguably distorted the band's reputation, since their albums show a much wider and stranger range.","Listen first to Young's two-note figure and count how often it returns; then focus on the calm of Stills's voice. Notice that the band never builds to a climax. Follow it with Buffalo Springfield Again for their fuller sound, and with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young's 'Ohio' for a later protest song from the same musicians, written in anger rather than unease."],"buffalo-springfield-again":["Ten songs by three writers that rarely sound like the same band. Neil Young opens with 'Mr. Soul', a hard, riff-driven song, and contributes 'Expecting to Fly', a lush, orchestral dream, and 'Broken Arrow', a collage that splices in crowd noise and a jazz piano trio. Stephen Stills offers 'Bluebird', which moves from electric guitars to banjo, and 'Rock & Roll Woman'. Richie Furay brings brighter, country-tinged songs like 'A Child's Claim to Fame'.","Recorded in Los Angeles across 1967 and released by Atco late that year, while the band was coming apart: Young left and rejoined more than once, and the members often recorded separately with session players. The result is less a group statement than three solo projects sharing a sleeve. The band had become central to the Laurel Canyon scene, and its members would go on to Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young, Poco and Young's solo career.","'Expecting to Fly' is essentially a Neil Young solo track made with the arranger Jack Nitzsche, who had worked with Phil Spector, and its echo-laden strings come straight from that school. 'Broken Arrow' ends with the sound of a heartbeat, an early sign of Young's interest in sound collage. Listen to the banjo and fiddle at the end of 'Bluebird': country music is already inside this rock band.","It was well reviewed and is often called the band's best album, though its fragmentation is plain. Critics have argued that its very lack of unity is what makes it interesting: a portrait of three major songwriters before they went their own ways. Its influence on country rock and on the singer-songwriters of the early seventies is now widely recognised.","Start with 'Mr. Soul' and 'Bluebird' for the band's electric side, then 'Expecting to Fly' on headphones for Nitzsche's orchestration. Finish with 'Broken Arrow' and listen to how its sections are cut together. Then hear Crosby, Stills and Nash's first album and Neil Young's Everybody Knows This Is Nowhere to follow the band's members outward."],"jefferson-airplane-surrealistic-pillow":["Eleven songs that set the template for the San Francisco sound: folk-rock harmonies, Jorma Kaukonen's piercing lead guitar and Jack Casady's melodic, rumbling bass. Grace Slick's voice, sharp and declamatory, dominates 'Somebody to Love' and 'White Rabbit', a slow crescendo that recasts Alice in Wonderland as an allegory for drug experience. Marty Balin's tender 'Today' and 'Comin' Back to Me' provide the gentle side, and 'Embryonic Journey' is a short acoustic guitar instrumental.","Recorded in Los Angeles late in 1966 with producer Rick Jarrard and released by RCA Victor early in 1967. Slick had just joined from the Great Society, bringing two songs from that band; 'Somebody to Love' was written by her brother-in-law, Darby Slick. The album and its two hit singles helped make San Francisco the focus of national attention in the run-up to the Summer of Love.","'White Rabbit' is built on a steady bolero rhythm and a slow crescendo; Slick has said she was thinking of Ravel's Boléro and of Miles Davis and Gil Evans's Sketches of Spain, which she had listened to obsessively. Jerry Garcia of the Grateful Dead plays on several tracks and is credited on the sleeve as 'musical and spiritual adviser'. 'My Best Friend' was written by the band's former drummer, Skip Spence, who had left to join Moby Grape.","It was a commercial breakthrough and the definitive record of San Francisco psychedelia. Some critics have noted that it is more polished and folk-rooted than the band's live shows, which were looser and louder. Its two hit singles are now among the most familiar songs of the sixties, and 'White Rabbit' in particular is endlessly used in film and television to signal the era.","Listen to 'White Rabbit' alongside Ravel's Boléro to hear how the crescendo works in each, then try 'Comin' Back to Me' for the album's gentler side. Notice Casady's bass, which often carries a melody of its own. Follow it with the Grateful Dead's Live/Dead to hear the San Francisco scene stretched out on stage."],"jefferson-airplane-volunteers":["The Airplane's most openly political album, opening with 'We Can Be Together', a call for youth revolution sung in shouted unison, and closing with the title song, a rallying chant. Between them come 'Good Shepherd', an old hymn rearranged by Jorma Kaukonen, the country-flavoured 'The Farm', the post-apocalyptic 'Wooden Ships', and 'Meadowlands', a brief organ rendering of a Soviet song. Nicky Hopkins's piano runs through much of the album.","Recorded in San Francisco during 1969 and released by RCA late that year, a few months after the band played Woodstock. It was the last album with the original drummer Spencer Dryden and the last before the band splintered into side projects. The political mood reflected the Vietnam War, the urban riots and student uprisings of 1968, and the counterculture's turn from flower power toward confrontation.","'Wooden Ships' was written jointly by Paul Kantner, David Crosby and Stephen Stills, and Crosby, Stills and Nash released their own version the same year; hearing the two side by side shows the difference between the bands. Listen for Jerry Garcia's pedal steel guitar on 'The Farm', a sign of the Grateful Dead's simultaneous turn to country. 'Meadowlands' is a Red Army song, chosen as a provocation.","It was a commercial success and is often regarded as the Airplane's last great album, though some critics found its revolutionary slogans naive even at the time. RCA objected to some of its language, and the original title was shortened after a charity with a similar name complained. It is now valued as a document of the counterculture's most militant moment and as a showcase for some of the band's best playing.","Start with 'Wooden Ships' and compare it with the version on Crosby, Stills and Nash's debut. Then hear 'Good Shepherd' for Kaukonen's guitar and the band's harmonies at their best. Notice how Hopkins's piano fills the spaces. Pair it with Fortunate Son and 'Ohio' for other protest songs from the end of the decade."],"grateful-dead-live-dead":["A double album of seven performances in which songs dissolve into long collective improvisations. It opens with a twenty-three-minute 'Dark Star', where Jerry Garcia's guitar spins slow, searching lines over a floating, unhurried groove. 'St. Stephen' and 'The Eleven', the latter in a rolling eleven-beat cycle, follow without a break. Then comes Bobby Bland's 'Turn On Your Lovelight', sung by the organist Pigpen, the Reverend Gary Davis's 'Death Don't Have No Mercy', a passage of pure feedback, and a closing a cappella hymn.","Recorded at the Fillmore West and the Avalon Ballroom in San Francisco early in 1969 and released by Warner Bros. late that year. The band had spent heavily on studio albums that failed to capture their live energy, and they now recorded with a sixteen-track machine, then a new technology for live work. The line-up was at its largest, with two drummers, Bill Kreutzmann and Mickey Hart, and the keyboard player Tom Constanten.","Both Phil Lesh, the bassist, and Constanten had studied composition with Luciano Berio at Mills College, and Lesh's bass playing, which moves melodically rather than holding down the root, owes a debt to classical counterpoint and to free jazz. 'The Eleven' takes its name from its unusual eleven-beat metre, a feat few rock bands of the time would have attempted, sustained here for minutes at a time.","It was widely praised as the first record to capture the Grateful Dead's real nature, and it remains a reference point for the band's fans and for improvising rock musicians. Critics debate whether its long improvisations are profound or self-indulgent; the band's admirers point out that 'Dark Star' sounds different every time, and that this recording is only one of hundreds of versions.","Set aside time for 'Dark Star' and listen to it as you might a long jazz improvisation, following Garcia's line as it wanders and returns. Notice how Lesh's bass talks back. Then compare it with Coltrane's Live at the Village Vanguard Again or Miles Davis's Bitches Brew to hear what rock was borrowing from jazz."],"grateful-dead-workingmans-dead":["Eight compact songs, mostly acoustic and country-flavoured, a sharp turn from the long improvisations of Live/Dead. 'Uncle John's Band' opens with layered harmonies over acoustic guitars and a shifting rhythm. 'Dire Wolf' is a mock ballad, 'Cumberland Blues' a bluegrass-tinged song about a mining town, 'Black Peter' a slow lament from a dying man. 'Casey Jones' rides a train-like beat to tell the story of a reckless engineer. Robert Hunter's lyrics draw on American folk tales and work songs.","Recorded quickly in San Francisco early in 1970 and released by Warner Bros. in the spring. The band was deep in debt and reacting both to the expense of their earlier experiments and to the violence at the Altamont festival the previous December, which haunts 'New Speedway Boogie'. They had been spending time with Crosby, Stills and Nash, whose close harmony singing they set out to imitate.","Listen to the harmonies in 'Uncle John's Band': the band were not natural singers, and they worked hard to learn harmony from Crosby, Stills and Nash, which is why the singing is careful and slightly fragile. The song changes metre in its instrumental passage, a trace of the band's improvising past inside a folk song. 'Casey Jones' carries a passing drug reference in its chorus, set to the steady rhythm of a train.","It was the band's first commercial success and was immediately recognised as a turning point, opening up a large new audience. It is now regarded as one of the defining country-rock albums of its era. Some longtime fans regretted the loss of the long jams, but most critics see this and American Beauty as the band's finest studio work.","Start with 'Uncle John's Band' and listen to the harmonies and the shifting rhythm, then hear 'Cumberland Blues' for the bluegrass influence. Compare the album's compact songs with the sprawl of Live/Dead. Pair it with the Band's Music from Big Pink and the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo for other records of rock's return to American roots."],"grateful-dead-american-beauty":["Ten songs that complete the turn begun on Workingman's Dead, warmer and more varied. Phil Lesh sings 'Box of Rain', written for his dying father, over a gentle country rhythm. 'Friend of the Devil' is a fast, fingerpicked song about an outlaw on the run; 'Ripple' a near-hymn about the passage of life; 'Brokedown Palace' a farewell; 'Sugar Magnolia' a sunny love song; and 'Truckin'', the closer, a rambling account of life on the road.","Recorded in San Francisco in the late summer of 1970 and released by Warner Bros. in November, only months after Workingman's Dead. The sessions came during a hard year for the band, including the death of family members, and the album's gentleness carries a shadow of grief. The title is a pun on 'American Reality', and the sleeve's lettering can be read both ways.","The mandolin player David Grisman, later a leading figure in new acoustic music, plays on 'Friend of the Devil' and 'Ripple'. Listen to the harmonies of 'Attics of My Life', where the band's three voices move in close chords almost like a church choir, a skill they had developed from Crosby, Stills and Nash. 'Truckin'' became the band's anthem, and the drama of its lyrics was drawn from their own lives.","It was received as one of the band's best records and has been regarded as a classic ever since; many critics rate it as their finest studio album. It is often paired with Workingman's Dead as a single statement. Some fans who preferred the improvising band missed the long jams, but these songs became the core of their live sets for decades.","Start with 'Box of Rain' and 'Ripple', listening to the voices and acoustic guitars, then hear 'Truckin'' for the band's rocking side. Notice the slow tempos and the space between instruments. Pair it with Workingman's Dead and with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young's Déjà Vu, made at the same time and in the same atmosphere."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-green-river":["Nine songs in under half an hour, played by a four-piece band with almost no studio decoration. John Fogerty's rasping voice and twangy, echo-drenched guitar dominate the title track, a homesick song about a river from childhood. 'Bad Moon Rising' sets a warning of disaster to a bright, almost cheerful country shuffle. 'Lodi' is a weary song about a musician stranded in a small town, and 'Commotion' a fast, rattling rockabilly about modern noise.","Recorded at Wally Heider's studio in San Francisco and released by Fantasy in 1969, the second of three albums the band released that year. Creedence came from El Cerrito, across the bay from San Francisco, but ignored the psychedelic scene around them, playing short, tight songs drawn from rockabilly, Memphis soul and Louisiana swamp music. John Fogerty wrote, sang, arranged and produced nearly everything.","Fogerty has said the title came from the label of a soft-drink syrup, but the song's imagined river comes from childhood summers spent at a creek in Northern California. The 'swamp' sound of Creedence, which sounds as if it came from Louisiana, was entirely invented by a band who had never lived there; they built it from records by Sun artists, Bo Diddley and Louisiana swamp-pop singers.","It was a major success and has remained one of the band's most admired records. Critics praised its economy, a sharp contrast to the long jams of the San Francisco bands. Creedence was sometimes dismissed by the underground press as a singles band, but their reputation has grown steadily, and they are now regarded as one of the great American rock bands of the era.","Start with 'Green River' and listen to Fogerty's guitar echo and the push of the rhythm section, then 'Bad Moon Rising' for the gap between its bouncy tune and its dark warning. Notice how short and lean the songs are. Compare it with the Band's records from the same years for a different take on American roots music."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-fortunate-son":["Two and a quarter minutes of furious, driving rock, with John Fogerty's voice at its most raw. The song is an attack on the privileged families whose sons avoided military service in Vietnam while working-class boys went to fight. It starts with a sudden crash of drums and a jangling guitar riff, then rides a relentless beat with no slow-down, no solo of any length and no softening chorus. The anger is in the speed.","Released by Fantasy in the autumn of 1969 as a double-sided single with 'Down on the Corner', and included on the album Willy and the Poor Boys. Fogerty had served in the Army Reserve and was disgusted by the way the children of the powerful escaped the draft. The song appeared at the height of the anti-war movement, in the same season as the large Moratorium protests across America.","The song is often misheard or misused as patriotic, and has been played at political events in support of the very things it attacks; it is a song against privilege, not against soldiers. Listen to the band's restraint: the rhythm section plays straight, with no fills, so that the force comes from Fogerty's voice and the hard-strummed guitar. It is among the shortest songs in the band's catalogue.","It was a hit and became one of the defining protest songs of the Vietnam era, used in countless films about the war. Critics praise its directness and its class consciousness, which set it apart from more abstract anti-war songs. Its frequent use in contexts that ignore its meaning has become a subject of discussion in itself, and Fogerty has objected to its misuse.","Listen to the first second, the drum crash, and notice how the song never lets up. Focus on Fogerty's voice and the way his phrasing seems to spit the words out. Pair it with 'Ohio' and 'For What It's Worth' for three very different protest songs, and with Pete Seeger's 'Waist Deep in the Big Muddy' for an earlier generation's approach."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-cosmos-factory":["Eleven tracks that mix new John Fogerty songs with covers of the records he grew up on. The originals include 'Travelin' Band', a Little Richard-style rocker; 'Lookin' Out My Back Door', a cheerful, almost childlike song; 'Run Through the Jungle', dark and swampy, with backward guitar; 'Up Around the Bend'; and 'Who'll Stop the Rain'. The covers include Bo Diddley, Roy Orbison, Arthur Crudup and an eleven-minute version of 'I Heard It Through the Grapevine'.","Released by Fantasy in the summer of 1970, the band's fifth album in two years, at the peak of their commercial success. The title comes from the band's rehearsal space, a warehouse in Berkeley that the drummer Doug Clifford, nicknamed Cosmo, called 'the factory'. Within months, the band's guitarist Tom Fogerty, John's brother, would leave, tensions over John's control of the group having grown impossible.","'Run Through the Jungle' is often taken to be about Vietnam, but Fogerty has said it was about gun violence in America. Listen to the long 'Grapevine', where the band stretches out into a slow, groovy jam; it is a rare example of Creedence playing at length, and the result is closer to a Memphis soul band than to the San Francisco jam bands. The saxophone on some tracks is played by Fogerty himself.","It was the band's biggest selling album and is often regarded as their finest. Critics praise its range, from simple pop to swamp-blues menace, and its tribute to the band's influences. Some have found the long 'Grapevine' unnecessary, but others see it as a clue to the band's roots. It is now one of the most played rock albums of its time.","Start with 'Run Through the Jungle' and 'Who'll Stop the Rain' for the darker side, then 'Lookin' Out My Back Door' for the lighter. Then hear the 'Grapevine' and compare it with Marvin Gaye's version. Notice how tight the band is even when stretching out. Pair it with Green River to hear how much the band developed in a year."],"big-brother-and-the-holding-company-cheap-thrills":["Seven tracks, built around Janis Joplin's voice, which moves from a whisper to a scream within a phrase, over a ragged, overdriven band. 'Piece of My Heart' became a hit; 'Summertime' turns George Gershwin's lullaby into a slow, twisting guitar duet and a cry of pain; 'Ball and Chain', from the repertoire of Big Mama Thornton, runs nearly ten minutes and ends in a howl. 'Turtle Blues', a Joplin original, is a quiet, piano-backed blues.","Released by Columbia in 1968 after the band's explosive appearance at the Monterey Pop Festival the year before made Joplin a star. The producer John Simon struggled to capture the band in the studio, and the record was assembled mostly from studio takes with crowd noise added, to give it a live feeling; 'Ball and Chain' alone was recorded at a real concert. The cover was drawn by the underground cartoonist Robert Crumb.","Listen to 'Summertime': the two guitarists, Sam Andrew and James Gurley, weave an interlocking, almost Baroque counterpoint, a strange and touching meeting of classical habits and acid rock. The album's 'live' atmosphere is mostly an illusion, created in the studio. Joplin's voice owed its rawness to her deep study of blues singers like Bessie Smith, whose grave she helped to mark with a headstone.","It was a huge success, topping the American album chart, and made Joplin the leading female voice in rock. Some critics found the band sloppy and believed Joplin was held back by them; others argued their rawness was the right setting for her. Joplin left the band soon after, and the album is now valued as the best record of her with the group that made her famous.","Start with 'Summertime' and compare it with the original in Gershwin's Porgy and Bess, then hear 'Piece of My Heart' and 'Ball and Chain' for Joplin at full power. Notice how she uses breaks and cracks in her voice as expression. Pair it with Pearl to hear her with a tighter band."],"janis-joplin-pearl":["Ten tracks with the Full Tilt Boogie Band, a tighter and more soulful group than Big Brother. Kris Kristofferson's 'Me and Bobby McGee', a song about a drifter remembering a lost companion, starts soft and acoustic and builds to a joyful, rough-voiced climax. 'Cry Baby' and 'Get It While You Can' are soul ballads at full volume. 'Mercedes Benz' is sung unaccompanied, a wry prayer for luxury goods. 'Buried Alive in the Blues' is an instrumental.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1970 with the producer Paul Rothchild, best known for his work with the Doors. Joplin had left Big Brother, struggled with a first solo band, and found a sympathetic group in Full Tilt Boogie. She died in October 1970, before the sessions were finished, and the album was completed and released by Columbia in 1971. Pearl was her nickname.","'Buried Alive in the Blues' is an instrumental because Joplin had been scheduled to record her vocal the day after she died; the band left the track as it stood. 'Mercedes Benz' was recorded in a single take, and you can hear her laugh at the end. Listen to 'Me and Bobby McGee' for how she changes the song's mood from a folk ballad to a soul celebration.","It was a posthumous success, topping the American album chart, and 'Me and Bobby McGee' became a number one single. Critics have praised it as her most accomplished record and lamented the unfinished career. The debate is whether the polished band brought out her best or tamed her; most listeners now regard it as the best showcase for her voice.","Start with 'Me and Bobby McGee' and listen to how the arrangement builds with her voice, then hear 'Cry Baby' for her soul style. Notice the control as well as the power. Compare it with Cheap Thrills to hear how the band changes the singer, and with Aretha Franklin's records of the same years."],"moby-grape-moby-grape":["Thirteen short songs, written and sung by all five members, played with three interlocking guitars. 'Omaha' is a speedy, shouted song that crashes in and out; 'Hey Grandma' a bright, jangling rocker; '8:05' a gentle acoustic country ballad; 'Sitting by the Window' a wistful folk song. The sound is tight and concise, more like a British beat group or a country band than the long-jamming San Francisco bands around them.","Recorded in Los Angeles with producer David Rubinson and released by Columbia in 1967, at the height of the San Francisco boom. The label, trying to promote the band, released five singles at once, which confused radio and became a notorious marketing failure. Legal troubles followed soon after, and the band never recovered its momentum. Skip Spence, one of the guitarists, had been Jefferson Airplane's first drummer.","Listen to the three guitars of Spence, Jerry Miller and Peter Lewis, which play distinct parts that fit together like pieces of a puzzle, a texture very different from most San Francisco bands. On the original cover, the drummer Don Stevenson is making a rude gesture with his finger, which was airbrushed out of later pressings. Spence went on to make a cult solo album, Oar, in 1969.","It received excellent reviews but sold poorly, due in part to the label's mishandling. It has since been recognised as one of the finest debut albums of the sixties, and many musicians cite it as an influence. The band's story is often told as a cautionary tale about the music industry, but the record itself is now praised for its energy and songwriting.","Start with 'Omaha' and 'Hey Grandma' for the guitar interplay, then '8:05' for the country side. Listen to how quickly each song gets to the point. Pair it with Jefferson Airplane's Surrealistic Pillow and the Byrds' Younger Than Yesterday for other West Coast records of the same year."],"van-dyke-parks-song-cycle":["Ten songs that sound like a musical collage of American history, played by an orchestra and studio musicians and sung by Van Dyke Parks in a slightly nasal, playful voice. The arrangements shift constantly: ragtime piano, banjos, string swells, marching bands, accordions, music-box sounds. 'Vine Street', by Randy Newman, opens it; 'Palm Desert' evokes the Californian landscape; 'The All Golden' is a portrait of a friend. The production uses tape speed changes, echo and sudden cuts.","Released by Warner Bros. in 1968 and produced by Lenny Waronker, with engineer Bruce Botnick. Parks had been Brian Wilson's lyricist on the unfinished Smile project, and he made this album with lavish studio resources. It sold very poorly, and Warner Bros. responded with self-mocking advertisements making a joke of how much money it had lost. Parks went on to work as an arranger and producer for others.","Listen to the way Parks quotes and alludes to older American music: a fragment of a hymn, a parlour song, a march. The influence of Charles Ives, who also layered American tunes on top of one another, is everywhere. Parks's lyrics, as on Smile, are dense with puns and wordplay, and his studio tricks owe as much to radio drama as to pop records.","It was greeted with confusion and some admiration, and it sold poorly. Its reputation has grown slowly, as a cult record and a forerunner of later orchestral pop. Critics are still divided: some hear a visionary work about America, others a self-indulgent and impenetrable exercise. Musicians have often championed it, while general listeners find it hard work.","Treat it as a sound collage rather than a set of songs. Start with 'Vine Street' and 'Palm Desert', and listen for the constant shifts in orchestration. Pair it with the Beach Boys' Smile sessions and with Ives's Three Places in New England to hear its roots."],"crosby-stills-and-nash-crosby-stills-and-nash":["Ten songs built on close three-part harmony, a sound in which no single voice leads. 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' opens the album with a seven-minute piece in several sections, played on tuned-down acoustic guitars. 'Marrakesh Express' is Graham Nash's bright travelogue; 'Guinnevere' a slow, modal ballad by David Crosby; 'Wooden Ships' imagines survivors after a nuclear war; 'Helplessly Hoping' a delicate song with tight harmonies; and 'Long Time Gone' a slow, angry response to the assassination of Robert Kennedy.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1969 and released by Atlantic in May, a few months before the trio played Woodstock. Each member came from a successful band: Crosby from the Byrds, Stephen Stills from Buffalo Springfield, Graham Nash from the British group the Hollies. They found their sound singing together at a party, and the album helped establish the singer-songwriter era in rock. Stills played most of the instruments, with Dallas Taylor on drums.","The harmonies are recorded with each voice close to the microphone and carefully blended, so that you hear a single sound rather than three people. Listen to 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' and notice how the guitar tuning lets Stills play rich, ringing chords, and how the song moves through sections with different tempos. The closing section is sung in Spanish.","It was a huge success and became one of the defining records of the late sixties and early seventies. Critics praised the harmonies but some found the songs too sweet or self-satisfied. Its influence on soft rock and on the Laurel Canyon singer-songwriters is enormous, and its harmonies became a model for many bands, including the Grateful Dead.","Start with 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' and follow its sections, then 'Helplessly Hoping' for the harmonies. Notice how the voices move together. Pair it with Buffalo Springfield Again and the Byrds' Younger Than Yesterday to hear where the members came from, and with Déjà Vu for what came next."],"crosby-stills-nash-and-young-deja-vu":["Ten songs by four writers, with Neil Young joining the trio and bringing a rougher edge. 'Carry On' opens with a burst of acoustic guitars and harmonies; 'Teach Your Children' is a country song with pedal steel; 'Almost Cut My Hair' a ragged, angry Crosby blues; 'Helpless' Young's slow, aching song about a northern Ontario town; 'Woodstock' Joni Mitchell's song, turned into a hard rocker; 'Our House' Nash's cosy domestic portrait; and 'Déjà Vu' a jazzy, shifting meditation on reincarnation.","Recorded in San Francisco and Los Angeles in late 1969 and released by Atlantic in March 1970. Young, Stills's old colleague from Buffalo Springfield, had joined for live work in 1969, including Woodstock. The sessions were long and difficult, with each member often recording separately. Crosby was grieving the death of his partner, Christine Hinton, in a car accident, and the album's mood carries that loss.","Jerry Garcia of the Grateful Dead plays the pedal steel on 'Teach Your Children', a sign of the close ties between the two bands. Listen to the title track, where Crosby's song shifts metre and key, a nod to his interest in jazz. Joni Mitchell wrote 'Woodstock' without attending the festival, and this version, loud and electric, is very different from her own gentle recording.","It was a massive success and has remained one of the best-selling albums of its era. Critics praised its range but noted the divisions within the band. Young's songs are often singled out as the strongest, and the record is seen as the high point of the four together. Its harmonies and its picture of the counterculture made it one of the defining records of 1970.","Start with 'Helpless' and 'Teach Your Children', then hear 'Woodstock' and compare it with Joni Mitchell's version on Ladies of the Canyon. Notice the different voices of the four writers. Pair it with American Beauty and Neil Young's After the Gold Rush, made at the same time."],"crosby-stills-nash-and-young-ohio":["A three-minute song of grief and fury about the shooting of students by soldiers at a university campus in Ohio. Neil Young's song is built on a dark, menacing guitar riff and a slow, heavy beat. The band's harmonies, usually sweet, are here strained and anguished. Young's guitar and Stephen Stills's trade brief, stinging lines. At the end, the voices repeat a single phrase, building to a cry.","Written by Young in May 1970 after National Guard troops shot four students dead at Kent State University during a protest against the expansion of the Vietnam War into Cambodia. The band recorded it within weeks, live in the studio in Los Angeles, and Atlantic released it in June, with Stills's 'Find the Cost of Freedom' on the other side. It was rushed out while 'Teach Your Children' was still on the charts.","The recording was made in just a few takes, with the band playing together in one room; you can hear the rawness in the slightly rough edges. Listen to the end, where David Crosby's voice breaks away from the harmony into anguished shouts. The song names the president of the day, which made some radio stations refuse to play it. It was the band's most direct political statement.","It was a hit despite radio bans and has become one of the most powerful protest songs of its era. Critics praise its directness and urgency. It is often paired with Fortunate Son and For What It's Worth as a defining anti-war song, and it is now widely regarded as one of the greatest examples of rock responding to current events.","Listen to the opening riff and notice its darkness, then focus on the voices, especially in the final minute. Notice how the song never resolves into comfort. Pair it with 'Fortunate Son' and 'For What It's Worth' for three different approaches to protest, and with Dylan's 'Blowin' in the Wind' for an earlier generation's."],"the-mamas-and-the-papas-california-dreamin":["A song about longing for California's warmth during a cold East Coast winter, sung by four voices in tight harmony over a folk-rock backing. Denny Doherty sings lead, with the others answering in close echoes; Cass Elliot's voice gives the harmonies their warmth. The guitar intro, a descending figure, is instantly recognisable. In the middle, a solo on alto flute adds a soft, jazzy colour.","Written by John and Michelle Phillips in New York during the winter of 1963, before they moved to California. It was recorded in Los Angeles in 1965 with producer Lou Adler and released by Dunhill late that year, becoming a hit early in 1966. The group came from the New York folk scene and the record helped bring the harmony-rich folk rock of Los Angeles to the charts.","The backing track was originally recorded for the singer Barry McGuire, with the group on backing vocals; Doherty's lead vocal later replaced McGuire's. The alto flute solo was played by the jazz musician Bud Shank. Listen to how the harmonies are arranged as call and response, with the backing voices echoing the lead line, a trick borrowed from gospel.","It was a huge success and has remained one of the defining songs of the sixties. Critics have praised its harmonies and its evocation of longing. It is often seen as the start of the Laurel Canyon era and has been widely covered. Its use in films and advertising has made it one of the most familiar songs of its time.","Listen to the guitar intro and then the harmonies, noticing how the voices answer each other. Wait for the flute solo and notice its jazzy colour. Pair it with the Byrds' 'Mr. Tambourine Man' and the Beach Boys' 'California Girls' for other visions of California in the mid-sixties."],"steely-dan-cant-buy-a-thrill":["Ten songs that sound like a working band and a pair of sly songwriters at once. 'Do It Again' opens on a patient Latin groove of hand percussion and bass, then gives its middle to two strange solos, one on electric sitar and one on a thin, buzzing combo organ. 'Reelin' in the Years' rides a sprinting guitar break; 'Dirty Work' is a soft soul ballad sung by a different voice. Polished, jazz-inflected chords sit under deceptively breezy pop.","Walter Becker and Donald Fagen met at Bard College in upstate New York, scraped by in the New York song trade and were hired as staff writers at ABC Records in Los Angeles through the producer Gary Katz. Their songs were too odd for other singers, so Katz helped them assemble a band of their own, named after an object in William S. Burroughs's novel Naked Lunch. This debut, made quickly in Los Angeles, was the result.","The lead vocals are split: Fagen sings most songs, but David Palmer, brought in because Fagen was nervous about singing, takes 'Dirty Work' and 'Brooklyn'. Listen to the guitar solo on 'Reelin' in the Years': it is played not by a band member but by the session guitarist Elliott Randall, an early sign of the hired-hand method that would later define Steely Dan entirely.","Both main singles became radio staples and the album sold well, an unusual start for a group whose lyrics were cryptic and sardonic. Early reviewers heard a clever pop band; later critics heard the first draft of a sensibility, ironic, literate and harmonically rich, that the duo would refine through the decade. Some fans still prefer this looser, band-like record to the studio perfectionism that followed.","Start with 'Do It Again' and wait for the two solos in the middle, each with its own odd timbre. Then play 'Reelin' in the Years' for the guitar. Compare 'Dirty Work' with Fagen's own singing elsewhere to hear why he eventually took over. Afterwards jump to Aja to hear how far the method travelled in five years."],"steely-dan-pretzel-logic":["Eleven songs in barely more than half an hour, most of them under three minutes and packed tight with chords. 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number' opens with a gentle wooden marimba and a loping bass figure before a sunny chorus; 'Night by Night' and the title track lean on horns and blues; 'Parker's Band' is a breathless tribute to Charlie Parker; and a cover of Duke Ellington's 'East St. Louis Toodle-Oo' turns a 1920s jungle-band number into a guitar showcase.","By their third album Becker and Fagen were already drifting from the idea of a fixed band, calling on Los Angeles session players alongside the original members. It was the last record Steely Dan made as a touring group: after the 1974 shows they withdrew from the road and became a studio partnership. ABC gave them unusual freedom because the earlier singles had sold, and they used it to pursue their love of jazz.","The bass line under the opening of 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number' is borrowed from the piano vamp of Horace Silver's 'Song for My Father', a deliberate tip of the hat to a Blue Note record. In the Ellington cover, Walter Becker's guitar, bent and filtered, is imitating the growling plunger-muted trumpet that Bubber Miley played on the original. Both are clues to the duo's real record collection.","'Rikki' became their biggest hit single and the album was well reviewed, though some critics found the duo's irony chilly and their brevity frustrating. Over time the record has been reappraised as their most compact statement, a bridge between the rock band of the first two albums and the jazz-session laboratories of Aja and Gaucho. Jazz writers noticed the Ellington and Parker homages with some amusement and some respect.","Play 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number', then put on Horace Silver's 'Song for My Father' and hear the borrowing. Next, play 'East St. Louis Toodle-Oo' beside an early Ellington recording and listen for Miley's trumpet in Becker's guitar. Notice how short every song is: the whole record moves like a book of sharp short stories."],"steely-dan-aja":["Seven long, glossy songs played by a rotating cast of the best session musicians in Los Angeles and New York. 'Black Cow' rides a slinky groove; 'Deacon Blues' unfolds a loser's dream of becoming a saxophonist over rich horn chords; 'Peg' is bright and bouncy; and the eight-minute title track moves through several sections like a small jazz suite, climaxing in a tenor saxophone solo over furious drumming. Every surface is clean, warm and exactly placed.","Becker and Fagen had stopped touring and now worked purely in the studio with the producer Gary Katz and engineers including Roger Nichols, auditioning player after player for single parts. The method was expensive and slow, made possible by ABC's faith and the late-seventies boom in album sales. Aja arrived at the height of the punk year, as a deliberate opposite: the most carefully made record imaginable.","On the title track, the tenor solo is by Wayne Shorter of Weather Report and the drums by Steve Gadd, who reportedly played his famous fills in very few takes. On 'Peg', several guitarists were tried before Jay Graydon's solo was kept; Michael McDonald's high harmony stacks the chorus. A decade later De La Soul looped 'Peg' for 'Eye Know', carrying the record into hip-hop.","It became the duo's best-selling album and won a Grammy for its engineering, and hi-fi shops used it to demonstrate speakers. Critics argued about whether such perfection was soulless; jazz musicians generally admired its harmonic sophistication. Its reputation has only grown, revived by later generations of producers who study its drum sounds, and it is now a reference point for so-called yacht rock and for studio craft generally.","Play the title track on good speakers and wait for Shorter's entrance, then follow Gadd's drums under him to the end. 'Deacon Blues' rewards attention to its horn voicings. Then hear De La Soul's 'Eye Know' to catch 'Peg' in a new life. Any decent remaster will do; this is a record made for careful listening."],"steely-dan-gaucho":["Seven songs with a cooler, more nocturnal sheen than Aja. 'Babylon Sisters' opens on a slow, heavy groove with women's voices answering Fagen; 'Hey Nineteen' is a wry song about an older man discovering how little he shares with a younger generation; 'Time Out of Mind' has a guest guitar line from Mark Knopfler; the title track drifts over a long saxophone-led groove. Grooves are slower and the horn writing is denser than ever.","The album took around two years and an enormous studio budget, during a period when the label situation changed and MCA absorbed ABC. Becker was injured when struck by a car in New York, the duo were exhausted, and a finished track, 'The Second Arrangement', was accidentally erased by an assistant engineer. After its release Becker and Fagen went their separate ways for more than a decade.","The title track's main theme closely resembles Keith Jarrett's 'Long As You Know You're Living Yours' from his 1974 quartet album Belonging; after Jarrett objected, he was given a writing credit. Listen for how often the songs fade on a groove rather than ending, and for the drum machine-like precision that Roger Nichols pursued with an early sampling device of his own design.","It sold well and 'Hey Nineteen' was a hit, but reviewers were divided: some heard the duo's richest harmonies, others a sterile, airless record made by men who had lost interest in rock. The punishing sessions became legend. Later listeners, and the producers who sample it, rank it close to Aja, and its quiet, adult melancholy now sounds more personal than it seemed in 1980.","Begin with 'Babylon Sisters' at night and turn it up enough to feel the bass. Then play the title track beside Jarrett's 'Long As You Know You're Living Yours' to hear the borrowed melody. 'Time Out of Mind' is worth hearing for the Knopfler line, which sounds like a guest from Dire Straits walking into the room."],"eagles-take-it-easy":["A three-and-a-half-minute road song about a restless young man telling himself not to let life's troubles weigh him down. It opens with a bright strum of acoustic guitar, settles into a rolling mid-tempo beat, and lifts into a chorus of close, high four-part harmony. A twanging electric guitar and a banjo threaded through the final choruses tie it to country music, while the drums and volume keep it firmly rock.","The Eagles formed in Los Angeles in 1971 from musicians who had backed Linda Ronstadt: Glenn Frey, Don Henley, Bernie Leadon and Randy Meisner. Signed to David Geffen's new Asylum label, they recorded their debut in London with the producer Glyn Johns. 'Take It Easy' was the first single. Jackson Browne had begun the song and could not finish it; Frey, his neighbour, completed it.","The song's best-known verse, set on a street corner in Winslow, Arizona, was Frey's contribution to Browne's unfinished draft, and the town later built a park around it. Listen for Leadon's banjo, played in a fast bluegrass roll that sits low in the mix: he was the band's bridge to bluegrass and the Flying Burrito Brothers, and his departure later changed their sound.","The single was a hit and launched the band, and Browne released his own version the next year. Critics in the early seventies sometimes found the Eagles too smooth beside the country-rock pioneers they followed, Gram Parsons above all. The song has outlasted the argument, becoming a standard of American radio and the blueprint for the Southern Californian sound of the decade.","Listen first to the chorus harmonies, four voices stacked so close they almost blur. Then find the banjo in the closing minute. For background, play the Flying Burrito Brothers' Gilded Palace of Sin and the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo, then Browne's Late for the Sky to hear where the writing went next."],"eagles-hotel-california":["Nine songs that together form a portrait of Los Angeles excess and disillusion. The title track opens with a twelve-string figure over a lilting, almost reggae rhythm, tells a dreamlike story of a traveller trapped in a luxurious hotel, and ends with two electric guitars trading lines and then playing in harmony for two minutes. 'New Kid in Town' is soft and wistful, 'Life in the Fast Lane' hard and riffing, and 'The Last Resort' a long elegy.","By 1976 the Eagles were among the biggest bands in America, and success had changed both them and their city. Bernie Leadon had left and been replaced by Joe Walsh, a hard-rock guitarist, pulling the band away from country. The producer Bill Szymczyk recorded them in Miami and Los Angeles over many months. Henley and Frey intended the album as a commentary on the American dream and the music business.","The music of the title track began as a demo by guitarist Don Felder; Henley and Frey wrote the words. The famous outro is a dialogue between Felder and Walsh, each taking alternate phrases before they lock into harmony. 'Try and Love Again' is sung and written by Randy Meisner, his last major contribution before leaving: listen for his gentler, higher voice amid Henley's and Frey's.","It sold enormously and the title track won the Grammy for Record of the Year. Rock critics of the punk years treated the Eagles as everything wrong with corporate rock, while listeners kept buying the record. The title song attracted fanciful rumours about hidden meanings, which the band dismissed. Today the album is widely read as the band's most ambitious statement, and the guitar outro remains one of rock's most studied solos.","Play the title track and follow the two guitars in the last two minutes, one in each speaker. Then listen to 'The Last Resort' as the album's real conclusion, a long piano-led lament. For contrast, put on the Ramones' first album from the same year: two utterly different answers to what rock should be in 1976."],"allman-brothers-band-at-fillmore-east":["A double live album of only seven performances. It opens with Duane Allman's sliding bottleneck guitar tearing into Blind Willie McTell's 'Statesboro Blues', moves through Elmore James and T-Bone Walker, then stretches out: Dickey Betts's instrumental 'In Memory of Elizabeth Reed' builds through modal, jazz-style improvising, and Gregg Allman's 'Whipping Post' runs past twenty minutes. Two drummers lock together underneath while two guitars weave melodies in and out of harmony.","The band formed in Jacksonville in 1969 and based itself in Macon, Georgia, on Phil Walden's Capricorn label, as an unusually integrated Southern group with a Black drummer, Jaimoe, alongside Butch Trucks. Their studio albums had not captured them, so the producer Tom Dowd recorded several shows at Bill Graham's Fillmore East in New York in March 1971. Months after release, Duane Allman died in a motorcycle accident in Macon.","Duane said he had absorbed Miles Davis's Kind of Blue and John Coltrane's modal playing, and you can hear it in 'Elizabeth Reed', which sits on long stretches of one harmony while soloists build tension the way a jazz quartet would. Listen too for Berry Oakley's bass, which roams like a third melodic voice rather than holding the root, and for the two drummers splitting the beat between them.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and is routinely named among the greatest live rock albums. Critics praised it at once as proof that long improvisation could be disciplined rather than self-indulgent. Later expanded editions, presenting the full shows, have allowed comparison between nights; most listeners still consider Dowd's original selection the definitive shape, and the record is the founding document of Southern rock.","Start with 'Statesboro Blues' to hear Duane's slide, then give 'In Memory of Elizabeth Reed' your full attention and follow how the solos rise and fall. Save 'Whipping Post' for when you can sit through all of it. Afterwards, play Kind of Blue and listen for the shared sense of space."],"allman-brothers-band-eat-a-peach":["A double album that is half studio and half stage. The studio side includes 'Blue Sky', a sunny country-tinged song by Dickey Betts in which two guitars trade warm, singing solos, and 'Little Martha', a short acoustic duet. Other studio songs, such as 'Melissa' and 'Ain't Wastin' Time No More', were cut by the band after Duane's death. The live half is dominated by 'Mountain Jam', more than half an hour of improvisation from the same Fillmore concerts.","Duane Allman died in October 1971 while the band was midway through recording. The surviving five completed the studio songs, and Capricorn filled out the release with live material left over from the March Fillmore East shows. Released early in 1972, it became a memorial and a statement that the band would carry on; Berry Oakley died the following year in a similar accident a few streets away in Macon.","'Little Martha' is the only song Duane Allman ever wrote, a delicate fingerpicked duet with Betts. 'Mountain Jam' grows out of a melody from Donovan's 'There Is a Mountain', which the band turned into a launching pad for long solos; listen for the tune surfacing at the start and returning near the end. 'Blue Sky' holds some of Duane's last studio solos, alternating with Betts.","It sold strongly and was received with affection and grief. Critics singled out 'Blue Sky' and 'Melissa' as the band's warmest songs, while opinions on the long 'Mountain Jam' divided along familiar lines about improvisation. It now stands as a companion to At Fillmore East, the second half of Duane's legacy, and 'Melissa' has become one of Gregg Allman's most enduring ballads.","Play 'Blue Sky' first and follow the two guitars passing the solo back and forth. Then 'Little Martha' for its intimacy. If you have half an hour, 'Mountain Jam' rewards listening for how the band shifts tempo and mood. Compare the guitar duet with the twin-lead style that Thin Lizzy later made into a rock signature."],"lynyrd-skynyrd-free-bird":["A nine-minute song in two halves about a man telling a lover he must leave because he cannot change. The first half is a slow ballad, with piano, organ and a weeping slide guitar under Ronnie Van Zant's plain, unhurried voice. Then the tempo doubles and the last four minutes are an extended guitar finale, the band's lead players overlapping and racing through fast, repeated phrases until the song finally collapses.","Lynyrd Skynyrd came from Jacksonville, Florida, and named themselves, with a twist of spelling, after a gym teacher who disliked long hair. Al Kooper, who had played organ for Bob Dylan, signed them to his Sounds of the South label, distributed by MCA, and produced their 1973 debut album. 'Free Bird' closed that record and became the band's signature, particularly in concert, where it often ran far longer.","The piano introduction was added by Billy Powell, who had worked as a roadie before the band heard him play. In concert the band dedicated the song to Duane Allman, and the slide guitar of the first half is a clear homage to his style. Listen in the coda for how the three guitars are layered, one holding a rhythm while two others trade and double the lead.","Released as an edited single in 1974, it became a radio standard and a staple of album-oriented rock stations. Its length and its finale made it a byword for the guitar epic, and shouted requests for it became a running joke at concerts of every kind. Beneath the joke, guitarists continue to study the coda, and it remains Southern rock's best-known song.","Hear the full album version, not the single edit. In the first half, notice how slowly the song is allowed to move. Then, when the tempo jumps, follow one guitar and try to keep track of it as the others swirl around. Afterwards play the Allman Brothers' At Fillmore East to hear the model it honours."],"lynyrd-skynyrd-sweet-home-alabama":["A mid-tempo anthem of regional pride, written partly as a reply to Neil Young's criticisms of the South. It opens with a clean, chiming guitar riff of three chords that cycle through the whole song, a relaxed drum groove and a spoken instruction to turn up the volume. Ronnie Van Zant half-sings, half-talks the verses; women's voices answer him in a gospel-style chorus, and Billy Powell's piano rolls under the final instrumental section.","It opened the band's second album, Second Helping, again produced by Al Kooper. Neil Young had written 'Southern Man' and 'Alabama', songs condemning racism in the South, and Van Zant, an admirer of Young, answered on behalf of Southerners who felt caricatured. The song also refers to Alabama's governor and to Watergate, at a moment when American politics was in crisis, and to the Muscle Shoals session musicians.","The riff and solo come from Ed King, then one of the band's three guitarists, who said the music came to him in a dream. The friendly feud with Neil Young was mostly public theatre: Van Zant often wore Young's T-shirts on stage and Young later admired the song. Listen for the backing singers, whose call-and-response gives a gospel colour to a song about the white South.","It became the band's biggest hit. Arguments about its politics began at once and have not ended: some hear a defence of Governor George Wallace, others note that the lyrics seem to boo him and that the song is more ambiguous than its reputation. Musicians admire the riff's economy, and it has become a fixture of sports grounds and films, where its meaning is often flattened.","Listen to the riff first: it never changes, yet the song does not tire. Then play Neil Young's 'Southern Man' on After the Gold Rush and 'Alabama' on Harvest to hear both sides of the argument. Notice how relaxed the drumming is; the song swings rather than pushes."],"zz-top-tres-hombres":["Ten tough, compact songs by a Texas power trio. Billy Gibbons's guitar is thick, greasy and full of pinched harmonics; Dusty Hill's bass and Frank Beard's drums are spare and heavy. 'Waitin' for the Bus' slides straight into the slow, gospel-tinged blues 'Jesus Just Left Chicago'; 'La Grange', about a long-tolerated brothel outside La Grange, Texas, rides a whispered boogie before exploding into a stinging solo; 'Beer Drinkers and Hell Raisers' simply rocks.","ZZ Top formed in Houston at the end of the sixties under the manager and producer Bill Ham, who kept tight control of their image and sound. Their first two albums sold modestly; this third one, released on London Records, made them stars across the American South and then nationally, helped by relentless touring. They styled themselves as working-class Texans with a deep respect for the region's blues guitarists.","The rhythm of 'La Grange' borrows the hypnotic one-chord boogie of John Lee Hooker's 'Boogie Chillen', by way of Canned Heat; the copyright owners of the Hooker song later took ZZ Top to court. Listen for Gibbons's tone, a fat, slightly distorted sound he got partly by playing with the edge of a coin as a pick, and for the way the songs run into one another on side one.","It was their breakthrough and remains the album most fans and critics name as their best. Reviewers in 1973 praised its lean, unpretentious sound against the excesses of progressive rock. Later generations of hard rock and stoner rock bands cited its tones. The 'La Grange' lawsuit became a reference case in debates about where blues tradition ends and copyright begins.","Play 'Waitin' for the Bus' and 'Jesus Just Left Chicago' together, as they were meant to be. Then 'La Grange', and listen to the first minute's near-whisper before the guitar breaks loose. Afterwards hear John Lee Hooker's 'Boogie Chillen' to find the root of the groove."],"zz-top-eliminator":["Eleven songs in which a blues-rock band meets the machines of the eighties. The beats are steady, punchy and perfectly regular, and synthesizers pulse under the guitar; 'Gimme All Your Lovin'' and 'Sharp Dressed Man' run on driving, straight eighth notes, while 'Legs' adds a bright synthesized arpeggio. Through it all Billy Gibbons's guitar is still the old Texas sound, stinging and bluesy, now laid over a glossy dance-floor pulse.","By the early eighties ZZ Top were a veteran touring act, and Gibbons was fascinated by synthesizers, sequencers and the new music of dance clubs. Much of the album was engineered by Terry Manning in Memphis. Its release coincided with the rise of MTV, and the band's videos, featuring a red customised 1933 Ford coupé, a trio of glamorous women and the members' long beards, turned them into cartoon icons.","Listeners have long argued about how much of the drumming is Frank Beard and how much is programmed or heavily processed; the band have been vague, and the steady tempos suggest machines played a large part. Listen to 'Sharp Dressed Man' with headphones and notice how the synthesizer bass doubles Dusty Hill's bass guitar, a trick that gives the record its unusual weight.","It became the band's best-selling album by far and one of the defining rock records of the MTV era. Long-time fans and some critics complained that the old grit had been sacrificed to technology; others admired the boldness of a blues band embracing the drum machine. In retrospect it reads as an early example of rock musicians absorbing electronic production without abandoning their identity.","Play 'Sharp Dressed Man' and listen to how mechanical the beat is, then how loose the guitar solo feels on top. Then go back to Tres Hombres and compare the drum sounds across ten years. For contrast with another rock-meets-machine story, try Kraftwerk's Computer World or Talking Heads' Remain in Light."],"thin-lizzy-whiskey-in-the-jar":["A traditional Irish ballad about a highwayman betrayed by the woman he loves, reimagined by a rock trio. Eric Bell's electric guitar plays a lilting, ornamented melody that echoes the fiddle and whistle tunes of Irish folk, opening the song and returning between verses. Phil Lynott sings the story in a soft, almost conversational voice over Brian Downey's light, rolling drums, and the chorus has the swing of a pub singalong.","Thin Lizzy formed in Dublin in 1969 around Lynott, a Black Irishman raised by his grandmother in Crumlin. They recorded the song almost as a throwaway, playing around in rehearsal, and Decca released it as a single. It became their first big hit, in Ireland and Britain, at a time when the Dubliners and the Irish folk revival had made the old song well known in pubs and on radio.","Listen to Eric Bell's guitar line: he reportedly worked hard to make an electric guitar phrase like a traditional tune, with grace notes and slides in place of a fiddle's ornaments. Lynott was uneasy that the band's first hit was a folk cover rather than one of his own songs, and Bell left soon afterwards. The arrangement is the template behind most later rock versions, including Metallica's.","Its success put Thin Lizzy on British television but briefly typecast them as a folk-rock novelty, an image they shed with harder records later. Irish critics have often seen it as an early meeting of traditional music and rock, part of the same current as Horslips and Planxty. Metallica's Grammy-winning cover in the late nineties introduced the arrangement to a new audience.","Listen for the guitar introduction and imagine it on a fiddle; the phrasing is folk, the tone is rock. Then hear Planxty or the Bothy Band play traditional Irish tunes to recognise the source of the ornaments. Finally jump to Thin Lizzy's Jailbreak to hear how far the band travelled in four years."],"thin-lizzy-jailbreak":["Nine songs of swaggering hard rock built around two lead guitars playing melodies in harmony. The title track opens on a stop-start riff and a siren-like guitar wail; 'The Boys Are Back in Town' is a jaunty celebration of friends reuniting for a night out, with twin guitars singing its hook; 'Cowboy Song' borrows from westerns; and 'Emerald' is a pounding, Celtic-flavoured song steeped in Irish martial legend, ending in a long guitar duel.","Lynott rebuilt Thin Lizzy in 1974 with two young guitarists, the Californian Scott Gorham and the Glaswegian Brian Robertson, and the twin-guitar format was born. After two albums that failed to break through, the band faced being dropped by Vertigo. Jailbreak, produced by John Alcock, was effectively their last chance, and its success, particularly in America, transformed their fortunes.","The guitar harmonies grew naturally from having two lead players of equal standing: instead of trading solos, they often play the same melody together, a few notes apart. Lynott was a great admirer of Bruce Springsteen, and the street-gang romance of 'The Boys Are Back in Town' owes something to Born to Run. Listen to 'Emerald' for melodies built on Irish modal scales.","'The Boys Are Back in Town' became their biggest hit in America and the album their best-selling. Critics praised Lynott's lyrical warmth, unusual in hard rock. Later, the twin-guitar sound was taken up by the new wave of British heavy metal, Iron Maiden above all, and by countless metal bands, so the album's influence reaches well beyond its own genre.","Start with 'The Boys Are Back in Town' and listen for the two guitars entering together on the hook. Then 'Emerald', loud, for the Celtic melodies and the duelling solos. Afterwards play Iron Maiden's debut to hear where the twin-lead sound went, and Springsteen's Born to Run to hear Lynott's model."],"t-rex-electric-warrior":["Eleven songs that marry the boogie of fifties rock and roll to a dreamy, cosmic sensibility. 'Get It On' struts on a simple guitar riff, handclaps and Marc Bolan's breathy, purring voice; 'Jeepster' bounces on a shuffle; 'Mambo Sun' slinks; 'Cosmic Dancer' floats on strings, a song about a life lived dancing from birth to death. High, falsetto backing voices and Bolan's chopped, distorted guitar are everywhere, and the whole record sounds playful and close-up.","Bolan had led Tyrannosaurus Rex as an acoustic hippie duo, writing whimsical, Tolkien-flavoured songs. In 1970 he plugged in, shortened the name to T. Rex, added a rhythm section and worked with the producer Tony Visconti. When he appeared on Top of the Pops with glitter on his cheeks, British teenagers responded in a wave of 'T. Rextasy', and Electric Warrior became the first great album of glam rock.","The high backing voices are Mark Volman and Howard Kaylan, former members of the Turtles known as Flo and Eddie, who added a California pop sheen. Listen to 'Get It On': its riff is Bolan's tribute to Chuck Berry's 'Little Queenie', which he acknowledged. Visconti's string arrangements on 'Cosmic Dancer' and 'Girl' show the producer's classical leanings, which he would bring to Bowie as well.","It topped the British album chart and 'Get It On' reached the American top ten under the title 'Bang a Gong'. Some critics dismissed Bolan as a teen idol; others saw at once that he had reinvented rock and roll as theatre. Its influence on Bowie, on punk's simple riffs and on later British guitar pop is now widely acknowledged.","Play 'Get It On' and listen to how little is happening: a riff, handclaps, a voice. Then 'Cosmic Dancer' for the strings and the melancholy beneath the glitter. Afterwards hear Chuck Berry for the source and Bowie's Ziggy Stardust, made a year later with the same producer's circle, for what followed."],"mott-the-hoople-all-the-young-dudes":["A slow, swaying anthem in which a young generation in the glam era announces itself to a world that does not understand it. A descending guitar figure and a stately organ set a march-like pace; Ian Hunter sings in a weary, sardonic drawl; the chorus swells into a mass singalong with handclaps. In the fade, Hunter ad-libs over the chorus as if addressing a crowd, and the song ends sounding like a gathering rather than a performance.","Mott the Hoople, from Herefordshire, had built a loyal live following without hits and were on the point of breaking up. David Bowie, a fan, offered them a song; when they turned down 'Suffragette City', he wrote 'All the Young Dudes' for them and produced the recording. Released in the summer of 1972, during Bowie's Ziggy Stardust period, it became their first hit and saved the band.","Listen for the guitar line that walks down at the start, which gives the song its gentle, falling momentum and returns under every chorus. Hunter's improvised asides in the fade were his own idea, adding a rough, conversational edge to Bowie's polished song. Bowie later recorded his own version, and comparing the two shows how much the band's warmth contributed.","It was a major British hit and became a glam rock anthem, adopted by audiences far beyond the band's original fans. Some heard it as a coded statement of gay or outsider identity; Bowie gave ambiguous answers. It is now regarded as one of the defining songs of the early seventies and one of the best things Bowie wrote for someone else.","Listen to the chorus with the volume up and notice how the handclaps and massed voices make it feel like a crowd. Then compare Bowie's own recording. For context, play the Who's 'My Generation' from seven years earlier, a louder, angrier youth anthem, and Bowie's Ziggy Stardust from the same year."],"mott-the-hoople-mott":["Nine songs in which a band reflects on its own rise and fragility. 'All the Way from Memphis' opens with a rolling barrelhouse piano and a honking saxophone, a comic tale of a musician separated from his guitar; 'Honaloochie Boogie' is a jaunty glam stomp; 'Ballad of Mott the Hoople' is a rueful look back at the band's struggling years; 'I Wish I Was Your Mother' is tender and folky; 'Hymn for the Dudes' closes in slow grandeur.","After the Bowie-produced breakthrough, the band chose to produce themselves rather than depend on him. The band was strained by success and by the question of who would lead it. Ian Hunter emerged as the main songwriter and voice, with lyrics increasingly concerned with what it meant to be a rock band. Shortly afterwards guitarist Mick Ralphs left to form Bad Company and Hunter would soon go solo.","Listen for the self-referential threads: several songs are about Mott the Hoople itself, its history, its audience and its uncertain future, an early example of a rock album about being a rock band. The saxophone on 'All the Way from Memphis' is Andy Mackay of Roxy Music, a link between two arms of British glam. Hunter's piano drives many songs more than the guitars do.","It sold well in Britain and is often counted as the band's best album, prized for Hunter's honest, witty writing. Critics compared his voice to Bob Dylan's, a debt he acknowledged. Punk musicians a few years later, including members of the Clash, cited Mott as an influence for their directness and their bond with their audience.","Begin with 'All the Way from Memphis' for the piano and sax. Then 'Ballad of Mott the Hoople' to hear the band telling its own story. Afterwards play the Clash's first album and listen for Hunter's attitude, or Roxy Music's debut to hear Mackay in his own band."],"big-star-number-1-record":["Twelve songs of ringing guitars, close harmonies and teenage longing. 'Feel' opens with a crunching riff; 'The Ballad of El Goodo' is a slow, determined anthem of perseverance with soaring harmonies; 'In the Street' celebrates aimless youth; 'Thirteen' is a whispered acoustic song of early adolescent love. The sound balances the bright jangle of the Byrds and the melodies of the Beatles with a Memphis band's rawness, and two contrasting songwriters' voices.","Big Star formed in Memphis around Chris Bell and Alex Chilton, who as a teenager had sung the Box Tops' hit 'The Letter'. They recorded at Ardent Studios with the engineer and label owner John Fry, and the album was released by Ardent through Stax, the great Memphis soul label. Stax's distribution problems meant that, despite glowing reviews, the record was hard to find in shops.","Listen for the two lead voices: Bell's earnest, polished songs such as 'Feel' and 'My Life Is Right' sit beside Chilton's looser, more ironic ones, and on several tracks they sing together. The title was a hopeful joke. 'In the Street' was later covered by Cheap Trick as the theme for the television series That '70s Show, giving the song a far larger audience than the original ever had.","Critics loved it, but sales were tiny, and Bell, frustrated, left the band. The album's reputation grew slowly through the eighties as R.E.M., the Replacements and many alternative bands championed Big Star; the Replacements even wrote a song called 'Alex Chilton'. It is now regarded as a founding document of power pop and of indie guitar music in general.","Start with 'The Ballad of El Goodo' and listen to the harmonies climbing in the chorus. Then 'Thirteen', one of the quietest and most affecting songs of its era. Afterwards play the Replacements' 'Alex Chilton' and R.E.M.'s Murmur to hear the line of descent, then Radio City."],"big-star-radio-city":["Twelve songs, looser and more jagged than the debut, made by Big Star as a trio. 'O My Soul' is a long, rambling funk-rock opener; 'Back of a Car' crashes with nervous energy; 'Daisy Glaze' shifts tempo and mood like a drunken ballad that suddenly runs; 'Life Is White' and 'Mod Lang' snarl; and 'September Gurls' chimes with some of the most celebrated jangling guitars in rock. Chilton's voice is now the dominant one, cracked and vulnerable.","After Chris Bell left, Alex Chilton, Andy Hummel and Jody Stephens briefly split up and then regrouped in Memphis to record this second album at Ardent. Stax was by then in financial trouble and had shifted distribution to Columbia, which declined to handle the record properly, so Radio City suffered the same fate as #1 Record: excellent reviews and almost no copies in shops.","The cover is The Red Ceiling, a photograph by the Mississippi-born photographer William Eggleston, a friend of the band, showing a bare bulb against a saturated red ceiling; its stark colour fits the record's unsettled mood. Listen to the drums on 'Daisy Glaze', where Jody Stephens changes tempo as if the song were being written as it goes. Bell is believed to have contributed to some songs without credit.","Critics were enthusiastic but the band dissolved soon after, and Chilton's reputation became that of a cult figure. In the eighties Radio City, with 'September Gurls' in particular, was rediscovered by indie musicians who regarded it as the band's high point. Many listeners now prefer its ragged honesty to the debut's polish, though opinion remains split.","Play 'September Gurls' first for the guitars, then 'Back of a Car' and 'Daisy Glaze' to feel the band's nerves fraying. Compare it with #1 Record for how the loss of Bell changed the sound. Then move on to Third/Sister Lovers for Chilton's darker last word."],"todd-rundgren-something-anything":["A sprawling double album of pop songs, rockers, studio experiments and jokes. 'I Saw the Light' is a sparkling piano ballad; 'Hello It's Me' is a slow, soulful love song with rich chords; 'Couldn't I Just Tell You' is a crunching power-pop gem. On three of the four sides Todd Rundgren plays every instrument and sings every voice, sometimes stacking dozens of harmonies; the fourth side is a looser band session, recorded live in the studio.","Rundgren, from Philadelphia, had led the Nazz and worked as a staff producer and engineer for the manager Albert Grossman's Bearsville label. He set up in a Los Angeles studio and built most of the album himself, overdubbing part by part. He had grown up on Philadelphia soul and on the songwriting of Laura Nyro, and both can be heard in his chord changes and his falsetto.","On one track Rundgren speaks to the listener, leading a game in which he points out the faults of a recording, hiss, clicks, distortion, and invites us to listen for them: a producer's in-joke about studio craft. 'Hello It's Me' is a re-recording of a song he had written for the Nazz, slowed down and transformed. Listen for how he plays the drums like a pianist, simply and to serve the song.","'I Saw the Light' and 'Hello It's Me' were hits and the album made Rundgren's name. Critics admired his pop craft while some found the double length indulgent. It became a model for the one-man studio album, and later bedroom pop musicians and power-pop bands have cited it constantly. Rundgren soon turned away from this style, to fans' frustration.","Start with 'I Saw the Light' and 'Hello It's Me' to hear the songwriter. Then pick a rocker like 'Couldn't I Just Tell You'. Listen to the spoken introduction for Rundgren's game. Afterwards hear Laura Nyro's Eli and the Thirteenth Confession and Carole King's Tapestry for the songwriting world it belongs to."],"todd-rundgren-a-wizard-a-true-star":["A dizzying, stream-of-consciousness record in which nineteen songs and fragments rush past, many under two minutes. Side one jumps from 'International Feel' through electronic noise, music-hall pastiche and psychedelic rock without pause; side two includes a medley of soul classics sung with deep affection, and closes with 'Just One Victory', a rousing anthem of hope. Synthesizers, sped-up voices and sudden edits make the record feel like flicking through radio stations of the mind.","Having succeeded with polished pop, Rundgren deliberately refused to repeat himself. Working partly in his own new studio in New York, Secret Sound, he was experimenting with synthesizers, tape effects and abrupt editing. He crammed close to an hour of music onto one LP, pushing the limits of vinyl, in a deliberate break with commercial expectations.","The soul medley on side two links songs associated with the Delfonics, Smokey Robinson and the Miracles, the Impressions and the Capitols, the Philadelphia and Detroit records of Rundgren's youth, and he sings them straight, without irony. Listen to how songs on side one are spliced together with no gaps: the album was designed as a continuous experience, more like a radio collage than a set of tracks.","It sold poorly compared with Something/Anything? and puzzled many listeners and reviewers. Its reputation has grown greatly, and many musicians, from electronic producers to indie songwriters, cite it as a favourite. Critics now often call it his most original album, a precursor of the restless, genre-hopping records that sampling culture made common.","Play side one straight through without skipping and let the changes wash over you. Then the soul medley, and then 'Just One Victory' to finish. Compare with Frank Zappa's We're Only in It for the Money and with Brian Eno's Here Come the Warm Jets for other ways of making a studio collage."],"electric-light-orchestra-roll-over-beethoven":["A Chuck Berry rock and roll song, about a teenager telling classical music to make way for rhythm and blues, rebuilt as a clash between orchestra and rock band. It opens with the strings sawing the famous four-note opening of Beethoven's Fifth Symphony, then the guitar riff breaks in and the band charges into Berry's song. Through the album version, cellos and violin keep quoting Beethoven between verses, and the two worlds argue until the end.","Electric Light Orchestra grew out of the Move in Birmingham, founded by Roy Wood and Jeff Lynne with the stated aim of picking up where the Beatles' 'I Am the Walrus' had left off, with cellos and violins as rock instruments. Wood left after the first album, and Lynne led the band into ELO 2. This track, a single edit of which became a hit, made their joke explicit.","Berry wrote 'Roll Over Beethoven' in 1956 as a cheeky claim that rock and roll would replace the classics; ELO answer by actually playing the classics and letting them fight back. Listen for how the strings quote not only the opening of the Fifth but phrases from later in the first movement. The amplified cellos, played aggressively, are as loud as the guitars, which is the whole point.","The single was a hit in Britain and helped establish the band. Critics then and since have treated it as a novelty, but a revealing one: it is the clearest statement of ELO's idea that the orchestra could be a rock band's instrument. Classical listeners tend to enjoy it as a joke; rock historians cite it alongside Deep Purple's orchestral experiments.","Hear the full eight-minute album version. First, listen to the opening of Beethoven's Fifth itself, then Chuck Berry's original. Then play ELO's version and notice when Beethoven returns between verses. Compare Deep Purple's Concerto for Group and Orchestra for a more earnest attempt at the same meeting."],"electric-light-orchestra-out-of-the-blue":["A lavish double album of seventeen songs, combining Beatles-style melodies with an orchestra, a choir and synthesizers. 'Turn to Stone' gallops; 'Sweet Talkin' Woman' bounces on falsetto harmonies; 'Telephone Line' is a slow ballad of a man waiting for a call. Side three, 'Concerto for a Rainy Day', is a four-part suite that begins with thunder and ends in 'Mr. Blue Sky', a jubilant celebration of sunshine after rain, with a robotic vocoder voice closing it.","By 1977 ELO were one of the world's biggest-selling bands. Jeff Lynne wrote the songs in a rented chalet in Switzerland and recorded at Musicland Studios in Munich, the base of Giorgio Moroder's disco empire. The orchestral arrangements were done with Louis Clark, and the band toured it with an enormous spaceship stage set. Lynne's role as writer, producer and arranger was now total.","'Mr. Blue Sky' is built like a miniature classical finale, adding layer after layer, choir, strings, piano, synthesised voices, until it is almost overwhelming, then dissolving into a coda of orchestra and vocoder. Listen for Lynne's affectionate borrowings from the Beatles throughout: chord changes, piano rhythms and production tricks that echo the Abbey Road era. The opening of 'Telephone Line' imitates an American telephone ringing tone.","It sold in vast quantities, though critics of the punk era mocked ELO as bloated. Lynne's reputation as a producer rose later, when he worked with George Harrison, Tom Petty and Roy Orbison, and the album was reappraised as pop craft of a high order. 'Mr. Blue Sky' has become a fixture of films and adverts, beloved by audiences who never knew the album.","Play side three, 'Concerto for a Rainy Day', from beginning to end, and notice how the weather sounds frame the suite. Then 'Telephone Line' for the ballad writing. Compare with the Beatles' Abbey Road medley and Queen's A Night at the Opera, its closest relatives in orchestral ambition."],"little-feat-sailin-shoes":["Eleven songs mixing blues, country, rock and odd humour. 'Easy to Slip' is a bright, sad pop song; 'A Apolitical Blues' is a slow, sly blues; 'Willin'' is a lonely long-haul trucker's lament sung tenderly over piano; 'Trouble' is soft and wistful; 'Teenage Nervous Breakdown' and 'Tripe Face Boogie' rattle along. Lowell George's slide guitar is liquid and singing, Bill Payne's piano rolls with New Orleans and gospel figures, and Richie Hayward's drums are loose and agile.","Lowell George had played with Frank Zappa's Mothers of Invention, and formed Little Feat in Los Angeles with Bill Payne and fellow Zappa alumnus Roy Estrada. Their debut sold poorly; for this second album, produced by Ted Templeman, they tightened their songs. Warner Bros. supported them as critics' favourites, though sales stayed modest. George's songwriting was admired by many fellow musicians.","Lowell George often played slide not with the usual glass or metal tube but with a spark-plug socket wrench, which gave his tone its thick, sustained edge. 'Willin'' had already appeared on the debut, but this slower, more intimate version became the definitive one, and Linda Ronstadt later covered it. The cover painting by Neon Park parodies Fragonard's rococo painting The Swing, with a cake on the swing.","Critics adored it and sales disappointed, a pattern that dogged the band. Musicians from the Rolling Stones to country singers admired George's writing and slide playing. It is now widely regarded as the band's best studio album and a central document of the Los Angeles roots rock that bridged the Band, Gram Parsons and New Orleans R&B.","Play 'Willin'' first and listen to how simply it is sung. Then 'Easy to Slip' for the pop side and 'A Apolitical Blues' for the slide. Afterwards hear Linda Ronstadt's Heart Like a Wheel, which includes her version of 'Willin'', and the Band's second album for kindred spirits."],"little-feat-dixie-chicken":["Ten songs with a loose, rolling New Orleans groove at their heart. The title track struts on a swaying rhythm and a sing-along chorus about a romance gone wrong in Memphis; 'Two Trains' chugs with interlocking percussion; 'Fat Man in the Bathtub' rides a syncopated, stuttering beat; 'On Your Way Down', by Allen Toussaint, is slow and smoky. Congas and two guitars weave around the drums, giving the band a supple, funky lilt that rock rarely had.","After Sailin' Shoes, bassist Roy Estrada left, and Little Feat expanded to six with the guitarist Paul Barrere, the bassist Kenny Gradney and the percussionist Sam Clayton. Lowell George had fallen in love with New Orleans music, the Meters, Dr. John and Allen Toussaint, and steered the band towards it. They recorded in Los Angeles, but the sound points firmly to New Orleans.","Listen to the drums and congas in 'Fat Man in the Bathtub': Richie Hayward plays around the beat in a way borrowed from New Orleans second-line drumming, while Clayton fills the gaps. The choice of Toussaint's song is a direct tribute to the city's great producer. The title track's horn-like piano lines from Bill Payne echo the barrelhouse tradition.","Sales were again modest, but the album's reputation grew steadily, and the title song became a bar-band standard. Critics now often pair it with Sailin' Shoes as the band's peak. Its fusion of rock with New Orleans funk influenced jam bands and Americana for decades, and musicians frequently cite it as a lesson in groove.","Start with the title track and follow the piano. Then 'Fat Man in the Bathtub' and listen only to the drums and congas. Afterwards hear the Meters' 'Cissy Strut' and Rejuvenation to understand the groove's source, and compare with the Band for a different American roots synthesis."],"supertramp-crime-of-the-century":["Eight songs forming a loose story of alienation, from school through breakdown to isolation. 'School' opens with a lonely harmonica and children's playground voices before a funky piano groove; 'Bloody Well Right' is sardonic; 'Dreamer' bounces on a hammering Wurlitzer electric piano and Roger Hodgson's high voice; 'Rudy' tells of a lonely young man; and the title track ends in a vast, slow piano and orchestral finale. Saxophone and clarinet add warmth and colour throughout.","Supertramp had been formed in London around Rick Davies with funding from a wealthy Dutch patron, but their first two albums failed. With a new lineup including the saxophonist John Helliwell, the bassist Dougie Thomson and the drummer Bob Siebenberg, and the producer Ken Scott, who had worked with the Beatles and Bowie, they made this album their make-or-break. It was the breakthrough.","Listen for the two songwriters' contrasting voices: Hodgson's high, boyish tenor on 'Dreamer' and 'School', Davies's gruffer, bluesier baritone on 'Bloody Well Right' and 'Rudy'. The album's sound effects, children's voices, train noises, crowd sounds, were carefully placed by Scott to link songs into a continuous narrative. The cover image, hands gripping bars floating in space, sums up its theme.","It was a major success in Britain and Europe and established the band. Critics placed it on the more accessible edge of progressive rock, admired for its production clarity. Its themes of school as a cage foreshadow Pink Floyd's The Wall, and the album remains a favourite of audiophiles for its sound.","Start with 'School' and listen to the playground sounds and the piano groove. Then 'Dreamer' for the Wurlitzer, and end with the title track's finale. Compare with Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon for the same producerly care and with The Wall for a later treatment of school."],"supertramp-breakfast-in-america":["Ten tight, bright pop songs. 'The Logical Song' bounces on a Wurlitzer and handclaps as Hodgson laments how education stripped away a child's wonder; 'Goodbye Stranger' is a breezy Davies song with falsetto backing voices; 'Take the Long Way Home' opens with harmonica and swells with clarinet; the title track is a cheery, oom-pah sketch of a young Englishman's fantasy of America. The sound is crisp and clean, with saxophone solos and electric piano at the centre.","Supertramp had moved to Los Angeles in the late seventies and recorded the album there, co-producing with Peter Henderson. It was their deliberate turn toward concise pop after the long concept albums of the earlier seventies. Arriving just before the new wave reshaped rock, it became one of the best-selling albums of 1979 around the world, a high point for the soft, crafted rock sound of the decade.","The title track was one of Hodgson's older songs, written years before the band revived it for the album. The cover, a waitress posed like the Statue of Liberty against a New York skyline made of breakfast items, won a Grammy for packaging. Listen for how the saxophone and Wurlitzer play off each other on 'The Logical Song'.","It topped charts in several countries and sold in vast quantities. Critics were divided, some seeing well-made but bland pop, others admiring its songwriting craft. Its reputation has since stabilised, with 'The Logical Song' and 'Take the Long Way Home' seen as among the era's best-crafted pop songs. Hodgson left the band a few years later.","Play 'The Logical Song' and notice how the lyric's theme matches the chirpy music only on the surface. Then 'Take the Long Way Home' for the harmonica and clarinet. Compare with Crime of the Century to hear how the band traded long forms for compact songs, and with Steely Dan's Aja for another view of polished late-seventies craft."],"bob-seger-night-moves":["Nine songs of Midwestern rock and roll and reflection. 'Rock and Roll Never Forgets' is a rousing statement of faith; the title track is a slow, acoustic-led song looking back on adolescence in the Michigan countryside, which stops almost to nothing before building again; 'Mainstreet' is a moody, lilting memory of small-town nights; 'The Fire Down Below' is a gritty rocker. Seger's husky, soulful voice carries everything, backed by piano, organ and saxophone.","Seger had been a Detroit legend for a decade without national success. His live double album Live Bullet, released earlier in 1976 with his Silver Bullet Band, finally broke him, and Night Moves followed months later on Capitol. The record was split between the Silver Bullet Band and the Muscle Shoals Rhythm Section, the Alabama session players famed for their soul recordings, and the mix of the two gives it its warmth.","Listen to the title track's middle, where the band drops away almost to nothing and Seger sings nearly alone before the drums and voices return; it is the moment the song turns from nostalgia to adult reflection. Then compare the feel across the record: the Muscle Shoals players sit further back on the beat than the Detroit band, a looser, soul-trained swing.","It made Seger a national star and became one of the defining heartland rock records. Critics compared him to Springsteen, sometimes unfairly, and praised his honesty and voice. The title song in particular is now regarded as one of the great American rock songs about memory. Seger's work was absent from streaming services for many years, which has slightly lowered his profile among younger listeners.","Play the title track and listen for the moment the music almost stops. Then 'Mainstreet' for its melancholy guitar. Compare with Springsteen's Born to Run from the year before, a more operatic take on similar material, and with Tom Petty's debut album for a contemporary voice from the South."],"tom-petty-tom-petty-and-the-heartbreakers":["Ten short, lean songs in barely half an hour. 'Rockin' Around (with You)' and 'Anything That's Rock 'n' Roll' are tight, punchy rockers; 'Breakdown' is a slow, smouldering groove with a menacing guitar riff and Tom Petty's sneering, intimate vocal; 'Luna' is a dreamy organ-led ballad; and 'American Girl' closes on a burst of ringing guitar. Everything is played with a garage band's energy and a craftsman's economy, with no song outstaying its welcome.","Petty and the guitarist Mike Campbell and keyboardist Benmont Tench had played in Mudcrutch in Gainesville, Florida, before moving to Los Angeles, where Denny Cordell signed them to his Shelter label. When Mudcrutch fell apart, the Heartbreakers were formed with Ron Blair on bass and Stan Lynch on drums. Cordell produced this debut, which emerged in 1976 just as punk and new wave were erupting.","The album initially sold poorly in America, and it was British audiences, who embraced the band on tour as part of the new wave, that gave them their first momentum. Listen to 'Breakdown': the guitar riff that enters near the end, and the long, simmering fadeout around it, show the band at its tightest and most restrained. Tench's organ on 'Luna' shows his gospel and soul roots.","'Breakdown' became an American hit only on re-release, more than a year after the album appeared, and the record then found its audience. Critics placed Petty somewhere between the Byrds and new wave, and he resisted both labels. Today the debut is seen as the template for his career: short, hooky, rooted in sixties rock and devoid of excess.","Play the album straight through: it takes only half an hour. Listen to 'Breakdown' closely for the restraint of the playing, then to 'Luna' for Tench's organ. Compare with Damn the Torpedoes to hear how the band's sound grew bigger and more polished in three years."],"tom-petty-american-girl":["A three-and-a-half-minute song about a young woman restless for a bigger life than her small town offers. It opens with a driving, chugging beat, a near cousin of the Bo Diddley rhythm, and a jangling electric twelve-string guitar; Petty's voice is urgent and slightly nasal. The chorus lifts on chiming guitars, and the song ends in a burst of ringing harmonised guitar that sounds like the Byrds rewired for the punk era.","The song closed the Heartbreakers' debut album and was released as a single. Petty, who grew up in Gainesville, wrote it in Los Angeles in his early years there, with the Byrds and the British Invasion bands of his youth as his models. Its release coincided with the American Bicentennial year, giving its title an extra resonance it did not intend.","Roger McGuinn of the Byrds, whose twelve-string jangle the song echoes, recorded his own version soon after, reportedly remarking that it sounded like something he might have written. Listen to how Mike Campbell and Petty split the guitar parts: one keeps the chugging rhythm while the other rings out the melody. The spare drum pattern drives the song without ever filling it in.","It was not a big hit on release, but it has become one of Petty's most loved songs and a staple of American rock radio and films. Critics often cite it as the purest distillation of his style. Its blend of sixties jangle and seventies urgency influenced many later bands, from power pop to the American alternative rock of the eighties.","Listen first to the rhythm, then to the twelve-string guitar jangling above it. Then play the Byrds' 'Mr. Tambourine Man' and Bo Diddley's 'Bo Diddley' to hear the two ingredients. For what followed, play R.E.M.'s Murmur and Big Star's 'September Gurls'."],"soundgarden-badmotorfinger":["Twelve tracks of heavy, knotted rock built on detuned guitars and odd meters. Rusty Cage opens with a jittery, feedback-flecked riff before collapsing into a lurching coda in an uneven time signature; Outshined grinds in a slow, crooked groove; Jesus Christ Pose is a wall of drum tom thunder and shrieking guitar. Over all of it sits Chris Cornell's voice, which can swing from a low croon to a sustained high wail inside a single phrase.","Soundgarden had been the first Seattle band of their generation to sign to a major label, and Badmotorfinger, produced by Terry Date and released in October 1991, was their second album for A&M and the first with Ben Shepherd on bass. It arrived within weeks of Nirvana's Nevermind and Pearl Jam's Ten, and for a while it was overshadowed by them, kept afloat by touring with Guns N' Roses and heavy rotation of its videos on MTV's metal shows.","Kim Thayil and Cornell used unusual guitar tunings, some with several strings tuned to the same note, which is why chords ring with a droning, sitar-like buzz rather than the bright shape of standard rock voicings. Listen to how often a riff seems to drop or add a beat; the band counted songs in sevens and fives, yet the swing never sounds academic.","Critics at the time heard it as the moment Soundgarden's Black Sabbath debt turned into something of their own, and it earned a Grammy nomination in a metal category. Johnny Cash's later acoustic cover of Rusty Cage on his Unchained album showed how sturdy the songwriting was underneath the noise. Many fans still rank it above the more successful Superunknown for its rawness.","Start with Rusty Cage and stay for the final minute, when the tempo slows and the meter turns lopsided; that switch is the whole band in miniature. Then play Outshined loud, listening to Matt Cameron's drums rather than the guitars. Compare it with Black Sabbath's Master of Reality to hear exactly what Seattle inherited and what it bent."],"soundgarden-superunknown":["Fifteen songs and seventy minutes, far wider in palette than anything Soundgarden had made. Let Me Drown and the title track keep the old weight, but Fell on Black Days moves in a weary waltz-like six, Half drifts into Indian-tinged drones, Spoonman features a street performer playing spoons, and Black Hole Sun swims in guitar run through a rotating Leslie speaker. The closing 4th of July ends the record with a slow, heavy, almost doom-laden descent.","Made in 1993 with producer Michael Beinhorn after the band had toured behind Badmotorfinger, it was released in March 1994 and debuted at number one on the Billboard 200, a sign of how far the Seattle sound had travelled. Its success came just weeks before Kurt Cobain's death, and for many listeners the album's preoccupation with depression and isolation became part of how that spring was remembered.","Several songs sit in odd meters disguised by groove: The Day I Tried to Live alternates between bars of different lengths, and Limo Wreck's riff never quite lands where the ear expects. Matt Cameron's drumming carries much of this; listen for how he keeps the backbeat steady while the guitars lean against it, so the strangeness feels like swagger rather than arithmetic.","It won two Grammys in 1995, for Black Hole Sun and Spoonman, and has been treated ever since as Soundgarden's central statement. Some early fans felt the polish and length softened the band's edge, while later critics argued that its range, from metal to psychedelia, is exactly what makes it outlast more single-minded grunge records. A 2014 reissue added demos and alternate mixes.","Do not start with the hit. Play Fell on Black Days first, for Cornell's restrained singing over that sad lilting pulse, then 4th of July for the heaviest ending in the band's catalogue. Hear it as a long album with sequencing in mind, and compare it with Alice in Chains' Dirt, made in the same city and the same mood."],"soundgarden-black-hole-sun":["A slow, swaying song of surreal, apocalyptic images, it opens with a guitar arpeggio that seems to wobble and shimmer, because it was played through a Leslie speaker, the rotating cabinet usually used for organs. The verses float on unusual, sliding chords; the chorus lifts into a hooky, almost Beatles-like melody before the band drop into heavy riffing. A long, wailing guitar solo over a crushing descending pattern closes the song.","Chris Cornell wrote it alone, and later said it began with a phrase he misheard on television and built the song around the picture it gave him. Released as a single from Superunknown in 1994, it was carried around the world by a lurid music video directed by Howard Greenhalgh, in which a smiling suburban neighbourhood melts into a vortex while the band play on.","Cornell built the chord sequence with a songwriter's ear trained on 1960s pop rather than metal; the harmonies keep slipping chromatically sideways, which is why the verse feels queasy even though the tempo is gentle. Underneath, the bass part moves against the guitar rather than doubling it. That tension between sweet tune and sickly harmony is the song's real subject.","It won the Grammy for Best Hard Rock Performance in 1995 and became Soundgarden's most played song. The band themselves were ambivalent about how far it overshadowed the rest of their catalogue. After Cornell's death in 2017, tributes and covers multiplied, and the song has been reinterpreted in very different styles, a sign that its melody holds up without the guitars.","Listen first to the opening thirty seconds on headphones to hear the Leslie's circling motion in the guitar. Then follow the bass line through a verse. When the chorus arrives, notice how Cornell holds back power and lets the melody do the work, saving his full voice for the end. Compare it with the Beatles' Tomorrow Never Knows, the root of this psychedelic sound."],"alice-in-chains-dirt":["A dark, heavy album whose songs deal with addiction, depression and war, Dirt is held together by its sound: Jerry Cantrell's thick, sludgy guitar riffs and, above all, the eerie harmonies he sang with Layne Staley, two voices locked a close interval apart so that they seem to wail as one. Them Bones jolts in a lurching rhythm; Rooster builds slowly from a quiet verse to a massive chorus; Would? closes the album on a prowling bass line.","Produced by Dave Jerden and released by Columbia in September 1992, Dirt came out at the height of the Seattle boom, a year after Nevermind. Alice in Chains had begun closer to metal than to punk, and their audience straddled both camps. Rooster was written by Cantrell about his father's service in the Vietnam War, and Would? had already appeared on the soundtrack of the film Singles.","The harmonies are the secret: rather than the usual third above, Cantrell often sings a fourth or a fifth away from Staley, an open, medieval-sounding interval that gives the band its haunted quality. Listen also for the short interludes between songs, and for Slayer's Tom Araya, who contributes a brief, distant scream on the short track Iron Gland.","Reviewers were divided at first, some admiring its power and others troubled by its bleakness, but it sold steadily for years and is now usually named the band's finest album and one of the essential records of its moment. Discussion of it has inevitably been shadowed by Staley's death in 2002, and critics now debate how to listen to it without turning it into a cautionary tale.","Start with Rooster to hear the band's quiet-to-loud architecture, then Down in a Hole for the two voices at their most mournful. Listen past the gloom to the craftsmanship of the riffs and the drumming of Sean Kinney. For the other side of the band, follow it with the acoustic EP Jar of Flies."],"alice-in-chains-jar-of-flies":["Seven songs, about half an hour, and almost entirely gentle: acoustic guitars, brushed and restrained drums, string arrangements on I Stay Away, and the same two-voice harmonies that made Dirt so chilling. Rotten Apple opens with a slow bass figure and a talk-box guitar; Nutshell is a sparse, fragile meditation on loneliness; Whale and Wasp is a slow, wordless instrumental for guitars; Don't Follow lets Cantrell sing over harmonica.","Written and recorded in about a week at London Bridge Studio in Seattle in 1993, with Mike Inez now on bass, it was released as an EP in January 1994 and became the first EP to debut at number one on the Billboard album chart. Alice in Chains had already shown an acoustic side on the earlier EP Sap, and this was its fullest flowering.","Even without distortion, the songs keep the band's strange harmonic voice: Cantrell's chords often move by small, unexpected steps, and the vocal harmonies still sit at open intervals. On I Stay Away, the strings are arranged to swell against the beat rather than with it. Listen to how rarely Staley raises his voice; restraint makes the despair more audible.","It surprised critics who had pigeonholed the band as metal, and its commercial success showed how hungry audiences were for quieter grunge, a year before Nirvana's Unplugged. Nutshell in particular has become a memorial song for Staley. Some listeners consider it the band's most perfect release precisely because it is so short and unadorned.","Play it straight through in one sitting; at thirty minutes it is meant to be heard whole. Begin by listening to Nutshell in a quiet room, focusing on the space around the guitar. Then hear Whale and Wasp as chamber music. Pair it with Nirvana's MTV Unplugged in New York to hear grunge turned inward."],"smashing-pumpkins-siamese-dream":["Thirteen songs in which guitar is layered on guitar until the sound becomes a thick, glowing wall, part heavy rock, part shoegaze haze. Cherub Rock opens with a lone guitar and a drum roll before the full roar arrives; Today sets a bright, chiming hook against a sad subject; Disarm is an orchestral ballad with strings and tubular bells; Soma and Mayonaise build slowly from quiet to overwhelming. Jimmy Chamberlin's jazz-trained drumming drives it all.","Produced by Butch Vig and Billy Corgan and released in July 1993, the album was made in Marietta, Georgia, partly to keep the Chicago band away from distractions. The sessions were notoriously hard, with personal strife inside the group and Corgan, by many accounts, playing many of the guitar and bass parts himself. Released in the post-Nevermind boom, it became their breakthrough.","The guitar sound is built from dozens of overdubs, some tracks carrying many layers of rhythm guitar, which is why chords seem to shimmer rather than simply strike. Listen for the tiny details buried in the wash: a reversed sound here, a high harmonic there. Disarm's strings and bells nod to Corgan's love of 1970s orchestral pop rather than to punk.","It was widely praised on release and sold in the millions, though some in the indie world saw the band as careerist outsiders to the alternative scene. Over time, its reputation has only grown, and it is now frequently named among the best rock albums of the decade. Debate continues about how much of it was a band record versus Corgan's solo vision.","Start with Cherub Rock and listen to how the single guitar becomes an army. Then hear Mayonaise, which balances fragility and noise better than any other track. Notice Chamberlin's fills, which constantly push the rhythm forward. Follow it with My Bloody Valentine's Loveless to hear the layered-guitar dream the band were chasing."],"smashing-pumpkins-mellon-collie-and-the-infinite-sadness":["A double album of twenty-eight songs and two hours, split into two halves named Dawn to Dusk and Twilight to Starlight. It begins with a quiet piano instrumental and moves through the orchestral swell of Tonight, Tonight, the snarling Bullet with Butterfly Wings, the metallic Zero, the drum-machine glide of 1979 and sprawling epics such as Porcelina of the Vast Oceans, ending with a lullaby. Few rock records of the decade cover so much ground.","Produced by Billy Corgan with Flood and Alan Moulder and released by Virgin in October 1995, it followed the success of Siamese Dream and reflected Corgan's ambition to make a definitive, era-spanning statement. Its concept of a single day and night, and a youthful arc from hope to despair, recalls the double albums of the 1970s, from the Beatles' White Album to Pink Floyd's The Wall.","Flood and Moulder pushed the band to record in a rehearsal space with live energy, then layered in textures: the strings on Tonight, Tonight were recorded with an orchestra, and 1979 is built around a looped drum pattern and a sampled vocal fragment. Listen for how the quiet piano pieces that open and close the set act as a frame around the noise.","It became one of the best-selling double albums ever and earned several Grammy nominations. Critics praised its ambition but some found it bloated, a complaint the band partly embraced. Today it is often viewed as the last great overreaching rock double album of the century, and listeners still argue about which twelve songs would make the perfect single LP.","Begin with 1979 for its wistful calm, then Tonight, Tonight for orchestral grandeur, and Zero for brute force. Treat the double album as two separate listening sessions. Compare it with the Beatles' Sgt. Pepper or the Who's Tommy to hear the tradition of sprawling rock statements Corgan wanted to join."],"hole-live-through-this":["Twelve songs about womanhood, motherhood, fame and the female body as public property, delivered with a pop songwriter's hooks and a punk singer's howl. Violet starts quiet and erupts; Miss World alternates sweet melody with snarl; Doll Parts begins as a strummed lament and builds into a repeated, desperate refrain; Credit in the Straight World is a cover of the Welsh band Young Marble Giants, turned into a roar. Courtney Love's voice cracks, croons and screams, often within a single line.","Produced by Sean Slade and Paul Q. Kolderie and released by DGC in April 1994, it came out days after the death of Love's husband, Kurt Cobain, and was received in the shadow of that event. Two months later the band's bassist Kristen Pfaff also died. The album came from a band that grew partly out of the riot grrrl and Los Angeles punk scenes, and Love sought to bring that politics to a mass audience.","Love and guitarist Eric Erlandson structured many songs on simple, strong melodic hooks borrowed from classic pop, under the noise. Listen to Patty Schemel's drumming and Pfaff's busy, melodic bass lines, which give the songs their momentum. The album's title and imagery play on the idea of surviving public scrutiny, an idea that took on a darker resonance after its release.","It topped many critics' end-of-year lists, including the Village Voice's Pazz and Jop poll, and is regularly named among the great albums of the decade. Rumours that Cobain wrote it have been repeatedly refuted by band members and are now regarded as part of the sexism the record itself diagnosed. Its standing has grown as later generations reclaim Love as a songwriter.","Start with Violet, played loud, then Doll Parts, which shows how much tenderness sits under the rage. Listen for the dynamics: the band are constantly switching between whisper and explosion. Pair it with PJ Harvey's Rid of Me and Sleater-Kinney's Dig Me Out to hear three very different women reshaping nineties rock."],"hole-celebrity-skin":["Twelve glossy, melodic songs, shining with layered guitars and California light, very different from the rawness of Live Through This. The title track bursts in with a bright riff and a sing-along chorus; Malibu rolls like a West Coast pop record from the 1970s; Awful is pure power pop; and softer songs like Northern Star lean on strings and acoustic guitar. The subject is fame, Hollywood and reinvention, sung by Courtney Love with more control than before.","Produced by Michael Beinhorn and released by DGC in September 1998, it was written in Los Angeles, partly with Billy Corgan of the Smashing Pumpkins, who co-wrote several songs. Love had become a film actor in the intervening years, and the band, now with Melissa Auf der Maur on bass, aimed squarely at the polished rock of Fleetwood Mac and the Beach Boys' California mythology.","Corgan's influence can be heard in the dense, shimmering guitar overdubs and the sophisticated chord movement, very close to the Pumpkins' own Mellon Collie. Listen also for how Love's lyrics invert the sunny surfaces: the music sounds like a celebration of California while the words read it as a beautiful trap.","It was widely praised and earned Grammy nominations, though some fans of the earlier punk-rooted Hole felt the gloss was a betrayal. Others heard it as an intelligent study of fame. Its reputation has risen in recent years, and it is now often read as a self-aware companion to Live Through This rather than a retreat.","Start with the title track and listen to how the guitars stack up into a wall of brightness. Then hear Malibu, the most Fleetwood Mac moment, side by side with Rumours. Notice Love's phrasing, which has become sweeter but still bites. Compare with Siamese Dream to hear Corgan's fingerprints."],"janes-addiction-nothings-shocking":["An album that welds hard rock riffs, funk bass, punk energy and art-rock atmosphere into one swirling sound. Up the Beach opens with a slow instrumental swell; Ocean Size crashes in with Dave Navarro's huge, Zeppelin-scale guitar; Mountain Song rides a thick, swaggering groove; and Jane Says, a sad portrait of a struggling woman, is played on acoustic guitar with steel drums. Perry Farrell's high, reverb-drenched voice sounds like no other singer in rock at the time.","Jane's Addiction emerged from the Los Angeles underground in the mid-1980s, when the city was dominated by glossy hair metal. Produced by Dave Jerden and Farrell and released by Warner Bros. in 1988, Nothing's Shocking was their major-label debut. Its cover, a sculpture by Farrell, was refused by some retailers, the first of many confrontations between the band and the mainstream.","Eric Avery's bass is the band's secret engine: he plays long, melodic, repeating lines that function almost like riffs of their own, so that Navarro's guitar can float above. Listen to how often songs are built on two-chord vamps that open out into trance-like grooves, a sign of Farrell's interest in psychedelia and world music rather than standard rock song form.","Critics greeted it as a breath of fresh air in late-eighties Los Angeles rock, though sales were modest at first. It has come to be seen as one of the records that bridged hard rock and the alternative explosion that followed. The band's influence on later groups, from Rage Against the Machine to the Smashing Pumpkins, is often noted.","Start with Ocean Size for the full power of Navarro's guitar, then Jane Says for the band's gentler, almost Caribbean side. Listen to the bass first in Mountain Song. Pair it with Led Zeppelin's Physical Graffiti to hear where the swagger came from, and with the Velvet Underground to hear where the darkness came from."],"janes-addiction-ritual-de-lo-habitual":["An album in two halves. The first is a run of tight, explosive rock songs: Stop! with its manic tempo, the funky Been Caught Stealing with its barking dogs, the dense No One's Leaving. The second side is a long, connected suite of slower pieces, including the ten-minute Three Days, which builds from a slow pulse into a guitar climax, and the string-laden Then She Did. The whole record moves from party to elegy.","Produced again by Dave Jerden and Perry Farrell and released by Warner Bros. in August 1990, it was the band's last studio album before their first break-up. The second side is dedicated to a close friend of Farrell's, Xiola Blue, whose death haunts the suite. In 1991 Farrell launched the touring festival Lollapalooza partly as the band's farewell, a key moment in the rise of alternative culture.","Three Days is built like a classical crescendo: a quiet bass and drum pattern, then layer upon layer of guitar and voice, before Navarro's solo breaks the tension. Then She Did uses a string arrangement to blur the line between rock and chamber music. The record's sequencing, with the party songs first and the mourning later, is deliberate.","It became the band's biggest success, and Been Caught Stealing was a major hit on alternative radio. Its cover art, another Farrell sculpture, again caused a retail controversy and was released in an alternative version for some stores. Critics now regard the second side as the band's most ambitious music.","Hear the two sides separately. Start with Been Caught Stealing for the band's funk swing, then put on Three Days and give it your full attention for ten minutes. Notice how Stephen Perkins's drumming changes from tight to tribal. Compare with Pink Floyd or the Doors to hear the psychedelic heritage the band claimed."],"rage-against-the-machine-rage-against-the-machine":["Ten songs that fuse heavy metal riffing, funk rhythm and hip-hop delivery into a single political weapon. Zack de la Rocha raps and screams his lyrics about power, racism and resistance over Tom Morello's guitar, which can sound like a turntable, an alarm or a synth. Tim Commerford's bass and Brad Wilk's drums lock into a funk-tight groove. Bombtrack, Killing in the Name, Bullet in the Head and Wake Up all build from tense, quiet verses into crushing riffs.","Formed in Los Angeles in 1991, the band drew on punk, the political hip-hop of Public Enemy and the heavy rock of Led Zeppelin and Black Sabbath. Produced by Garth Richardson and released by Epic in November 1992, the album carried a liner note declaring that no samples, keyboards or synthesizers were used. Its cover reproduced Malcolm Browne's 1963 photograph of a Buddhist monk's protest against the South Vietnamese government.","Every strange sound is a guitar: Morello used a toggle switch to cut the signal on and off for stuttering effects, a pitch-shifting pedal for squeals, and scraped strings to mimic a DJ scratching records. Listen to Know Your Enemy, which features Maynard James Keenan of Tool on guest vocals, and to Freedom's long, slow build into the final assault.","It sold steadily on word of mouth and touring rather than radio play, and was soon recognised as the defining rap-rock album, one whose many imitators rarely matched its politics or precision. Some critics questioned the paradox of a radical band signed to a major label; the band answered that they used the platform deliberately. It is now widely ranked among the most influential rock albums of the decade.","Start with Bombtrack and listen to how the bass and drums create a funk pocket before the riff arrives. Then follow Morello's solos across the record, treating each as a puzzle of how he made that noise. Pair it with Public Enemy's It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back to hear its political roots."],"rage-against-the-machine-killing-in-the-name":["A protest song against racism in policing and institutional power, built on one of the most famous riffs of the decade: a heavy, drop-tuned figure that lands with the force of a hammer. The song alternates between near-silent tension and full-band explosion. Tom Morello's solo, played through a pitch-shifting pedal, shrieks an octave up like a siren. The ending is a slow, relentless build in which a single defiant phrase is repeated louder and louder until the band cut out.","Released as the band's first single in 1992, it came out months after the riots that followed the acquittal of the Los Angeles police officers who beat Rodney King, and its anger was heard as part of that moment. In December 2009, a Facebook campaign organised against the dominance of the television talent show The X Factor made it the UK Christmas number one.","The riff grew out of a guitar-lesson moment: Morello stumbled on it while teaching a student drop-D tuning. Listen to how the band play the riff with perfect unison, then break it into syncopated fragments. Brad Wilk's drumming switches between half-time and full-tempo feel to create the song's push and pull.","Its explicit language kept it from most radio play at first, but it became an anthem at rock clubs and festivals. The 2009 Christmas campaign revived debate about whether a protest song could be consumed as a joke; the band donated proceeds to charity and played a free concert in London in thanks. It remains one of the most-played rock songs of the decade.","Listen first to the opening riff and count how often the band stop and start. Then follow Morello's solo, imagining how he made those sounds with a guitar. Hear the song alongside Public Enemy's Fight the Power, its direct ancestor in political anger, and then Woody Guthrie's This Land Is Your Land to hear a much older American protest tradition."],"beck-loser":["A slacker anthem of self-mockery and absurdist wordplay, Loser stitches a slide-guitar blues riff to a hip-hop drum loop. Beck half-raps the verses in a rush of nonsense images and sings the chorus, partly in Spanish, in a lazy drawl. The sound is cheap, lo-fi and immediately catchy: a sitar-like drone, a dusty beat and a hook that sticks after one listen.","Beck Hansen recorded it in 1992 at the home of producer Carl Stephenson in Los Angeles. Released first on the small label Bong Load Custom Records in 1993, it spread on college and alternative radio until a bidding war led to its re-release by DGC on the album Mellow Gold in 1994. It became one of the defining songs of the so-called slacker generation.","The drum loop is a sample taken from Johnny Jenkins's recording of I Walk on Guilded Splinters, a song by Dr. John, which gives the song its swampy New Orleans swing. Stephenson added the sitar-like texture and the layered production. Beck has said he intended the song as a joke on his own rapping, which makes its success all the more ironic.","Some critics initially dismissed it as a novelty, and Beck worried he would be a one-hit wonder. The later success of Odelay proved otherwise, and Loser is now heard as a manifesto for the genre-blending, sample-based songwriting that defined Beck and much of nineties alternative music.","Listen to the drum loop and the slide guitar separately; they come from two different musical worlds. Then notice how Beck's vocal phrasing borrows from both folk singers and rappers. Compare it with the Beastie Boys' Check Your Head, which mixed instruments and samples in a similar spirit."],"beck-odelay":["Thirteen songs that collage rock, hip-hop, country, funk, bossa nova and noise into a bright, playful whole. Devils Haircut opens with a riff and a sampled shriek; Where It's At is a lounge-funk party piece built on keyboards and turntables; The New Pollution sways on a sixties pop groove with a sax line; Jack-Ass is a slow, dreamy country lament; Hotwax stomps like a hip-hop record. Everything sounds cut and pasted, yet the songs hang together.","Produced by Beck with the Dust Brothers, John King and Mike Simpson, who had built the Beastie Boys' Paul's Boutique from hundreds of samples, Odelay was released by DGC in June 1996. Beck had followed his hit Loser with several small, lo-fi releases and wanted to prove he was more than a one-hit novelty. The album's cover shows a shaggy Komondor dog leaping over a hurdle.","Several songs are built from samples of older recordings: Devils Haircut and Jack-Ass both draw on recordings by the Northern Irish band Them, and the sample-based production uses fragments of funk, soul and rock records. Listen for the small voices and sound effects buried in the mix, which reward headphone listening.","It was hailed as one of the best albums of 1996, won a Grammy for Best Alternative Music Performance and was nominated for Album of the Year. Critics praised it as a landmark of postmodern pop, a record that treated the whole history of popular music as raw material. It remains Beck's most celebrated album.","Start with Where It's At for its laid-back swing, then Devils Haircut for its punch. Listen for the transitions within songs, where one style melts into another. Pair it with the Beastie Boys' Check Your Head and Daft Punk's Homework to hear how sample collage spread through the decade."],"liz-phair-exile-in-guyville":["Eighteen songs of frank, witty and often bruised writing about relationships, sex and gender in Chicago's male-dominated indie-rock scene. The sound is spare and dry: Liz Phair's deep, deadpan voice over clean electric guitar, simple bass and drums, sometimes just guitar and voice, sometimes piano. Songs like 6'1, Help Me Mary, Never Said, Divorce Song and Stratford-on-Guy move between deadpan humour and wounded honesty. Phair's guitar playing, with its odd open tunings, is an instrument of its own.","Phair had made a series of home-recorded tapes under the name Girly-Sound, which circulated in the underground. Signed to Matador, she recorded Exile in Guyville with producer Brad Wood in Chicago, and it was released in June 1993. She described the album as a song-by-song reply to the Rolling Stones' Exile on Main St., though how literally to take that claim is still debated.","The comparison with Exile on Main St. is worth testing: the albums have the same number of songs, and some of Phair's pieces mirror the mood or position of the Stones' tracks. Listen to how Brad Wood's production leaves room around her voice; the bareness makes the lyrics feel like overheard confessions rather than performances.","It topped the Village Voice Pazz and Jop critics' poll for 1993 and was hailed as a landmark of feminist rock, though some indie-scene critics resented its success. Phair's later move to glossy pop divided fans, but Exile in Guyville has remained a central text of nineties indie rock and was reissued in an expanded anniversary box set in 2018.","Start with Never Said and Divorce Song to hear her songwriting at its most direct. Listen to how her low, flat delivery makes the jokes and the hurt land at once. Then play the Rolling Stones' Exile on Main St. side by side, or compare with Pavement's Slanted and Enchanted to hear the male indie scene she was answering."],"built-to-spill-perfect-from-now-on":["Eight long songs, most between six and nine minutes, that unfold like guitar suites rather than verse-chorus rock. Randy Described Eternity opens with interlocking guitar lines and a long instrumental build; I Would Hurt a Fly moves between brooding and soaring; Velvet Waltz and Untrustable stretch out into slow, layered climaxes. Doug Martsch's high, plaintive voice sings of time, eternity and small moments, against a backdrop of shimmering, interwoven guitars and occasional cello.","Built to Spill came from Boise, Idaho, far from the industry centres. Martsch signed to Warner Bros. while keeping a strong indie identity, and Perfect from Now On, produced with Phil Ek, was released in January 1997. It was a deliberately uncommercial major-label debut, built on long songs and complex arrangements in an era when labels wanted short alternative-radio singles.","Martsch layers several guitars that each play a separate melodic line, like the voices of a polyphonic choir, rather than strumming chords. Listen for how the lines interlock and how a riff introduced early in a song comes back transformed at its end. The cello adds a low, warm counter-melody that is easy to miss beneath the guitars.","Critics praised it as one of the most ambitious indie-rock records of the decade, comparing Martsch's guitar playing with Neil Young and Dinosaur Jr's J Mascis. It did not sell in big numbers but became a cult touchstone, cited by later bands such as Modest Mouse and Death Cab for Cutie. Many fans consider it the band's best.","Give Randy Described Eternity your full attention and listen for the moment when all the guitar lines lock together. Then try following one guitar through a whole song. Compare with Neil Young's Everybody Knows This Is Nowhere for long, loose guitar epics, and with Dinosaur Jr's You're Living All Over Me for the indie side."],"built-to-spill-keep-it-like-a-secret":["Ten songs that pack Built to Spill's guitar polyphony into tighter, catchier forms. The Plan opens with a jagged riff and a sudden shift into melody; Carry the Zero has one of the band's most graceful hooks; Center of the Universe is bright and bouncing; Time Trap and Else are slower and more searching; Broken Chairs, the long closer, returns to the epic scale of Perfect from Now On. The guitars still interweave, but the songs are shorter and more direct.","Released by Warner Bros. in February 1999 and again produced with Phil Ek, the album came after the band had toured widely and Martsch had settled on a steadier line-up with bassist Brett Nelson and drummer Scott Plouf. In the last years of the decade, indie rock was shifting toward cleaner production and more song-based writing, and this album reflected that change.","Many songs hide unusual structures under their pop surfaces: sections change key or tempo without warning, and some riffs only appear once. Listen for You Were Right, whose lyrics are woven from fragments of famous classic-rock songs, a playful commentary on rock clichés. The guitar tone is also cleaner than before, making each line easier to follow.","Reviewers welcomed it as a more accessible album that kept the band's ambition, and it became one of their best-loved records. Some critics consider it the perfect balance between the long-form experiments of Perfect from Now On and pop songwriting. It is often cited as a key influence on the indie rock of the following decade.","Begin with Carry the Zero for its rising melody, then The Plan to hear how the band can shift mid-song. Listen to Broken Chairs at the end as a summary of everything the band do well. Compare with Modest Mouse's The Moon and Antarctica, a record that grew partly out of this one."],"neutral-milk-hotel-on-avery-island":["Jeff Mangum's first full album as Neutral Milk Hotel mixes fuzzy, strummed folk-punk songs with long, droning instrumentals. Song Against Sex and Naomi are short, urgent and raw, built on overdriven acoustic guitar and Mangum's nasal, wavering voice. Gardenhead and Three Peaches add organ and fuller band arrangements. The closing Pree-Sisters Swallowing a Donkey's Eye is a thirteen-minute instrumental built from organ drones, tape textures and slowly shifting noise.","Mangum was part of the Elephant 6 collective, a loose group of friends from Ruston, Louisiana, who shared a love of 1960s psychedelia and home recording. On Avery Island was recorded with Robert Schneider of the Apples in Stereo in Denver and released by Merge in 1996. It was far less noticed than its successor, In the Aeroplane Over the Sea, which made the band a cult legend.","The long instrumental closer is as important as the songs: its drones connect the album to the minimalism of La Monte Young and Terry Riley as much as to indie rock. Listen also for the distorted acoustic guitar, which Mangum played through cheap amplifiers and microphones to make it buzz like an electric. The fuzz is deliberate, not a flaw.","Reviewers at the time gave it modest attention, and it was later overshadowed by In the Aeroplane Over the Sea. As the band's legend grew, critics returned to it and found the seeds of the later record's sound and themes. Some fans prefer its rawness and its willingness to experiment with long forms.","Start with Naomi for Mangum's voice at its most tender, then Song Against Sex for the fuzzy rush. Give the closing instrumental a patient listen, treating it as a piece of drone music. Then play In the Aeroplane Over the Sea to hear how the ideas matured, and La Monte Young's The Well-Tuned Piano for a much deeper drone."],"modest-mouse-the-lonesome-crowded-west":["A sprawling, seventy-four-minute album about strip malls, sprawl, truck stops and loneliness in the American Northwest. Teeth Like God's Shoeshine opens with jagged, twitchy guitar and Isaac Brock's yelping voice; Cowboy Dan is a slow, burning epic; Trailer Trash is a gentle, rueful ballad; Truckers Atlas rolls on for ten minutes of motorik groove. Brock's guitar plays in jangling, sharp, harmonic patterns that sound unlike anyone else's.","Modest Mouse formed in Issaquah, Washington, a town being swallowed by suburban growth near Seattle. The Lonesome Crowded West was released by the small Seattle label Up Records in November 1997, after the grunge boom had faded. It made the band a cult favourite and led to their signing with Epic Records.","Brock plays guitar with a lot of chiming harmonics, high bell-like tones produced by touching the string lightly. Listen to how often the band shift between rhythmic, almost danceable passages and slow, dirgelike ones. The album's sense of geography, with freeways, malls and empty landscapes, is built into the sound, which alternates between claustrophobia and wide-open space.","It was praised by the indie press as a major statement, and its reputation has grown steadily. Many critics now regard it as one of the most important indie-rock albums of the decade, an influence on later bands. The band's later commercial success with Float On led new fans back to this rawer record.","Start with Trailer Trash for the band's softer side, then Cowboy Dan for its full force. Listen to Brock's guitar harmonics and how they ring out. Play it on a long drive through anonymous suburbs if you can. Compare with Built to Spill's Perfect from Now On and Pavement's Crooked Rain, Crooked Rain."],"modest-mouse-the-moon-and-antarctica":["An hour-long album about mortality, space and the self, darker and more spacious than anything the band had made. 3rd Planet opens with chiming guitars and a slow build; Gravity Rides Everything is calm and almost pretty; Dark Center of the Universe is twitchy and anxious; The Stars Are Projectors is a nine-minute epic; Tiny Cities Made of Ashes adds a funky bass line. Strings, keyboards and electronic textures widen the band's jagged guitar sound.","Modest Mouse's first album for the major label Epic, produced by Brian Deck, was released in June 2000. Fans worried that the band would compromise; instead, they made a more ambitious and atmospheric record. It was reissued in 2004 with additional songs after the band's commercial breakthrough with Float On.","Many songs seem to circle the same set of images and ideas, giving the album the feel of a concept album about life, death and the universe. Listen for the subtle production touches by Deck: strings that enter quietly, reversed sounds and studio effects that make the guitars feel weightless.","Critics praised it highly as an example of a band that went to a major label and became more adventurous. It has since been named among the best albums of its decade. Some fans of the earlier records missed the rawness, but many now consider it the band's peak.","Listen in the dark, with headphones, starting at the beginning. Follow how 3rd Planet opens out from a small guitar figure. Then go straight to The Stars Are Projectors for the epic scale. Compare with Built to Spill's Keep It Like a Secret and with Radiohead for another band who used electronics to widen rock."],"sleater-kinney-call-the-doctor":["A half-hour of tight, urgent punk songs played by two guitars and drums with no bass player. Corin Tucker's powerful, vibrato-laden voice and Carrie Brownstein's sharper, cooler one trade lines and overlap, while the guitars interlock in a way that fills the space a bass would normally occupy. The title track, I Wanna Be Your Joey Ramone and Good Things move between anger, humour and longing, often within one song.","Sleater-Kinney formed in Olympia, Washington, in 1994, out of the riot grrrl movement, the feminist punk scene that included Bikini Kill and Heavens to Betsy. Call the Doctor, their second album, was produced by John Goodmanson and released in 1996 on Chainsaw, a small label run by Donna Dresch. It brought them national attention.","With no bass, Tucker and Brownstein tune their guitars down so that one can play the low lines while the other plays higher melodies, and the two parts weave in and out like a counterpoint. Listen to how the vocal lines also interlock, with each singer often taking a different melodic line at the same time.","Critics welcomed it as a great punk record and a mature statement of riot grrrl ideas. It led to the band signing with Kill Rock Stars. Over time, it has come to be seen as the first great record of a band who would become one of the most celebrated rock groups of their generation.","Start with the title track for the band's sheer energy, then I Wanna Be Your Joey Ramone for its wit. Listen first to the two guitars, trying to hear where one ends and the other begins. Compare with Bikini Kill's Rebel Girl and the Ramones' Blitzkrieg Bop for the punk roots."],"sleater-kinney-dig-me-out":["Thirteen songs, all short and sharp, played with even more force and precision than before. The title track explodes with a swaggering riff; One More Hour is an aching song about a breakup; Words and Guitar celebrates the power of music itself; Little Babies is a mock-cheerful satire. Corin Tucker and Carrie Brownstein's two guitars and voices interweave over Janet Weiss's powerful, propulsive drumming, which locked the band into their classic sound.","Released by Kill Rock Stars in April 1997 and produced by John Goodmanson, it was the band's first album with Janet Weiss, whose drumming gave them a new muscle. By then riot grrrl had passed its peak as a movement, but the band carried its ideas into a more polished, wide-reaching rock. The album's cover parodies the Kinks' record The Kink Kontroversy.","Listen to how the band use space: with no bass, there are moments when only one guitar and drums play, which makes the return of the second guitar feel huge. The vocal harmonies are often dissonant, with Tucker and Brownstein singing slightly clashing notes. That roughness is intentional, adding to the urgency.","It was greeted as one of the best albums of 1997, and its reputation has risen ever since. Many critics now regard it as one of the essential rock records of the decade, and Sleater-Kinney as among the greatest American rock bands. It is frequently cited as an influence by later bands.","Start with the title track and feel the drums push it forward, then One More Hour for the band's emotional peak. Listen for how the two voices answer each other. Compare it with Hole's Live Through This and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me to hear three different visions of women in nineties rock."],"bikini-kill-rebel-girl":["A celebration of solidarity and admiration between girls, Rebel Girl is a fast, driving punk song with a pounding drumbeat, a fat bass line and a simple, chiming guitar riff. Kathleen Hanna's voice moves from a speak-singing verse to a powerful, shouted chorus. The structure is simple, but the song's energy and joy make it a rallying cry rather than a protest dirge.","Bikini Kill formed in Olympia, Washington, in 1990, and became the central band of riot grrrl, the feminist punk movement that also produced fanzines, meetings and a call for women to take space at punk shows. The song was recorded several times; the best-known version, produced by Joan Jett, was released as a single on Kill Rock Stars in 1993.","Joan Jett's production gives the single version a fuller, more polished sound than the album take; listen for the cleaner, stronger guitar tone. The song's drum pattern, played by Tobi Vail, is simple but slightly off-kilter, giving it a lurching, insistent swing.","It was ignored by mainstream radio at the time, but became an anthem of the riot grrrl movement and later of feminist punk more broadly. It is now regarded as one of the most important punk songs of its decade, and the band's reunion tours have drawn audiences across generations.","Listen to the drums first, then the bass, and only then the voice. Notice how the song builds energy through repetition. Compare it with the Slits' Typical Girls and with Sleater-Kinney's Call the Doctor to trace the line of women in punk from the late seventies to the late nineties."],"fugazi-13-songs":["Thirteen songs combining Fugazi's first two EPs. The opening Waiting Room begins with a reggae-influenced bass line and a sudden pause before the band crash in, one of the great openings in punk. Ian MacKaye's barked vocals and Guy Picciotto's more theatrical voice share the songs, and the guitars switch between taut, choppy riffs and dissonant noise. Bad Mouth, Suggestion, Margin Walker and Provisional show the band's mix of punk, dub and funk.","Fugazi formed in Washington, DC, in 1987, led by Ian MacKaye of the hardcore band Minor Threat and co-founder of the Dischord label. They held to strict ethics: five-dollar shows, all ages admitted, no merchandise and no major label. 13 Songs collected the Fugazi EP of 1988 and Margin Walker of 1989, and set the template for what became known as post-hardcore.","Joe Lally's bass is central: his lines are built on the dub and reggae records the band loved, with empty space between notes, while Brendan Canty's drums are crisp and precise. Listen to how songs stop dead and restart, and how the guitars often play slightly clashing lines. Suggestion addresses sexual harassment of women, a rare subject for a male punk band then.","It became one of the best-selling records in American independent music without any major-label support. Critics hailed Fugazi as a model of integrity and artistic growth beyond hardcore. Today the album is regarded as a founding document of post-hardcore, and Waiting Room as one of punk's greatest songs.","Start with Waiting Room and listen to the bass line, then the moment the band explode. Follow the shifts between tight and loose in Bad Mouth. Compare with Minutemen's Double Nickels on the Dime and Gang of Four's Damaged Goods to hear the punk-funk roots."],"fugazi-repeater":["Fugazi's first full album proper: eleven tight, explosive songs with complex arrangements and abrupt changes of tempo and texture. Turnover opens with a slow, menacing build; the title track repeats a cutting riff; Merchandise attacks consumerism over a powerful groove; Blueprint is surprisingly melodic; Brendan #1 is an instrumental. Ian MacKaye and Guy Picciotto alternate vocals, and the band's sound is tighter, more muscular and more inventive than on the EPs.","Released by Dischord in April 1990 and produced by Ted Niceley with the band, it came as Fugazi were touring constantly under their independent principles. In a year when major labels were starting to sign alternative bands, Fugazi refused every offer, making the album a statement about the economics of rock as well as its sound.","Listen to the interplay of the two guitars: MacKaye usually plays the rhythm while Picciotto adds high, scraping lines, creating a jagged counterpoint. The rhythm section's dub influence is still present, with space between the notes. The title song turns repetition itself into the point, a riff hammered home until it feels like an argument.","It sold widely without a major label, and critics praised it as one of the most intelligent punk records ever made. It has since been regarded as a landmark of post-hardcore and an inspiration for countless bands across punk, emo and indie rock.","Start with Turnover and listen to the slow build before the explosion, then Merchandise for its political edge. Notice how often the band stop and restart. Compare with Slint's Spiderland and Shellac's At Action Park to hear the post-hardcore world that grew from it."],"fugazi-red-medicine":["The most adventurous Fugazi album: thirteen tracks that mix tight punk songs with dub, noise, instrumentals and ambient interludes. Do You Like Me opens with a deliberately muffled, distant sound before the band suddenly burst into full clarity. Bed for the Scraping is a taut, catchy anthem; Long Distance Runner is slow and melodic; Version is an instrumental built from studio textures; Target hits with a sharp riff.","Self-produced by the band with longtime engineer Don Zientara and released by Dischord in 1995, it came out at the height of alternative rock's commercial success, a world Fugazi had refused. The band had built their own way of working and used the studio more freely than before, experimenting with sound and structure.","The muffled opening sounds as if heard through a wall, a deliberate wrong-footing before the real sound bursts in. Listen to the instrumentals, which borrow from dub's use of echo and space, and to how the band treat the studio itself as an instrument.","Critics welcomed it as proof of Fugazi's continued growth, though some fans missed the rawness of the earlier records. Over time, it has come to be seen as one of the band's best and most inventive albums, an influence on later experimental rock.","Start at the beginning to hear the muffled opening and the sudden blast. Then go to Bed for the Scraping for a perfect Fugazi song. Listen to the instrumentals as dub pieces rather than filler. Compare with Tortoise's Millions Now Living Will Never Die for a different 1990s experiment with dub and rock."],"shellac-at-action-park":["Ten stark, minimal, punishing songs played by guitar, bass and drums with no overdubs or decoration. Steve Albini's guitar is sharp, metallic and dissonant; Bob Weston's bass is thick and loud; Todd Trainer's drums are huge and precise. The songs, such as The Admiral, Crow and Song of the Minerals, are built from repetitive, angular riffs, sudden silences and odd rhythms, often stretching or compressing the beat in unexpected ways.","Shellac formed in Chicago in 1992 around Steve Albini, already famous as a recording engineer for Pixies, PJ Harvey and Nirvana's In Utero. At Action Park, released by Touch and Go in 1994, was their first album. Albini insisted on analogue recording and on the band's live sound, opposing the gloss of commercial rock.","Albini recorded the band to sound as they would in a room, with microphones placed to capture the air around the instruments; that is why the drums sound so large and natural. Listen to how riffs repeat with tiny variations, creating tension through minimalism. Notice how little the guitar and bass double each other; each instrument has its own job.","It was praised by the indie press as a model of uncompromising rock. Critics noted the band's influence on math rock and noise rock. Albini's death in May 2024 brought renewed attention to his work as both musician and engineer, and At Action Park was widely revisited as a key statement of his aesthetic.","Play it loud, on good speakers, to hear the drums and bass in full. Start with The Admiral, an instrumental, to hear the band's rhythmic precision. Then compare the sound with Nirvana's In Utero and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me, both engineered by Albini."],"rodan-rusty":["Six long songs that shift between whispered calm and violent noise, a signature of the Louisville sound. Bible Silver Corner is quiet and delicate; Shiner moves from a slow, intricate guitar figure to a crashing climax; The Everyday World of Bodies stretches out for many minutes of tension and release. The guitars interlock in complex, chiming patterns, and three band members share the vocals, from spoken to screamed.","Rodan formed in Louisville, Kentucky, a city whose small punk scene produced Slint and other bands that rethought rock form. Rusty, released on Touch and Go's imprint Quarterstick in 1994, was their only full studio album; the band split soon after, and its members went on to form June of 44, Rachel's, Shipping News and Tara Jane O'Neil's solo work.","The songs are built like classical pieces, with long sections that develop a single idea before switching to another; listen for how motifs return later in a song. The interplay between Jeff Mueller's and Jason Noble's guitars is complex, and the bass often plays melody rather than root notes. Tara Jane O'Neil's voice adds a quiet counterpoint.","It was little noticed outside the indie underground at the time, but it has grown in reputation as a key album in the development of math rock and post-rock. Critics now hear it as a companion to Slint's Spiderland and a link to the bands that followed.","Listen to it as one continuous piece, starting with Bible Silver Corner. Notice the dynamics, from almost silent to very loud. Compare it with Slint's Spiderland and Tortoise's Millions Now Living Will Never Die to hear the world of post-rock that Louisville and Chicago shared."],"the-jesus-lizard-goat":["Nine songs of tight, menacing noise rock. Duane Denison's guitar is sharp and angular, sometimes bluesy, sometimes dissonant; David Wm. Sims's bass is huge and grinding; Mac McNeilly's drums are precise and powerful. Over the top, David Yow howls, mutters and shrieks lyrics that sketch grotesque characters. Then Comes Dudley, Mouth Breather, Nub and Seasick show the band's mix of swing, menace and control.","Formed in Austin, Texas, and based in Chicago, the Jesus Lizard recorded Goat with Steve Albini, and Touch and Go released it in 1991. The band became famous for intense live shows in which Yow threw himself into the crowd. They shared the Chicago and Touch and Go world with Shellac and Slint.","The band's precision is easy to miss under the noise: Denison and Sims often play intricate, interlocking lines, and McNeilly's drumming is tight and swinging, close to jazz in feel. Albini's recording captures the room sound and the bass's physical punch, so the album feels like a live performance.","It was praised in the indie press as one of the best noise-rock albums of its time and is now regarded as a classic of the genre. Critics often cite it as the band's best record and a major influence on later heavy, noisy rock. Nirvana's Kurt Cobain was an admirer, and the two bands released a split single in 1993.","Start with Then Comes Dudley to hear the band's controlled menace. Listen to the bass first, then the guitar. Play it loud. Compare it with Shellac's At Action Park and Nirvana's In Utero to hear Albini's sound in three different bands."],"black-flag-nervous-breakdown":["A four-song seven-inch record that lasts barely five minutes. Keith Morris shouts rather than sings, Greg Ginn's guitar is thin, harsh and slightly out of control, and the bass and drums race underneath without any of the swagger of mid-1970s punk. The title track lurches between a stop-start riff and a sprint. Nothing is decorated: no solos to speak of, no harmonies, no fade-outs. Each song states its frustration and slams shut.","Greg Ginn had already started a small electronics business selling radio equipment by mail, and when no label would touch his band he simply turned that business into SST Records; this EP was its first release. It came out of Hermosa Beach, a sunny, affluent beach town south of Los Angeles, far from the art-school punk of New York or London. Police harassment of the band's shows, and the suburban teenagers who flocked to them, became part of its legend.","The catalogue number SST 001 is a history lesson in itself: the label that began here went on to release Hüsker Dü, the Minutemen, Sonic Youth, Bad Brains and the Meat Puppets. Listen to how little the guitar sounds like the Ramones; Ginn's lines already bend and smear, hinting at the atonal, almost free-jazz soloing that would dominate Black Flag's later records.","Barely noticed outside southern California at first, the EP is now treated as one of the founding documents of American hardcore, the moment punk got faster, younger and less stylish. Historians argue over whether Black Flag or the Bay Area and Washington bands deserve the title of first hardcore group, but almost all agree this record set the template of tempo, length and do-it-yourself release.","Play it loud, all four songs in a row, and notice how fast five minutes passes. Then put on the Ramones' first album and hear how much more tuneful and cartoon-like New York punk was by comparison. Follow it with Damaged to hear how the band's sound thickened once Henry Rollins arrived."],"black-flag-damaged":["Fifteen songs in about thirty-five minutes. Henry Rollins, newly recruited from Washington, D.C., barks and howls over two guitars, Greg Ginn's lead lines twisting into squealing, atonal shapes while Dez Cadena holds the rhythm. Chuck Dukowski's bass and Robo's drums alternate between sprinting hardcore and heavy, almost sludgy grooves. 'Rise Above' opens with a chant of defiance; 'TV Party' is a deadpan joke about suburban boredom; the closing title tracks sink into slow, claustrophobic despair.","By 1981 Black Flag had become the most notorious band in Los Angeles, their gigs regularly broken up by police and their name spray-painted across the city by fans. Rollins, a fan who had jumped on stage at a New York show, joined that summer. The album was meant to be distributed through a label tied to the major company MCA, whose distribution chief reportedly rejected it as an anti-parent record, leaving SST to release it themselves amid lawsuits.","Listen to Ginn's solos: they owe less to rock guitarists than to free jazz, deliberately wrong-sounding and rhythmically loose. The famous four-bar logo, four black bars of uneven height drawn by Ginn's brother, the artist Raymond Pettibon, is a visual version of the music, a flag torn into pieces. Pettibon's drawings across the band's records are part of the album's world.","Damaged was ignored by mainstream reviewers and fought over in court, yet it is now routinely named the definitive American hardcore album. Some fans of the earlier lineups felt Rollins made the band too much about one man's rage; others hear the slow final songs as the seed of grunge and sludge metal. Its reputation has only grown as Rollins became a writer and broadcaster.","Start with 'Rise Above' for the battle cry, then 'TV Party' to hear the band's dry humour, then sit with the closing 'Damaged I' to hear how heavy and slow they could be. Compare it with Minor Threat, cleaner and tighter, and then with Nirvana's Bleach, which borrowed Black Flag's sludge."],"black-flag-my-war":["A record in two halves. Side one is recognisable fast Black Flag: short, furious songs led by the title track. Side two is something else entirely: three long, grinding pieces at a crawling tempo, each around six minutes, with Ginn's guitar dragging like a heavy weight and Henry Rollins sounding exhausted and cornered. The slowness is the shock; a hardcore band choosing to play at the speed of Black Sabbath's heaviest songs.","Legal battles with their former distributor had prevented Black Flag from releasing records under their own name for about two years, and the band were broke, touring constantly and frustrated with the narrowing rules of the hardcore scene. Bassist Chuck Dukowski had left; Greg Ginn played bass himself under the pseudonym Dale Nixon, and Bill Stevenson of the Descendents played drums. Long hair and metal influences were, to many punks, a betrayal.","That bassist credited as Dale Nixon does not exist; it is Ginn, overdubbing. Listen on side two for how the drums keep a steady, almost plodding beat while the guitar slips out of time, creating a seasick drag. The young Melvins in Washington state heard this side and built a whole aesthetic on it, which in turn fed Nirvana.","Hardcore audiences in 1984 often hated it, jeering the slow songs at gigs, and some early critics wrote it off as self-indulgent. Since the 1990s it has been rehabilitated as one of the most influential records of its decade, a direct ancestor of sludge metal, grunge and doom-laden noise rock. The argument now is less about whether it works than about whether Black Flag ever bettered it.","Play side one, then pause before side two, as if turning the record. When the slow songs start, resist the urge to wait for them to speed up; let the drag become the point. Then listen to Black Sabbath's first album and Nirvana's Bleach to hear the line running from one to the other."],"minor-threat-straight-edge":["A song that lasts well under a minute. Ian MacKaye's voice is hoarse and urgent, the guitar plays a simple, choppy riff, and the drums lock into a tight, fast beat with almost no ornament. There is no chorus in the ordinary sense, just a burst of statement and a sudden stop. The playing is cleaner and more disciplined than most early hardcore; every hit lands exactly where it should.","The song declares that the singer chooses to live without alcohol and drugs, and it gave its name to a movement. It appeared on Minor Threat's first seven-inch, released on Dischord, the label MacKaye and drummer Jeff Nelson ran from a group house in Arlington, Virginia, and recorded cheaply at Inner Ear Studios with engineer Don Zientara. Washington's punk scene was young, many fans under drinking age; the black X marked on minors' hands at clubs became a badge.","MacKaye has always said the song was a personal statement, not a manifesto; he was alarmed when 'straight edge' hardened into a rulebook, with some later followers turning it into an aggressive creed. Notice how little the song actually says beyond one person's choice. The tight, almost military precision of the band is itself part of the message: control instead of chaos.","Few songs under a minute long have had such consequences. Straight edge became a worldwide subculture, branching into vegetarian, vegan and religious offshoots, and critics have debated it ever since as either a healthy rebellion against rock excess or a puritan turn in punk. Musically the track, and the EP, are respected as models of economy and speed.","Listen to it three times in a row; it takes less than three minutes. Notice the snap of the snare and the way the band stops dead. Then hear Out of Step for the more developed band, and compare with Black Flag's looser, angrier Damaged. Fugazi, MacKaye's later band, is the obvious next step."],"minor-threat-out-of-step":["Nine songs in about twelve minutes, Minor Threat's only full record beyond their first singles. With a second guitarist added, the sound is thicker and more melodic than before: riffs ring out, choruses arrive, and there are even moments of something close to pop. Ian MacKaye still shouts every line, but the band now push and pull the tempo, dropping into half-time grooves before snapping back to full speed.","By 1983 Washington's hardcore scene had grown big and violent at the edges, and the band were under pressure from both sides: hardline fans who wanted purity, and voices around the band who wanted them to grow. Brian Baker switched from bass to guitar and Steve Hansgen joined on bass. The band split up later that year, and MacKaye went on to Embrace and then Fugazi, while Dischord became one of the most respected independent labels.","The title track was re-recorded for this release with a spoken passage in the middle, in which MacKaye steps outside the song to explain that his choices are not meant as rules for anyone else; it is a band correcting its own legend in real time. Listen for the clean, ringing guitar tones, closer to the Clash than to Black Flag, which hint at the melodic hardcore of the later 1980s.","Some early fans heard the record as a softening, but it has come to be seen as Minor Threat's most mature statement, and with their singles it remains a touchstone for every hardcore band that cares about tightness and principle. Arguments about straight edge and its later militant offshoots tend to circle back to the spoken passage here.","Start with the title track and listen for the spoken interruption, then 'Betray', with its shift from fury to almost melodic sadness. Notice how the drums and two guitars lock together like a machine. Compare with Bad Brains, the band who taught Washington to play this fast and precise."],"bad-brains-bad-brains":["Fifteen songs that alternate between the fastest, most precise hardcore anyone had recorded and slow, spacious roots reggae. On the punk songs Dr. Know's guitar is a blur, Darryl Jenifer's bass and Earl Hudson's drums play with a jazz musician's control at tempos that seem impossible, and H.R. yelps, preaches and shrieks. Then the band drop into reggae tracks like 'Jah Calling' and 'I Luv I Jah', all echo, bass weight and devotion.","Bad Brains were four Black musicians from Washington, D.C., who began as a jazz-fusion group called Mind Power before discovering the Sex Pistols and the Ramones. After being effectively banned from D.C. clubs, they moved to New York. This debut was released only on cassette by ROIR, a label specialising in tapes. The band had also become Rastafarians after seeing Bob Marley in concert, which explains the reggae.","Listen to the musicianship: the jazz-fusion training shows in the way the drummer can play this fast without losing the swing, and the way the guitar solos have actual melodic shape. 'Banned in D.C.' tells, in a couple of minutes, the story of the ban and the move north. The reggae tracks are not pastiche; their mixing borrows from dub's use of echo and dropouts.","The cassette was passed hand to hand and became one of the most influential underground records of the 1980s. Musicians from the Beastie Boys to Henry Rollins and Dave Grohl have named Bad Brains the best live band they saw. Critics now stress what was long overlooked: that one of hardcore's founding bands was Black, in a scene and later a genre often seen as white.","Start with 'Sailin' On' for the joy and speed, then 'Banned in D.C.', then let 'I Luv I Jah' slow your pulse. Listen to the drums at top speed and notice how relaxed they sound. Then hear Bob Marley's Catch a Fire and Minor Threat back to back to understand the two worlds this record joined."],"bad-brains-i-against-i":["Ten songs that move Bad Brains away from pure hardcore toward a hybrid of metal riffs, funk bass, punk speed and soaring, almost soul-singer vocals from H.R. The title track opens with a huge, chunky riff and a slap-and-pop bass line; 'Re-Ignition' builds slowly from a dub-like groove into a hymn. Dr. Know's solos are long and fluid, closer to Jimi Hendrix than to hardcore. The production by Ron Saint Germain is big and polished.","After internal splits and a period apart, the band regrouped and signed to SST, the label founded by Black Flag's Greg Ginn, for what became their most commercially accessible record. The mid-1980s American underground was moving toward heavier, more ambitious music, and metal and hardcore audiences were beginning to overlap. Bad Brains, as virtuosos who had always loved rock guitar, were well placed to lead the crossover.","The vocal on 'Sacred Love' was famously recorded over a telephone line, because H.R. was in jail at the time; listen for the slightly compressed, distant quality of his voice. Notice too how Darryl Jenifer's bass carries much of the melody, a funk technique that bands such as Living Colour and the Red Hot Chili Peppers would soon take to the charts.","Reviewers praised it on release, and many critics now rank it with the debut as Bad Brains' best, though some punk purists felt the metal gloss betrayed the early fury. It is often cited as a founding text of funk metal and alternative metal; musicians across rock and hip-hop name it as an influence. Arguments over whether it is a hardcore record at all are part of its appeal.","Start with the title track, then 'Re-Ignition' for the slow build and 'Sacred Love' for the strange phone-line vocal. Listen to the bass on its own for one song. Then compare with the first Bad Brains album to hear how much the band changed, and with Jimi Hendrix for the guitar ancestry."],"dead-kennedys-california-uber-alles":["A three-minute satire set to a marching, ominous riff. Jello Biafra sings in a quivering, theatrical tremolo, half cabaret villain and half newsreader, over East Bay Ray's surf-tinged, reverb-heavy guitar. The drums thud like a parade beat, then the song lurches into a faster punk section before returning to the march. It sounds like no other punk single of its year: more mocking, more theatrical, and more musically odd.","The song imagines California's then governor, Jerry Brown, as a smiling, mellow dictator imposing liberal lifestyle choices by force. It was Dead Kennedys' first single, released in 1979 in San Francisco, and helped launch Biafra's label Alternative Tentacles. That same year Biafra ran for mayor of San Francisco as a prank candidate and finished fourth. The band's name alone guaranteed outrage.","The title borrows the opening phrase of the German national anthem, long associated with Nazi rule, which is the joke: a hippie governor imagined as an authoritarian. Listen to East Bay Ray's guitar, which owes as much to 1960s surf records and spaghetti-western soundtracks as to punk. The band later rewrote the song as 'We've Got a Bigger Problem Now', aimed at Ronald Reagan.","It became an underground hit and remains Dead Kennedys' signature song, admired as one of the sharpest satires in punk. Critics have noted the irony that Jerry Brown later served again as governor and became associated with environmental policy; the song's target now looks surprising. Its sound influenced the theatrical, politically barbed punk of the following decades.","Listen for the shift from the march to the fast section and back. Notice how Biafra's vibrato makes everything sound sarcastic. Then hear the Fresh Fruit album version, and compare with the Clash's political songs, which are earnest where Dead Kennedys are mocking."],"dead-kennedys-fresh-fruit-for-rotting-vegetables":["Fourteen songs of savage political satire. East Bay Ray's guitar mixes punk power chords with twangy surf lines and spidery minor-key melodies, Klaus Flouride's bass is nimble, and Ted's drums are fast but varied. Jello Biafra's trembling, sardonic voice mocks yuppies, police, liberals and Las Vegas. 'Holiday in Cambodia' builds from a creeping bass-and-guitar introduction into a frantic gallop; a cover of 'Viva Las Vegas' turns Elvis into a grotesque.","Released in 1980 in Britain on Cherry Red and soon worldwide, the album came out of San Francisco's lively punk scene, centred on clubs like the Mabuhay Gardens. The election of Ronald Reagan and the rise of a new conservative politics gave the band endless targets. Biafra's mayoral run of the previous year had already made the band local celebrities and enemies of the establishment.","Listen to the guitar more than the voice: East Bay Ray's sound, with its reverb and surf picking, comes from Dick Dale and spaghetti-western scores, and gives the album an eerie, cinematic quality unusual in punk. 'Holiday in Cambodia' satirises comfortable American students who romanticise revolution, contrasting them with the Khmer Rouge regime in a single sharp clause; its slow introduction is one of punk's great builds.","The album was an underground success and remains the best known Dead Kennedys record, regularly cited among the greatest punk albums. Later years were clouded by a bitter legal dispute between Biafra and his former bandmates over royalties and rights, which complicated the band's legacy. Critics still praise it as proof that hardcore could be funny, literate and musically inventive.","Begin with 'Holiday in Cambodia' and give the introduction time; then 'California Über Alles' and 'Stealing People's Mail'. Listen for the surf guitar under the noise. Compare with the Clash's London Calling for a different idea of political punk, earnest and broad rather than satirical and sharp."],"dead-kennedys-plastic-surgery-disasters":["Dead Kennedys' second album is darker, faster and stranger than the first. Short hardcore blasts sit beside slower, more atmospheric pieces like 'Moon Over Marin', a ghostly surf ballad about environmental ruin, and songs linked by sound collages and short instrumental fragments. D.H. Peligro's drumming pushes the band harder, East Bay Ray's guitar grows more angular, and Biafra's voice ranges from crooning parody to outright scream.","Released on Biafra's own Alternative Tentacles label, the album appeared as the American hardcore scene reached its peak and the Reagan years hardened into everyday politics. The band had become international touring favourites and were increasingly influenced by the speed of younger hardcore groups, while Biafra was turning his lyrics toward consumerism, conformity and the press. Winston Smith's collage artwork became part of the band's identity.","Listen for the short linking pieces between songs, which turn the album into something closer to a continuous programme than a set of singles. 'Moon Over Marin' imagines the wealthy suburbs north of San Francisco after an oil spill; its slow surf guitar is a sound that later bands in post-punk and desert rock would borrow. The record's packaging includes a booklet of collages.","Less famous than Fresh Fruit, it has its champions who consider it the band's finest work, more adventurous and musically confident. Others find it scattered. Critics generally agree that it shows Dead Kennedys absorbing hardcore's speed while keeping their satirical, theatrical voice, and that 'Moon Over Marin' is among their most haunting songs.","Start with 'Moon Over Marin', then 'Winnebago Warrior' and 'Trust Your Mechanic'. Notice the short pieces that stitch the album together. Then return to Fresh Fruit and compare how much faster and denser this record feels, and set it beside Black Flag's Damaged from the same moment."],"minutemen-what-makes-a-man-start-fires":["Eighteen songs, most around a minute and a half, played by a trio who sound like a jazz-funk band disguised as hardcore. D. Boon's guitar is sharp and trebly, full of chopped chords and quick, angular melodies; Mike Watt's bass leaps around like a lead instrument; George Hurley's drums skip and swing. Songs stop abruptly, change direction mid-phrase and end almost before you notice they have begun.","The Minutemen were working men from San Pedro, the port district of Los Angeles, and part of the SST label family with Black Flag. This was their second full album, made cheaply and quickly, which was how they believed records should be made. The trio wrote about politics, work and friendship rather than teenage anger, and their idea of punk was freedom of form rather than a set sound.","Listen to how often the bass carries the tune while the guitar plays rhythm, a reversal of normal rock roles. Some songs have no chorus at all and simply end when the idea is finished. The band's concept of punk as self-made and endlessly flexible, which they called jamming econo, is audible in every abrupt stop.","It was admired in the underground press and is often seen as the record where the Minutemen found their mature voice, a step toward the sprawling Double Nickels on the Dime a year later. Critics praise its economy and warmth; it is sometimes overshadowed by its successor but remains a favourite among fans who prefer the band compact.","Listen to Mike Watt's bass first and let the guitar and drums arrange themselves around it. Do not expect choruses. Then go on to Double Nickels on the Dime, and compare with Gang of Four's Entertainment! to hear the shared love of funk rhythm in punk."],"x-los-angeles-los-angeles":["Nine songs in under half an hour. Exene Cervenka and John Doe sing together in slightly off-kilter harmony, their voices clashing and blending like a country duet gone wild. Billy Zoom's guitar is pure rockabilly, all fast picking and twang, played with a fixed smile; D.J. Bonebrake's drums are tight and driving. The album includes a cover of the Doors' 'Soul Kitchen' and portraits of the seedy underside of the city in its title.","X were the leading band of the first Los Angeles punk wave, before hardcore took over the city. The album was released by Slash, the label that grew out of a local punk magazine, and produced by Ray Manzarek, keyboard player of the Doors, who adds organ to some songs. The band's world was Hollywood's cheap apartments and bars, and its poetry came from Cervenka and Doe, both serious writers.","Listen for the way the two voices are rarely in perfect unison; the slight roughness is deliberate, and it is the band's signature. Billy Zoom's guitar style draws on 1950s rockabilly and early rock and roll, not on British punk, and that gives X a roots quality unusual in 1980. Manzarek's organ ties the record to the Doors' vision of Los Angeles.","The album was acclaimed on release and has been ranked among the best American debut records of its era. X are often called the great American punk band, a bridge between rock and roll tradition and punk urgency, and an inspiration for later roots-punk. Some critics prefer the next album, Wild Gift, but this one is the statement.","Listen first for the two voices together, then focus on Billy Zoom's guitar. Start with the title track and 'The World's a Mess; It's in My Kiss'. Compare with the Doors' first album to hear what Manzarek brought, and with the Ramones for a different American idea of punk."],"x-los-angeles-wild-gift":["Thirteen short songs about love, jealousy and struggling to get by, played faster and more confidently than on the debut. Exene Cervenka and John Doe trade lines like a couple arguing, then come together in rough harmony. Billy Zoom's rockabilly guitar sparkles and races; D.J. Bonebrake's drums are crisp. Songs such as 'We're Desperate' and 'The Once Over Twice' are compact, tuneful and fierce, with hooks that would fit on the radio.","Again produced by Ray Manzarek for Slash Records, the album came out as Los Angeles punk was splitting between the older, arty bands like X and the faster, younger hardcore scene from the beach suburbs. Cervenka and Doe were then married, and many of the songs draw on their relationship. The band also appeared in Penelope Spheeris's documentary The Decline of Western Civilization.","The record is in a sense a portrait of a marriage, with both singers offering their side; listen to how often they sing different words at the same time. Billy Zoom plays with very little distortion, relying on speed and clean twang. 'White Girl', with its sharp, wistful melody, hints at the country and folk side the band would later explore.","Critics were enthusiastic, with several naming it among the best albums of its year, and many consider it the band's best record. X never achieved commercial success to match their reputation, and debates about why are part of American rock history: too punk for radio, too rooted for hardcore. Their influence on alternative country and roots rock is widely acknowledged.","Start with 'We're Desperate' and 'White Girl', then listen to the album straight through; it is short. Pay attention to the call and response between the two singers. Then compare with the Replacements' Let It Be, another American band who mixed punk with older rock traditions."],"germs-gi":["The only studio album by the Germs: sixteen songs of loud, chaotic punk. Darby Crash's voice is a slurred, desperate snarl, Pat Smear's guitar is thick and buzzing, Lorna Doom's bass and Don Bolles's drums hold a heavy, pounding beat. Most songs are short and fast, but the album closes with the near ten-minute 'Shut Down (Annihilation Man)', a long, grinding piece that pushes the band's noise toward something hypnotic.","The Germs began in 1977 as teenagers barely able to play, notorious for their shambolic shows in Hollywood. By 1979 they had become a real band, and Slash Records released this album, produced by Joan Jett of the Runaways. Darby Crash, a fan of philosophy and David Bowie, wrote dense, cryptic lyrics. He died in December 1980 at the age of twenty-two, and the band ended with him.","The title (GI) stands for Germs Incognito. Listen to how coherent the album sounds, given the band's reputation for chaos; Joan Jett's production made them sound big and tight. Darby Crash's lyrics are full of references to Nietzsche and to power; read them separately and they are more literary than the delivery suggests. Pat Smear later played with Nirvana and the Foo Fighters.","On release it was a cult record, but it is now regarded as a cornerstone of Los Angeles punk and an early bridge toward hardcore. The Germs' story became a subject of films, books and the 2007 biopic What We Do Is Secret. Critics debate how much Crash's legend overshadows the music, but the record's power is widely acknowledged.","Start with 'Lexicon Devil' and 'Forming', then listen to the long closing track all the way through and notice how the sound becomes almost trance-like. Compare with X's Los Angeles, from the same scene but more tuneful, and with Black Flag's Damaged, the next step."],"descendents-milo-goes-to-college":["Fifteen songs in twenty-two minutes, mixing hardcore speed with surprisingly catchy melodies. Milo Aukerman's voice is nasal, earnest and tuneful, singing about rejection, girls, parents and suburban boredom. Bill Stevenson's drums are relentlessly fast but never sloppy; Frank Navetta's guitar and Tony Lombardo's bass play bright, ringing riffs. 'Suburban Home', 'Hope' and 'Bikeage' are short, catchy songs that sound like the Beach Boys filtered through hardcore.","The Descendents came from Hermosa Beach and Manhattan Beach, part of the same South Bay scene as Black Flag. The album was released by New Alliance, the label run by the Minutemen. The title refers to the band's singer leaving to study biology at university, which interrupted the band, a running joke that became their identity. Aukerman later earned a doctorate in biochemistry.","Listen for the melodies hidden in the speed; slow any of these songs down and you have a pop song. The cartoon of Milo on the cover, with glasses and a tie, became the band's logo and a symbol of the nerd-punk sensibility. Several songs are deliberately petty and adolescent, a refreshing contrast to the political anger of other hardcore bands.","Small on release, the album grew to be one of the most influential punk records ever, a founding text of pop-punk, cited by Green Day, Blink-182 and many others. Critics value its honesty and craft; some find the adolescent lyrics dated. Its importance in showing that hardcore could be personal and melodic is beyond dispute.","Start with 'Hope' and 'Suburban Home' for the melodies, then the whole album in one go. Listen for Bill Stevenson's drums. Then go to Green Day's Dookie to hear the influence, and compare with Minor Threat for a more serious version of the same speed."],"descendents-i-dont-want-to-grow-up":["The Descendents' second album keeps the speed of their debut but leans further into melody. Milo Aukerman's voice is stronger and sweeter, and the songs have real choruses and guitar hooks. Songs about crushes, insecurity and refusing adulthood alternate with jokes and short blasts. Bill Stevenson's drumming is precise and fast, and the guitars sound fuller and cleaner than before, closer to power pop than to raw hardcore.","Released on New Alliance in 1985, the album marked Aukerman's return from university and a lineup change, with Ray Cooper on guitar and Doug Carrion on bass. In between, Stevenson had played drums for Black Flag. The mid-1980s hardcore scene was splintering, with some bands getting heavier and others more melodic; the Descendents were leading the melodic path.","Listen to the title song as a manifesto for everything pop-punk would become: a celebration of youthful refusal, delivered with a smile. Some songs were written by Stevenson and Carrion as well as Aukerman, and the variety of voices shows. The bright production is deliberate; the band wanted to sound like a pop record played at hardcore speed.","It was less noticed than the debut but is admired by fans as a step toward the polished pop-punk of the band's 1986 album Enjoy! and 1987's All. Critics see it as transitional, showing the band growing up even as they sing about not doing so. Its influence on melodic punk is clear.","Start with the title track, then listen for the choruses and guitar hooks that would sit happily on a power-pop record. Compare with Milo Goes to College to hear how much the band grew in three years. Then listen to Green Day's Dookie, which carried this sound into the mainstream a decade later."],"crass-the-feeding-of-the-5000":["Eighteen songs of harsh, primitive punk with a political message. Steve Ignorant's shouted, Cockney-accented vocals, sometimes joined by Eve Libertine's and Joy De Vivre's, deliver lyrics against war, religion, the state and even other punk bands. The guitars are raw and clattering, the drumming by Penny Rimbaud is martial and repetitive. Some tracks feature tape collages and spoken word. The sound is crude by design, more an act of propaganda than of craft.","Crass were a commune living at Dial House, an open house in Essex, and saw punk as a call for anarchist and pacifist revolution rather than a fashion. The album was released by Small Wonder Records in 1978; workers at the Irish pressing plant refused to press the opening track, an attack on the Christian church, so the record began with two minutes of silence titled 'The Sound of Free Speech'. Crass later founded their own label.","That silent opening, a protest against censorship, connects the record to the avant-garde: Penny Rimbaud had worked in experimental art and music, and the gesture recalls John Cage's 4′33″. Listen for the band's visual identity too, Gee Vaucher's collages and Dave King's stencilled logo, which were as important as the music. The album title's number refers to the number of copies of the first pressing.","The album was an underground success and started the anarcho-punk movement, which spread across Britain and the world. Critics have often dismissed Crass's music as crude while praising their ideals and independence; others hear real power in the primitive sound. Their influence on DIY ethics, crust punk and activist music is considerable.","Do not expect polish. Listen for the energy of the shouting and the stark rhythm. Start with 'Do They Owe Us a Living?' and 'Punk Is Dead'. Then compare with the Sex Pistols, whom Crass considered sell-outs, and with Discharge, who took the anarchist message toward hardcore."],"crass-stations-of-the-crass":["A double album with three studio sides and one live side. Crass's sound is rougher and more varied: thrashing punk songs, slower chants, tape collages and Eve Libertine's sharp, almost operatic vocals alongside Steve Ignorant's shouting. The songs attack war, consumerism, religion, sexism and the music industry. The live side, recorded at a London pub, captures the band's intensity and the audience's response.","Released on Crass Records, the band's own label, the album was sold at a deliberately low price printed on the sleeve. The title refers to the band's campaign of stencilled graffiti on advertising posters in London Underground stations, part of their direct action. Britain was entering the Thatcher years, and Crass became a focus for a growing anarchist and pacifist movement.","Listen for Penny Rimbaud's drumming, influenced by military rhythms and avant-garde percussion rather than rock. The price printed on the sleeve was a principle: Crass wanted their music to be affordable and not exploited by retailers. The tape collages draw on news broadcasts and found sound, a technique from experimental music.","The album confirmed Crass as the leaders of anarcho-punk and sold well for an independent release. Critics remain divided, some finding the music monotonous, others praising its uncompromising radicalism. It is now seen as a key document of British political punk and of DIY culture.","Start with the live side to hear the band at their most direct. Then listen to Eve Libertine's songs, which show a different, more theatrical side of the band. Compare with Discharge for the hardcore development and with Gang of Four for a more musically sophisticated political punk."],"discharge-hear-nothing-see-nothing-say-nothing":["Fourteen short, brutal songs built on one relentless drum pattern, a pounding, galloping beat that came to be called the d-beat after the band. Cal Morris shouts short, slogan-like lyrics about the horror of nuclear war and state power. Bones's guitar is a wall of distortion, the bass thick and the drums by Garry Maloney unstoppable. There are no melodies to speak of: just noise, rhythm and rage, delivered with crushing force.","Discharge came from Stoke-on-Trent, an industrial city in the English Midlands, and were part of the UK82 wave of harder, faster British punk. The album was released by Clay Records in 1982, a time of renewed nuclear fears, the Falklands War and mass unemployment. The band's black-and-white imagery of war and their minimal lyrics were designed to shock.","That drum pattern is the key to the whole record and to a genre: a fast, insistent beat that every crust punk, grindcore and extreme metal band would later borrow. Listen for how the guitar almost disappears into the drums. Discharge's influence on metal is huge; Metallica covered two of their songs and Napalm Death and Sepultura name them as ancestors.","Initially seen by many critics as crude noise, the album is now regarded as one of the most influential punk records, the root of crust punk, d-beat and much extreme metal. Debate continues over whether it is music or a weapon, but its importance is clear.","Listen to the drums first, then let the guitar and voice wash over you. Do not look for melody. Compare with Motörhead, whom Discharge admired, and with Metallica's Master of Puppets and Napalm Death's Scum to hear what grew from it."],"refused-songs-to-fan-the-flames-of-discontent":["Refused's second full album is heavy, metallic hardcore with flashes of the experimentation to come. Dennis Lyxzén screams political slogans over chugging, palm-muted riffs and stop-start rhythms. There are breakdowns, sudden shifts of tempo and moments of quiet that hint at the band's interest in jazz and electronics. The sound is dense and aggressive, closer to American metallic hardcore than to classic punk.","Refused came from Umeå, a university town in northern Sweden, with a thriving hardcore scene tied to straight edge, veganism and radical politics. The album was released by Burning Heart Records. Its title borrows from the Industrial Workers of the World's famous songbook, linking the band to a long tradition of protest music.","The title nods to the Little Red Songbook of the American labour union, whose subtitle spoke of fanning the flames of discontent; it ties Swedish hardcore back to Joe Hill and Woody Guthrie. Listen for the moments of quiet and dissonance that point toward the band's next album. Lyxzén's lyrics draw on situationist and Marxist writing.","Respected in the hardcore scene but little noticed outside it at the time, the album is now seen as an important step in Refused's development and in European hardcore. Critics generally rank it below The Shape of Punk to Come, but it has its champions.","Listen for the breakdowns and the sudden shifts of tempo, and for the brief quiet passages that break the assault. Compare with Minor Threat for the straight edge lineage and with The Shape of Punk to Come to hear what the band became two years later. Woody Guthrie is an unexpected but real ancestor."],"refused-the-shape-of-punk-to-come":["Refused's final album is a collage of hardcore, jazz, electronic music and noise. Dennis Lyxzén screams and sings over riffs that lurch between crushing heaviness and quiet ambience. 'New Noise' builds from a tense electronic introduction to an explosion of guitar; other songs fold in jazz interludes, drum-machine sections, spoken passages and ambient electronic textures. The album is long, ambitious and deliberately disorienting.","Released by Burning Heart in 1998, the album was meant as a manifesto for reinventing punk. The band broke up acrimoniously shortly after its release, during a chaotic American tour, issuing a statement declaring themselves finished. Umeå's radical hardcore scene and the late 1990s European interest in electronic music shaped the record.","The title deliberately echoes Ornette Coleman's 1959 album The Shape of Jazz to Come, signalling that Refused saw themselves as free-jazz revolutionaries for punk. Listen for the jazz passages and electronic sections hidden between songs. The liner notes include a manifesto drawing on situationist writing.","Little noticed at first, the album grew into one of the most influential hardcore records ever, inspiring post-hardcore, metalcore and experimental punk bands worldwide. Critics regularly rank it among the greatest punk albums. Refused reunited in 2012, partly because of the album's growing reputation.","Start with 'New Noise' and give the introduction its full time. Then listen for the jazz and electronic sections. Compare with Ornette Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come and with Turnstile's Glow On, which shares its ambition to reinvent hardcore."],"idles-brutalism":["IDLES' debut is a thick, pounding record of post-punk and hardcore. Joe Talbot's voice is a bellow, half shout and half chant, over driving bass lines, jagged guitars and relentless drumming. The songs are built on repetition, with simple riffs hammered until they become hypnotic. Subjects include class, masculinity, grief and austerity Britain, delivered with a mix of rage, humour and vulnerability.","IDLES formed in Bristol in 2009 but took years to find their sound. The album was released by Balley Records in 2017 during a period of austerity, Brexit and political division in Britain. Talbot's mother died during the making of the album, and grief runs through it, and several songs are addressed to her.","The title refers to the architectural style of exposed concrete, and the album's sound is deliberately raw and blocky, like a brutalist building. Listen for the bass lines, which drive the songs as much as the guitars. 'Mother' is one of several songs that mix anger with grief.","The album was praised by critics and built a devoted following, establishing IDLES as one of the leading British rock bands of their generation. Some critics questioned the band's sloganeering and the label 'punk', which Talbot himself has rejected; others hailed their emotional honesty.","Start with 'Mother' and 'Well Done', then listen to the bass and drums on their own for a minute and notice how much of the song they carry. Compare with Gang of Four's Entertainment! and the Fall for the post-punk roots, and with Fontaines D.C.'s Dogrel for a contemporary parallel from across the Irish Sea."],"idles-joy-as-an-act-of-resistance":["IDLES' second album is louder, more confident and more emotionally open. Joe Talbot sings and shouts about vulnerability, immigration, masculinity, love and grief, over huge, distorted guitars and driving rhythms. 'Danny Nedelko' celebrates an immigrant friend; 'Samaritans' attacks toxic masculinity; 'June' is a quiet, painful song about the loss of Talbot's daughter. The band's sound is fuller and more varied than on the debut.","Released by Partisan Records in 2018, the album came at the height of Brexit debates and rising anti-immigrant politics in Britain. Its message of joy, kindness and solidarity as forms of resistance resonated with a wide audience, and the band's live shows became famous for their communal energy. The album reached the UK top five.","Listen for the quiet moments, where the band strip back to almost nothing; they make the loud passages hit harder, and the album's sequencing places tenderness right beside fury. Talbot has spoken of the record as an attempt to answer grief and anger with vulnerability, and the title itself states that idea: being openly joyful as a political act.","The album was widely acclaimed, appearing on many best-of-year lists, and established IDLES as one of Britain's most important rock bands. Critics praised its emotional honesty and political directness, though some found the slogans heavy-handed. It remains the band's best-loved record.","Start with 'Danny Nedelko' and 'Samaritans', then listen to 'June' in quiet surroundings and on its own. Notice how the band balance noise and silence across the whole running order. Compare with Fontaines D.C.'s Dogrel and with the Clash's first album for the long British tradition of political guitar music."],"fontaines-dc-dogrel":["Fontaines D.C.'s debut is a set of driving, guitar-heavy songs about Dublin. Grian Chatten half sings, half speaks in a thick Dublin accent over jangling, chiming guitars, steady bass lines and propulsive drums. 'Big' opens with a roar of ambition; 'Boys in the Better Land' is a fast, swaggering anthem; 'Dublin City Sky' is a waltz-time ballad with folk roots. The album captures the city's streets, pubs and changing character.","The band formed in Dublin after meeting at music college and bonding over poetry, including Joyce, Kavanagh and Yeats; they published poetry pamphlets before releasing music. Released by Partisan Records in 2019 and produced by Dan Carey, the album appeared during Dublin's rapid gentrification and the aftermath of Ireland's economic boom and bust. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize.","The title refers to doggerel, comic or popular verse, and Irish street ballads; the band wanted to write rock songs in the tradition of Dublin's working-class poetry. Listen to 'Dublin City Sky', which nods to the folk ballad tradition of Luke Kelly and the Dubliners. Chatten's repetitions of short phrases owe something to the Fall's Mark E. Smith.","The album was widely acclaimed, making many best-of-year lists, and established the band as leaders of a new wave of British and Irish post-punk. Critics praised the songwriting and sense of place; some found the post-punk revival crowded. It is now seen as a defining debut of its decade.","Start with 'Big' and 'Boys in the Better Land', then 'Dublin City Sky'. Listen for the Dublin accent and the poetry. Compare with the Fall's Hex Enduction Hour and Joy Division for the post-punk roots, and with IDLES for a contemporary parallel."],"fontaines-dc-skinty-fia":["The band's third album is darker, more atmospheric and more varied. Grian Chatten's voice is deeper and more brooding, and the guitars move from jangling post-punk toward swirling, echoing textures influenced by shoegaze and goth. 'Jackie Down the Line' has a sinister, swinging groove; 'I Love You' builds from a tender opening into a furious denunciation of Ireland's politics; the opening track is sung partly in Irish.","Released by Partisan Records in 2022 and again produced by Dan Carey, the album was written after the band moved to London and reflects their experience of Irish identity in England. The opening song, sung partly in Irish, was inspired by the case of an Irish woman in England whose family were initially told her gravestone inscription in Irish needed an English translation. The album topped the charts in Ireland and the UK.","The title is an Irish phrase meaning roughly 'the damnation of the deer', an expression used by the drummer's great-aunt, and the extinct Irish elk stands as a symbol of lost Irishness. Listen for the use of Irish language and the shift in sound toward the Cure and My Bloody Valentine. 'I Love You' starts as a love song and becomes a protest.","The album was widely acclaimed and is often considered the band's best. Critics praised its emotional depth, its exploration of identity and its sonic adventure. It confirmed the band as one of the most important rock groups of their generation.","Start with 'Jackie Down the Line' and 'I Love You', then go back to the opening track for the Irish language. Notice the shift from jangle toward atmosphere and echo. Compare with the Cure's Pornography and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the sonic influences, and with Dogrel to hear the distance travelled."],"fontaines-dc-romance":["Fontaines D.C.'s fourth album broadens their sound dramatically. Grian Chatten's voice ranges from rapped, staccato verses on 'Starburster' to soft, melodic singing. The arrangements include synths, strings and studio effects, and the songs move between grunge-tinged rock, dreamy ballads and chaotic, almost industrial noise. 'Here's the Thing' and 'Favourite' have bright, catchy melodies, while the title track is dark and cinematic.","Released by XL Recordings in 2024 and produced by James Ford, the album marked a major label shift and a deliberate move toward a bigger, more commercial sound. The band adopted a colourful new visual style. The album was a major chart success in Ireland and Britain and broke the band more widely in America.","Listen to 'Starburster', where Chatten's breathless gasps between lines were inspired by a panic attack, built into the rhythm of the song. The album's new sound owes as much to 1990s alternative rock, such as Nirvana and the Smashing Pumpkins, as to post-punk. The title is ironic and sincere at once.","The album was widely acclaimed and brought the band to a much larger audience, filling bigger venues and festival stages. Some longtime fans missed the raw post-punk of their early records and heard the gloss as a compromise; others hailed the band's ambition and refusal to repeat themselves. Critics generally praised the album's range and confidence.","Start with 'Starburster' and 'Favourite', then listen to the title track with the lights low. Notice how varied the sound is from song to song, and how much the production does. Compare with Dogrel to hear how far the band have travelled, and with Nirvana's In Utero for the 1990s influence."],"turnstile-time-and-space":["Turnstile's second album is short, explosive hardcore with unexpected twists. Brendan Yates's voice is a hoarse shout over crunching guitars, bouncing bass and pounding drums. Songs are full of stop-start breakdowns built for the mosh pit, but there are also moments of melody, piano interludes and surprising grooves borrowed from funk and alternative rock. The album rushes past in under half an hour.","Turnstile came from Baltimore's hardcore scene and signed to Roadrunner Records, a label known for metal, for this album. Produced by Will Yip, it reached beyond the hardcore audience and set the stage for the band's breakthrough. The band's live shows, with fans jumping from the stage, became legendary. Its brevity, under half an hour, was a statement of loyalty to hardcore tradition.","Listen for the brief piano and electronic interludes between the hardcore blasts; they hint at the band's ambitions. The grooves owe much to 1990s alternative and funk rock, such as Rage Against the Machine and the Red Hot Chili Peppers.","The album was praised by critics and built the band a wider audience, landing on year-end lists outside the hardcore press. Some hardcore purists were wary of the experiments and the metal-label home; others welcomed a band willing to stretch the form. It is now seen as the bridge between Turnstile's scene beginnings and the crossover of Glow On.","Listen for the breakdowns first, the moments where everything drops to half speed, and then for the short interludes that interrupt them. Play it start to finish; it is barely longer than a sitcom episode. Compare with Bad Brains' I Against I, a clear ancestor, and with Glow On to hear the band's growth."],"turnstile-glow-on":["Turnstile's third album blends hardcore with dream pop, funk, R&B and alternative rock. Brendan Yates sings as much as he shouts, over riffs that range from crushing breakdowns to shimmering, reverb-drenched textures. 'Mystery' opens with a bright synth; 'Blackout' is a pounding anthem; 'Alien Love Call' features Blood Orange's soft vocals over a dreamy groove. The album is joyful, colourful and surprising.","Released by Roadrunner Records in 2021 and produced by Mike Elizondo, the album came out during the pandemic and became a crossover success, praised by mainstream critics and embraced by fans far beyond hardcore. Turnstile's live shows after lockdown became celebrations, and the band earned Grammy nominations. Clips of their shows spread widely online, turning a hardcore band into a festival draw.","Listen to the Blood Orange collaborations, which bring R&B softness into a hardcore record; they would have been unthinkable a decade earlier. The album's bright synths and reverb draw on 1980s dream pop and 1990s alternative rock. The band's dancing, bouncing grooves owe much to Bad Brains.","The album was widely acclaimed as one of the best of 2021, appearing on year-end lists from music magazines to newspapers, and is seen as a landmark in hardcore's expansion. Some purists objected to the polish and the guest stars; most critics hailed its inventiveness and joy, and argued it proved hardcore could grow without losing its body.","Start with 'Mystery' and 'Blackout', then 'Alien Love Call'. Notice how the band mix heaviness and lightness, sometimes within a single bar. Compare with Bad Brains' I Against I and Refused's The Shape of Punk to Come for the shared ambition to reinvent hardcore, and with Blood Orange's own records."],"amyl-and-the-sniffers-amyl-and-the-sniffers":["The Melbourne band's debut is fast, loud pub punk. Amy Taylor's voice is a snarling, high-energy shout, delivered with a broad Australian accent, over riffs that draw on 1970s pub rock and punk. The songs are short and catchy, led by 'Some Mutts (Can't Be Muzzled)' and 'Got You'. The sound is raw but tight, with chugging guitars and a driving rhythm section.","Amyl and the Sniffers formed in Melbourne in 2016, recording their first EP in a few hours in a share house. Their debut album, released in 2019, won the ARIA Award for Best Rock Album. The band draw on Australia's tradition of pub rock and punk, from AC/DC and Rose Tattoo to the Saints and Radio Birdman.","Listen for the influence of Australian pub rock, with its heavy, boogie-based riffs, as much as punk; many songs sit on a single churning chord pattern. Amy Taylor's lyrics, often comic, celebrate working-class identity, independence and refusing to be pushed around. The band's mullets and 1970s style are a deliberate nod to the pub circuit.","The album was praised by critics and won the ARIA Award for Best Rock Album, establishing the band as one of the leading punk groups of their generation. Some critics saw them as a revival act; others praised their energy and freshness.","Listen to the riffs first, then to Taylor's voice and the way she rides the beat rather than singing a melody. Play it loud; it was made for small rooms. Compare with AC/DC's Let There Be Rock for the pub rock roots and with the Stooges' Raw Power for the punk energy."],"amyl-and-the-sniffers-comfort-to-me":["The band's second album is harder, faster and more varied. Amy Taylor's voice is more confident and expressive, ranging from snarling punk to almost melodic singing. The songs cover anxiety, love, feminism and women's safety, over riffs that blend pub rock, hardcore and power pop. 'Guided by Angels' opens with a rush; 'Hertz' is about longing to escape the city; 'Security' is a catchy, comic song.","Written and recorded during Melbourne's long pandemic lockdowns, the album was released by Rough Trade in 2021 and produced by Dan Luscombe. The band's energy, confined during lockdown, poured into the record. It was widely praised and expanded the band's international audience. With touring impossible, the band rehearsed and wrote constantly, and the record sounds like a live set compressed into a studio.","Listen for Taylor's more vulnerable moments, where she sings about anxiety and insecurity; they balance the band's brash image. The guitar riffs draw on hardcore as well as pub rock, giving the album more speed. Several songs address women's experiences of safety in public spaces, in a straightforward feminist voice.","The album was widely acclaimed and is often considered the band's best. Critics praised its energy, its tighter songwriting and Taylor's growth as a lyricist, noting that the comic bravado of the debut now sat beside anxiety and self-examination. It took the band from Australian favourites to international festival regulars.","Start with 'Guided by Angels' and 'Hertz', then listen for how Taylor's voice moves between snarl and something closer to song. Notice how much faster the drums are than on the debut. Compare with the debut and with IDLES' Joy as an Act of Resistance for a contemporary parallel."],"elton-john-your-song":["A love song that pretends to be embarrassed about being a love song: the singer fumbles for a gift and offers the song itself. Elton John's piano carries it, a rolling, hymn-like accompaniment with gospel turns in the left hand, while his voice stays close and conversational. Strings arrive gradually and never swamp the piano. Four minutes, no solo, no big finish, just a melody that keeps climbing to its title and settling back again.","Elton John wrote the music to a lyric by Bernie Taupin, the teenage poet he had met through a 1967 music-paper advertisement for new songwriters. The two were living in London, writing to order for publisher Dick James. The song appeared on the album Elton John in 1970, produced by Gus Dudgeon with orchestral arrangements by Paul Buckmaster, and became his first hit on both sides of the Atlantic, turning a session pianist and songwriter for hire into a star.","Listen to how the piano part, not the vocal, carries the song's structure: the little rising run before each verse works like a breath before speaking. Taupin's lyric deliberately stumbles, with the singer forgetting things and correcting himself, and Elton sets those hesitations to slightly lopsided phrases rather than smoothing them. Buckmaster's strings, trained on classical scoring, enter so softly that many listeners never notice they are there until the last verse.","Critics heard it at once as a new kind of piano ballad, owing something to Leon Russell and to gospel but plainer and more open. John Lennon later said it was the first new thing he had heard since the Beatles. It has never really left the repertoire, covered by singers from Rod Stewart to Ellie Goulding, and is generally treated as the template for the confessional piano pop of the decade that followed.","Listen first to the piano alone: the left hand walks while the right hand chimes, like a church accompanist playing for a wedding. Then follow the strings as they creep in. Compare Carole King's Tapestry, made the same season, and Billy Joel's Piano Man three years later, which takes the same piano-confessional voice into story-telling."],"elton-john-tiny-dancer":["Six minutes that take their time. The song opens with solo piano and voice, adds pedal steel guitar and a country lilt, and only after two long verses lets the chorus break open with drums, strings and backing choir. It is a portrait of a young woman glimpsed in Los Angeles, and the music matches its unhurried admiration: a long slow build, then a chorus that circles round and round as if no one wants it to end.","Bernie Taupin wrote the lyric after the pair's first American tour in 1970, when California was still new and dazzling to two young Englishmen. It opened Madman Across the Water, released in 1971, with Paul Buckmaster's strings and Gus Dudgeon's production. As a single it did only modestly in America, staying an album track for most listeners through the seventies.","The song is almost all verse and pre-chorus; the famous chorus does not arrive until well past the two-minute mark, which is why radio programmers found it awkward. Listen for the pedal steel weaving behind the second verse, a Nashville sound placed inside an English orchestral arrangement. Buckmaster's strings save their entry for the chorus, so the moment of release is also the moment the orchestra appears.","For years it was a fan favourite rather than a hit. Its reputation changed in 2000, when Cameron Crowe's film Almost Famous staged a scene of a touring band's bus singing along to it, and a new generation adopted it. It is now one of Elton John's most streamed songs, and critics often single it out as the best example of his early, patient, orchestral style.","Do not skip ahead to the chorus. Sit through the verses and notice how each instrument is added one at a time, so the arrival of the full band feels earned. Then hear Your Song for the same team in a shorter form, and Goodbye Yellow Brick Road for where that orchestral ambition went next."],"elton-john-goodbye-yellow-brick-road":["A double album of seventeen songs that swings between grand ballad, glam stomp and pastiche. It opens with Funeral for a Friend, a wordless synthesizer-and-piano overture that runs straight into the hard rock of Love Lies Bleeding, eleven minutes in all. Then come Candle in the Wind, a ballad about Marilyn Monroe; Bennie and the Jets, a strutting, stop-start piano number; the wistful title song; and the glam rocker Saturday Night's Alright for Fighting.","Elton John and his band of Davey Johnstone on guitar, Dee Murray on bass and Nigel Olsson on drums had planned to record in Jamaica, but the sessions fell through and they moved to the Château d'Hérouville outside Paris, where they had made Honky Château. Taupin's lyrics drew on old Hollywood, rock and roll and his own rural English childhood. It was 1973, the peak of glam rock, and Elton was becoming the biggest live act in the world.","Bennie and the Jets sounds like a concert recording, but the crowd noise and handclaps were added afterwards in the studio, a fake live atmosphere around a band playing in a French château. Funeral for a Friend opens with an ARP synthesizer playing slow funereal chords before the piano enters, a nod to the prog rock of the time. The title song's falsetto chorus was a new colour for Elton's voice.","It sold enormously and has stayed the album most people mean when they talk about Elton John at his height. Some contemporary reviewers found a double album too long and padded; later critics mostly agree that its sprawl is its point. Candle in the Wind gained a second life when it was rewritten in 1997 for the funeral of Diana, Princess of Wales, a version separate from this recording.","Begin at the beginning: the overture-into-rocker is the album's statement of intent. Then jump to Bennie and the Jets and listen for the clapping that is not quite real. Hear it alongside David Bowie's Ziggy Stardust and Queen's Bohemian Rhapsody to place it in British glam's theatrical, piano-and-guitar ambition."],"elton-john-captain-fantastic":["A song cycle telling, in order, the story of Elton John and Bernie Taupin's struggling first years in London before success. Captain Fantastic is Elton, the Brown Dirt Cowboy is Taupin. The title song opens with acoustic guitar and a country feel; later songs move through hard rock, music-hall jaunts and big ballads. The centrepiece, Someone Saved My Life Tonight, is a slow six-minute song about a narrowly avoided early engagement, built on a stately piano and a choir of overdubbed voices.","By 1975 Elton was the best-selling act in the world, and he and Taupin looked back at the years between 1967 and 1969 when they shared rooms, wrote songs to order and wondered if anything would come of it. The album was recorded at Caribou Ranch in the Colorado mountains with his touring band, the last time the original rhythm section of Dee Murray and Nigel Olsson would play on an Elton album for some years.","The songs follow the chronology of their story closely, and Elton reportedly wrote much of the music aboard the liner SS France while crossing the Atlantic. Listen in Someone Saved My Life Tonight for the way the backing vocals swell exactly where the narrator's resolve changes. The album was packaged with a booklet of diary extracts and memorabilia, and the cover painting by Alan Aldridge is crowded with hidden figures and creatures.","It is widely reported to be the first album to enter the American Billboard chart at number one, a sign of Elton's commercial power at that moment. Critics at the time admired its craft but found its autobiography inward-looking. Elton himself has often called it his favourite of his albums, and later critics tend to rank it with Goodbye Yellow Brick Road as the peak of his partnership with Taupin.","Listen in order: the songs tell a story. Start with the title song's country-acoustic opening, then wait for Someone Saved My Life Tonight and listen to how slowly it is allowed to unfold. Compare Goodbye Yellow Brick Road for the outward-looking showman, and Billy Joel's The Stranger for another seventies piano man writing about his own city."],"billy-joel-piano-man":["A slow waltz sung by a bar pianist describing the regulars around him on a Saturday night: the bartender, a would-be novelist, a lonely old man, a waitress. Billy Joel plays piano and harmonica in a neck rack, alternating a rolling three-beat piano figure with wheezing harmonica breaks between verses. The chorus is written to be sung by the whole room, and the arrangement swells with accordion and mandolin like a bar band joining in at closing time.","After a bad first contract left him without royalties, Joel moved from New York to Los Angeles in 1972 and played for about six months in a piano bar under the name Bill Martin. The song is a composite of the people he watched there. It became the title song of his first album for Columbia Records in 1973, a modest hit at the time but the record that made his name.","The waltz time is the key: almost no rock or pop single of the period was in three, and it gives the song its old-fashioned, beer-hall swing. The harmonica-and-piano combination is borrowed from Bob Dylan, but Joel plays the piano part like a cocktail pianist, with show-tune turnarounds. Each verse introduces one character in a few strokes, like a short story, and the singer himself is the last portrait.","At the time some critics found it sentimental and Joel's persona too knowing. Its audience paid no attention: it became his signature, the song he closes concerts with, sung back to him by stadiums. Joel himself has sometimes been sardonic about it. It is now regarded as one of the defining pieces of seventies storytelling pop and was added to the US National Recording Registry.","Feel the waltz: count one-two-three along with the piano. Then listen for how each verse adds a new person and the band thickens slightly each time. Compare Elton John's Your Song for the British piano-ballad cousin, and Bob Dylan for where the harmonica rack came from. A live version shows how the chorus belongs to the crowd."],"billy-joel-new-york-state-of-mind":["A slow, bluesy ballad about coming home to New York after time away. Billy Joel's piano opens alone with a long improvised introduction in a jazz-club style, and his voice enters low and relaxed. The song is built like a pre-rock standard, with a bridge and a lingering tenor saxophone solo, and it sounds less like seventies soft rock than like something Tony Bennett might have sung in a supper club twenty years earlier.","Joel wrote it in 1975, after returning from Los Angeles to live in New York state, at a moment when the city was close to bankruptcy and widely written off. It appeared on Turnstiles in 1976, the first album he produced himself with his own touring band, including saxophonist Richie Cannata. The album sold poorly at first, but the song became his love letter to the city.","Listen to the piano introduction: it borrows the harmony and voicings of jazz ballad playing, with crushed blue notes and walking bass, a sign of Joel's background as a lounge pianist. The form follows the old Broadway and Tin Pan Alley model, with a contrasting bridge, which few rock songwriters were still using. The saxophone solo answers the vocal phrase by phrase, as in a jazz combo.","It was not a big hit on release but grew into a standard. Barbra Streisand recorded it within a year, and Tony Bennett later sang it with Joel. After 2001 it became a regular in tributes to the city. Critics who were cool on Joel's rock songs often make an exception here, praising it as his most convincing use of the older song tradition.","Treat it like a jazz ballad: listen to the opening piano as an improvisation and the sax solo as a reply to the singer. Then hear Tony Bennett's I Left My Heart in San Francisco for the tradition Joel was reaching back to, and Gershwin's songs for the older city music it quietly imitates."],"billy-joel-the-stranger":["Nine songs set mostly in and around New York. Movin' Out is a sardonic rocker about working people chasing status; the title track opens with a lonely whistled melody before turning into a hard rock song; Just the Way You Are is a soft, electric-piano love ballad; Vienna is a gentle piece of advice to slow down. The centrepiece is Scenes from an Italian Restaurant, a seven-minute suite of linked sections telling the story of a high-school couple, from bar-room jazz to rock and roll.","Joel's career was stalling when Columbia paired him with producer Phil Ramone, who had engineered for Paul Simon and many jazz and Broadway records. Ramone let Joel record with his working band rather than session players and encouraged a sharper, more polished sound. Released in 1977, it became Joel's commercial breakthrough, and Just the Way You Are won Grammys for Record and Song of the Year.","Scenes from an Italian Restaurant is put together like a small musical: a slow opening, a jazzy middle with clarinet and tuba, a rock-and-roll story, and a return to the first melody at the end, so the suite closes as a circle. The alto saxophone solo on Just the Way You Are is by the jazz musician Phil Woods. The whistled theme of The Stranger reappears at the very end of the album.","Critics in 1977 were divided; some found Joel's cynicism and craft calculated. The public decided otherwise, and the album became one of the best-selling of the decade. Its reputation has risen steadily, and Scenes from an Italian Restaurant is now often cited as Joel's finest piece of writing, a pop answer to Broadway storytelling.","Start with Scenes from an Italian Restaurant and treat it as a short stage show in sections. Then hear Just the Way You Are and listen for the sax solo. Compare Elton John's Captain Fantastic for another piano man's autobiography, and Steely Dan's Aja, from the same year, for a cooler, jazzier take on polish."],"carpenters-close-to-you":["A gentle love song in which the singer marvels at how the whole world seems drawn to the person she loves. Karen Carpenter sings in her low, warm contralto, close to the microphone, over Richard Carpenter's piano, which plays a distinctive skipping figure of repeated triplet chords. A flugelhorn takes a soft solo. Near the end the brother and sister's voices are stacked in many overdubbed layers into a wordless choir, and the song drifts away on a repeated cooing phrase.","Burt Bacharach and Hal David wrote the song in 1963; it had been recorded by Richard Chamberlain and Dionne Warwick without success. Herb Alpert, co-founder of A&M Records, suggested it to Richard, who rearranged it for the duo. Released in 1970, it became Carpenters' first number one in the United States and turned them, almost overnight, into the decade's leading soft pop act.","Listen to the stacked backing vocals: Richard and Karen recorded their own voices over and over until they sounded like a small choir, a technique borrowed from the Beach Boys and from Les Paul and Mary Ford. The piano's triplet figure is Richard's own invention and is the thing most people hum. Karen's voice is placed unusually close and dry for the period, so it sounds almost confiding.","Rock critics in the early seventies tended to dismiss Carpenters as square, middle-of-the-road music. That judgment collapsed over later decades, as Sonic Youth and many later musicians praised Karen's voice and Richard's arranging. Today the record is often heard as a model of studio craft, as carefully made as anything by Brian Wilson.","Listen first to Karen's lowest notes, which are warmer than most pop singers ever reach. Then concentrate on the final minute, when the overdubbed voices take over. Compare Dionne Warwick's Walk On By for Bacharach and David's songwriting in its first incarnation, and the Beach Boys' God Only Knows for another lesson in stacked harmony."],"carpenters-superstar":["A song about a fan waiting in vain for a touring musician who has long since moved on. An oboe plays a lonely opening phrase, then Karen Carpenter enters very low and almost flat in tone, singing with a numb restraint. Over four minutes Richard Carpenter's arrangement builds with strings and brass to a full, almost cinematic chorus, but the voice never breaks into display, which is what makes the song so unsettling.","Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell wrote it, and it had been sung by Rita Coolidge on Joe Cocker's Mad Dogs and Englishmen tour. Richard Carpenter heard Bette Midler sing it on television and saw it as a vehicle for Karen. Released in 1971 on A&M, it became a major American hit and sits on the album Carpenters, often called the Tan Album.","By Richard's account, Karen's vocal was recorded quickly, close to a first run-through, and kept because it could not be bettered. Listen for how little vibrato she uses, which makes the voice sound unusually direct. The oboe introduction, a classical colour rarely used in pop, sets the mood of loneliness before a word is sung. The arrangement keeps the voice dry and forward even when the brass is at full strength.","It was a hit and has gained in stature ever since. After Karen's death in 1983 it became central to a reappraisal of her as one of pop's great interpreters. Sonic Youth's eerie 1994 cover and Todd Haynes's 1987 film about Karen, which took the song's title, helped turn it into a cult object for a younger, more alternative audience.","Listen to the gap between the music and the voice: the orchestra swells, Karen stays still. Then hear the Sonic Youth cover to hear how dark the song can sound. Compare Close to You for the sunny side of the same partnership, and Dusty Springfield's Dusty in Memphis for another restrained, devastating interpreter."],"carpenters-a-song-for-you":["Carpenters' fullest album, built like a song cycle with an opening title song and a reprise at the close. Leon Russell's A Song for You opens as a slow, devotional ballad; Hurting Each Other and It's Going to Take Some Time are lush mid-tempo pop; I Won't Last a Day Without You is a soft piano ballad; Top of the World a cheerful country-pop song; and Goodbye to Love, a slow ballad, ends with a howling fuzz-guitar solo that no one expected from this duo.","By 1972 Richard and Karen Carpenter were among the best-selling acts in the world, recording at A&M's Hollywood studios with session musicians and Richard's elaborate arrangements. The album collected songs by Leon Russell, Carole King and Toni Stern, Paul Williams and Roger Nichols, and Richard and his writing partner John Bettis. Several of its tracks became separate hit singles over the next two years.","The fuzz-guitar solo on Goodbye to Love was played by Tony Peluso, who was told to play the melody first and then let loose; it is often called the first power ballad guitar solo. Listen for the album's frame: the title song returns briefly at the end, like a reprise in a musical. Karen plays drums on some of the duo's records, and her timekeeping is part of the sound.","Carpenters received fan mail accusing them of selling out over the Goodbye to Love solo, a reminder of how firmly their audience placed them outside rock. Critics at the time often patronised the album as easy listening. Later reassessments, including from Richard himself, single it out as their most complete statement, and it is a favourite among musicians who admire Richard's arrangements.","Start with Goodbye to Love and wait for the guitar solo to burst in after Karen's verse. Then go back to the title song and listen to the album in order. Compare Carole King's Tapestry, which shares a songwriter, and Laura Nyro's records for a more restless take on the same Brill-and-soft-pop tradition."],"bee-gees-main-course":["The album on which the Bee Gees became a rhythm band. Jive Talkin' rides a clipped guitar rhythm and a rubbery synthesizer bass line; Nights on Broadway builds from a soft verse to a chorus in which Barry Gibb first sings in his high falsetto; Fanny (Be Tender with My Love) is lush, soul-styled balladry. Alongside these sit the softer, harmony-led ballads of the brothers' earlier style, so the album sounds like a group changing in mid-stride.","By 1974 the Gibb brothers' career had sagged after their sixties hits. Eric Clapton suggested they record at Criteria Studios in Miami, and Atlantic Records paired them with producer Arif Mardin, who had made soul records with Aretha Franklin. Mardin pushed them towards contemporary rhythm and blues. Released on RSO in 1975, the album revived their career and set up the sound that exploded two years later.","According to the band, the chugging rhythm of Jive Talkin' came from the sound of their car's tyres crossing a bridge on their daily drive to the studio. Barry's falsetto on Nights on Broadway began as an ad-lib that Mardin encouraged him to keep. Listen to the bass on Jive Talkin': it is a synthesizer played by keyboardist Blue Weaver, an early example in a pop hit.","Critics welcomed the album as a comeback and were surprised by how convincingly a group of white Australian-English balladeers had absorbed Philadelphia and Miami soul. Black radio stations played Jive Talkin' before many listeners knew who the band were. Historians of disco now treat Main Course as the hinge between the sixties Bee Gees and the Saturday Night Fever phenomenon.","Start with Jive Talkin' and listen for the bass line, then Nights on Broadway for the first falsetto, then a softer ballad to hear the old group. Compare Hall and Oates's Abandoned Luncheonette, also produced by Arif Mardin, and Stevie Wonder's records of the same years, which they plainly admired."],"bee-gees-stayin-alive":["A song about a working man getting through life on the city streets, set to a strutting dance beat. It opens with a choppy, damped guitar figure and a steady, swaggering drum pattern at a walking pace; Barry Gibb's falsetto lead floats high above, with his brothers' harmonies stacked behind. Strings and horns stab between phrases. The bridge drops into a minor, darker passage before the groove returns. It is instantly recognisable within two beats of the guitar.","The song was written for the film Saturday Night Fever, released in December 1977, about a young man from Brooklyn whose life comes alive on the disco floor. The brothers wrote it while mixing a live album at the Château d'Hérouville in France, without having seen the film. Produced by the Bee Gees with Albhy Galuten and Karl Richardson, it opened the film over John Travolta's walk down a Brooklyn street, and the soundtrack became one of the best-selling albums ever made.","The drumbeat is a tape loop. Their drummer had to leave the sessions, so the team cut a short loop of drums from another of their recordings and played it continuously, years before sampling and drum machines made this ordinary. The tempo, around one hundred beats a minute, matches the rhythm recommended for chest compressions in resuscitation, and it has been used to teach that technique.","It was a huge hit, and the backlash against disco at the end of the seventies made the Bee Gees' falsettos a punchline for years. That judgment has turned round entirely: musicians and critics now treat the record as a feat of rhythm production and vocal arrangement, and the Saturday Night Fever soundtrack was added to the US National Recording Registry. The song has outlived both the backlash and its film.","Listen first to the drums and the guitar together: that interlocking pattern is the whole song. Then follow the falsetto lead over the brothers' harmonies. Compare Donna Summer's I Feel Love and Chic's Le Freak to hear other routes into disco, and Main Course for how the brothers got here."],"bee-gees-how-deep-is-your-love":["A slow love ballad asking for reassurance from a partner. A warm, shimmering electric piano opens it and carries the harmony throughout; the Gibb brothers sing in close three-part harmony, mostly in their natural voices with falsetto touches rather than the full falsetto of their dance hits. The beat is soft and steady, and the chord changes keep sliding in unexpected directions, giving the song a gentle, unresolved ache.","Written at the Château d'Hérouville in France in 1977 during the same sessions that produced the Saturday Night Fever songs, it was released as the first single from the soundtrack before the film opened. Keyboardist Blue Weaver worked out its electric piano part with Barry Gibb. It won a Grammy for best pop vocal performance by a group and was a hit across the world.","Listen to the harmony: the chords shift in jazz-inflected ways more typical of Burt Bacharach or Stevie Wonder than of disco, which is why the song has been so popular with jazz and soul singers. The electric piano is a Fender Rhodes, an instrument that defined seventies soft soul. The brothers' blend is so close that it can be hard to tell how many voices there are.","Even critics hostile to disco tended to admire this one as a piece of songwriting. It has been covered many times, including a British number one by Take That in 1996, and the Bee Gees themselves often cited it as one of their best songs. It is now a standard of soft-pop balladry, heard well beyond the film.","Listen to the electric piano on its own for the first few bars, then follow how the three voices separate and rejoin. Compare Stayin' Alive to hear the same brothers in dance mode, and the Carpenters or Bread for American soft pop of the same years."],"hall-and-oates-abandoned-luncheonette":["A mix of Philadelphia soul and acoustic folk-pop. She's Gone, the best-known song, is a slow, aching soul ballad about a breakup, sung in turn by Daryl Hall's high tenor and John Oates's lower voice and building to a gospel-tinged climax. Around it are softer acoustic songs like When the Morning Comes, the gentle title track and the brisk, banjo-flecked Las Vegas Turnaround, giving the album a folk warmth that their later hits mostly dropped.","Hall and Oates met as students in Philadelphia in the late sixties, a city whose soul scene, centred on producers Gamble and Huff, shaped their tastes. Atlantic signed them, and their second album was produced in New York by Arif Mardin, who worked with Aretha Franklin and later the Bee Gees. Released in 1973, it sold modestly at first, and She's Gone became a hit only after re-release.","The title and cover refer to a real derelict diner, photographed in decay. Listen to She's Gone for the way Hall and Oates trade lines, the duet structure of Philadelphia soul groups like the Delfonics transferred to two white singer-songwriters. Mardin uses strings with soul-record restraint, underlining rather than sweetening. The album shows folk and soul sitting side by side before they fused.","Critics praised it as a fine, unusual record, but sales were slow and the duo moved to RCA for a more commercial direction. Its reputation has grown since, and many critics consider it the duo's best album, the one that shows their roots most clearly. She's Gone has been widely covered and is a staple of seventies soul-pop compilations.","Start with She's Gone and listen for the moment Hall's voice soars at the end. Then hear the acoustic songs to understand how folk-based the duo were. Compare Al Green's Let's Stay Together for the soul ideal they were reaching for, and the Bee Gees' Main Course for another Mardin production."],"hall-and-oates-sara-smile":["A tender love song in a slow, slinky soul groove. Daryl Hall sings lead in a smooth tenor that rises into falsetto, over a soft electric piano, a lightly syncopated guitar and a laid-back drum pattern. The song barely raises its voice; the climax comes in an improvised, gospel-style vocal coda where Hall lets loose over the repeating chords. It is three minutes of quiet, almost private devotion.","Hall wrote it for his girlfriend Sara Allen, with whom he would stay for decades. It appeared on the duo's 1975 album Daryl Hall and John Oates, sometimes called the Silver Album after its cover, their first for RCA. Released as a single in 1976, it became their first major American hit, launching a run that would make them the most commercially successful duo of the next decade.","Listen to how restrained the band is: the electric piano, guitar and drums each play very little, leaving space for Hall's voice in the soul-ballad manner of Philadelphia records he grew up with. The coda is the payoff, where Hall improvises like a gospel singer. The smooth electric-piano sound points ahead to what would later be called yacht rock.","Soul stations played it alongside Black artists, and it found a strong R&B audience. Critics have long regarded it as one of the most convincing soul songs by white American artists of the period. It has been covered by a range of soul and R&B singers, and remains a favourite of the duo's live shows.","Listen to Hall's falsetto and to how much space the band leaves him. Wait for the closing improvisation. Compare She's Gone on Abandoned Luncheonette for the duo's earlier style, and Michael McDonald or Boz Scaggs for where blue-eyed soul went next. A good soul-radio playlist of the mid-seventies is the right setting for it."],"10cc-im-not-in-love":["A man insists he is not in love while making it obvious that he is. The music is a soft, endless cloud: a choir of voices holding chords, shimmering and shifting under Eric Stewart's hushed lead, with a gentle electric piano and a heartbeat bass drum. There are no guitars and almost no drums. In the middle the song stops for a whispered spoken phrase, then the cloud of voices closes in again.","Eric Stewart and Graham Gouldman wrote it as a bossa nova, but the band did not like it until Kevin Godley suggested making it entirely out of voices. Recorded at their own Strawberry Studios in Stockport, near Manchester, it appeared on The Original Soundtrack in 1975 on Mercury Records. It topped the British chart and was a major hit in the United States.","The choir is the band themselves. They sang each note of the scale many times over and bounced the results onto long tape loops, one loop per note, then played the chords by raising and lowering faders on the mixing desk, as if the desk were an organ. The whispered spoken line is by the studio's receptionist. Listen for the chord changes that come from faders moving, not from fingers.","Listeners and critics recognised at once that it sounded unlike anything else on the radio. It is now regarded as one of the great feats of analogue studio invention, mentioned alongside Good Vibrations, and its wall of voices anticipated sampling keyboards and ambient pop. It is regularly sampled and covered, and producers still study how it was made.","Use headphones and let the choir surround you; try to hear it as an instrument rather than as singers. Listen for the stop and the whisper. Compare the Beach Boys' Good Vibrations for studio-built pop and Brian Eno's Discreet Music for tape loops in a different spirit."],"10cc-sheet-music":["Ten tightly built songs, each a little pop parody or satirical sketch. The Wall Street Shuffle mocks money men over a jaunty strut; Silly Love lampoons love-song clichés with a hard guitar riff; Hotel sends up tourist exoticism; Somewhere in Hollywood is a slow, sour portrait of film fantasy; Old Wild Men is a hushed ballad about ageing artists. The songs change style abruptly, sometimes in mid-verse, with harmonies, guitars and studio effects layered thick.","10cc were four Manchester musicians, Eric Stewart, Graham Gouldman, Kevin Godley and Lol Creme, who had all written and played on hits before forming the band and who owned Strawberry Studios in Stockport. Sheet Music, their second album, was released in 1974 on Jonathan King's UK Records. Every member wrote, sang and engineered, so the band behaved like a studio collective.","Listen for the jokes in the production: sudden changes of mic sound, sped-up voices, sound effects borrowed from radio comedy. Godley and Creme were already experimenting with a device for bowing guitar strings mechanically, later sold as the Gizmo, which hints at their sonic curiosity. The band's dual nature, two pop craftsmen and two art-school experimenters, is audible in nearly every song.","Reviewers admired the cleverness but some found the irony cold. Its reputation has risen, and many fans consider it the band's most consistent album. It is often compared to Queen and Sparks for its theatrical, multi-part songs, and to the Beatles for its studio play. The split of Godley and Creme in 1976 makes it a record of the band at its most balanced.","Start with The Wall Street Shuffle, then Somewhere in Hollywood for a slower, stranger side. Notice how often the style shifts within one song. Then hear I'm Not in Love for where the studio experiments led. Compare Roxy Music's For Your Pleasure and Queen's Bohemian Rhapsody."],"harry-nilsson-everybodys-talkin":["A song about a man tuning out the noise of the people around him and longing to escape somewhere warm by the sea. A fast, fingerpicked acoustic guitar figure drives it, and Harry Nilsson's voice floats over the top with effortless range, rising into a high, wordless crooning at the end. Strings sweep in softly behind. It lasts under three minutes and feels like constant movement, as if the singer were already walking away.","The song was written by the folk singer Fred Neil, who recorded it in 1966. Nilsson cut his version for his album Aerial Ballet in 1968 with arranger George Tipton. It became a hit only after director John Schlesinger used it as the theme of Midnight Cowboy in 1969, about a young Texan drifter in New York. It won Nilsson a Grammy and made his name far beyond the circle of admirers he already had.","Nilsson had written his own song for Midnight Cowboy, I Guess the Lord Must Be in New York City, but the filmmakers preferred this cover. Listen for the way his overdubbed voice harmonises with itself in the final wordless passage, the same technique he used across his albums. The guitar part, adapted from Neil's, keeps a steady motoric pulse that makes the song feel like travel.","It was Nilsson's breakthrough and remains his most recognisable recording after Without You. Critics have noted the irony that this gifted songwriter became famous with other people's songs. The pairing with Midnight Cowboy is so strong that it now carries the film's mood of urban loneliness, and it is often listed among the best uses of a pop song in cinema.","Listen to the guitar's steady pulse, then wait for the end, when Nilsson's voice leaves the words behind. Then hear Nilsson Schmilsson for his own songs and Fred Neil's original for the folk version. Compare Simon and Garfunkel's film songs of the same period. Better still, watch the opening of Midnight Cowboy with it."],"harry-nilsson-the-point":["A fable told in songs and spoken narration. In a land where everyone and everything has a point, a round-headed boy named Oblio is banished with his dog Arrow to the Pointless Forest, where he meets odd creatures and learns that everything has a point after all. Nilsson's songs range from vaudeville bounce to tender lullaby, linked by narration. Me and My Arrow, a jaunty little march, is the best-known song.","Nilsson conceived the story himself, and it was produced as an animated television film for ABC in 1971, directed by Fred Wolf, alongside the album. The film was narrated by Dustin Hoffman on first broadcast, and later by Ringo Starr for home video. It came out in the same year as Nilsson Schmilsson, when Nilsson was at the height of his reputation.","The album and the film are different works: on the record Nilsson narrates himself, with a casual, storyteller's voice. Listen for how each song works as a character piece, close to music-hall and children's song traditions rather than rock. The fable's quiet moral, about difference and prejudice, is told without sermonising, and the songs keep a light touch even when the story is about exclusion.","The album and film found a devoted audience, especially among children of the seventies, and the story was adapted for the stage in later years. Critics have tended to see it as a charming side project, but it is now often cited as one of the most original concept albums of its era, and an early example of a pop musician creating a full children's narrative.","Listen in order, as a story. Start with Me and My Arrow, then go back to hear it in context. Compare Prokofiev's Peter and the Wolf for another tale told in music and words, and the Who's Tommy for a rock fable on a much larger scale."],"olivia-newton-john-have-you-never-been-mellow":["A gentle song urging a restless, driven friend to slow down and take life easier. Olivia Newton-John sings in a clear, light soprano, unforced and bright, over soft piano chords and acoustic guitar. Strings and backing vocals fill in the chorus, which rises gently and then settles. Nothing is loud, nothing is rushed; the arrangement practises exactly what the song preaches, which is why it became a byword for seventies soft pop.","Newton-John was born in Cambridge, raised in Melbourne, and had built her career in Britain before finding a large American audience with country-tinged pop. The song was written and produced by John Farrar, an Australian guitarist and producer who shaped her sound through the seventies. It was the title track of her 1975 album and one of a run of hits that made her one of the decade's most popular singers.","Listen to the chorus harmony: the backing voices are blended so softly with the lead that they sound like an echo, a gentler cousin of the Carpenters' overdubs. The piano and acoustic guitar keep the arrangement almost folk-like under the strings. The lyric's gentle chiding of busyness made the song an unofficial anthem for the whole soft-rock movement, and its title is now a shorthand for the mellow seventies sound.","It was a major hit in the United States and Australia. Critics at the time often treated Newton-John's records as lightweight, and her success in country music, as a non-American pop singer, caused grumbling in Nashville. Later critics have been kinder, appreciating Farrar's craft and her clear, natural singing. The song is remembered fondly as a period piece of soft pop.","Listen to how little effort the voice seems to use. Follow the acoustic guitar under the strings. Compare the Carpenters' A Song for You for American soft pop at its most elaborate, and Bread's Make It with You for the male counterpart of the same mood."],"barry-white-cant-get-enough-of-your-love-babe":["A declaration of romantic devotion, opening with Barry White speaking softly in his deep bass voice over shimmering strings before the groove kicks in. Then come sweeping strings, chattering rhythm guitars, a bubbling bass and a steady, propulsive beat. White sings in a rich, gravelly baritone, more crooned than belted, and the orchestra swirls around him. It is lush and danceable at the same time, a sound that was entirely his own.","By 1974 White was a successful Los Angeles writer, arranger and producer who had built the female vocal trio Love Unlimited and their Love Unlimited Orchestra before stepping forward as a singer himself on 20th Century Records. This was the lead single from his album Can't Get Enough, and it became one of his biggest hits, confirming him as a major soul star. He worked closely with arranger Gene Page.","Listen to the spoken introduction: White's deep speaking voice was as much a trademark as his singing, and he used it on record after record. Under the strings, the rhythm section is pure funk: listen to how the guitars play short, choppy patterns that interlock, a technique taken from soul bands like James Brown's. The orchestra plays long, flowing lines over them, creating his characteristic contrast.","It was a huge hit on both pop and soul charts. Critics sometimes mocked White's romantic persona, but musicians admired his arrangements. His records are now recognised as an important bridge between orchestral soul and disco, and his sound has been widely sampled in hip-hop. This song remains one of his most loved.","Listen to the spoken opening, then to how the strings and the funk rhythm section play against each other. Compare Love's Theme for White's orchestra on its own, and Isaac Hayes's Hot Buttered Soul for an earlier, slower take on orchestral soul. Play it loud on a good system: the bass matters."],"barry-white-loves-theme":["A purely instrumental piece for a large orchestra over a dance beat, credited to the Love Unlimited Orchestra under Barry White. Sweeping strings carry a soaring melody, while underneath a wah-wah guitar chatters and a steady hi-hat drives the rhythm. There are no vocals. It sounds like a film score that decided to become a dance record, with the orchestra soaring and the rhythm section keeping everyone moving.","White formed the Love Unlimited Orchestra, a forty-piece ensemble of Los Angeles session musicians, to back his vocal group Love Unlimited. Love's Theme first appeared on their 1973 album Under the Influence of Love Unlimited and was released as a single on 20th Century Records. It topped the Billboard Hot 100 in early 1974, a rare achievement for an orchestral instrumental, as dance clubs were beginning to shape the charts.","Listen to the hi-hat: its steady sixteenth-note pattern became a defining sound of disco. The wah-wah guitar, borrowed from the funk and blaxploitation soundtracks of Isaac Hayes, makes the strings sound modern. The piece was reportedly popular in the early discotheques before radio caught on, one of the first records to show that DJs and dancers could make a hit.","It is widely considered one of the first disco records, predating the style's name. Critics at the time treated it as easy listening, but historians of dance music now credit it with helping to invent disco's orchestral sound. Its influence can be heard in the later Philadelphia International records and in the disco boom of the late seventies.","Listen for the contrast between the soaring strings and the chattering guitar. Notice how steady the beat is. Compare Isaac Hayes's Theme from Shaft for the wah-wah's origins, and Donna Summer's Love to Love You Baby for where the disco orchestra went next. The full-length album version gives the groove more room."],"bread-make-it-with-you":["A tender song about wanting to build a shared life with someone. David Gates sings in a soft, sweet tenor over gently picked acoustic guitar and a light, unhurried beat. A melodic electric guitar answers the voice, and strings fill in quietly behind the chorus. Everything is understated and clean, three minutes of intimate, easy-going warmth that defined the softer side of early seventies American pop radio.","Bread formed in Los Angeles in 1968 around David Gates, an experienced session musician, arranger and producer, and singer-songwriters James Griffin and Robb Royer. This song, written by Gates, came from their second album On the Waters, released on Elektra in 1970, and was their breakthrough hit, the first of many soft ballads that made them one of the most successful acts of the early seventies.","Listen to the guitar parts: Gates's background as a session player shows in how the acoustic and electric guitars fit together without crowding. The strings are kept unusually discreet, entering only to lift the chorus. The song's restraint, with no big climax, was part of a new aesthetic of intimacy that soft rock built on, and that later artists, often called yacht rock, would polish further.","It was a big hit, but rock critics often dismissed Bread as bland. Their reputation has been gently revived, and Gates is now respected as a fine craftsman of the soft-rock ballad. The song has been widely covered and remains a staple of easy-listening and oldies radio, a pure example of the early seventies soft sound.","Listen to how the two guitars talk to each other, and to the softness of the voice. Compare the Carpenters' Close to You from the same summer for the female counterpart, and America's A Horse with No Name for another soft, guitar-led hit of the time."],"america-a-horse-with-no-name":["A song about a journey through the desert on horseback, told as a dreamlike series of images. Two strummed acoustic guitars alternate between just two chords throughout, over a gently loping bass and light percussion. Dewey Bunnell sings in a high, thin, slightly nasal voice, and the chorus is a wordless, chant-like refrain in harmony. The effect is hypnotic: no real change, just the landscape rolling by.","America were three sons of American servicemen stationed in Britain, Dewey Bunnell, Gerry Beckley and Dan Peek, who formed the group in London. Bunnell wrote the song with memories of the American desert in mind. It was not on their debut album as first issued in Britain in 1971, but after becoming a hit as a single it was added to later pressings, and it topped the American charts in 1972.","Listen to the two chords that never change: the entire song is built on them, and the variety comes only from the voices and the strumming patterns. Many listeners at the time thought the singer was Neil Young, whose voice Bunnell's resembles. The song was banned by some American radio stations over suspicions about its title, which Bunnell has always denied was a drug reference.","It was a huge hit, but critics often mocked its lyrics and its resemblance to Neil Young. It has remained a staple of seventies radio and has become a cult favourite, used in films and television. Its hypnotic, minimal form now seems more interesting to many listeners than its imagery, and its plain two-chord strum has kept it a favourite of beginning guitarists.","Count the chords: there are only two. Listen to how the voices create variety on top. Compare Neil Young's Harvest for the singer it was mistaken for, and Crosby, Stills and Nash's debut for the harmony folk-rock it drew on. Then try singing the wordless chorus along with it, which is what the song invites."],"christopher-cross-sailing":["A song about the freedom and calm of sailing out on open water. It opens with a chiming twelve-string-style acoustic guitar figure; Christopher Cross sings in a soft, high, gentle tenor over electric piano, a slow beat and an orchestra that swells gradually. The arrangement is glossy and spacious, every instrument carefully placed. It drifts rather than drives, and by the final chorus the strings have lifted the whole song like a sail filling with wind.","Cross was a Texan singer-guitarist who signed with Warner Bros. His debut album, Christopher Cross, produced by Michael Omartian and released at the end of 1979, featured backing vocals by Michael McDonald on Ride Like the Wind. Sailing was the second single. At the 1981 Grammys Cross won Record, Song, Album and Best New Artist, the first artist to win all four in one year.","Listen to the guitar intro: the shimmer comes from the ringing open strings of the acoustic guitar part, picked rather than strummed. The strings, arranged by Omartian, are mixed very softly at first and gradually brought forward, so the song seems to open out. The polished, smooth sound is the purest example of what was later named yacht rock, a term coined in jest in the 2000s.","Sailing was a big hit and the Grammy sweep made Cross a star, but critics soon found the record bland, and his career was overshadowed in the MTV era. The yacht-rock revival of the 2000s, which began as affectionate mockery, made him a cult figure. The song is now valued, half-ironically, as the epitome of late-seventies smooth Californian pop.","Listen to the strings gradually filling in, and to the gentle guitar figure that repeats throughout. Compare Michael McDonald's What a Fool Believes, from the same studios and circle, and Steely Dan's Aja, the sophisticated jazz-rock record that set the standard for this polished sound."],"the-doobie-brothers-what-a-fool-believes":["A song about a man who fools himself about a past romance that never really meant much to the other person. It is built on a bouncing, syncopated electric piano figure that never stops, over a busy, insistent drum pattern. Michael McDonald sings lead in his husky, soulful baritone, and the backing vocals are stacked in his trademark layered harmonies. The song moves quickly, full of shifting chords, and feels breathless and restless.","The Doobie Brothers had been a hard-rocking California band until McDonald joined in 1975 and moved them toward soul. He wrote this song with Kenny Loggins, who released his own version the same year. The Doobies' version appeared on Minute by Minute in 1978, produced by Ted Templeman for Warner Bros, and won Grammys for Record and Song of the Year.","Listen to the electric piano: McDonald's syncopated chordal pattern, shifting slightly in each bar, became one of the most imitated sounds of the era. The stacked backing vocals, all McDonald, are a signature of his sound, heard on many records by other artists. Notice too that the chorus never fully resolves, which keeps the song spinning forward.","It was a huge hit, but the Doobies' turn to soul alienated some early fans. The song is now regarded as one of the defining tracks of the era and a cornerstone of what was later called yacht rock. Its electric piano style has been widely sampled and imitated, and McDonald became one of the most recognisable voices of the late seventies.","Listen to the electric piano throughout: it is the engine of the song. Then follow the backing vocals. Compare Kenny Loggins's version for a different approach, Steely Dan's Peg, on which McDonald sang backing vocals, and Christopher Cross's Sailing for where this sound went next."],"michael-mcdonald-i-keep-forgettin":["A song about a lover who keeps forgetting that a relationship is over every time the other person is near. It opens with a smooth, sinuous bass line and a crisp, light drum groove, joined by shimmering keyboards and synthesizer. Michael McDonald sings lead in his husky baritone, stacked with his layered backing vocals. The groove is laid back but tight, and the bass line is the thing most listeners remember.","After the Doobie Brothers broke up in 1982, McDonald released his first solo album, If That's What It Takes, on Warner Bros, produced by Ted Templeman and Lenny Waronker. He co-wrote this song with Ed Sanford. It shares its title and idea with an earlier song by Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller, recorded by Chuck Jackson in 1962, and some later releases credit them as co-writers.","The bass line was famously sampled in Warren G and Nate Dogg's Regulate in 1994, one of the defining records of West Coast hip-hop. Listen to how the bass, drums and keyboards lock into a groove: it is a lesson in smooth late-seventies Los Angeles session playing carried into the eighties. The stacked vocals are all McDonald.","It was a hit and established McDonald's solo career. Critics found it polished and soulful, if conservative. Its reputation has grown thanks to the Regulate sample and the yacht-rock revival, which made McDonald a cult figure. It is now regarded as one of the smoothest records of the era.","Listen to the bass line first, then follow McDonald's voice over the groove. Then hear Regulate to hear how hip-hop transformed it. Compare What a Fool Believes for McDonald with the Doobie Brothers, and Hall and Oates for blue-eyed soul. Headphones help with the bass."],"boz-scaggs-silk-degrees":["Ten songs of polished, soulful pop. What Can I Say opens with a bright, easy groove; Lowdown is a cool, slinky, minor-key song with a supple bass line and electric piano; Lido Shuffle is an upbeat, piano-driven boogie; Harbor Lights is a languid ballad; and We're All Alone closes the album as a slow, string-laden ballad. Boz Scaggs sings in a warm, slightly husky tenor, and everything is crisp, tight and carefully arranged.","Scaggs had played with the Steve Miller Band in the sixties and then pursued a solo career as a blues and soul singer in San Francisco. For Silk Degrees, released by Columbia in 1976 and produced by Joe Wissert, he recorded in Los Angeles with session musicians including David Paich, Jeff Porcaro and David Hungate, who soon formed the band Toto. The album was a huge success.","Listen to the rhythm section on Lowdown: Porcaro's drumming, with its relaxed, shuffling hi-hat, and Hungate's bass line became models for the smooth Los Angeles studio sound. Paich co-wrote several songs, including Lowdown and Lido Shuffle. We're All Alone became a big hit for Rita Coolidge the following year, showing how the album's songs travelled beyond Scaggs.","It was Scaggs's commercial breakthrough and Lowdown won a Grammy for best R&B song. Critics praised its craftsmanship, though some found it too slick. It is now seen as one of the founding albums of what would later be called yacht rock, and its musicians shaped the Los Angeles sound for years.","Start with Lowdown and listen to the bass and drums. Then hear Lido Shuffle for the upbeat side and We're All Alone for the ballad. Compare Steely Dan's Aja for a jazzier take on the same polish, and Hall and Oates for East Coast blue-eyed soul."],"steely-dan-do-it-again":["A song about a man who keeps repeating the same mistakes, set to a hypnotic Latin groove. A shuffling, conga-driven beat and a steady bass line repeat throughout; Donald Fagen sings in his sardonic, nasal voice. Midway comes a twangy electric sitar solo, then a squealing organ solo, both strange, unexpected sounds for a pop single. It is long, repetitive and seductive, a cool, cynical groove.","Steely Dan formed in Los Angeles in 1972 around Walter Becker and Donald Fagen, two New York songwriters who had been working as staff writers for ABC Records. Do It Again was the lead single from their debut album, Can't Buy a Thrill, produced by Gary Katz, and it was their first hit, introducing their mix of jazz harmony, rock and cynical lyrics.","The electric sitar solo is by guitarist Denny Dias, and Fagen's organ solo is played on a small Yamaha organ with a pitch-bending feature that gives it a strange, whining sound. Listen to the repeated chord pattern: it barely changes, giving the song its hypnotic, circular quality. The Latin rhythm was unusual for rock radio at the time.","It was a hit and established Steely Dan. Critics were intrigued by their cynicism and jazz influences. The song has remained one of their most popular, and its odd solos still surprise first-time listeners. It shows the beginnings of the sophistication that would culminate in Aja.","Listen to the steady groove, then wait for the sitar and organ solos. Notice how little the chords change. Compare Aja for where the band went, and Santana for Latin rock of the period. The full album version, nearly six minutes long, is the one to hear rather than the shorter radio edit, because the solos need room."],"roxanne-shante-roxannes-revenge":["A fourteen-year-old from the Queensbridge housing projects raps, apparently in one long improvised take, over the instrumental of UTFO's hit Roxanne, Roxanne. The record is a reply: UTFO had rapped about a woman who ignored them, and Shanté answers in the woman's voice. The beat is borrowed, plain and repetitive, a drum machine and a skeletal keyboard line, so everything you notice is the voice: high, nasal, cheeky, quick, and completely unafraid of the older men she is mocking.","Shanté recorded it in late 1984 with the Queensbridge producer Marley Marl, who would soon build the Juice Crew around her and the radio DJ Mr. Magic. It came out on the small Pop Art label and spread through New York radio and street-corner tape trading. Hip-hop records in 1984 were still mostly made by men about men; a girl from the projects answering a chart hit, in her own name and on her own terms, was something new.","Listen for how loose it is. The rhymes wander, she stumbles and recovers, and the take simply keeps going: this is a freestyle caught on tape rather than a written verse, and that rawness is part of the point. Her record also set off the Roxanne Wars, a flood of answer records by other rappers, many claiming to be the real Roxanne. Hip-hop learned from it that a diss record could be a career.","It sold in huge numbers for an independent twelve-inch and made Shanté a celebrity before she could legally drive. Contemporary reaction from the older rap establishment was partly condescending, and UTFO answered with records of their own. Historians now treat Roxanne's Revenge as the first great answer record and as the moment a woman's voice entered hip-hop as a combatant rather than a subject; it is a standard reference point in histories of battle rap.","Play UTFO's Roxanne, Roxanne first, then this, so you hear the same beat changing hands. Notice the speed of her comebacks and the way she laughs at her targets more than she insults them. Then jump forward to Queen Latifah's Ladies First to hear where that confidence went five years later. Any clean reissue of the twelve-inch will do."],"mc-lyte-lyte-as-a-rock":["MC Lyte's debut is a lean, hard Brooklyn record: crisp programmed drums, short funk and soul loops, scratching, and a voice that sits low and steady in the middle of it all. Ten or so songs run past in about forty minutes. Lyte does not shout; she talks with total control, every syllable placed on the beat, and the album's tracks range from battle boasts to small, carefully observed stories about the people around her on the street and at home.","Lana Moorer was still a teenager when she signed to First Priority, a Brooklyn label run by the family of the duo Audio Two, whose members helped produce the record. It is often described as the first full-length solo album by a woman rapper. It arrived in 1988, the year of Public Enemy and Eric B. and Rakim at their peak, when New York rap was getting denser, more serious and more competitive by the month.","Listen to the narrative songs rather than the boasts. Lyte writes in scenes, with dialogue and changes of point of view, a storyteller's craft that most rappers of 1988 had not yet tried. Notice too how little she raises her voice: her authority comes from timing and diction, a cool delivery that later rappers from Queen Latifah to Rapsody inherited.","Reviewers at the time singled out her poise and her writing, and Paper Thin became an underground favourite. The album was never a big seller, and for years it was remembered mainly as a historical first. Recent critics hear more than that: a fully formed artistic voice that held its own against the best East Coast records of a remarkable year, and a model of a woman rapping without playing a role.","Start with Paper Thin, then the title track, and listen to how calmly she rides each beat. Compare her low, conversational tone with the higher, faster delivery of Roxanne Shanté four years earlier. Then put on Eric B. and Rakim's Follow the Leader from the same year to hear the cool, exact style she shared with the best of her peers."],"mc-lyte-paper-thin":["Paper Thin is a song about a partner whose promises turn out to be worthless, and it plays out over one of the starkest beats of its year: a stiff, heavy drum pattern and a sparse loop, with long gaps of near silence between hits. MC Lyte fills those gaps with a calm, almost deadpan voice. She never raises it. The whole record works by restraint: the more patient she sounds, the more final her verdict becomes.","It was the standout single from Lyte as a Rock, released in 1988 on First Priority, the small Brooklyn label that had signed her as a teenager. Rap singles by women were still rare, and those that existed were often novelty answer records or party tracks. Paper Thin was something else: a serious, first-person story about a relationship, told without sentimentality and without asking for sympathy.","Notice how she structures the song like a scene in a play, setting up a meeting, a confrontation and a decision, rather than as a string of couplets. Listen for the deliberate flatness of her delivery: she lands the key words just behind the beat, which makes them sound considered rather than angry. That cool, unhurried technique is the song's real argument about dignity.","Paper Thin became Lyte's signature record and a fixture on New York radio. It is regularly listed among the great rap singles of the late 1980s, and writers on women in hip-hop treat it as a turning point: the moment a woman rapper's private life became the subject of serious art rather than a punchline. Later rappers from Lauryn Hill to Noname have been compared to its directness.","Listen once just to the drums and the silences between them. Then listen to how her voice sits inside those silences, never rushing. Compare it with Lauryn Hill's Doo Wop (That Thing), a decade later, which takes a similar subject and dresses it in soul and harmony. A standard reissue of the album is the best way to hear it."],"mc-lyte-ruffneck":["Ruffneck is a song about the kind of tough, street-smart man the narrator prefers to a smooth one, delivered over a rolling, bass-heavy groove with a slow, swaggering bounce. Lyte's voice is deeper and more relaxed than on her 1980s records, riding just behind the beat. The hook is chanted rather than sung, and the production leaves plenty of room for her phrasing, which has become rounder and more playful without losing its exactness.","By 1993 MC Lyte had been recording for six years and the sound of New York rap had shifted towards heavier, darker grooves and towards the radio-friendly blend of rap and R&B. Ruffneck, from her album Ain't No Other, met that moment head on. It became one of the first singles by a solo woman rapper to be certified gold, and it earned a Grammy nomination in the rap categories.","Listen to how the beat leaves the second and fourth beats of each bar almost bare, so Lyte's voice supplies the snap that a snare would usually give. Notice too the role reversal that made the song talked-about: a woman rapper describing the man she prefers, with the confidence and humour male rappers had long used.","The single was a commercial breakthrough and a turning point in Lyte's career, proving a veteran could thrive in the new, harder era. Some critics found it a concession to fashion after the austerity of her early records; others hear her most confident performance. It is now often cited as an early landmark in women rappers speaking about romance on their own terms.","Start with the groove: the slow bass line and the space in the drums. Then follow how Lyte bends her phrasing around it. Play it after Paper Thin to hear how far her delivery travelled in five years, and before Da Brat's Funkdafied to hear the bouncier, radio-ready style that followed."],"queen-latifah-all-hail-the-queen":["Queen Latifah's debut moves between styles with ease: hard boom bap, house music, reggae toasting and soul. Her voice is rich and commanding, a rapper who can also sing, and she switches between a regal declaiming tone and a quick dancehall patter. Highlights include Ladies First with Monie Love, the house-flavoured Come into My House and a duet with KRS-One. It sounds like a party thrown by someone with a lot to say.","Dana Owens grew up in Newark, New Jersey, and came up through the Flavor Unit crew around the producer DJ Mark the 45 King. Tommy Boy released the album in 1989, the high-water mark of politically engaged, Afrocentric New York rap, when De La Soul, Public Enemy and Boogie Down Productions were all reshaping what a rap album could say. Latifah's royal name and African imagery placed her squarely in that conversation.","Listen to the production credits as a map of the scene: the 45 King, Prince Paul of De La Soul fame, KRS-One and Daddy-O of Stetsasonic all contributed, so the album quietly samples the whole range of late-1980s New York production. Notice too how naturally the house tracks sit next to the rap ones, a mix of club and street most rap albums of the time kept apart.","The album was well received and made Latifah a star at nineteen. Critics praised her authority and range; a few found the stylistic variety unfocused. Its reputation has grown as hip-hop history has been rewritten to give women their place: it is now seen as one of the defining albums of the golden age, and Ladies First as one of hip-hop's essential statements.","Start with Ladies First, then Come into My House, and listen to how the record slides from rap to house without changing its personality. Then compare it with De La Soul's 3 Feet High and Rising from the same year: two very different answers to what a playful, positive rap album could be."],"queen-latifah-ladies-first":["Ladies First is a song about women's equality and leadership, performed as a duet by Queen Latifah and the London rapper Monie Love. Over a bright, bouncing beat built by the 45 King, the two trade verses: Latifah's voice deep and deliberate, Monie Love's quicker and higher, with a British lilt. The contrast between them is the record's engine, and the chanted title hook is simple enough for a whole room to shout back.","It appeared on All Hail the Queen in 1989. Its video set the song against images of Black women leaders such as Sojourner Truth, Harriet Tubman and Angela Davis, and against footage of apartheid South Africa, with Latifah moving pieces on a map. It was one of the first rap records to make women's solidarity its whole subject, at a time when rap's view of women was being widely debated.","Listen to the transatlantic exchange: Monie Love was a London rapper, and her accent and phrasing show that hip-hop had already become an international language by 1989. Notice how the verses pass the lead back and forth mid-thought, a trick of tag-team rapping borrowed from crews like Run-D.M.C., here used to make a point about partnership rather than rivalry.","The song was an immediate critical favourite and remains the single most cited example of feminist hip-hop of its era. Scholars of Black feminism have written about it at length, and it regularly appears in lists of the most important rap songs ever made. Some later critics note that its message is gentle next to later records; its pioneering place is not disputed.","Listen first for the handover between the two voices and how each changes the energy of the beat. Then watch the video, which is part of the work. Compare it with Salt-N-Pepa's Expression from the same year and Lauryn Hill's Miseducation a decade later to trace one line of thought across three records."],"queen-latifah-unity":["U.N.I.T.Y. is a song against the disrespect of women, set over a mellow, jazzy groove led by a sampled saxophone and a loose, swinging drum pattern. Queen Latifah raps in a measured, conversational voice, more counsel than complaint, and the chorus spells out the title as a chant. The mood is warm and unhurried; the anger is all in the words and the steadiness of the delivery, not in the music.","It was the lead single from Black Reign, released by Motown in 1993, a period when gangsta rap dominated the charts and arguments about rap's language about women had reached Congress and the newspapers. Latifah was by then also a television actor and a businesswoman managing other artists through Flavor Unit. The song won the Grammy for Best Rap Solo Performance.","Listen to how gently the beat moves: it borrows the laid-back sound of jazz rap, close to A Tribe Called Quest, rather than the hard, aggressive sound one might expect for a protest song. That choice is deliberate. Notice too that the verses address men and women in turn, so the song is really an argument for mutual respect, not a one-sided rebuke.","It was a hit and a Grammy winner, and became one of the most widely taught rap songs in schools and universities. Some contemporaries thought it preachy; others felt it said exactly what needed saying at that moment. Its reputation has only grown, and it is now seen as a central statement in the long debate about gender within hip-hop.","Let the saxophone groove settle first, then listen to how Latifah's tone stays calm even at the song's most pointed moments. Play it next to A Tribe Called Quest's Midnight Marauders to hear the shared jazz feel, and then next to Dr. Dre's The Chronic to hear the very different world it was answering."],"salt-n-pepa-hot-cool-vicious":["Salt-N-Pepa's debut is a short, punchy record of drum machines, stabbing samples and two voices trading lines with comic timing. Cheryl James and Sandra Denton sound like friends finishing each other's sentences: Salt sharper and more precise, Pepa louder and more theatrical. The beats are classic mid-1980s New York, boxy and loud, and the songs are mostly playful battle and party records, with a strong sense of performance and attitude.","The duo began while working at a Sears store in Queens alongside Hurby Azor, who produced the album and wrote much of it. Their first single was The Show Stoppa, an answer to Doug E. Fresh and Slick Rick's The Show. Next Plateau released the album in 1986, the year Run-D.M.C.'s Raising Hell proved rap could sell to everyone; Salt-N-Pepa would soon show that women rappers could too.","Listen to the answer-record DNA: like Roxanne Shanté, Salt-N-Pepa entered rap by talking back to a famous male record, and that call-and-response habit runs through the album. Notice too how much of the material came from their producer; the tension between the duo's personalities and Azor's songwriting would shape their later fight for creative control.","The album started slowly, then took off when a remix of one of its tracks by a San Francisco DJ became a club and radio hit, eventually making it one of the first albums by women rappers to go platinum. Critics have sometimes dismissed it as producer-led pop rap, but it is now recognised as the start of the most commercially successful women's group in rap history.","Play Slick Rick and Doug E. Fresh's La Di Da Di and The Show first, then The Show Stoppa, and listen to how the duo flip the original's swagger. Notice the speed of the handoffs between the two voices. Then jump to Expression to hear how much the group grew in three years."],"salt-n-pepa-expression":["Expression is a song about being yourself, and it sounds like it: a buoyant, danceable groove with a snapping beat, a funky bass line and a sung chorus that sits halfway between rap and new jack swing. Salt leads with a clear, assured voice and the record moves with the swing of a club track. It is one of the earliest rap singles to feel at home on both a dance floor and a school playground.","It came from Salt-N-Pepa's third album, Blacks' Magic, recorded with DJ Spinderella now in the line-up, and released by Next Plateau. Salt took a far larger role in writing and producing this time, a turn towards creative independence from their producer Hurby Azor. The record arrived as hip-hop and R&B were merging into new jack swing, the slick, drum-machine sound of Teddy Riley and others.","Listen for the house and new jack swing elements underneath the rap: the bouncing bass and the shuffled hi-hats owe as much to dance music as to boom bap. Notice too that the message is unusually universal for rap of 1989; there is no target and no battle, just encouragement, and that openness is a big part of why it travelled so far.","The single was a major hit and earned a platinum certification, and it stayed at the top of the rap chart for many weeks. Critics praised it as a confident, self-written statement from a group often treated as their producer's puppets. It has since become a standard of positive, uplifting hip-hop and a much-sampled source.","Notice the groove first, then the confident, almost conversational tone Salt takes. Compare it with Queen Latifah's Ladies First from the same year, two different ways of saying something similar. Then play Janet Jackson's Rhythm Nation 1814 to hear the new jack swing sound it was brushing against."],"lil-kim-crush-on-you":["Crush on You is a light, flirtatious song about a mutual attraction, built on a sunny jazz-funk keyboard loop and a lazy, head-nodding beat. Lil' Kim's voice is high, bright and playful; she sounds amused rather than tough here, and Lil' Cease of Junior M.A.F.I.A. trades verses with her. It is the gentlest record in her early catalogue, and its warm, glossy surface is the side of her that radio took to most easily.","Lil' Kim came up in Brooklyn as the only woman in Junior M.A.F.I.A., the crew built around the Notorious B.I.G., who mentored her and helped write and shape her debut album of 1996. Crush on You was released as a single in 1997, shortly after Biggie's death, and became one of her biggest hits. Its video, filmed in bold single colours, was much imitated.","Listen to the keyboard loop: it comes from Rain Dance by the Jeff Lorber Fusion, a jazz-fusion record, an example of how mid-1990s New York producers mined late-1970s jazz-funk for smooth, bright textures. Notice too how Biggie's influence lingers in the phrasing and humour of the writing; he was closely involved in shaping her early records.","The song was a commercial success and showed a softer, pop-friendly side of an artist known mainly for her frank, provocative persona. Critics then and since have debated how far Kim's image was her own design; most now see her as a skilled stylist and performer who changed what a woman rapper could look and sound like, and opened the door for Nicki Minaj and Cardi B.","Start with the keyboard loop and the slow, relaxed beat, then follow the back-and-forth between the two rappers. Compare it with the Notorious B.I.G.'s Big Poppa to hear the smooth, sample-driven Bad Boy sound that shaped it. Then play Nicki Minaj's Super Bass to hear how her playful style echoed down the years."],"foxy-brown-ill-be":["I'll Be is a breezy, glamorous duet between Foxy Brown and Jay-Z about loyalty and devotion, built on a sung R&B hook and a smooth, rolling beat. Foxy's voice is husky and confident, with quick internal rhymes, and Jay-Z plays the cool counterpart. The production from the Trackmasters is polished and radio-ready, with the warm, expensive gloss that defined New York rap's crossover moment.","Inga Marchand was a teenager from Brooklyn when guest verses on records by LL Cool J and Jay-Z made her name; Def Jam released her debut album in 1996, and I'll Be became her biggest single in 1997. It belongs to the era when Bad Boy and Roc-A-Fella turned New York rap towards luxury, R&B hooks and big pop samples.","Listen to the hook: it is built from René and Angela's 1985 R&B song I'll Be Good, slowed and looped, a typical Trackmasters move of reworking a recognisable mid-1980s R&B record for a new generation. Notice too how closely Foxy's rhythmic style mirrors Jay-Z's; the two were close collaborators at the time, and the song plays like a conversation in a shared language.","The single was a major chart hit and confirmed Foxy Brown as one of the leading women rappers of the late 1990s. Critics at the time often paired her with Lil' Kim as rivals; later writers have tended to stress her technical skill as a rapper, sometimes overshadowed by her image and by later legal troubles.","Play René and Angela's original if you can, then this, to hear the sample transformed. Listen to how quickly Foxy packs rhymes into each line. Compare with Jay-Z's Reasonable Doubt to hear the shared, cool Brooklyn style, and then with Cardi B's Bodak Yellow for a later woman rapper's swagger."],"eve-let-there-be-eve":["Eve's debut is a hard, bright record of synthesiser stabs, booming programmed drums and the clipped, futuristic keyboard sounds of the Ruff Ryders camp. Her voice is raspy, tough and quick, with a cutting edge that never sounds strained. The tracks move from battle records to party songs to serious stories, and the album is long, in the manner of its time, with skits and guest verses from her Ruff Ryders crewmates, DMX among them.","Eve Jeffers grew up in Philadelphia and briefly signed to Dr. Dre's Aftermath label before joining the Ruff Ryders, the Yonkers crew around DMX and the producer Swizz Beatz, who supplied much of the album's sound. Released in 1999, it debuted at number one on the Billboard 200, a rare feat for a woman rapper, and made her the crew's figurehead in a very male world.","Listen to the production: Swizz Beatz built many of these beats on cheap-sounding keyboard presets, played in short, stuttering patterns, a deliberately plastic, synthetic sound that set Ruff Ryders apart from the sample-based smoothness of Bad Boy. Notice how Eve's raspy, staccato delivery matches those rhythms, a voice designed for the beats rather than layered on top of them.","The album was a commercial success and won good reviews for Eve's toughness and storytelling. Some critics found it overlong, typical of late-1990s rap albums, and her later records are often thought more polished. It remains her landmark: proof that a woman could lead a hardcore crew and top the charts without softening her sound.","Start with the singles and listen for the stuttering keyboard patterns and how Eve's voice locks into them. Compare the sound with the sample-heavy smoothness of the Notorious B.I.G.'s Life After Death to hear two rival New York production styles. Then play Let Me Blow Ya Mind to hear where she went next."],"eve-let-me-blow-ya-mind":["Let Me Blow Ya Mind is a cool, confident song about self-belief and staying power, built on a slinky, minimal beat: a loping bass line, crisp drums and a few sharp keyboard notes. Eve raps with relaxed swagger, and Gwen Stefani of No Doubt sings the hook in a light, airy voice. The space in the production is the point; it is a record that sounds expensive by leaving almost everything out.","It was the lead single from Eve's second album, Scorpion, released in 2001. The beat came from Dr. Dre and Scott Storch, the team behind Dre's 2001 album, and the pairing with Stefani was an early, high-profile example of a rap and rock-pop crossover duet. The song won the first Grammy Award for Best Rap/Sung Collaboration.","Listen to how little is happening: the bass line, a skeletal drum pattern and Storch's keyboard figure repeat almost unchanged, a trademark of Dre's early-2000s style, where restraint makes every sound feel heavy. Notice how Stefani's hook floats against that grid rather than locking into it; the contrast between rapper and singer is built into the rhythm.","It was a huge international hit and Eve's biggest single. Critics praised the production and the chemistry between the two artists. Some hip-hop purists regarded the pop crossover with suspicion, but it is now seen as a well-made example of rap's assimilation into the mainstream, and as one of the defining sounds of 2001 radio.","Listen to the bass line and the gaps in the beat first, then follow Eve's flow and Stefani's floating hook. Compare it with Dr. Dre's Still D.R.E. to hear the same production fingerprints. Then play Missy Elliott's Get Ur Freak On, from the same year, to hear a very different idea of a futurist rap single."],"da-brat-funkdafied":["Funkdafied is a laid-back party record built on a slow, silky R&B groove and a rolling, head-nodding beat. Da Brat raps in a rapid-fire, bouncing flow, tumbling syllables across the bar lines with the agility of the Midwest's quick rappers, while the beat stays cool and unhurried underneath. The chorus is a soft, sung refrain. The contrast between the relaxed groove and her speed is the record's charm.","Shawntae Harris grew up in Chicago and was discovered by Jermaine Dupri after winning a rap contest. Dupri's Atlanta label So So Def released the single and the album of the same name in 1994. The album became the first by a solo woman rapper to be certified platinum, a commercial milestone in the era of G-funk and East Coast hardcore.","Listen to the groove: it is built from the Isley Brothers' 1983 slow jam Between the Sheets, the same source the Notorious B.I.G. used on Big Poppa around the same time. Hearing both side by side is a lesson in how two producers can take the same sample in very different directions. Notice too the G-funk influence in the slow tempo and the warm synthesiser tone.","The single and album were big commercial successes. Critics were divided: some saw Da Brat as Dupri's creation, a Snoop Dogg imitation; others heard a nimble, original rapper with Chicago roots. Her platinum milestone is now treated as a landmark, and her quick, playful flow has been widely acknowledged as influential.","Play the Isley Brothers' Between the Sheets first, then Funkdafied, then Big Poppa, to hear one sample in three moods. Then listen to Da Brat's flow on its own, counting how many syllables she fits into each line. Compare it with Dr. Dre's Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang to hear the G-funk model."],"nicki-minaj-pink-friday":["Nicki Minaj's debut album moves between hard rap, glossy pop and R&B ballads, built on bright synthesisers and booming drum programming. Her voice is the main event: she switches between characters, accents and pitches, from a growling alter ego to a sweet sung tone, sometimes within a single verse. Songs like Roman's Revenge show the fierce rapper; Moment 4 Life and Your Love show the pop singer.","Onika Maraj was born in Trinidad and raised in Queens, New York. Her mixtapes and a show-stealing verse on Kanye West's Monster in 2010 made her a phenomenon before the album appeared. Pink Friday came out on Lil Wayne's Young Money label in November 2010 as streaming and social media were changing how pop stars were made.","Listen to the voices: Minaj built distinct characters, including the snarling Roman Zolanski and the softer Harajuku Barbie, each with its own accent and pitch, which she uses like stage roles. Notice too how the album was built to work in two markets at once, pop radio and rap, a deliberate strategy that most labels had believed was impossible for a woman rapper.","It was a major commercial success. Critics praised her rapping and inventiveness, while some felt the pop tracks diluted her talent. Over time it has become clear that the album rewrote the rules for women in rap, opening the commercial road for a whole generation, from Cardi B to Doja Cat.","Start with Roman's Revenge for the rapper, then Moment 4 Life for the pop star, and listen to how differently she uses her voice. Compare with her verse on Kanye West's Monster, which explains the hype. Then play Lil Wayne's Tha Carter III to hear the Young Money sound she came out of."],"nicki-minaj-super-bass":["Super Bass is a bubbly, teen-pop-flavoured song about a crush, with a booming bass line, bright synthesiser chords and a sung chorus that sounds like a playground chant. Nicki Minaj raps the verses in a quick, playful, exaggerated voice, full of comic emphasis and sudden shifts of pitch, then switches to a sweet sung hook. It is three minutes of sugar and rhythm, and it was made for singing along.","It first appeared as a bonus track on the deluxe edition of Pink Friday and became a hit as a single in 2011, helped by viral videos of fans, including children, rapping along. The production came from Kane Beatz. It arrived as Minaj was becoming a household name and as electronic pop dominated radio.","Listen to the bass line underneath the chorus: it bounces in the same register as the melody, a trick borrowed from electronic dance pop that makes the song feel heavier than its light surface. Notice how Minaj changes her voice from line to line in the verses, almost like a cartoonist drawing different faces, a technique that became her signature.","The single became one of the biggest hits of 2011 and a top-ten pop single in America. Critics praised its charm and craft, while some rap fans saw it as a pop concession. It is now seen as the record that made her truly mainstream and as an influential example of rap-pop blending.","Listen to the bass line first, then follow how Minaj changes her voice between lines. Compare with Lil' Kim's Crush on You to hear a playful earlier take on a crush song. Then play Cardi B's Bodak Yellow to hear a very different, harder commercial breakthrough."],"cardi-b-bodak-yellow":["Bodak Yellow is a brash song of self-made success, built on a dark, menacing trap beat: deep 808 bass, skittering hi-hats and a sinister synthesiser line. Cardi B raps with a hard-edged Bronx accent and a rolling, clipped flow, delivering every line with comic bite and total conviction. The beat is minimal and ominous; the personality on top of it is enormous and very funny.","Belcalis Almánzar grew up in the Bronx and became famous through social media and reality television before signing to Atlantic. Released in June 2017 and produced by J. White Did It, Bodak Yellow reached number one on the Billboard Hot 100, making her the first solo woman rapper to top the chart since Lauryn Hill in 1998.","Listen to the flow: it is modelled on Kodak Black's 2014 track No Flockin', and the title nods to him. Cardi B takes his slurred, rolling cadence and sharpens it into something brisk and percussive. Notice too how the beat barely changes, putting all the weight on the voice; it is a trap track run as a one-woman show.","It was a major hit and a cultural event, and earned Grammy nominations. Critics praised its energy and Cardi B's charisma; some questioned whether a reality star could last, a question her later career answered. It is now seen as a landmark for women in trap and one of the defining singles of the late 2010s.","Listen to the 808 bass and the eerie synthesiser first, then follow the rhythm of the flow, which sits tight on the beat. Compare with Gucci Mane's Trap House to hear the roots of the sound. Then play Lauryn Hill's Doo Wop (That Thing) to hear the last record by a solo woman rapper to reach the same summit."],"cardi-b-invasion-of-privacy":["Cardi B's debut album ranges from hard trap to Latin boogaloo to R&B, held together by her brash, funny, emotionally open voice. Beats include dark, heavy trap, glossy pop, and on I Like It a bright Latin groove with guests Bad Bunny and J Balvin. Songs like Be Careful show a vulnerable side; others are pure swagger. It is a record that wants to show every side of its maker.","It came out on Atlantic in April 2018, months after Bodak Yellow's success, as streaming made rap the most popular genre in America. Cardi B's Dominican and Trinidadian heritage informed the record's Latin turns, and her openness about her life made her a celebrity far beyond rap. It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, the first solo woman to win the award.","Listen to I Like It: it is built on Pete Rodriguez's 1967 boogaloo I Like It Like That, a Latin soul record from New York's Puerto Rican scene, making the song a bridge between Bronx histories fifty years apart. Notice too how carefully the album is sequenced, alternating hard and soft tracks so no mood lasts too long.","The album was a commercial and critical success, landing on many year-end lists, and its Grammy win was a milestone. Some critics questioned how much of the writing was collaborative; others argued that collaboration is normal in pop. It is now seen as a defining rap album of the late 2010s.","Start with I Like It, then Be Careful, to hear the range. Listen to how her voice shifts from swaggering to vulnerable without losing its character. Compare with Bad Bunny's X 100pre to hear the Latin trap world she drew on, and Nicki Minaj's Pink Friday for an earlier crossover."],"rapsody-lailas-wisdom":["Laila's Wisdom is warm, soulful and dense with words. The beats, mostly by 9th Wonder and his Soul Council producers, are built from soul and gospel samples, chopped and layered with live instruments, giving the record a rich, vintage sound. Rapsody's voice is clear, measured and conversational, and her verses pack in references and internal rhymes. Guests include Kendrick Lamar, Busta Rhymes and Anderson .Paak.","Marlanna Evans grew up in rural North Carolina and came up through 9th Wonder's Jamla label. Her guest verse on Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly in 2015 brought her wider attention. Laila's Wisdom, named after her grandmother, was released in 2017 through Jamla and Roc Nation and was nominated for the Grammy for Best Rap Album.","Listen to the title track: it draws on a sample of Aretha Franklin, a deliberate link to soul's tradition and to a family elder's guidance. The album is shaped around wisdom passed between generations, and the samples act like the voices of older relatives. Notice too how Rapsody's lyrical density rewards repeated listening; much passes on first hearing.","The album received widespread critical praise and its Grammy nomination brought Rapsody wider recognition. Some listeners found it demanding, dense and low on hit singles. Critics often compare her with MC Lyte and Lauryn Hill, and the record is now seen as one of the strongest conscious rap albums of its decade.","Start with the title track and listen to the soul sample. Then pick a single verse and follow it closely; Rapsody rewards attention. Compare with Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly to hear the shared jazz and soul language, and with Lauryn Hill's Miseducation for a forerunner."],"doechii-alligator-bites-never-heal":["Doechii's mixtape moves restlessly between styles: hard boom bap, jazz-house, Miami bass, R&B and theatrical spoken passages. Her voice is the thread: elastic, dramatic, capable of a dense rap flow one moment and a sung melody the next. Tracks like Denial Is a River turn therapy-session confession into rapid-fire comedy; others are pure club. It sounds like a performer showing off her whole range.","Jaylah Hickmon grew up in Tampa, Florida, and built a following through viral songs before signing with Top Dawg Entertainment, Kendrick Lamar's former label. Released in August 2024, the mixtape won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, making her only the third woman to win it, after Lauryn Hill as a member of the Fugees and Cardi B.","Listen to the Florida roots: the bass-heavy grooves and swamp imagery draw on Miami bass and the culture of the Gulf Coast, the alligator of the title perhaps an emblem of home. Notice too how she uses skits and spoken interludes to build characters, a tradition from 1990s rap albums that she revives with a theatre performer's timing.","The mixtape won widespread critical praise and its Grammy win was all the more notable because it was released as a mixtape. Critics have highlighted her versatility and theatricality; some find the stylistic leaps restless. It has been widely seen as a breakthrough and a sign of hip-hop's continuing openness to experiment.","Start with Denial Is a River and listen to how quickly she switches tone. Then sample the house and boom bap tracks to hear her range. Compare with Missy Elliott's Supa Dupa Fly, a forerunner in playful experiment, and with Tyler, the Creator's Igor for another restless genre-blender."],"tierra-whack-whack-world":["Whack World is fifteen songs, each exactly one minute long, in fifteen minutes. Each is a sketch with its own sound: a country-tinged ballad, a squeaky trap song, a spooky lullaby, a funky dance track. Tierra Whack changes voices constantly, from a childlike squeak to a deep growl, and the one-minute limit gives the record the feel of a sketchbook or a run of short films, full of ideas that never outstay their welcome.","Tierra Whack grew up in Philadelphia and rapped from childhood. Whack World was released in May 2018 with a fifteen-minute film made up of a separate surreal video for each song. In an era of short attention spans and Instagram clips, the format seemed both a joke and a canny response to how people now listened.","Listen for how carefully each song is built to end at exactly sixty seconds; many feel complete, with intro, verse and hook compressed into a minute. Notice too how the visuals and the songs were conceived together; the film is not a promotional extra but part of the work, and the colour schemes and props match each track's mood.","Critics loved its inventiveness and it appeared on many year-end lists. Some wondered whether the format was a gimmick; others saw it as a bold art statement. It is now seen as a landmark of experimental rap in the streaming era and established Tierra Whack as one of the most original artists of her generation.","Watch the film first, all fifteen minutes in one sitting. Then listen to the songs without pictures and notice how much each manages in a minute. Compare with Madlib's short beat sketches and Tyler, the Creator's Flower Boy for other playful, colourful visions."],"ms-dynamite-booo":["Booo! is a UK garage track credited to the producer Sticky featuring Ms. Dynamite, built on a dark, stuttering two-step beat, a deep wobbling sub-bass and sparse synthesiser stabs. Ms. Dynamite's voice is quick and confident, switching between rapping in a London accent and singing. The track is bare and menacing; the space between its beats makes the bass hit harder and gives the voice room to dominate.","Niomi McLean-Daley grew up in north London, and Booo! was her breakthrough in 2001, as UK garage was turning darker and more MC-led, on the path towards grime. It was an underground hit on pirate radio before crossing to the charts. Her rise from the garage scene to a major-label career was unusual for a woman MC.","Listen to the beat: Sticky's production leaves out the swing and bounce of earlier garage, replacing it with a stark, clipped pattern and dark bass, a step towards the sound of grime that Wiley and others developed in the following years. Notice too how Ms. Dynamite's mix of singing and MCing anticipated later UK artists.","It was a club and pirate-radio hit and launched her career. Critics have since seen it as a key record in the transition from UK garage to grime, and as evidence of how women MCs shaped that sound. It is sometimes overshadowed by her later Mercury win, but scene historians rate it highly.","Listen to the space in the beat and the depth of the bass; it is a track to play loud. Compare with Artful Dodger's Re-Rewind for an earlier, lighter garage sound, and Wiley's Eskimo for the darker grime sound that followed."],"ms-dynamite-a-little-deeper":["Ms. Dynamite's debut album blends hip-hop, R&B, reggae and garage into a warm, socially conscious record. Her voice moves easily between rapping and singing, and the production is lush, with live instruments, soulful samples and reggae rhythms. Songs like It Takes More criticise celebrity materialism; Dy-Na-Mi-Tee is a bright, upbeat single. It feels like a personal and political statement from a young Londoner.","Released in 2002 on Polydor, the album won the Mercury Prize that year, and Ms. Dynamite also won BRIT Awards. Her Jamaican and Scottish heritage and her north London upbringing shaped the record's mix of styles. It arrived as British rap and R&B were growing in confidence, and she was among the first British women to break through at that level.","Listen to the reggae influence: several tracks draw on Jamaican rhythms and the chatting style of dancehall, a link to her family's heritage and to the long history of reggae in London. Notice too how much of the album is about responsibility, community and self-respect, themes that set it apart from the party-focused garage scene she came from.","The album won widespread acclaim and the Mercury Prize, though some critics thought its message heavy-handed. Her career afterwards was less commercially prominent, and the album became something of a cult favourite. It is now seen as a pioneering British record that opened the door for later artists, from Little Simz to Stormzy.","Start with Dy-Na-Mi-Tee and It Takes More to hear the range. Listen to how easily she moves between rap and song. Compare with Lauryn Hill's Miseducation, an obvious influence, and with Little Simz's Grey Area to hear the line of British women rappers that followed."],"leikeli47-acrylic":["Acrylic is a bright, punchy record of bouncing beats, chanting hooks and loud, colourful production, drawing on Baltimore club, Jersey club, trap and R&B. Leikeli47's voice is raspy and playful, switching between quick rap and soft singing. The album is conceived as a visit to a beauty salon, and its songs feel like chatter, gossip and celebration among friends. Energy and humour run throughout.","Leikeli47 is a Brooklyn-based artist who performs in a mask to keep attention on her music rather than her image. Acrylic, released in 2018 on RCA, was the second in a trilogy of albums themed around Black beauty culture, following Wash & Set (2017) and preceding Shape Up (2022). It celebrates the salon as a community space for Black women.","Listen to the club rhythms: several tracks draw on the frantic, chopped beats of Baltimore and Jersey club, regional dance styles rarely heard on rap albums. Notice too the trilogy's titles, each named after a beauty service, so the albums form a kind of concept cycle about grooming, self-care and the communities built around them.","Critics praised the album's energy and originality, and her masked persona drew curiosity. She remains less widely known than many peers, which some writers see as a deliberate choice. Acrylic is often cited as one of the most underrated rap albums of its year and as a celebration of Black women's everyday culture.","Start anywhere and let the energy carry you; then listen for the club rhythms under the rap. Compare with Missy Elliott's Under Construction for a similar spirit of play, and with Megan Thee Stallion's Fever for another celebration of Southern and Black women's culture."],"sylvester-you-make-me-feel-mighty-real":["A song about the dizzy joy of being with someone who makes you feel completely alive, carried on a sequenced synthesizer pulse that never stops. Sylvester's falsetto soars over a churning electronic bass, crisp handclaps and a four-on-the-floor kick drum, while Martha Wash and Izora Rhodes, later the Weather Girls, answer him in gospel-trained harmony. The long build, the shimmering synth arpeggios and that unbroken rhythmic engine make it sound like a gospel shout performed by machines.","Recorded in San Francisco for Fantasy Records and released on the album Step II, the track was produced by Harvey Fuqua, a veteran of the Moonglows and of Motown. Sylvester, an openly gay Black singer raised in the Pentecostal church in Los Angeles, had moved north to the Castro, where gay liberation and the disco boom were meeting on the same dance floors. The year before, Donna Summer and Giorgio Moroder had shown what a fully electronic dance record could do.","The electronic architecture came largely from Patrick Cowley, a lighting technician turned synthesist who had heard the band's demo and offered to add his sequencers. His contribution turned a fairly conventional disco arrangement into something close to the later Hi-NRG sound. Listen for how the synth bass ticks in steady eighth notes against the kick, a pattern that would become the default setting for gay club music through the 1980s.","It was a hit on both sides of the Atlantic and made Sylvester an unlikely star of mainstream television. Rock critics who disliked disco often dismissed it, but its reputation grew steadily, and it is now regarded as a founding record of Hi-NRG and a landmark of queer visibility in popular music. Jimmy Somerville's 1990 cover confirmed its status as a gay anthem for a second generation.","Start by listening only to the bass synthesizer: notice that it never rests, and that everything else floats above it. Then follow Sylvester's voice as it climbs from conversational to ecstatic. Play it next to Donna Summer's I Feel Love to hear two different answers to the same idea, one cool and Bavarian, one hot and churchy."],"sylvester-do-you-wanna-funk":["A playful invitation to the dance floor, built almost entirely from Patrick Cowley's synthesizers. A fast, rubbery sequenced bass line gallops underneath, drum-machine handclaps snap on the off-beats, and bright synth stabs punctuate Sylvester's vocal, which moves between a teasing low register and his trademark falsetto. The track runs at a brisk tempo with very little harmonic movement, relying on texture, repetition and sudden drop-outs to keep the energy climbing.","Written by Cowley and Sylvester and released on Megatone, the San Francisco label Cowley had co-founded, the record was made while Cowley was seriously ill with what was then a barely understood new disease. He died in November 1982, one of the first well-known musicians lost to AIDS. The song's frantic exuberance sits against a community that was beginning to see its friends fall sick.","Most of the sounds were played and programmed by Cowley in a small home setup, which is why the track has such a dry, close, almost toy-like electronic sheen compared with the strings-and-horns disco of a few years earlier. A year later the record turned up in the film Trading Places, carrying the San Francisco underground sound into a mainstream Hollywood comedy.","On release it was a club staple and a hit in several European countries, though American radio largely ignored it in the wake of the disco backlash. Later writers have treated it as one of the defining Hi-NRG records, and as a memorial, whether intended or not, to a creative partnership that ended too soon.","Turn it up and listen to how little is going on harmonically: the whole track lives on one bass riff. Notice the breakdowns, where the bass drops out and the drums carry on alone before the riff slams back. Then hear Cowley's own Menergy for the instrumental version of the same aesthetic."],"patrick-cowley-menergy":["A celebration of the crowded gay clubs of San Francisco, set to a relentless machine pulse. The track is a long, fast instrumental with occasional chanted vocals: a thick sequenced bass line in rapid octaves, layered synth melodies that interlock like gears, and a hard electronic kick on every beat. Over nine minutes Cowley adds and subtracts layers, letting a single riff mutate gradually rather than building verses and choruses.","Released in 1981 on Fusion Records, Menergy was Cowley's breakthrough as a solo artist after years as Sylvester's synthesist. The disco backlash had driven much of the genre off American radio, but in gay clubs the music kept evolving, becoming faster, harder and more electronic. Menergy captured that underground at its most confident, in the last moment before AIDS changed everything for the community it was made for.","Cowley built his tracks from analogue synthesizers he knew intimately from years at the City College of San Francisco electronic music studio, and you can hear a composer's attention to timbre in the way the synth voices are filtered and opened up over time. The bass pattern, octave jumps at high speed, became one of the signatures of the whole Hi-NRG style that followed.","Club audiences made it a hit on the dance charts, while the mainstream press barely noticed. Its standing has grown with each reissue, and later electronic producers, from Italo and techno to the 2000s electro revival, have cited Cowley as a forerunner whose work was cut short just as it was gathering force.","Listen for how long Cowley lets one idea run before changing anything, and how small each change is. Compare the bass line with Moroder's work for Donna Summer to hear how Cowley sped it up and hardened it. Play it loud; it was made for big club speakers."],"patrick-cowley-mind-warp":["Cowley's final album: a set of long, propulsive synth tracks that sound darker and stranger than his earlier work. The sequenced bass lines are still there, racing in octaves, but the harmonies turn minor, the synth textures grow colder, and the vocals feel more distant and processed. The title track and its companions run long, built on repetition and slow transformation, with sudden electronic sweeps cutting through the groove like interference on a radio.","Cowley recorded the album in 1982 while he was gravely ill, and it was released by Megatone around the time of his death that November. San Francisco's gay community was beginning to understand the scale of the epidemic. The music still serves the dance floor, but many listeners have heard in its unease the sound of a scene shadowed by loss.","Listen for the way the title track seems to slip in and out of focus, with filters opening and closing on the synths as though the music were being heard through fever. Several tracks suggest Cowley's background in experimental electronic music, with passages that could sit on an avant-garde tape album rather than a club record.","On release it was largely overshadowed by Cowley's death. In later decades, as labels reissued his work and his unreleased experimental recordings, critics began to treat Mind Warp as the most personal statement of a major electronic musician whose career lasted only a few years.","Hear it straight after Menergy to notice how much darker the palette has become. Listen for the moments where the beat drops away and only the swirling synths remain. Then go back to Sylvester's records and hear Cowley's hand behind them with fresh ears."],"grace-jones-la-vie-en-rose":["Édith Piaf's song about seeing the world through the haze of love, slowed into a languid bossa-nova disco groove. Grace Jones sings in a cool, slightly theatrical contralto, moving between English and French, while a gentle Latin rhythm, soft strings and a rippling guitar unfurl for seven unhurried minutes. Where most disco charges forward, this one sways, keeping the tempo low and letting the strings swell slowly around her voice.","The track appeared on Portfolio, Jones's debut album for Island Records, produced by Tom Moulton, the mixer who had more or less invented the extended disco remix. Jones was a Jamaican-born model working in Paris and New York, already a fixture at Studio 54 and in gay clubs, and Island packaged her as a disco diva. The record became a hit in parts of Europe.","Moulton's real art was length and pacing: listen for how patiently the arrangement withholds its climax, adding a layer every minute or so rather than repeating a hook. Jones's slightly flat, deadpan delivery, which some heard as a weakness, is the first sign of the cool, androgynous persona she would perfect a few years later at Compass Point.","Contemporary reviewers treated Jones mainly as a model trying to sing, and her disco period was long dismissed by critics who preferred the reggae-new wave albums that followed. More recent writing hears it as the moment she found her voice as an interpreter who could make any song strange, and the track remains one of the best-loved of her early records.","Listen to Piaf's original first, then to this, and notice how the slower tempo and the Latin rhythm change the song's mood from wistful to seductive. Focus on the strings as they gradually thicken. Then jump to Nightclubbing to hear how quickly Jones left disco behind."],"grace-jones-nightclubbing":["Nine songs, most of them covers, played by a reggae rhythm section with new wave coolness and sung in a voice that is half chant, half sneer. The Compass Point All Stars, led by Sly Dunbar on drums and Robbie Shakespeare on bass, lay down tight, spacious grooves with dub-like gaps. Jones turns songs by Iggy Pop and David Bowie, Bill Withers, Sting and Astor Piazzolla into her own icy, commanding performances, alongside originals such as Pull Up to the Bumper.","Recorded at Compass Point Studios in Nassau, Bahamas, Island Records founder Chris Blackwell's studio, with Blackwell and Alex Sadkin producing. After three disco albums, Jones and Blackwell had remade her the year before on Warm Leatherette, aiming for a fusion of Jamaican rhythm and post-punk attitude. Her image, sculpted by the French designer Jean-Paul Goude, was as much a part of the project as the music.","I've Seen That Face Before is Piazzolla's Libertango with new words, and the tango survives inside the reggae groove: listen for the accordion-like melody line and the insistent three-plus-three-plus-two rhythm. Use Me, Bill Withers's sly funk song, is slowed and stripped until it sounds almost menacing. The title track reworks Iggy Pop's dirge into something colder still.","Critics who had ignored her disco records took notice, and the album was widely named among the best of 1981. Its standing has only grown: it is now seen as a hinge between disco, reggae, new wave and the art-pop of the decade to come, and Jones's androgynous presentation as a precedent for later performers who treat image as part of the music.","Start with I've Seen That Face Before, then play Piazzolla's Libertango and hear the bones of it. Listen to how much space Sly and Robbie leave between beats. Then put on Pull Up to the Bumper loud and notice the bass carrying the whole song."],"grace-jones-slave-to-the-rhythm":["A whole album built from one song. Trevor Horn and his team take the title track apart and reassemble it eight different ways: as go-go funk with clattering percussion, as orchestral pop, as a ballad, as a collage of fragments. Between and over the music come spoken passages and interview recordings about Jones's life and image. The result plays like a documentary portrait in sound, held together by the same chord sequence and bass figure.","Released on ZTT, the London label Horn ran with journalist Paul Morley, at the height of their studio-as-instrument ambitions after Frankie Goes to Hollywood. The song had reportedly been intended for Frankie before it went to Jones. Horn's team used the Fairlight sampler and the Synclavier, which allowed sounds to be captured and rearranged with a freedom that had been impossible a few years earlier.","The spoken readings come from Jean-Paul Goude's book about Jones and his other work, read aloud by the actor Ian McShane, so the album is partly about the making of her image by a man who styled her. Listen for the moment in the title track where the go-go drums drop out and a full orchestra takes over: it is the same song, wearing a different body.","Reviewers were divided between admiration for the production and suspicion that Jones herself was a guest on her own record. The title track was a substantial hit. Later criticism tends to read the album as a deliberate meditation on authorship and image, and as one of the most ambitious productions of the 1980s.","Play the single version first so you know the song, then the whole album straight through, listening for how each section reshapes the same material. Notice the Washington go-go rhythm under the opening. Compare with Nightclubbing to hear how producer-led the project has become."],"cerrone-love-in-c-minor":["A sixteen-minute side-long suite about desire and seduction, built on Cerrone's own drumming. After a spoken introduction, a hard, steady four-on-the-floor beat locks in, and the track unfolds in long sections over a minor-key groove: sweeping strings, stabbing brass, chanted female backing vocals and a funky bass line. Breakdowns strip the music to drums alone before the full orchestra crashes back in, a structure designed entirely for the dance floor.","Cerrone, a French drummer who had played in rock and soul bands, made the record in 1976 and initially released it himself in France. It found its way to New York record shops and clubs as an import, where DJs embraced it, and Atlantic's Cotillion label picked it up for the American market. European producers were starting to treat disco as a studio art in its own right, independent of soul singers.","The drums are mixed unusually loud and dry for the time, with the kick right at the front: Cerrone was a drummer making a drummer's record, and that clarity anticipated the way later dance music would put the beat first. Listen for how the strings play long, held chords rather than the busy figures of Philadelphia disco, giving the track a colder, more European feel.","Its suggestive cover and spoken intro brought controversy, but club audiences in America made it a major disco record. Critics often grouped Cerrone with Moroder as a Eurodisco producer of the long-form, studio-built track, though he has received less attention. Dance historians now credit it as one of the first side-long European disco suites.","Listen through the whole sixteen minutes rather than an edit: the length is the point. Follow the drums and notice where they become the only instrument. Compare with Donna Summer's Love to Love You Baby, made around the same time, to hear two European approaches to the long disco track."],"cerrone-supernature":["A science-fiction parable about chemical experiments that breed monstrous creatures who turn on humankind, set to throbbing synthesizers. A low, pulsing synth bass and a slow, heavy drumbeat create a mood of creeping menace; eerie electronic swoops and treated voices drift over the top. It is slower and darker than most disco, closer to a horror soundtrack in places, with a hypnotic, almost marching pulse.","The title track of Cerrone's third album, released in 1977, the same year as Donna Summer's I Feel Love, when synthesizers were moving from progressive rock into dance music. The lyrics were written by Lene Lovich, then unknown, who would soon become a new wave star in her own right. Ecological anxiety, from pesticides to pollution, was part of the decade's mood, and the song channels it.","The words are an environmental warning, not a love song, which made it unusual on the dance floor; listen past the groove and the story is of nature taking revenge. The bass synth line is close in spirit to what electro and early techno producers would build on a few years later.","It became a hit in Europe and an American club favourite, and is generally regarded as Cerrone's finest track. Critics who dismissed Eurodisco as mechanical have since been answered by a long line of electronic musicians who cite it, and its ominous mood is now heard as a bridge between disco and later electronic dance music.","Notice how slow the tempo is compared with other disco of the time, and how the menace comes from the synth bass rather than the melody. Play it next to I Feel Love: two synthesizer disco records from the same year, one ecstatic, one ominous."],"arthur-russell-kiss-me-again":["A longing song about wanting a lover to return, set to loose, ragged disco funk. Released under the name Dinosaur, it has a looser, more improvised feel than polished commercial disco: guitars scratch and wander, bass and drums keep a steady but slightly unruly groove, and Myriam Valle's vocal floats over a texture that keeps shifting. Cello lines and odd instrumental colours surface and vanish, so the track feels more like a jam than a finished arrangement.","Arthur Russell was a classically trained cellist from Iowa who had become music director of the Kitchen, the downtown New York experimental space, and moved among composers such as Philip Glass. Like many in that scene he was also going to clubs such as the Gallery, where Nicky Siano played. Kiss Me Again, co-produced with Siano, was released by Sire Records as the label's first disco twelve-inch.","David Byrne of Talking Heads is reported to have played guitar on the session, a sign of how closely downtown art-rock and the disco underground mixed in 1978 New York. Listen for the way the arrangement refuses to settle: instruments drop in and out not on the usual eight-bar cues but at odd moments, a habit that comes from Russell's experimental music background.","It was a modest club success, and Russell disliked the way the label edited his material. For years the record was mainly known to collectors. As his reputation grew after his death, Kiss Me Again was reissued and recognised as the first sign of his idea that disco and avant-garde composition could share the same space.","Listen to it as a jam rather than a song: follow one instrument at a time and notice when it disappears. Compare the slick sound of Chic from the same year to hear how deliberately loose this is. Then move on to Is It All Over My Face."],"arthur-russell-is-it-all-over-my-face":["A teasing song about whether the excitement of falling for someone shows on your face, released under the name Loose Joints. The groove is skeletal: a fat, wandering bass line, choppy guitar, clanking percussion and a beat that feels played rather than programmed, with a group of voices chanting more than singing. Everything sounds slightly off-balance and homemade, yet the rhythm is so insistent that it pulls the body forward from the first bar.","Russell made the record with producer Steve D'Acquisto and a loose crowd of musicians and friends; it came out on West End Records in New York. It became a fixture at the Paradise Garage, where Larry Levan was turning his DJ booth into a laboratory for strange new dance records, and his remix became the version that clubs played.","Levan's female vocal mix replaced the original male chorus with a woman's voice and rebuilt the arrangement, turning a quirky curio into a club weapon. Listen for the long stretches where only bass and percussion play; that stripped-down, raw approach is one of the direct ancestors of Chicago house, where Ron Hardy and others played it heavily.","At first it was an underground hit known almost only to New York and Chicago dance floors. Later critics and DJs have named it one of the essential records of mutant disco, the space between disco, funk and the avant-garde, and a key link between the Paradise Garage and the birth of house music.","Seek out both the male and female vocal versions and compare what Levan changed. Listen to the bass line, which seems to wander but never loses the beat. Then play Larry Levan's other Garage mixes and any early Chicago house record to hear the family resemblance."],"arthur-russell-go-bang":["A joyful track about losing yourself in the moment, credited to Dinosaur L. Over a loping, slightly lopsided groove, a chorus of voices chants and sings, a trombone blurts, keyboards ripple and the rhythm section plays with jazz-like looseness. The track feels like a band of composers improvising on a dance beat, with sudden changes in texture and an ecstatic, communal mood that makes it sound bigger than its small forces.","Go Bang! comes from the album 24→24 Music, released in 1981 on Sleeping Bag Records, the label Russell co-founded with Will Socolov. The players came from the downtown New York new music scene, including the composer and singer Julius Eastman and the trombonist Peter Zummo. The following year François Kevorkian remixed it into the version that became a club standard.","The album's tracks are numbered rather than named in the usual way, as if they were sections of a composition, and that is how Russell thought of them: as minimalist process music that happened to be danceable. Listen for Eastman's voice in the ensemble, a link to his own pulsing works such as Stay On It, which sit on the same border between new music and dance.","Kevorkian's remix made it a hit at the Paradise Garage and beyond. Critics have come to treat it as Russell's defining dance record and a key text in the argument that disco was as experimental as any music of its time. It has been sampled and covered widely.","Hear the Kevorkian remix first, then the original album version to notice what he tightened. Listen for the trombone and for the moments where the groove almost falls apart and recovers. Pair it with Julius Eastman's Stay On It or Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians."],"arthur-russell-world-of-echo":["Arthur Russell alone with his cello, his voice and a bank of echo effects. Short songs and fragments bleed into one another: the cello is bowed, plucked and strummed like a guitar, often amplified and drenched in delay so that each note blooms into a cloud, while Russell murmurs or croons above in a soft, unplaceable voice. There are no drums, yet many tracks have a pulse, as if the dance music had dissolved and only its ghost remained.","Released in 1986 on Upside Records, the album came from years of solo performances and studio experiments in New York, made alongside his dance records rather than instead of them. Russell was by then known to clubs as a producer and to new music audiences as a cellist and composer, but this record belonged to neither world, and it sold poorly.","Many of the songs exist in other versions on Russell's dance records and in his vast archive of unfinished tapes; World of Echo is in a sense the same music heard from the inside. Listen for the way the echo becomes a second instrument, answering the cello with delays timed to create rhythms that are not actually being played.","It was largely ignored on release. After Russell's death in 1992, reissues and the 2004 compilation of his songs brought a rush of reappraisal, and World of Echo is now widely regarded as one of the most original solo albums of its decade, an influence on ambient, folk and electronic artists alike.","Listen on headphones in a quiet room. Do not wait for songs to start and stop; let the album run as one long piece. Notice the cello's percussive strumming. Then play Go Bang! and hear how the same person made both."],"gloria-gaynor-never-can-say-goodbye":["Gloria Gaynor's breakthrough album, whose first side is a single continuous dance sequence: Honey Bee, Never Can Say Goodbye and Reach Out, I'll Be There run into one another without a break, propelled by bright strings, punchy horns, a galloping hi-hat and Gaynor's strong, clear voice. The second side holds conventional songs. The opening side is what people remember, a nonstop ride designed to keep a dance floor moving for nearly twenty minutes.","Released in 1975 on MGM, the album caught disco at the moment it was becoming a mainstream genre. The title song had been a hit for the Jackson 5 a few years earlier, written by Clifton Davis; Reach Out was a Four Tops classic. Disco was still a club phenomenon driven by DJs, and record companies were only beginning to learn how to make records specifically for them.","The continuous first side was mixed by Tom Moulton, who had been making tape edits to extend songs for club play and here brought that technique onto a commercial album. Listen for the joins between songs: they are planned like DJ mixes, with tempos matched and percussion carrying you across the gap. It was one of the first albums designed around what DJs actually did.","Club DJs and listeners embraced it, and the title track became a hit in America and Britain, establishing Gaynor as one of disco's first stars. Critics treat it as a milestone in the history of the extended mix, though Gaynor's own later fame rests more on a single song from a few years later.","Play side one straight through without stopping, as intended. Listen for the moments where one song gives way to the next. Then compare the Jackson 5's original Never Can Say Goodbye to hear how the disco arrangement transforms a gentle ballad."],"gloria-gaynor-i-will-survive":["A song about a woman who has been abandoned and discovers her own strength. It opens with a slow, almost rubato piano and voice introduction before the beat crashes in: a driving disco rhythm with pumping bass, swirling strings and a hard four-on-the-floor kick, all circling a descending chord pattern that repeats throughout. Gaynor's voice grows steadily more defiant as the strings climb, and the track never really lets up once it starts.","Written by Freddie Perren and Dino Fekaris and produced by Perren, the song was originally released by Polydor as the B-side of a cover called Substitute. Gaynor had recently spent months recovering from a serious spinal injury after a fall on stage, and she sang the song with that recovery in mind. Club DJs flipped the record and made the B-side the hit.","That chord sequence steps downward through a cycle of fifths, the same falling chain Vivaldi and Handel used to generate momentum, which is part of why the song feels both sad and unstoppable at once. Listen for the strings: they keep pushing upward against the falling harmony, and that tension between descent and ascent gives the music its feeling of struggle and resolve.","It reached number one in the United States and Britain and won the only Grammy ever given for Best Disco Recording. It has since become an anthem for many causes, from women's independence to gay liberation and survivors of illness, and is often ranked among the greatest songs of its decade. Disco's critics have rarely argued with this one.","Listen to the slow opening carefully, then notice the moment the beat arrives. Follow the strings as they rise against the falling chords. Then hear a Baroque concerto built on a falling cycle of fifths, any Vivaldi fast movement will do, and notice the same harmonic engine doing very different work."],"thelma-houston-dont-leave-me-this-way":["A plea to a lover not to walk away. It opens quietly, with Thelma Houston's voice almost alone over soft keyboards, then accelerates into a full-tilt disco gallop: churning hi-hat, stomping kick, surging strings and a gospel choir answering her. Houston's voice moves from tender to ferocious, cracking with emotion at the peaks, and the arrangement keeps raising the stakes until it feels like a revival meeting on a dance floor.","The song was written by Kenneth Gamble, Leon Huff and Cary Gilbert and first recorded by Harold Melvin and the Blue Notes with Teddy Pendergrass. Houston's version, produced by Hal Davis, appeared on Motown in 1976 at a time when the label was adapting its Detroit soul sound to the disco market. Houston, from Mississippi, had been recording for years without a major hit.","Listen to the opening: the slow, intimate start is a gospel device, building tension before the release of the beat. The arrangement is slightly faster and more driving than the Philadelphia original, a sign of how disco was pushing tempos up. The Communards' 1986 cover turned it into a Hi-NRG anthem in Britain.","It reached number one in America in 1977 and won Houston a Grammy for Best Female R&B Vocal Performance. It is now regarded as one of the great disco vocals, a record where the singer's power matters as much as the beat. Comparisons with the Blue Notes' version continue to divide soul purists.","Listen to Harold Melvin's version first, then Houston's, and notice how the change of singer and tempo transforms the song. Follow her voice from the quiet opening to the climax. Then hear the Communards' cover for its synth-driven Hi-NRG version."],"loleatta-holloway-hit-and-run":["A song about a lover who leaves as quickly as he arrives, delivered by one of the most powerful voices in disco. Over a Philadelphia-style rhythm section with clipped guitar, rolling percussion, horns and strings, Loleatta Holloway sings with a gospel force that seems to strain at the edges of the recording. The best-known version stretches to around eleven minutes, with long instrumental breaks and passages where her improvised cries ring out over the groove.","Holloway, a Chicago singer raised in gospel, recorded for Gold Mind, a subsidiary of the Salsoul label, which was then central to New York disco. Salsoul used the same pool of Philadelphia musicians who had powered the Philly soul sound. Club DJs were becoming the most important tastemakers in dance music, and the label commissioned special long mixes for them.","The famous long version is a remix by Walter Gibbons, one of the first DJs to work as a remixer, who reworked the original into something rawer and stranger. He pulled Holloway's ad-libbed lines out of the original session tapes and extended the percussion breaks, foreshadowing how house producers would later treat the voice as material to be cut up.","The single was a club success, while the remix became legendary among DJs and collectors. Critics now treat Gibbons's version as a landmark in the history of the remix, and Holloway's performance as among disco's finest, even though she never became a mainstream star.","Find the long Gibbons mix and listen to how he lets the drums run before bringing the voice back. Notice how Holloway's improvisations are treated as a featured instrument. Then compare the shorter single version to hear what the remixer added."],"loleatta-holloway-love-sensation":["A rapturous song about the overwhelming feeling of falling in love. Dan Hartman's production sets a bright, bouncy groove with chiming keyboards, buzzing synth bass and lush strings, and Loleatta Holloway sings over it with full gospel power, holding notes and pushing into cries that cut through the whole arrangement. The track rides a steady mid-tempo bounce and breaks down to let her voice stand almost alone before the band returns.","Released in 1980 on Gold Mind, by which time disco was in commercial retreat in the United States after the backlash of the previous year. Dan Hartman, a rock musician and producer who had scored a disco hit himself with Instant Replay, wrote and produced it. Club culture continued underground, especially in gay and Black clubs in New York and Chicago, where records like this kept their audience.","In 1989 the Italian group Black Box built their huge hit Ride on Time out of Holloway's vocal from this record, sampled without her permission or credit, and a model lip-synced it in the video. The dispute led to a settlement. Her voice has been sampled many times since, making her one of the most heard but least named singers in dance music.","It was a club hit on release but did little on mainstream charts. Its fame grew through sampling, and it is now regarded as a link between disco and house, a record whose vocal became the raw material of later dance music. The Black Box affair is often cited in debates about sampling and credit.","Listen for the moments where Holloway's voice breaks out of the arrangement. Then play Black Box's Ride on Time and hear the same voice, cut up and reset on a house beat. Notice what is lost and gained. Then seek out her earlier Hit and Run for comparison."],"klein-mbo-dirty-talk":["A flirtatious, half-spoken come-on set to a rubbery synthesizer groove. A bouncing synth bass line, a dry drum machine and simple, bright keyboard chords loop under a woman's voice speaking and singing in English with an Italian accent. The production is sparse and slightly naive, with little beyond bass, beat and voice, and it is precisely that bareness that makes the groove so hypnotic: it repeats and repeats, gaining force from what it leaves out.","Klein + M.B.O. was a studio project of the producers Mario Boncaldo and Tony Carrasco, released in 1982 on the Italian label Zanza. Italian studios were making cheap, synth-driven dance records for a European market that still loved disco even as America turned away. Many of these records crossed the Atlantic as imports and ended up in the crates of Chicago and New York DJs, who were hungry for anything new and electronic.","Listen to the bass line, which sounds almost like the ones Chicago house producers would program on cheap drum machines and synths a few years later. Dirty Talk was a favourite of Chicago DJs, Ron Hardy at the Music Box among them, and producers there have cited it as a direct influence. Its lo-fi sound is the sound of a budget studio, not a choice of style, but it became a style.","It was a modest European hit and a cult record in American clubs. Dance historians now treat it as one of the clearest bridges between Italo disco and Chicago house, and its bass line and spare arrangement have made it a favourite for revival DJs and samplers.","Listen only to the bass for a full minute and notice how few notes it uses. Then play an early Chicago house record such as Marshall Jefferson's Move Your Body and hear the family resemblance. Seek the original 12-inch rather than edits."],"alexander-robotnick-problemes-damour":["A wry little song about the troubles of love, sung in French over a dry, jittery synthesizer groove. A chattering drum machine and a bouncing synth bass hold the rhythm while a simple melody line, almost a nursery tune, repeats above, with deadpan vocals delivered more as asides than as a performance. The texture is thin and clinical, and the effect is half dance record, half art-school joke.","Alexander Robotnick was the alias of Maurizio Dami, a Florentine musician who came to dance music from the city's art and experimental scenes rather than from the disco industry. The record was released in 1983 by the Florentine label Materiali Sonori. Italian studios were turning out large numbers of synth dance records, but Dami approached the form from the angle of new wave and conceptual art.","Listen to the way the bass line and drum machine seem to have been programmed by someone more interested in the machines than in the dance floor: tiny glitches and quirks are left in. It is that slightly awkward, homemade electronic feel that later Detroit and Chicago producers, and the electroclash revival of the 2000s, would admire.","It was a modest European release that found its true audience later, among DJs who discovered it in second-hand bins. It has since been reissued and remixed many times and is often named among the cult classics of Italo disco, proof that the genre included experimenters as well as hit-makers.","Notice the contrast between the deadpan voice and the bouncing machines. Listen for how simple the melody is. Then compare with Klein + M.B.O.'s Dirty Talk to hear two sides of Italo, one aimed at the club, one smirking at it."],"evelyn-thomas-high-energy":["A song about the electric excitement a lover generates, set to a pounding, fast synthesizer beat. The track is pure Hi-NRG: octave-jumping synth bass racing at high tempo, crashing electronic drums, bright stabbing chords and Evelyn Thomas's big soul voice belting over the top. There is little subtlety and no wish for it; everything is designed for maximum impact on a crowded floor, and the chorus hammers home.","The record was produced by Ian Levine, a British DJ who had come up through Northern Soul and became the leading producer of the Hi-NRG sound at Heaven, the London gay nightclub. Thomas, a singer from Chicago, had worked with Levine before. Released in 1984 on Record Shack, it became a hit across Europe at a time when the British gay scene was creating its own dance music.","The style was already called Hi-NRG in clubs, but this record's title fixed the name in the public mind. Listen for the synthesizer bass: it uses the same rapid octave figure Patrick Cowley had pioneered in San Francisco, now made heavier and more mechanical. Levine co-wrote the track with Fiachra Trench, an arranger who also worked in orchestral pop.","It was a major hit in Britain and parts of Europe and remains one of the best-known Hi-NRG records. Critics have often treated Hi-NRG as camp or disposable, but historians of gay club culture now see records like this as central to how a community found its own space and its own sound in the 1980s.","Play it loud and notice the tempo, noticeably faster than classic disco. Listen to how the bass line and the kick drum lock together. Then compare with Patrick Cowley's Menergy to hear where the sound came from, and with the Pet Shop Boys to hear where it went."],"moloko-sing-it-back":["A song asking a lover to give back the feeling of the music that drew them together. The hit version rests on a warm, rolling house groove: a deep, bouncing bass line, a steady kick, shuffling hi-hats and filtered disco chords, with Róisín Murphy's breathy, flirtatious voice gliding on top. The melody is simple and repeated, and the track builds gently rather than climaxing, more sway than stomp.","Moloko, the Sheffield duo of Róisín Murphy and Mark Brydon, first released the song on their 1998 album I Am Not a Doctor in a slower, quirkier version that went nowhere. The German DJ Boris Dlugosch remixed it into a house track, and the remix became a summer hit in 1999, carrying the band from trip-hop curiosity to dance-floor stars.","Compare the album version with the remix: the vocal is the same, but almost everything around it has changed. Dlugosch added the bass line and the four-on-the-floor beat, reframing Murphy's voice as a disco diva's. This moment, a remix turning an art-pop act into a club act, set the course for Murphy's whole later career.","The remix was a hit across Europe and is often named among the best dance records of its decade. Critics continue to note the irony that the band's defining record was largely made by someone else, but Murphy's voice is what everyone remembers, and the track established her as one of the great dance-floor singers.","Play both versions back to back. Listen to the bass line in the remix and notice how it changes the mood of the vocal. Then hear The Time Is Now to hear the band absorbing the lesson on their next album."],"moloko-the-time-is-now":["A song about seizing the present moment with someone before it passes. It opens with a looping acoustic guitar figure and a soft groove, then swells with lush strings in the manner of 1970s disco, while Róisín Murphy's voice moves between hushed intimacy and a full-throated chorus. The arrangement keeps a gentle mid-tempo bounce and lets the strings carry the emotional climax.","The lead single from Moloko's third album, Things to Make and Do, released in 2000 on Echo. After the success of the Sing It Back remix, the duo deliberately moved toward dance music while keeping their song-craft and eccentricity. Disco revivalism was in the air, with French house producers sampling old records, but Moloko chose to write a new song in a disco idiom with live strings.","Listen for the guitar figure under the opening: it carries the whole song unchanged, while the strings arrive only gradually, like a Philadelphia disco record slowly coming into focus. Murphy's vocal is layered in places, giving it a slightly unreal, shimmering quality, and the chorus melody is far simpler than the verse, a classic disco trick for getting a crowd to sing along.","It was one of the band's biggest hits in Britain and won wide praise for combining dance-floor appeal with real song-writing. Critics have since treated it as a bridge from the late-1990s house scene to the disco revival of later decades, and as the moment Murphy became a pop star.","Notice the moment the strings arrive. Follow the guitar loop and notice that it never changes. Then play a Philadelphia disco string arrangement, such as Thelma Houston's Don't Leave Me This Way, to hear what the band was reaching back to."],"roisin-murphy-overpowered":["Róisín Murphy's second solo album: a sleek, playful dance-pop record that runs from disco and house to electro and soul. Synth bass lines pulse, strings and keyboards glitter, and Murphy sings with her characteristic mixture of warmth, wit and theatrical poise. Tracks such as the title song, Let Me Know and You Know Me Better are built on clean grooves and simple, catchy hooks, with arrangements that feel both retro and futuristic.","After Moloko split and a first solo album of experimental songs made with Matthew Herbert, Murphy signed to EMI and made a deliberate move toward the dance-pop mainstream. She worked with a range of London dance producers on the record. The late 2000s saw a revival of interest in disco and electro in Britain, with acts reworking the sounds of the late 1970s and early 1980s for a new generation.","Listen for the way Murphy's voice is treated as part of the arrangement rather than placed above it: in several songs it is chopped, layered and echoed like a synth. The title track's arrangement owes something to the way Moroder and Cowley built dance tracks around a single repeated bass figure.","Critics praised it widely and many named it among the best pop albums of its year. Commercially it did less well than expected, and it became a cult record, cited often as a pop album that deserved more success. It is now seen as a key work of the late-2000s disco revival.","Start with the title track, then You Know Me Better. Notice how each song is built on a simple bass groove. Compare with Kylie's Light Years to hear another version of British disco-pop, and with Moroder for the source. Listen on good speakers, since the bass carries everything."],"roisin-murphy-roisin-machine":["A long, continuous-feeling album of deep disco and house, built on hypnotic, slow-burning grooves. Synth bass lines roll for minutes at a time, drum machines tick with loose swing, and Murphy sings over them with a mix of seduction, melancholy and commanding cool. Tracks such as Simulation, Narcissus, Murphy's Law and Something More stretch out, building through small changes rather than verse-and-chorus structures.","Made over several years with the Sheffield producer Crooked Man, the DJ and producer Richard Barratt, who had been part of the city's dance scene since the late 1980s. Some tracks had appeared as singles years earlier; the album, released in 2020 on Skint, gathered them into a whole. It arrived during the pandemic, when clubs were closed and dance music was heard mainly at home.","Listen for the way Barratt's grooves borrow from both Italo disco and early Chicago house, with drum programming that swings slightly rather than locking to a grid. The album plays almost like a DJ set, with tracks flowing into each other, a nod to the continuous mixes of disco's early days.","Critics greeted it as perhaps Murphy's finest record, praising its patience and cohesion, and it appeared on many year-end lists. It is often cited alongside Dua Lipa's Future Nostalgia and Kylie's Disco as one of the albums that made 2020 a year of disco revival, though it is the most underground of the three.","Play it in one sitting, ideally loud. Start with Simulation and notice how long the groove runs before anything changes. Then compare with Kylie's Disco and Dua Lipa's Future Nostalgia to hear three versions of disco revival in the same year."],"kylie-minogue-light-years":["Kylie Minogue's return to the dance floor: a bright, glossy album of disco-pop that draws on the sounds of the late 1970s. Strings glide, filtered disco loops shimmer, guitars chop in the manner of Nile Rodgers, and Minogue sings with a light, playful touch. Spinning Around, the lead single, rides a bouncing groove, while On a Night Like This and Your Disco Needs You lean further into camp, celebratory disco.","Released in 2000 on Parlophone after Minogue had spent the late 1990s making more experimental records for Deconstruction. The album was a deliberate move back to pop, and Spinning Around, co-written by Paula Abdul, was promoted with a famous video featuring gold hot pants. French house had made disco loops fashionable again, and British pop was ready for a glamorous comeback.","Your Disco Needs You, co-written with Robbie Williams and Guy Chambers, is an affectionate parody of a wartime recruiting poster, with a marching-band beat and multilingual versions. Listen for the strings throughout the album, which are arranged in the style of Philadelphia disco rather than sampled from it.","It was a major commercial success in Britain and Australia and relaunched Minogue's career, setting up her biggest hit the following year. Critics praised it as a confident return to form, and later writers have seen it as one of the albums that brought disco back into the British mainstream.","Start with Spinning Around and listen for the chopped guitar. Then Your Disco Needs You for its camp. Compare with Chic's Le Freak to hear what the album is paying homage to, and with Can't Get You Out of My Head for what came next."],"kylie-minogue-disco":["A full-length homage to disco, made with warm affection. Glittering strings, rubbery bass lines, funky rhythm guitar and four-on-the-floor beats support Minogue's light, airy voice across a sequence of upbeat songs. Say Something, Magic and Real Groove lead the way, each built on a bouncing groove, a sweet melody and arrangements that nod to the late 1970s without sounding like a museum piece.","Released in November 2020 on BMG during the coronavirus pandemic. Much of the album was made under lockdown, with Minogue recording her own vocals at home and learning to work the recording software herself. With clubs closed worldwide, 2020 saw a striking wave of disco albums, from Dua Lipa, Jessie Ware and Róisín Murphy among others, offering escape at a time when dance floors were empty.","Listen for the string arrangements, which are layered in the manner of Philadelphia and Salsoul disco. The album is sequenced like a night out, starting bright, peaking in the middle and winding down at the end, a structure that recalls the continuous album sides of disco's golden age.","It was a commercial success, topping the British album chart and making Minogue the first woman to have number-one albums there in five consecutive decades. Critics praised its warmth and craft, though some found it less adventurous than other disco records of the same year.","Play it in order as a single sequence. Notice how the strings and guitar work together. Then compare with Light Years from twenty years earlier to hear how Minogue's take on disco has deepened, and with Róisín Machine for a more underground version."],"jessie-ware-whats-your-pleasure":["An album of sophisticated, sensual disco and boogie, made with care for texture and groove. Synth bass lines bounce, drum machines tick, strings swirl and guitars chop, while Jessie Ware sings with cool control, closer to a soul singer than a diva. Spotlight opens with a sweeping string introduction before the beat arrives; the title track rides an Italo-influenced synth groove; Save a Kiss pulses with Hi-NRG energy.","Released in June 2020, the album was produced largely by James Ford, a British producer known for his work with indie bands. Ware had made three albums of elegant, slow soul and had talked of stepping back from music; instead she decided to make a dance record. It arrived during lockdown, when clubs were shut, and became one of the most celebrated albums of the year.","Listen for the references: Moroder's sequencers in the title track, Italo disco in the bass lines, and the disco strings of Salsoul and Philadelphia. The album is assembled with the knowledge of a collector, but the songs are new, not pastiche. The Spotlight introduction deliberately evokes the overture-like openings of 1970s disco records.","Critics greeted it with almost universal praise, many naming it among the best albums of 2020, and it transformed Ware's career, reaching a younger club audience. It is now treated as one of the defining records of the 2020 disco revival, and perhaps the most knowing about disco's history.","Start with Spotlight and listen for the moment the beat arrives after the strings. Then the title track for its synth groove. Compare with Donna Summer's I Feel Love and Klein + M.B.O.'s Dirty Talk to hear the sources Ware and Ford drew on."],"jessie-ware-that-feels-good":["A brighter, brassier follow-up to What's Your Pleasure?, trading cool synths for big band disco. Horns blast, strings soar, percussion rattles and bass lines funk, while Jessie Ware sings with a freer, more theatrical abandon. Free Yourself charges forward with gospel energy; Pearls bounces with a Chic-like groove; the title track opens the album with a spoken invitation and a burst of brass. The mood is celebratory rather than seductive.","Released in April 2023 on EMI, after the success of What's Your Pleasure? had transformed Ware's career and brought her to festival stages. She wanted the new album to capture the energy of live performance after lockdown ended. Disco revivalism had become mainstream, and Ware's approach shifted from the club's dark room to the full dance floor.","Listen for how much of the album is played by live musicians: horns, strings and percussion give it the warmth of 1970s disco rather than the electronic sheen of its predecessor. The vocal performances have more improvisation and ad-libbing, as if the songs were captured in a club rather than a studio.","Critics praised it warmly, many finding it more joyful if less mysterious than its predecessor. It confirmed Ware as one of the leading voices of contemporary disco, and some writers compared her shift from cool to hot with the move from Moroder's machines to Chic's live band.","Start with Free Yourself and listen for the horns. Then Pearls for its bass. Play it next to What's Your Pleasure? to hear how one artist can treat the same genre two different ways, and next to Chic's Good Times for the source."],"dua-lipa-dont-start-now":["A breakup song in which the singer tells an ex she has moved on. The track is built on a prominent, bouncing bass line in the disco tradition, a four-on-the-floor kick, crisp handclaps and swirling strings, with Dua Lipa's low, confident voice cutting through. The chorus is followed by a strong instrumental hook rather than a sung one, a trick from contemporary dance-pop, and the whole song lasts barely three minutes.","Released in late 2019 as the lead single from Future Nostalgia, produced by Ian Kirkpatrick and co-written by Lipa with Caroline Ailin, Emily Warren and Kirkpatrick. Lipa, a British singer of Kosovar-Albanian heritage, had become a global star with New Rules. She wanted her second album to draw on disco and dance music, and this song announced the direction.","Listen for the bass line: it is played on a synthesizer but phrased like Bernard Edwards in Chic, with syncopations that pull against the steady kick. The string swoops during the chorus recall Philadelphia disco, and the handclaps nod to late-1970s production while the low end is thoroughly modern.","It became one of the biggest hits of 2020 worldwide and was nominated for Grammys. Critics praised its craft and its confident use of disco, and it is now seen as the opening shot of the disco revival of that year, the song that made disco pop's sound again.","Listen to the bass line through the whole song and notice its syncopations. Then compare with Chic's Good Times to hear the source. Play it next to Future Nostalgia's other tracks, or Kylie's Disco and Jessie Ware's What's Your Pleasure?, to hear the revival it started."],"mohammed-abdel-wahab-al-warda-al-bayda":["A musical film built around its star's voice: a melodrama of a poor clerk in love with his employer's daughter, punctuated by songs that stop the story and let Abdel Wahab sing at length. The songs alternate between the old takht sound, a handful of soloists weaving around the singer, and a fuller, string-heavy accompaniment that points toward the film orchestra to come. The marker is the young Abdel Wahab's light, supple tenor, sliding through ornaments with crooner-like intimacy.","Egyptian sound cinema was only a year or two old when Mohammed Karim directed this film, Abdel Wahab's first, and it became one of the biggest box-office successes the Arab world had seen. Cairo's studios were becoming the Hollywood of the region, and records, radio and film together were turning singers into stars across North Africa and the Levant. Abdel Wahab had studied Western music and watched European films, and he wanted a musical cinema of his own.","Listen to the instrumental introductions and interludes, not just the songs. Here the composer quietly smuggles in Western ideas: rhythmic patterns borrowed from dance music, passages where the strings move in parallel rather than all in unison, and a sense of scene-painting that comes from European film. These small borrowings, barely noticed in 1933, are the seeds of the orchestral style he later brought to Umm Kulthum.","Audiences loved it, and its songs circulated on record far beyond the cinemas. Traditionalists were wary of the Western touches, a complaint that would follow Abdel Wahab for sixty years. Film historians now treat it as the foundation of the Egyptian musical, the genre that made Cairo the centre of Arab popular culture until television took over, and as the moment the singer-composer became a screen idol.","Seek out the songs on a compilation first, as most Arab listeners know them, and listen for how short and shaped they are compared with the hour-long tarab performances of the same years. Then compare the composer's much later Enta Omri for Umm Kulthum to hear how far the small Western touches here eventually grew."],"mohammed-abdel-wahab-min-ghair-leh":["A long song that asks why, without answer, about love and life: a meditation rather than a romance. It unfolds in the grand Cairo manner, with an extended orchestral introduction, a string section moving in unison, and solo passages for qanun and nay that set up each vocal entry. Then the voice arrives, old, thin and frail, but phrasing with absolute control. The sound to recognise it by is that aged voice set against a full, glossy orchestra.","Abdel Wahab was by then the elder statesman of Arab music, a composer for Umm Kulthum, Abdel Halim Hafez and Fairuz, and he had largely stopped singing in public decades earlier. The words were by Morsi Gamil Aziz. Recording a new song in his eighties, after the deaths of most of the singers he had written for, was an event in itself, and it turned out to be the last song he sang.","Listen to how the composer writes around his own limitations: phrases are short, the line rarely climbs high, and the orchestra completes the thoughts the voice can no longer sustain. It is a lesson in writing for a singer, the craft he had practised for half a century, applied to himself. The question in the title returns again and again, each time given a slightly different melodic turn.","It was received in Egypt with enormous affection, as a farewell from the last survivor of the golden age, and it is still broadcast widely. Some listeners found the arrangement over-polished compared with his classic work; others hear it as the most personal thing he wrote. After his death in 1991 it took on the character of a testament.","Hear it after Enta Omri, the song he wrote for Umm Kulthum a quarter of a century earlier. The orchestral habits are the same, the long introduction and the unison strings, but here the voice is fragile and the mood reflective. Let the introduction run its whole length; it is part of the argument."],"asmahan-ya-toyour":["A song addressed to the birds, asking them to carry a message of longing. What makes it unique is the vocal writing: long wordless runs high in the voice, trills and leaps that imitate birdsong, closer to an operatic coloratura aria than to anything in Arab song of the time. Underneath, the orchestra mixes Arab instruments with Western-style strings. The marker is the moment Asmahan abandons words entirely and becomes a bird.","The music is by Mohammed al-Qasabgi, the oud player and composer who also wrote for Umm Kulthum and who was one of the boldest modernisers of Cairo song. Asmahan, a Syrian Druze princess raised in Egypt, had a voice of unusual range and lightness, trained partly in a European manner. She was also a film star, and her life, which included political intrigue during the Second World War, made her a figure of fascination.","Al-Qasabgi chose a maqam, or mode, whose intervals sit close to the Western scale for much of the song, which is what lets the operatic runs sound natural rather than forced. Listen for the places where the mode suddenly bends back to quarter tones: the song keeps both worlds alive at once. The orchestration, with its Western-style string writing, anchors that European colour.","Admired then and now as a vocal feat, it is often cited as the most daring piece al-Qasabgi wrote. Critics have long argued whether Asmahan's Westernised style pointed to a road Arab music might have taken had she not died at about thirty-two; Umm Kulthum's far more traditional path won out. The song remains a test piece for ambitious singers.","Listen first for the wordless passages and follow the voice as it climbs and trills; then compare a Mozart or Delibes coloratura aria to hear the model. After that, play Umm Kulthum's Al-Atlal to measure how different the two great female voices of Cairo were."],"asmahan-layali-al-uns-fi-vienna":["Nights of Delight in Vienna: a song about the pleasures of an imagined Vienna, set as a waltz. The orchestra sways in three-time, strings swelling and receding like a ballroom band, and Asmahan floats over it with light, clean high notes that recall operetta. Yet her ornaments and some of the melodic turns are pure Cairo. The marker is that lilting waltz rhythm, almost unheard of in Arab song before this.","Written by her brother Farid al-Atrash to words by Ahmed Rami, it appears in the film Gharam wa Intiqam (Love and Revenge), directed by Youssef Wahbi. Asmahan died in a car accident in July 1944, while the film was still being completed, and it was released after her death. Cairo in the war years was full of Allied soldiers and European refugees, and the city's light music absorbed their dances.","Listen to how Farid al-Atrash slips an Arab mode into a European dance: the waltz frame stays steady, but the melody keeps bending onto the quarter tones a Viennese band could never play. It is a tiny portrait of the whole al-Atrash project, the attempt to make a cosmopolitan Arab music that could sit in a Western ballroom without losing its accent.","It became one of her most loved recordings, given extra poignancy by her death. Some purists dismissed it as light entertainment; later critics see it as a model of how the Cairo film song absorbed foreign forms. It is still sung by Arab sopranos and has been reinterpreted by jazz and classical musicians from the region.","Put a Strauss waltz beside it and hear what is borrowed and what is not. Then listen only to the voice: notice how she sings the high notes almost without vibrato, a sound very different from the heavy, chesty tarab singers of her time."],"farid-al-atrash-afrita-hanem":["A musical fantasy comedy: a struggling singer finds a lamp, and a mischievous female genie, played by the dancer Samia Gamal, helps and teases him through a series of misadventures. The songs range from heartfelt ballads with Farid's oud to lively ensemble numbers and choreographed dance sequences backed by a big film orchestra. The marker is the mix of oriental dance rhythms and Hollywood-style production numbers.","Directed by Henry Barakat, it came at the peak of Egyptian studio cinema, when Farid al-Atrash and Samia Gamal were the most famous screen pairing in the Arab world and were also romantically linked. Egypt was months away from the end of the monarchy, and Cairo's film industry was turning out musicals for audiences from Morocco to Iraq. Farid wrote the music as well as starring.","Farid was first an oud virtuoso, and he lets that show: listen for the passages where the orchestra drops away and his own oud plays alone, fast and percussive, before a song begins. The genie's dance numbers use the rhythmic patterns of Egyptian raqs sharqi, the solo dance, but orchestrated with brass and strings, a hybrid sound that defined Cairo nightlife music for a decade.","A big commercial success, it is still shown on Arab television and is regarded as one of the classic Egyptian musicals. Critics of the time sometimes found Farid's films formulaic, built around a sad singer and a dancer; historians now value them as records of a lost cosmopolitan Cairo and of a song style that mixed Arab, Latin and European elements freely.","Watch it if you can, since the dance sequences are half the music. Otherwise find the songs on compilation and listen for the oud solos. Compare the genie scenes with a Hollywood musical of the 1940s to hear the shared language of film orchestras."],"farid-al-atrash-al-rabie":["A long song about the coming of spring and the return of love, shaped as a journey through contrasting sections, each in a different mode and mood. Bright, dancing passages give way to slow laments, and the orchestra paints birds and breezes. In live versions Farid adds long oud improvisations between sections. The marker is the oud itself, played with a speed and attack that made him famous.","Farid al-Atrash, Asmahan's brother, had grown up in Cairo as a refugee from the Druze mountains of Syria, and by the late 1940s he was one of the biggest stars of Egyptian film and radio. He composed and sang his own songs, unusual at a time when singers and composers were usually separate. This song became his signature, performed in concert for the rest of his life, often greatly extended.","Listen for how the song moves through maqamat, the Arab modes, one after another, so that the change of season is told by changes of scale rather than by words alone. The oud taqasim, the free improvisations, were never the same twice; in concert recordings Farid treats them like a cadenza in a Romantic concerto, a place for display and for leading the audience into the next mode.","It is generally counted among the great songs of the golden age, and concert recordings are prized for the oud playing. Some critics thought Farid's singing sentimental beside Abdel Wahab's or Umm Kulthum's; his reputation as an instrumentalist, the King of the Oud, has never been questioned. Oud players still learn his passages from records.","Choose a live concert recording, the longer the better, and listen for the audience reacting to the oud. Then compare Anouar Brahem's oud on an ECM record: the same instrument, but intimate and quiet where Farid is extrovert and fiery."],"warda-fi-youm-wa-leila":["In a Day and a Night: a song about a love that changes a life overnight. It follows the golden-age plan of long orchestral introduction, sung sections and returns, but the pace is quicker and the rhythms more danceable than in the classic Umm Kulthum songs. Warda's voice is strong, bright and slightly hard-edged, cutting through a big string section. The marker is the bouncing rhythmic introduction that brings audiences to their feet.","The music is by Mohammed Abdel Wahab, by then writing for the next generation of singers after Umm Kulthum's decline. Warda had returned to singing in 1972, after a decade's retirement in Algeria, and settled in Cairo, where she became one of the leading voices of Egyptian song through the 1970s. Arab music was moving from the radio concert toward cassette and television.","Abdel Wahab, ever the modernist, treats the accordion as a full member of the orchestra rather than a guest; listen for it doubling the strings. Notice also how Warda's Algerian accent sometimes peeps through her Egyptian Arabic, a reminder that she was an outsider who had conquered the Cairo stage.","It became one of her best-known songs and a regular in her concerts, often stretched by repeated sections when the audience demanded. Critics place it among the late flowering of the golden-age style, before shorter pop songs replaced the long concert song. Some find it formulaic beside Abdel Wahab's earlier work; audiences have never minded.","Listen to a live recording and notice the call and response between Warda and the crowd. Then set it beside Enta Omri: the same composer, a decade later, writing faster and lighter for a younger voice. Notice how quickly the rhythm returns after each slow passage."],"warda-batwanes-beek":["A song about feeling at home in a lover's company. It is shorter and lighter than her classic concert songs: a catchy refrain, a steady mid-tempo rhythm, a string section sweetened by synthesizers. Warda's voice, now darker and fuller, sits in front with warmth rather than display. The marker is the refrain, a simple rising phrase that a whole stadium can sing.","Composed by Salah El Sharnoubi to words by Omar Batisha, it came at a time when the long golden-age song was giving way to shorter, studio-made pop for cassette and satellite television. Warda was a senior star by then, and her willingness to adopt the newer format kept her current with a younger audience across Egypt, Algeria and the Gulf.","Under the modern production, the melody still moves through the ornaments and modal turns of tarab singing; listen for the brief places where Warda stretches a syllable over several notes, a gesture from the older art tucked inside a pop song. The synthesizer pads replace what would once have been sustained string chords.","It became one of the most popular Arabic songs of its decade and remains among her best known, often the first Warda song younger listeners meet. Traditionalists regretted the electronic sound; others credit it with bridging the classic and pop eras. It has been covered many times by singers across the region.","Listen for the refrain first, then for how Warda shapes the verses with small ornaments. Compare Fi Youm wa Leila to hear the same voice in the older, longer form, then Amr Diab's Nour El Ain to hear where Arab pop went next."],"sabah-zay-el-assal":["Like Honey: a light, flirtatious love song comparing a sweetheart to honey. It is short and bright, with a bouncing rhythm, a playful orchestral riff answered by the voice, and a chorus-like refrain. Sabah sings with a clear, forward tone and an obvious smile, decorating phrases with quick Lebanese-style ornaments. The marker is the sweetness and ease of the voice, a deliberate contrast to the heavy grandeur of Cairo tarab.","Sabah, born in the Lebanese mountains, made her name in Egyptian cinema and radio and was at the height of her fame through the 1950s and 1960s, moving between Cairo and Beirut. Beirut was then a booming centre of nightlife, festivals and recording, and Sabah, nicknamed al-Shahroura, the singing bird, was its glamorous face. The composer and date of this recording are not securely documented.","Listen to the ornaments: Sabah uses the quick, light trills of Lebanese mountain singing, in which she was trained, rather than the long, weighted melismas of Egyptian singers. That small difference of vocal technique carried a whole regional identity, the Levant answering Cairo, at a time when Lebanese singers were beginning to claim their own place.","Sabah was loved more than analysed; critics treated her as an entertainer, while audiences adored her across six decades. Recent writing has revised that view, recognising her as one of the most versatile Arab singers of the century and as a pioneer of a light Levantine style. Her many marriages and long career made her a byword for glamour.","Listen alongside Fairuz to hear the two great Lebanese voices of the age: Fairuz cool and still, Sabah warm and playful. Pay attention to the refrain and how she varies it each time, never singing it quite the same way twice."],"amr-diab-habibi-ya-nour-el-ain":["A buoyant love song calling the beloved the light of the eye. Strummed Spanish-style guitars, handclaps and a brisk dance beat drive a melody that lies easily in the voice, with a chant-along hook. Amr Diab sings cleanly and lightly, close to a Western pop singer, with only touches of Arab ornament. The marker is that flamenco-flavoured guitar figure and the hand-clap rhythm under the hook.","The title track of his 1996 album, it was composed by the Libyan musician Nasser al-Mizdawi. Amr Diab, from Port Said, had built a career since the 1980s on what Egyptians called youth music, short and dance-friendly songs for cassettes and, increasingly, satellite television. Its video, shot with a glossy, Western look, played across the newly connected Arab world and beyond.","The so-called Mediterranean sound of the song draws on Spanish guitar and Gipsy Kings-style rumba, which itself has deep Andalusian and Arab roots: listen for the cadences that would be at home both in flamenco and in North African song. That double ancestry is why the hybrid sounds natural rather than imported.","It was a hit far beyond the Arab world, one of the most recognised Arabic songs internationally, and set the template for a decade of Arab pop. Critics of the old school saw it as the end of tarab and the triumph of the producer; supporters saw a confident new pan-Arab mainstream. It is still played at weddings everywhere.","Listen for the guitar first, then for how short and repetitive the melody is compared with a golden-age song. Follow it with a flamenco recording to hear the shared roots, and then Warda's Batwanes Beek to hear the older generation's version of pop."],"amr-diab-tamally-maak":["Always with you: a song of devoted love that never leaves the beloved, even at a distance. It opens on gently picked guitar and a soft rhythm before the strings rise into a broad, singable chorus. Amr Diab keeps the vocal restrained, almost conversational, so the hook carries the emotion. The marker is that sweeping chorus melody, lifted on strings, which has become a staple of weddings and celebrations across the Arab world.","The title track of his 2000 album, composed by Sherif Tag, it arrived as satellite channels and music videos made a few stars truly pan-Arab. Amr Diab was by then the biggest name in Arab pop, and the album sold enormously. Production was in the hands of a new generation of Cairo studio musicians who blended Arab and Western sounds as a matter of course.","For all its Western polish, listen for the small modal inflections in the melody, a note bent slightly flat in passing that no Western ballad would use. And notice how the arrangement builds by adding layers rather than by changing key: Arab pop tends to intensify through texture rather than modulation.","It became perhaps the most widely known Arabic love song of its decade, covered by singers from Turkey to South Asia. Some critics dismiss it as sentimental; others admire its craft. Debates about whether Amr Diab's sound was too Westernised now seem dated, since the style he helped create became the mainstream.","Hear it as a ballad and follow the build from guitar to full strings. Then listen to Abdel Halim Hafez's Ahwak, a romantic song from forty years earlier, to hear how much of the old Egyptian lyrical tradition still underlies the gloss."],"nancy-ajram-akhasmak-ah":["A teasing song in Egyptian dialect about a lovers' quarrel that she threatens to drag out. The beat is the old Egyptian baladi rhythm, darbuka and hand drums up front, with accordion and synthesizer riffs bouncing over it and a sing-song hook. Nancy Ajram's voice is light, bright and knowing, with quick ornaments. The marker is the swaying baladi groove, an old street dance rhythm dressed up as modern pop.","From her 2003 album Ya Salam, it made the young Lebanese singer a star overnight, largely through its video, directed by Nadine Labaki, in which she plays a flirtatious waitress in a Cairo-style café full of men. The clip was condemned by some conservatives and became a talking point across the Arab world, at the height of satellite music channels.","Listen for the accordion, a sound linked with Egyptian popular music of the mid-century, especially the film songs and street music of Cairo. The production deliberately recalls that older world, a retro gesture beneath the modern sheen, and the video's café setting is part of the same nostalgia. Labaki later became an acclaimed film director.","It was a huge hit and Ajram became one of the best-selling Arab singers of the decade. The video's controversy fed debates about women, morality and pop culture in Arab media. Critics were split between those who saw empty commercial packaging and those who heard a clever, witty revival of baladi tradition.","Listen to the drums first and try to clap the baladi rhythm, a lopsided pattern of strong and weak beats. Then listen to Sabah, an older Lebanese star who made her name in Egyptian song, to hear the same playful persona a generation earlier."],"nancy-ajram-ah-w-noss":["Yes and a half: a cheeky song about saying more than yes to a lover. The groove is quick and tight, a dance beat built on darbuka and programmed drums, with a short string riff and a hook made of the title phrase. Ajram sings with a mischievous, clipped delivery, landing each syllable on the beat. The marker is the punchy title hook, which her audience shouts back.","The title track of her 2004 album, it confirmed the success of Akhasmak Ah and helped make her the leading Arab pop star of the mid-2000s, with endorsement deals and constant airplay on satellite channels. Beirut's studios and video industry were by then producing music for the whole Arab world, with Lebanese singers often performing in Egyptian dialect to reach the widest audience.","Listen to how the producers alternate bars of Arab percussion with bars of Western club beat, so the groove seems to switch languages under the voice. The string riff that answers each line is the descendant of the orchestral interludes of golden-age songs, cut down to a few seconds.","Hugely popular, it was criticised by some as further proof of the commercialisation of Arab music and praised by others for its sharp, compact craft. Ajram has since become a fixture of Arab pop and television, and the song is now heard as a classic of the mid-2000s satellite era.","Listen with the volume up and follow the drums. Then compare it with Amr Diab's Tamally Maak to hear the two faces of Arab pop of the period, the dance song and the ballad, built from the same studio palette."],"rachid-taha-rock-el-casbah":["A cover of The Clash's Rock the Casbah, a song about a ruler banning rock music, sung in Algerian Arabic. The punk riff is still there, but carried by darbuka, Arab strings and a swirling mandole, with Taha's rasping voice half singing, half declaiming. It is louder and rougher than the original. The marker is the moment the famous piano riff returns on Arab strings.","It appeared on Taha's 2004 album Tékitoi, produced by Steve Hillage. Taha had been a Clash fan since his youth in Lyon, and the band's original song of 1982, which imagined the Middle East through Western eyes, had been grimly misused as a slogan during the Gulf War. Taha's version took the song back into Arabic, a decade into a period of war and suspicion between the West and the Arab world.","Taha liked to tell interviewers that he had once pressed a Carte de Séjour cassette on The Clash in Paris, and to hint that it fed their song; true or not, the cover closes a loop. Listen for how the Arabic words rhythmically match the English ones almost syllable for syllable: the translation keeps the punk bounce while changing who is telling the story.","It became one of Taha's best-known songs and won wide praise in France and Britain; Mick Jones of The Clash later joined him on stage to perform it. Critics have read it as a sharp act of reclamation: a Western rock song about Arabs answered, in Arabic, by an Arab rock singer.","Play The Clash's original first, then this. Listen for what the Arab instruments add to the riff, and for how much more menace Taha's voice brings. Follow it with Ya Rayah to hear the gentler, traditional side of the same singer."],"rachid-taha-1-2-3-soleils":["A live concert album in which three Algerian-born singers, Rachid Taha, Khaled and Faudel, share one stage, singing their own hits, each other's songs and Algerian standards. Behind them a big band combines Egyptian-style string section, Arab percussion and rock rhythm section. The sound is festive and enormous, with a cheering arena audience. The marker is the moment the three voices join on one song, raspy, soaring and youthful side by side.","Recorded at the Palais Omnisports de Paris-Bercy in September 1998 and released in 1999, with arrangements by Steve Hillage. Algeria was in the midst of a brutal civil war, and France had just celebrated a multi-ethnic World Cup victory. Three North African stars filling the biggest hall in Paris was read as a statement about who belonged in France.","Listen to the three voices as types: Khaled, the raï star with the big ornamented voice of Oran; Faudel, the young prince of raï with a lighter tone; Taha, not a raï singer at all but a rocker steeped in chaabi. The concert blurs the boundaries between styles that North African audiences hear as quite distinct.","The album was a huge seller in France and became the most famous document of raï and Franco-Maghrebi music at its peak. Some purists saw the production as over-polished arena entertainment. Many still regard it as a high point of Arab music in France, and the songs, including Abdel Kader and Ya Rayah, became anthems for a generation.","Start with the group performance of Abdel Kader, then go back to Khaled's Didi and Taha's Ya Rayah in their studio versions. Listen for the crowd singing along; the album is as much about the audience as the singers. Each singer takes a solo turn, so you can compare them directly."],"souad-massi-raoui":["An album of intimate songs in Algerian Arabic, French and Berber, about exile, love and the storyteller of the title. The sound is built on Souad Massi's fingerpicked acoustic guitar and soft, clear voice, with oud, mandole and light percussion around it. It is closer to folk singer-songwriters than to raï or Arab pop. The marker is the warm acoustic guitar and voice, quiet enough to sound like a private performance.","Massi grew up in the Bab El Oued district of Algiers and played in the rock band Atakor in the 1990s, during the civil war, when women performing in public faced real danger. She moved to Paris after a festival appearance in 1999 and recorded this, her first album for an international label. It found a large audience in France among listeners far beyond the Algerian community.","Listen to the guitar parts: beneath the folk fingerpicking lie the melodic patterns of Algiers chaabi and Andalusian music, which Massi absorbed growing up, and flashes of country and flamenco technique. The songs switch language without warning, mirroring the multilingual reality of Algerian life.","It was widely praised in France and the world-music press and launched an international career. Critics compared her to Joan Baez and Tracy Chapman, comparisons she found limiting but which pointed to something real: a female songwriter's voice rare in Arab popular music. It is now regarded as a landmark of North African folk song.","Listen at low volume, attending to the guitar and the closeness of the voice. Then hear Joni Mitchell or a flamenco guitarist to catch the two traditions she sits between, and Rachid Taha's Ya Rayah for another view of Algeria from Paris."],"souad-massi-deb":["Heartbroken: a second album that deepens the first, with songs about lost love, home and the weight of exile. Acoustic guitar still leads, but the arrangements are fuller, with strings, oud and occasional rock-tinged electric guitar. Massi's voice sounds more confident and more melancholy. The marker is the mix of an Arab string section with a folk guitar strum, as if Cairo and a coffee-house folk club met.","Recorded in France after the success of Raoui, it confirmed her as one of the leading voices of the Algerian diaspora. By 2003 the worst of the Algerian civil war was over, but its aftermath and the experience of exile shaped her writing. Paris remained the crossroads where Maghrebi, African and French musicians recorded together.","Listen for the touches of chaabi in the mandole lines and of Andalusian music in the cadences, and for the rare moments when Massi bends notes like a tarab singer. The album also nods to her rock past with distorted guitars, a reminder that her acoustic style was a choice, not a limitation.","It was well received in France, though some critics found it less fresh than Raoui. Admirers argue it is the more complete record. Massi has since recorded many albums, including settings of classical Arabic poetry, and is regarded as a major voice of North African song.","Begin with the opening tracks and follow how the strings enter around the guitar. Then go back to Raoui and listen for what has grown. Fairuz is a natural companion: another woman's voice in which restraint carries the feeling. Notice how rarely she raises her voice, even at the emotional peaks."],"anouar-brahem-le-pas-du-chat-noir":["The Black Cat's Step: a drumless trio of oud, piano and accordion playing slow, spare compositions. The piano often repeats a few chords or a single figure while the oud states a melody and the accordion breathes long, held tones around it. Many pieces barely rise above a murmur. The marker is the piano's quiet, repeated patterns, closer to Satie than to jazz, with the oud drifting over them.","Brahem recorded it for ECM with the French pianist François Couturier and accordionist Jean-Louis Matinier, both from the European jazz and improvised scene. Coming after Thimar, it moved away from jazz conventions toward chamber music. Manfred Eicher's label, with its reverberant, carefully balanced sound, framed the trio as an art ensemble rather than a jazz group.","Listen to the piano's harmony: Brahem's melodies are modal, built on Arab maqam, and yet Couturier finds Western chords that do not contradict them, often by leaving out the notes that would clash. The accordion can bend pitch slightly with the bellows, which lets it shadow the oud's microtonal inflections in a way a piano cannot.","Widely praised in the jazz and world-music press, it is often named as Brahem's finest album. Some listeners find its stillness too precious; admirers hear an unusually pure kind of chamber music that belongs fully to neither Arab nor Western tradition. The trio recorded again, and the album remains a touchstone for ECM's sound.","Listen in quiet surroundings and let the repetitions settle. Follow the oud melodies, then switch attention to the piano. Compare Satie's Gymnopédies for the stillness, and Brahem's earlier Thimar for his more jazz-rooted sound. Notice how the accordion often enters so softly that you only realise afterwards it has been playing all along."],"anouar-brahem-the-astounding-eyes-of-rita":["A quartet album of oud, bass clarinet, electric bass and Arab hand drums, warmer and more rhythmic than Brahem's piano trio. The pieces are songful, with clear melodies passed between oud and bass clarinet over gentle, rolling grooves. The bass is often played high and melodically. The marker is the bass clarinet's dark, woody tone in close harmony with the oud, a sound both earthy and lyrical.","Recorded for ECM with the German bass clarinettist Klaus Gesing, the Swedish bassist Björn Meyer and the Lebanese percussionist Khaled Yassine. The title draws on a poem by the Palestinian poet Mahmoud Darwish, who had died in 2008, and the album was received as a tribute to him. Brahem had been working between Tunis and Europe for two decades.","The rhythms underneath are Arab cycles, played on darbuka and bendir, but Meyer's bass and Gesing's clarinet often phrase across them, so a piece can feel as though it has two different pulses at once. Listen for the way Yassine plays very softly, using fingertip strokes rather than the loud slaps of dance music.","It was warmly received and is often named alongside Le pas du chat noir among his best records. Critics noted its greater groove and accessibility; a few found it too smooth. Its Darwish dedication gave it extra resonance in the Arab world, where Brahem's ECM work had sometimes been seen as too European.","Start with the opening track and follow the oud and bass clarinet in conversation. Then compare Thimar, where the clarinet player is John Surman: two different reed voices beside the same oud. Read about Darwish afterwards, rather than looking for literal meaning in the music."],"anouar-brahem-blue-maqams":["Brahem's oud with a classic jazz rhythm section: piano, double bass and drums. The pieces are long and unhurried, with melodies in Arab modes stated by the oud and then opened up through improvisation, the drums brushing and swirling rather than keeping strict time. The marker is the sound of the oud floating over a jazz trio, the first time Brahem had worked with a drummer of this stature.","Recorded for ECM with the bassist Dave Holland, who had played on Thimar, the drummer Jack DeJohnette, who had played with Miles Davis and Keith Jarrett, and the British pianist Django Bates. It reunited Brahem with the American jazz tradition after years of chamber projects, and the title announces the meeting: blues and maqam side by side.","Listen for the moments when the jazz musicians must play around notes that do not exist on a piano. Bates often drops out or plays in the high register to leave room for the oud's microtones, and DeJohnette shapes his cymbals to the long Arab rhythmic cycles. The title's two words name two scale systems that, here, share the same tunes.","Critics welcomed it as a meeting of masters, and it was widely listed among the best jazz albums of its year. Some found it long and lacking in surprises; others heard an autumnal, generous record, a summation of Brahem's career. It strengthened the case that maqam and jazz can meet as equals.","Listen first to DeJohnette's drums, unusual on a Brahem record, and how they change the music's breathing. Then compare Thimar, made with Holland twenty years earlier. For the American side of the meeting, try Kind of Blue, another record built on modes."],"rabih-abou-khalil-blue-camel":["A septet record in which Abou-Khalil's oud leads a band of jazz horns, electric bass and Arab and Indian percussion through his own compositions. The pieces run in long, intricate rhythmic cycles, odd-numbered and lopsided, over which the saxophone and flugelhorn improvise as freely as in any jazz session. The marker is the bright, speaking oud lines answered by the warm, airy flugelhorn.","Abou-Khalil, a Lebanese oud player who left Beirut for Munich during the civil war, recorded it for the German label Enja with American jazz musicians including the saxophonist Charlie Mariano, the trumpeter Kenny Wheeler and the bassist Steve Swallow. It came at a time when European labels were actively documenting meetings between jazz and other traditions.","The rhythms are the key: Abou-Khalil writes melodies over Arab rhythmic modes of nine, ten or more beats, and his percussionists keep those cycles while the jazz players solo over them. Listen for how naturally Mariano, who had long experience of Indian music, threads his alto through the uneven bars.","It was praised in the jazz press as one of the most successful fusions of Arab and jazz traditions, and is still often recommended as the best introduction to Abou-Khalil. Some Arab musicians considered his approach too free with the oud tradition; jazz critics generally valued exactly that freedom.","Count along with the drums and see how quickly you lose your place: the cycles are long. Then just follow the oud's melodies. Compare Brahem's Thimar, a quieter and more introverted meeting of the oud with jazz. Notice how the flugelhorn softens every phrase it touches."],"rabih-abou-khalil-arabian-waltz":["Oud, string quartet, tuba and frame drums: an unusual chamber group playing Abou-Khalil's compositions. The strings provide sweeping, sometimes folkloric textures, the tuba supplies a growling, agile bass line and occasionally solos, and the frame drums hold intricate Arab cycles. The marker is the sound of a tuba playing nimble melodies against an Arab rhythm, both comic and moving.","Recorded for Enja with the Balanescu Quartet, a London string quartet known for crossing into pop and folk, the French tubist Michel Godard and the percussionist Nabil Khaiat. Abou-Khalil, by then established in Germany, had long wanted to write for strings, and the chamber setting drew on the Arab tradition of large string ensembles as well as Western quartet writing.","Listen to the title piece: a waltz in name, but the three-beat lilt is folded into a longer Arab cycle, so the dance keeps tripping over itself in an elegant way. The string quartet is asked to play the sliding, ornamented phrases of Arab violin playing, which is why it sometimes sounds more like a Cairo orchestra than a classical quartet.","Critics praised its originality and the string writing, and it is often counted among his best albums. Some classical listeners found the quartet writing thin; jazz listeners missed the horns. Over time it has come to be seen as one of the most successful attempts to write chamber music from within the Arab tradition.","Start with the title track and listen for the waltz hiding inside the Arab rhythm. Then compare Asmahan's Layali al-Uns fi Vienna, an older Arab waltz, and Ravel's Boléro for another obsessive rhythmic frame. Keep an ear on the tuba, which switches between bass line and lead voice without warning."],"dhafer-youssef-abu-nawas-rhapsody":["A jazz quartet record in honour of the medieval Baghdad poet Abu Nawas, famous for poems of wine and pleasure. Youssef plays oud and sings wordless, high falsetto lines that soar over a piano trio, the drums restless and modern, the piano harmonies dense. The marker is Youssef's voice: an extraordinarily high, pure falsetto that floats above the band like a muezzin's call.","Youssef, born in a coastal village in Tunisia, trained as a boy in Quranic chanting before turning to the oud and to jazz in Vienna. Here he worked with the Armenian pianist Tigran Hamasyan, the bassist Chris Jennings and the drummer Mark Guiliana, three younger players who were changing the sound of jazz rhythm sections in the 2010s.","Listen for how the vocal lines, though wordless, carry the melodic shape of religious recitation: long held notes ornamented at the end, short rising figures, a strong central pitch. Hamasyan brings his own Armenian folk modes, which overlap with Arab ones, so the pianist and singer often meet on shared modal ground.","It was widely praised in the European jazz press as one of his strongest records. Some critics felt the falsetto, so striking at first, could become a mannerism; others saw it as one of the most original voices in contemporary jazz. The album helped bring Hamasyan to wider notice.","Listen first just for the voice, then for Guiliana's drums underneath, very different in energy. For contrast hear Brahem's Blue Maqams, another oud-led jazz quartet, far calmer, and Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan for a voice that also reaches ecstasy through Islamic tradition."],"dhafer-youssef-birds-requiem":["A suite-like album of slow, ritual pieces, part requiem, part flight. Oud, voice and kanun carry the Arab and Turkish colours; a trumpet with electronic halo and a clarinet add long, plaintive lines; piano, bass and drums move softly underneath. The textures are wide and atmospheric, and Youssef's falsetto drifts above. The marker is the mix of misty trumpet and Turkish clarinet over a gently pulsing oud.","Youssef recorded it for Okeh with an international group that included the Norwegian trumpeter Nils Petter Molvær and the Turkish clarinettist Hüsnü Şenlendirici. It followed a period of personal loss, and its title and mood reflect mourning. By then he was one of the best-known Arab jazz musicians in Europe, performing at major festivals.","Listen to the meeting of two very different reed and brass traditions: Şenlendirici's clarinet bends and slides in the Turkish Roman style, while Molvær's trumpet is processed into a breathy, ambient cloud. Youssef places them against the oud so that three ways of ornamenting a melody sound at once.","Critics welcomed it as one of his most ambitious and moving records, and it reached audiences beyond jazz. A few found it slow and too atmospheric; admirers hear a contemporary spiritual music crossing three traditions. It cemented his place in European jazz.","Play it as a whole, as a single extended piece, preferably in the evening. Follow the voice and clarinet. Then compare Garbarek's Officium, a Norwegian saxophone over early sacred music, for a similar meeting of jazz and ritual. Let the silences between phrases register as part of the music."],"ibrahim-maalouf-diasporas":["A debut album that sets the quarter-tone trumpet, able to play Arab modes, in a small electric band of guitars, bass and drums. The pieces move from slow laments to driving, rock-tinged grooves, with the trumpet singing melodies in Arab maqam and then improvising. The marker is the trumpet's tone bending onto notes between the Western semitones, a sound that seems to belong to no one tradition.","Maalouf, born in Beirut and raised in France after his family fled the civil war, studied classical trumpet in Paris and played Baroque repertoire before turning to his own music. This was his first album under his own name, made as the French jazz scene was opening to musicians from immigrant backgrounds. The title names his family's experience of scattering.","The trumpet itself is the secret: Maalouf's father, Nassim Maalouf, added a fourth valve to the instrument so it could play quarter tones. Listen for the passages where the melody drops onto one of those in-between notes; it is the sound of an Arab singer's ornament transplanted into brass.","Modest at release, it gained a following in France and began a career that made Maalouf one of the best-selling instrumentalists in the country. Some jazz critics found his later albums too commercial; this debut is often valued as his most personal, rawest statement.","Listen for the quarter-tone moments, which are easy to hear once you know to listen. Then try Kalthoum, his later tribute to Umm Kulthum, to hear the same trumpet in an acoustic jazz quintet. For a classical trumpet comparison, hear any Baroque concerto he once played."],"ibrahim-maalouf-kalthoum":["An acoustic jazz quintet reimagines Umm Kulthum's Alf Leila wa Leila as a continuous suite. The trumpet takes the singer's role, the tenor saxophone and piano weave around it, and the rhythm section moves from swing to Arab rhythmic patterns. Long solos open up the original's sections. The marker is the trumpet phrasing a melody with a singer's pauses and swells, as if breathing her words without saying them.","Released on Impulse!, the historic American jazz label, it featured the pianist Frank Woeste, the saxophonist Mark Turner, the bassist Larry Grenadier and the drummer Clarence Penn, leading New York players. The original song, composed by Baligh Hamdi, was a hit of 1969. Maalouf issued it alongside a very different album, Red & Black Light, to show two sides of his music.","Listen to how the arrangement keeps the structure of the original, the long instrumental introduction and the episodic sections, the way a jazz musician might keep the form of a standard while improvising over it. Maalouf treats Umm Kulthum as a composer of standards, exactly as American jazz treats the Broadway songbook.","It was praised by jazz critics on both sides of the Atlantic and helped introduce Umm Kulthum to a jazz audience. Some Arab listeners found the treatment too free; others welcomed it as a sign of respect from a major jazz label. It is often cited as Maalouf's most serious jazz album.","Listen to the original Alf Leila wa Leila first, at least the opening, then this. Follow the trumpet as the singer and the saxophone as the orchestra. Compare Coltrane's treatment of My Favorite Things for another long jazz meditation on a popular melody."],"mashrou-leila-mashrou-leila":["The debut album of a Beirut band: songs in Lebanese Arabic about love, the city, politics and sexuality, sung with a rich baritone over a mix of indie rock guitars, keyboards and violin. The tunes are catchy and wry, the arrangements jumpy and theatrical. The marker is the electric violin weaving Arab ornaments between guitar riffs, and the singer's dark, expressive voice.","The band formed in 2008 among students at the American University of Beirut, and this first album came out the following year. Lebanon was still recovering from the 2006 war, and young Beirut was hungry for music that spoke about its own life in its own dialect. Singer Hamed Sinno was openly gay, rare in Arab pop, and the band's frankness was part of its appeal.","Listen to the violin, played by Haig Papazian, which often carries the melodic ornaments of Arab classical playing while the rest of the band plays Western indie rock. And notice the use of everyday Beirut slang and Arabic word play, which to local listeners is as important as the music, though we will not quote it here.","It became an instant cult record in Beirut and soon across the Arab world, especially among young urban listeners. Older audiences and conservatives found the subject matter shocking. Critics now regard it as a landmark in Arab independent music, the first album to bring a whole generation's language into rock.","Listen for the violin and the voice together, then for the mix of rhythms. Compare The Smiths for the wry, literate rock song, and Fairuz's Rahbani songs for the Lebanese tradition of witty, urban lyrics the band inherits. Notice how the drums shift between rock beats and Arab patterns."],"mashrou-leila-ibn-el-leil":["Son of the Night: a darker, more electronic album about Beirut nightlife, desire and the emptiness after the party. Synthesizers and drum machines push the songs toward dance music, with violin and guitars woven through, and the singer's baritone sits lower and more brooding. The marker is the throbbing synth bass and four-on-the-floor beat under an Arab-inflected melody.","By 2015 the band was internationally known, touring in Europe and North America, and facing growing hostility at home. In 2016 Jordan cancelled a concert, and in 2017 the waving of a rainbow flag at their Cairo show led to arrests. This album, recorded at the height of their fame, reflects a band confronting both freedom and its dangers.","Listen to how the dance beats are often slightly unsettled, with Arab rhythmic accents layered over the steady club pulse, so the music feels restless rather than euphoric. The album's arc moves through a single night, from going out to coming home, though the songs work alone too.","It was well received by critics in the Arab world and in the international press, which increasingly saw the band as a symbol of Arab LGBTQ visibility and free expression. Some fans missed the rawer earlier sound. The band later went on hiatus amid the pressure and Sinno's move abroad.","Listen at night, in sequence. Then compare the debut to hear how much the sound has changed. Pair with Nancy Ajram's Ah W Noss to hear two different visions of Beirut pop from a decade apart. Listen for where the violin survives inside the electronics, a thread back to the band's first sound."],"omar-souleyman-highway-to-hassake":["A compilation of wedding and party recordings from northeastern Syria: Omar Souleyman's hoarse, urgent voice over furiously fast synthesizer lines and drum machines playing dabke, the region's line dance. The keyboard imitates traditional reed pipes and fiddles with buzzing, bending sounds. The music hardly lets up. The marker is the frantic keyboard solo, a cascade of bent notes at breakneck speed.","Released by the American label Sublime Frequencies, which issued field recordings and cassette-culture music, it collected tracks from Souleyman's huge output of locally sold cassettes, many recorded live at weddings. He had sung in the Jazira region since the 1990s, with the keyboardist Rizan Sa'id and a poet who composed verses on the spot. Few outside Syria had heard of him.","Listen for the voice behind Souleyman's: the poet Mahmoud Harbi often whispers lines to him during performance, and Souleyman sings them back moments later. The keyboard's sound is tuned to Arab and Kurdish modes, quarter tones included, which is why it sounds so alien to Western ears used to synthesizers.","The release caused a stir in Western indie and electronic circles, where Souleyman was taken up as a cult figure. Some critics worried about exoticism and the packaging of Syrian wedding music as Western novelty; others argued it had always been dance music and simply found a new crowd. The debate shaped how such releases are discussed.","Turn it up and do not try to follow it all; dabke is for dancing. Listen for the keyboard solos and the breathless voice. Compare Tinariwen's desert blues for another regional music that crossed to Western audiences in the same years."],"omar-souleyman-wenu-wenu":["Souleyman's first studio album: dabke rebuilt with a cleaner, harder electronic sound. Rizan Sa'id's keyboards race and swirl, the beat thuds like club music, and Souleyman's voice, rough and declamatory, sits right in front. The songs are love songs, short and relentless. The marker is the title track's pulsing, almost hypnotic synth riff, closer to techno than to a village wedding, yet still dabke.","Produced by the British electronic musician Kieran Hebden, known as Four Tet, and recorded in New York, it was released by Ribbon Music. Souleyman had been touring Western festivals since the success of his compilations, and the civil war that broke out in Syria in 2011 had made returning home impossible. The album introduced him to a far wider electronic audience.","Hebden's role is mostly restraint: he leaves the keyboards and voice largely as they were, but sharpens the drums and bass so they hit like a dance-floor record. Listen to the keyboard, which still imitates the mizmar and rababa, the double reed and spike fiddle of rural Syrian dance.","It was praised widely in Western music press and brought Souleyman to major festivals; he also performed at the Nobel Peace Prize concert in 2013. Some critics felt the production smoothed his edges; most saw a respectful collaboration. He remains a symbol of Syrian culture abroad during the long war.","Play the title track loud and listen for how the beat and the keyboard interlock. Then go back to Highway to Hassake to hear the rawer cassette sound, and to Four Tet's Rounds to hear the producer's own world. Notice how little the voice changes; the keyboard carries the drama."],"zeki-muren-manolya":["A slow, tender song in the Turkish art-music style, usually credited to Müren himself, about a love compared to a magnolia flower. His voice carries everything: a soft, rounded tone that swells on long held notes and then turns into tiny ornamental curls. Behind him a small ensemble of oud, kanun zither, violins and ney flute doubles the melody in a loose, shimmering unison, with a light hand-drum keeping the gentle pulse.","By the 1970s Zeki Müren was the most loved singer in Turkey, known as the Sun of Art, a performer who filled nightclubs and casinos and later concert halls. He had trained in the classical Ottoman song tradition, built on the makam system of melodic modes, and he carried it into the age of the long-playing record, radio and television. His shows, with their sequinned costumes and extended stage runways, were an event in themselves.","Listen to how the instruments answer the voice rather than accompany it. After each sung phrase the kanun or violin repeats a fragment of the line, slightly varied, so the song moves in call and echo. Müren also bends certain notes in ways a piano cannot play, because the makam uses intervals smaller than the Western semitone; those slightly flattened pitches are what give the tune its ache.","Songs like this kept Turkish art music popular at a time when Western pop and the new arabesk style were pulling listeners away. Critics of the time sometimes treated Müren's glamour as kitsch, but singers of every later generation, from classical vocalists to pop stars, cite his diction and breath control as a model. His recordings remain the reference for how this repertoire should be phrased.","Listen first only to the voice and notice how clearly every syllable is shaped, even at the softest volume. Then follow the kanun's bright plucked answers. Compare a nineteenth-century song by Dede Efendi to hear the older tradition Müren grew out of, then a Sezen Aksu ballad to hear where Turkish pop took his phrasing next."],"zeki-muren-elbet-bir-gun-bulusacagiz":["One of the best-loved recordings in Müren's catalogue, a song of separation whose title promises that two parted lovers will surely meet again one day. The melody rises in long arches and falls back in small decorated steps, and Müren holds the climactic notes with a full, steady tone before releasing them into a soft sigh. The accompaniment is the classic fasıl group of plucked and bowed instruments moving with the voice.","Müren made his name on state radio in the early 1950s and by the following decade was recording prolifically for Turkish labels. He came from Bursa, studied at the Istanbul Academy of Fine Arts, and absorbed the art-song repertoire from older masters of the Ottoman tradition. This period saw Turkish art music move from the elite drawing room and the radio studio into nightclubs, films and the record shops of every city.","Notice the short instrumental passages between verses. They are not filler: they restate the melody in the instruments' own register and often move briefly into a neighbouring mode before slipping home, a modulation the singer then confirms. Müren's habit of pronouncing every consonant crisply, even in the most emotional moments, was deliberate and much imitated; he treated Turkish diction as part of the music.","The song became a standard, sung at gatherings and covered by later art-music and pop singers alike, and many listeners know it only through Müren's version. Writers on Turkish music point to recordings like this as the high point of a style that later lost ground to arabesk and pop. Others note that his popularity helped keep the art-music repertoire alive for a mass audience.","Hear it as a single long breath: follow the line from its quiet opening to the high held note and listen for how Müren lets the sound fade rather than end. Then compare Manolya to hear the same voice in a lighter mood, and Abdel Halim Hafez for a kindred Arabic crooner of the same years."],"sezen-aksu-firuze":["The title song of Sezen Aksu's 1982 album, a gentle song addressed to a young girl named Firuze, the Turkish word for turquoise, wishing her well as she grows up. It opens with a lilting melody over soft strings and builds into a warm chorus that rises in a single swoop. Aksu sings it with an intimate, slightly husky tone, close to the microphone, more confiding than performing.","Aksu had been recording since the mid-1970s and by the early 1980s was writing much of her own material, something unusual for a Turkish female singer then. Turkey had just come through the military coup of 1980, and television and radio were tightly controlled. Pop music that was neither Western imitation nor arabesk was still finding its shape, and Aksu, writing songs that sounded Turkish in melody but modern in production, was helping to define it.","Listen to the melody against the chords. Aksu writes tunes that lean on the intervals of Turkish modal music, but the arrangement places them over Western harmony, so certain notes sound gently bittersweet. That blend, folk-like contour inside a pop frame, became her signature. The song also works as a lullaby; its rocking rhythm is closer to a cradle song than a dance track.","Firuze became one of Aksu's best-loved songs and a perennial at family occasions, where parents sing it to their daughters. In retrospect critics see the album as a turning point, the moment Aksu moved from successful singer to the central songwriter of Turkish pop, a role she would hold for decades. Her influence on later writers and singers is very hard to overstate.","Listen first for the way the chorus lifts, then go back and hear how quietly the verse begins. Compare a Zeki Müren song for the older art-song phrasing she inherited, then play her 1991 album Gülümse to hear the mature Aksu sound in full, polished production."],"sezen-aksu-gulumse":["Sezen Aksu's 1991 album, whose title means Smile, gathers upbeat dance-pop, mid-tempo ballads and quietly sad songs into one of the most successful records in Turkish pop history. The sound is glossy early-nineties production, synthesisers and drum programming, but woven through it are strings, Turkish percussion and melodies built on modal turns. The title track is a song of encouragement to someone in grief, sung with restraint over a swaying beat.","By 1991 Aksu was the undisputed queen of Turkish pop and a mentor to a circle of younger songwriters and singers. Turkey's media were about to open up: private radio and television stations appeared in the early 1990s, ending the state broadcaster's monopoly and creating a boom for pop. This album arrived just as that market exploded, and its songs were everywhere for years afterwards.","Listen for how many of the arrangements stop the electronic beat for a moment and leave a single Turkish instrument, a ney flute or a clarinet line, alone in the mix. These small openings remind the listener where the melodies come from. Aksu also hides wry, conversational phrasing in the vocal, speaking lines almost as dialogue, which gives the dance songs a theatrical edge.","The album sold enormously and is often ranked among the greatest Turkish pop records. Some critics at the time found the production too slick, but its songs have aged well and are still covered by younger artists. It cemented Aksu's reputation as a songwriter whose work could be both commercially dominant and emotionally serious, a combination critics continue to praise.","Start with the title song and listen to how the melody stays low and calm, as if the singer is speaking to a friend. Then move to the dance tracks for contrast. Pair it with Firuze for her earlier, plainer sound and with Işık Doğudan Yükselir to hear where she turned next, toward the folk roots under her pop."],"sezen-aksu-isik-dogudan-yukselir":["An album whose title means Light Rises from the East, in which Sezen Aksu sets aside pop production for arrangements of folk songs and folk-style songs drawn from across Anatolia, the Black Sea, the Aegean and the Balkans. Each track carries the sound of its region: the bright bowed kemençe of the Black Sea coast, the long-necked bağlama of central Anatolia, the clarinet of the western towns. Her voice is plainer here, unadorned, closer to a village singer than a pop star.","The mid-1990s in Turkey were years of political violence, especially the conflict in the Kurdish southeast, and of fierce argument about national identity. Aksu made a record that celebrated the many peoples and languages of Anatolia side by side, a quietly political act. She worked with traditional musicians from different regions, and the album belongs to a wider return to roots that swept Turkish popular music in that decade.","Notice that the songs are not all in Turkish and not all from inside Turkey's borders. The album moves between languages and regions on purpose, so that its running order is itself an argument about a shared musical geography. Listen also for the rhythms: several tracks use the uneven, limping metres of Anatolian and Balkan dance, counted in groups of nine or seven.","Critics greeted the album as Aksu's most ambitious project, and it opened the door for other Turkish pop stars to record folk material without seeming old-fashioned. Some nationalist commentators objected to its multilingual vision, which only increased its standing among its admirers. It is now regarded as a landmark of the Turkish roots revival and a model of how a pop star can serve a tradition.","Treat it as a journey and listen in order, noticing how the instruments change as the songs move from region to region. Pick out the Black Sea track by its fast, buzzing kemençe. Then hear Âşık Veysel's Uzun İnce Bir Yoldayım for the minstrel tradition beneath it, and the Düğün ve Cenaze album with Goran Bregović for its Balkan sequel."],"sezen-aksu-dugun-ve-cenaze":["A collaboration between Sezen Aksu and Goran Bregović, the title meaning Wedding and Funeral. Bregović's orchestra supplies the brass, the deep male chorus and the stomping rhythms of his film scores; Aksu wrote Turkish words for many of his melodies and sings them in her own phrasing. The record swings between raucous brass dances and slow laments, the two sides of Balkan ceremonial life its title names.","Bregović had become internationally famous through his scores for Emir Kusturica's films and was touring Europe with his Wedding and Funeral Orchestra. Aksu, after her folk album of 1995, wanted to explore the shared music of the former Ottoman lands. The meeting of a Turkish pop queen and a Sarajevo-born composer, two years after the end of the Bosnian war, carried obvious symbolic weight across the region.","Many melodies here will sound familiar to anyone who knows the Kusturica soundtracks, because they are Bregović's tunes in new clothes. What changes is the vocal line: Aksu reshapes the phrasing to fit Turkish stress patterns, so tunes that were brass riffs become songs. Listen too for the moments when the chorus sings in a language other than hers, a reminder of where the material travelled from.","The album sold well in Turkey and toured widely, and many listeners consider it one of Aksu's finest records. Some reviewers found Bregović's arrangements formulaic, the same brass gestures recycled from his earlier work, and the question of his borrowing from Romani and traditional sources hangs over this project as over his others. Its best tracks still sound joyful and grand.","Begin with one of the fast brass numbers and listen for the moment the whole band drops out and Aksu is left with a single instrument. Then play Bregović's Underground soundtrack to hear the originals, and Fanfare Ciocărlia for the unpolished village brass that both of them draw on."],"baris-manco-daglar-daglar":["A slow, brooding rock song in the voice of someone calling out to the mountains in his loneliness, issued as a two-part single. A heavy, swaying beat and fuzzy electric guitar carry a melody that sounds like an Anatolian folk song, and Manço's deep voice cuts through with long, mournful lines. The guitar answers the singer in figures that imitate the bağlama, the long-necked lute of Turkish folk music.","Turkish rock bands of the late 1960s first copied Western hits, but a competition called the Altın Mikrofon, run by the newspaper Hürriyet, encouraged them to set Turkish folk material to rock instruments. Barış Manço, who had studied art in Belgium, moved between Istanbul and Europe in these years and became one of the leaders of what came to be called Anatolian rock. This single was among his first big hits at home.","Listen to the guitar between verses. Instead of Western blues licks it plays rapid repeated notes and slides between pitches, a direct translation of folk lute technique onto an electric instrument. The song's slow, heavy pulse also keeps a faint lilt of a folk dance rhythm underneath the rock backbeat, which is why it feels both familiar and strange to Western ears.","The record was a huge success and helped persuade a generation of Turkish musicians that rock could speak with a local accent. Western listeners discovered it decades later through reissues and compilations of Anatolian psychedelia, and it is now treated as a classic of the genre. Manço's later career as a television personality sometimes obscured how adventurous his early records were.","Start by following the guitar rather than the voice and listen for the moments where it sounds like a folk lute. Then compare Erkin Koray's Elektronik Türküler for the same idea taken further, and Altın Gün's Gece to hear how a Dutch band revived this sound half a century later."],"baris-manco-donence":["A long, slowly building song recorded with Manço's band Kurtalan Ekspres, about the turning of time and the seasons, the title meaning something like turning point or solstice. It starts quietly, almost spoken, over a soft keyboard figure, then gathers guitars, drums and strings into an epic, rolling climax. Manço's voice, deep and unhurried, gives the whole thing the feeling of a tale told by an elder.","Manço formed Kurtalan Ekspres in the early 1970s and worked with them for the rest of his career. By the early 1980s, after the 1980 military coup, Turkish rock had largely retreated, and Manço adapted by writing songs that mixed progressive rock, synthesisers and folk storytelling, often with a moral or philosophical message. He was becoming a national figure, beloved by children and adults alike.","Notice how the arrangement mirrors the song's subject: the music circles back on the same few chords and the same melodic shape, but each pass is louder and fuller, so the song itself turns like the cycle it describes. Listen near the end for the keyboard and guitar playing interlocking lines that resemble the layered patterns of Turkish folk ensembles.","Dönence has become one of Manço's most revered songs, regularly named among the greatest in Turkish rock and covered by many younger bands. Critics who dismissed his 1980s work as family entertainment have revised that view, hearing in songs like this a serious progressive-rock ambition. It is now often played at memorials and public gatherings in his honour.","Give it its full seven minutes without skipping; the payoff depends on the slow build. Listen for the first entry of the drums and then for each new layer. Compare Pink Floyd's Wish You Were Here for a kindred slow-burning epic, and go back to Dağlar Dağlar to hear how far Manço travelled in a decade."],"erkin-koray-debut-album":["Erkin Koray's first long-playing record, gathering songs he had been releasing as singles, and one of the first full albums of Turkish rock. The sound is raw late-sixties psychedelia: fuzz guitar, rumbling bass, organ and loose drums, with Koray's nasal, intense voice on top. Some tracks are love songs in a Western mould; others turn Turkish melodies into long, droning guitar workouts that point to his later experiments.","Koray had been playing rock and roll in Istanbul since the late 1950s, when he was still at school, and is often called the father of Turkish rock. Through the 1960s he moved from covers to original songs in Turkish, at a time when long hair and electric guitars drew hostility from conservative quarters and occasional violence. In the 45-rpm market of the day, an album was a statement.","Listen to the guitar tone. Koray sought ways to make Western instruments play Turkish intervals, bending strings and using slides, and he was among the first to electrify the saz, or bağlama, adding pickups to the folk lute. On this record you can hear him searching for that sound before he found it fully, with some solos hovering between blues and makam.","Little noticed outside Turkey for decades, the album was rediscovered by collectors in the 2000s and became prized as an early document of non-Western psychedelia. Turkish critics had long honoured Koray as a pioneer, though his uncompromising style kept him from the commercial success of Barış Manço or Cem Karaca. Today he is regarded as the most purely experimental of the Anatolian rock founders.","Listen with headphones for the guitar tone and its odd, bending pitches. Pick the most Turkish-sounding track and compare it with the most Western one. Then play Jimi Hendrix's Axis: Bold as Love for the model he admired, and Elektronik Türküler for the fuller fusion he reached the next year."],"erkin-koray-elektronik-turkuler":["The title means Electronic Folk Songs, and the record does exactly that: traditional Turkish türküs and Koray's own songs in the same style, played on electric instruments with heavy psychedelic arrangements. The sound is dominated by his electrified saz, whose buzzing, metallic tone sits somewhere between a sitar and a fuzz guitar. Rhythms come from folk dances, often in uneven metres, driven by a rock drum kit.","By 1974 the Anatolian rock movement was at its height in Istanbul, with bands competing to fuse folk melodies and rock power. Turkey was politically polarised between left and right, and folk music carried strong associations with rural life and with the left. Koray stayed largely apart from politics, pursuing a sound. Recording technology in Turkish studios was basic, and much of the album's rawness comes from that.","The electric saz is the secret. A saz has frets that divide the octave into more steps than a guitar, so Koray's electrified version could play the true folk intervals with rock volume, something an electric guitar could only fake. Listen for the drone strings ringing under the melody; they create a hypnotic hum that links this record to Indian and Middle Eastern music rather than to Western rock.","The album was admired by Turkish musicians but sold modestly. Its reputation grew enormously with reissues in the 2000s, when Western crate-diggers and labels turned Anatolian psych into a cult. Now it is a cornerstone of that canon, and revival bands such as Altın Gün have drawn openly on the Turkish records of this period.","Listen for the moment the electric saz takes its first solo and notice how its tone differs from a guitar. Count the beats in the dance tracks and you may find odd groupings. Then hear Altın Gün's Gece for the modern revival and Âşık Veysel for the acoustic minstrel tradition these songs come from."],"selda-bagcan-selda":["Selda Bağcan's first album, a set of folk songs and protest songs set to a rough, electric rock backing. Her voice is the centre: strong, hard-edged, full of grit, singing without ornament and with a directness that sounds like a public speech. Behind her, bağlama, fuzz guitar and early synthesiser swirl around dance rhythms, and several songs are adaptations of poems or traditional songs about injustice and the life of the poor.","Mid-1970s Turkey was torn by street battles between left and right, strikes and political killings. Bağcan, who had studied physics, became the leading voice of the left-wing folk-protest movement, singing songs by and about workers, peasants and political prisoners. Rock musicians, probably including members of the group Moğollar, backed her on these sessions, linking the protest-folk scene with Anatolian rock.","Listen to how the electric instruments are used. Rather than playing chords under her, they often double the bağlama's melody line, so the arrangement thickens a folk texture rather than replacing it. Some songs set the words of the great folk poets of the past, the aşıks, whose old verses of protest against landlords and governors took on fresh meaning in the 1970s.","Her songs were hugely popular and politically dangerous: after the 1980 coup she was imprisoned several times and for years was denied a passport. The album reached Western audiences only with a 2006 reissue that made it a cult object among psych collectors, and her songs were sampled by hip-hop producers. In Turkey she remains a symbol of principled resistance.","Listen first for the voice and its refusal to soften anything. Then focus on the guitar and synthesiser and hear how they bend around folk melodies. Compare Victor Jara for another singer whose folk songs became political acts, and Erkin Koray's Elektronik Türküler for the rock side of the same fusion."],"theodorakis-epitaphios":["A cycle of eight songs setting parts of Yannis Ritsos's poem Epitaphios, in which a mother mourns her son, a worker shot during a strike. In the 1960 recording that made it famous, the singer Grigoris Bithikotsis is accompanied by bouzouki player Manolis Chiotis and a small popular-music band. The melodies are simple, singable and grave, cast in the rhythms of Greek popular dances, the slow zeibekiko and the hasapiko.","Ritsos wrote the poem in 1936 after police fired on striking tobacco workers in Thessaloniki. Theodorakis, a classically trained composer studying in Paris, set it in 1958, deciding to use the bouzouki and the language of rebetiko instead of the concert hall. In 1960 two rival recordings appeared, one arranged by Manos Hadjidakis with Nana Mouskouri, polished and orchestral, and Theodorakis's own rawer version.","Listen to the bouzouki's role. Before Epitaphios the instrument belonged to the tavern and to rebetiko, music of the urban underclass, and many in the Greek establishment looked down on it. By giving it great poetry, Theodorakis claimed it for art. The two 1960 versions also present a choice that still divides listeners: Hadjidakis's lyrical European arrangement against Theodorakis's earthy popular one.","The record caused a sensation and set off an argument about whether high poetry belonged with popular instruments; it is often taken as the birth of éntekhno, the art-popular song. The right-wing government disliked Ritsos's communism and Theodorakis's politics, and after the 1967 coup their music was banned. Today the cycle is part of Greek cultural memory.","Start with the Bithikotsis version and listen for the bouzouki introduction to each song, then hear the Hadjidakis version with Mouskouri and decide which you prefer. Go back to Tsitsanis to hear the rebetiko world the bouzouki came from, and on to Axion Esti for Theodorakis's larger setting of Greek poetry."],"theodorakis-axion-esti":["A popular oratorio on Odysseas Elytis's long poem of the same name, about Greece, its landscape, its suffering in war and occupation, and its endurance. Theodorakis combines a symphony orchestra and chorus with a popular singer, originally Grigoris Bithikotsis, a narrator reciting the prose sections, and folk instruments like bouzouki and santouri. Choral passages that sound almost Byzantine alternate with songs in the rhythms of Greek dance.","Elytis published the poem in 1959; he would later receive the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1979. Theodorakis worked on the setting in the early 1960s and premiered it in 1964 in Athens, at a moment when he was also a left-wing member of parliament and a leading political figure. The work aimed to bring the high Greek literary tradition and the music of ordinary people together in one sound.","Listen to the choral writing in the slower sections. Theodorakis borrows the sound of Orthodox church chant, with its drone and its modal melodies, especially in the passages that parallel the poem's liturgical structure, which itself echoes the Byzantine service. The popular songs embedded in this frame then sound like voices from the street breaking into a cathedral.","The premiere was a major event and the songs from it quickly became standards. After the 1967 junta banned his music, Axion Esti became a symbol of resistance and was sung in secret. Some classical critics found the mixture of idioms uneven, but in Greece it is regarded as one of the central works of the twentieth century.","Begin with one of the popular songs sung by Bithikotsis, then listen to the full work to hear how they sit within the choral and orchestral frame. Notice the moment the bouzouki emerges from the orchestra. Compare the Byzantine Akathistos hymn for the church sound behind it and Stravinsky's Les noces for another composer setting folk ritual for art forces."],"theodorakis-zorba-the-greek":["Theodorakis's music for Michael Cacoyannis's film of Nikos Kazantzakis's novel, best known for the dance at its end, often called the sirtaki. That piece begins with a slow, stately bouzouki melody over a steady beat and accelerates, phrase by phrase, until it becomes a whirling, breathless dance. Around it the score sets Cretan-flavoured folk textures, laments and themes for the characters, all played by a small popular band and orchestra.","Cacoyannis shot the film on Crete with Anthony Quinn as Zorba and Alan Bates as the bookish Englishman he befriends. Theodorakis, already famous in Greece for Epitaphios, was asked for a score that would sound authentically Greek to a world audience. The final dance was created for the film, and its steps, though inspired by the old hasapiko, were essentially new; many tourists later took them for an ancient tradition.","Listen to the acceleration. The dance tune repeats the same simple melody over and over while the tempo climbs, so the excitement comes almost entirely from speed and the gradual addition of instruments. That is a trick taken from real Greek and Balkan dance, where musicians push the tempo as dancers tire. It is also why the piece works without any harmonic change.","The film was an international success and the dance became the most famous piece of Greek music in the world, played at weddings, in tavernas and at sports stadiums. Purists have objected that it fixed a tourist cliché of Greek culture. Theodorakis himself was ambivalent about its fame eclipsing his serious work, but its craftsmanship is real.","Listen to the final dance from the very quiet start and try to notice the precise moment it begins to speed up. Then hear the other cues, which are less known and more varied. Compare Hadjidakis's Never on Sunday for the other great Greek film song of the era, and Morricone's westerns for how film music made a regional sound global."],"maria-farantouri-mauthausen":["Maria Farantouri's recording of the four songs Mikis Theodorakis wrote on poems by Iakovos Kambanellis, a survivor of the Mauthausen concentration camp. The songs speak of love and loss inside and after the camp. Farantouri's deep, dark contralto, steady and almost without vibrato, carries melodies of great simplicity over bouzouki and a small band, and the restraint of the performance gives the subject its weight.","Kambanellis, later one of Greece's leading playwrights, had been imprisoned at Mauthausen during the war and wrote the poems from that memory. Theodorakis set them in 1965, and Farantouri, then still a teenager, became their voice; she would be his principal singer for decades. The cycle appeared just before the 1967 colonels' coup, after which Theodorakis was arrested and his music banned.","Listen for how little the singer does. Farantouri barely ornaments, rarely rises in volume, and lets the melody speak; the effect is closer to a chant than a pop song. The first song, often called Song of Songs, borrows the title of the biblical love poem, an ironic and moving choice for a song set among the camp's horrors.","The cycle has become one of the best-known musical memorials of the Holocaust and has been sung in many languages, including Hebrew and German, at commemorations across Europe. Critics consider Farantouri's performance definitive. Some listeners initially found her style too severe for popular song, but that severity is now heard as its strength and integrity.","Listen in a quiet room and follow only the voice through the first song, noticing how it stays low and level. Then compare Theodorakis's Epitaphios, sung by a man in a tavern style, to hear how different his music sounds in a woman's dark voice. Selda Bağcan offers a Turkish contemporary who also sang against repression."],"hadjidakis-never-on-sunday":["The song Melina Mercouri sings in Jules Dassin's film Never on Sunday, its Greek title meaning The Children of Piraeus, about the joy of living in the port city. It is a cheerful, light melody in a swinging dance rhythm, with a bouzouki playing bright runs between the phrases. The tune is short and repetitive, built to be sung by anyone, and its open, sunny character is unmistakable within the first few seconds.","Dassin, an American director blacklisted in Hollywood, made the film in Greece with Mercouri as a free-spirited Piraeus woman. Hadjidakis, already a respected composer who had championed rebetiko in a famous 1949 lecture, wrote a score drawing on popular bouzouki music. The song won the Academy Award for Best Original Song, the first such honour for Greek music, and turned the bouzouki into an international symbol.","Listen to the bouzouki part, which plays in a style taken from rebetiko but with the darker modes removed, so it sounds bright and tourist-friendly. Hadjidakis knew exactly what he was doing: he admired the real rebetiko and in this song deliberately polished it for a world audience. The original Greek song is quite different from the many English and other versions that followed.","The song became a worldwide hit in dozens of cover versions. Hadjidakis grew uneasy with its fame and with the clichéd image of Greece it helped spread, and in later years he sometimes spoke of it with irony. Critics regard it as a fine piece of light music whose success opened foreign ears to the deeper tradition behind it.","Listen to Mercouri's original in the film soundtrack and notice the bouzouki breaks between lines. Then hear Tsitsanis or Vamvakaris for the real rebetiko Hadjidakis was smoothing out, and Gioconda's Smile to hear how serious and private his own music could be."],"hadjidakis-giocondas-smile":["An instrumental suite of short pieces for a small orchestra, each a portrait of a mood or a woman, unified by Hadjidakis's gentle, melancholy melodies. There is no singing and almost no Greek folk colour; instead, delicate piano lines, sighing strings and soft woodwind solos trace tunes that sound like songs without words. The whole album has the hushed, intimate quality of chamber music heard late at night.","By the mid-1960s Hadjidakis was famous for Never on Sunday and for countless film and theatre scores, and he was increasingly restless with Greek musical life. Gioconda's Smile was a personal project, released internationally, and stands apart from his popular songs. He would soon spend several years living in New York, away from Greece and its politics, which the 1967 coup made harder still.","The title refers to the Mona Lisa, known in Italian as La Gioconda, and the music shares that painting's ambiguity: many melodies hover between major and minor, happy and sad. Listen for short motifs that pass between instruments and return in later pieces, quietly binding what sounds like a loose collection into a single cycle.","The album was admired on release and has grown in reputation, often named by Greek critics as Hadjidakis's finest record and by many listeners as a favourite. It is sometimes compared with French film music and with Satie for its quiet melancholy. Its refusal of obvious Greekness was itself a statement, a contrast to the tourist sound of his own hit.","Play it without distraction, perhaps late in the evening, and follow the piano through the opening piece. Notice the moments where a woodwind takes over the tune. Compare Never on Sunday to hear the public Hadjidakis, and Theodorakis's Axion Esti for a contemporary who took Greek music in the opposite, grand and public direction."],"hadjidakis-o-megalos-erotikos":["A song cycle whose title means The Great Erotic, setting love poetry from across Greek history, from ancient lyric and Euripides through medieval and folk verse to modern poets such as Cavafy, Solomos and Elytis. The singers, Flery Dandonaki and Dimitris Psarianos, alternate over sparse accompaniment centred on piano. The songs are slow, transparent and intensely lyrical, moving between art song and popular song without settling in either.","Hadjidakis made the record during the years of the military junta, partly while living abroad. Choosing to anthologise three thousand years of Greek love poetry was quietly defiant at a time when the colonels promoted a narrow, militarised idea of Greek identity. He took great care over the choice of voices, preferring untrained, pure tones to operatic or popular stars.","Listen to the way the cycle is arranged chronologically through the poetry, so that the music subtly changes character as the centuries pass, though the instrumental sound stays constant. Hadjidakis also writes melodies that follow the natural stress of each text, which in ancient Greek and modern Greek are quite different; the shift of rhythm is the clue to the era.","Many Greek critics regard the album as Hadjidakis's crowning work, a perfect balance of poetic refinement and melodic simplicity. It sold modestly at first and grew into a classic. Its quiet, inward style influenced a whole school of Greek art-song writers, and it is still performed in concert, though the original singers' versions are considered definitive.","Choose one song whose poet you know, perhaps a Cavafy setting, and listen for how the melody serves the words. Then hear the cycle in order. Compare Theodorakis's Epitaphios for a different way of setting Greek poetry, earthy and public, and Fairuz for another singer of art-popular song in the eastern Mediterranean."],"haris-alexiou-ta-tragoudia-tis-haroulas":["Haris Alexiou's album of songs by the composer Manos Loizos, its title meaning Haroula's Songs, Haroula being her affectionate nickname. The music draws on popular Greek dance rhythms, the zeibekiko, hasapiko and tsifteteli, with bouzouki and accordion, but the melodies and lyrics are reflective, urban and modern. Alexiou's voice is warm, slightly smoky, conversational in the verses and powerful in the climaxes.","Greece had returned to democracy in 1974 after seven years of military rule, and the late 1970s saw an outpouring of music that blended the éntekhno tradition with popular laïko styles. Loizos, a left-wing composer who had suffered under the junta, wrote these songs specifically for Alexiou, who was becoming the leading female singer of her generation. He died in 1982, making this one of his last major works.","Listen to the way Loizos's melodies borrow the modes of rebetiko but avoid its typical melodic clichés; the songs sound traditional at first and then turn in unexpected directions. Alexiou often begins a phrase almost speaking and moves into full voice only at its end, a technique that makes the songs feel like confessions overheard rather than performances.","The album was a major success and is now considered a classic of Greek popular music, often listed among the best Greek albums of the post-junta era. It established Alexiou as a singer who could carry serious song alongside popular repertoire, and Loizos's reputation has grown steadily since his early death. Its songs remain staples in Greek music halls.","Begin with a slow zeibekiko and listen to the bouzouki's introduction before the voice enters. Then compare Tsitsanis to hear the older rebetiko these songs grow from, and Hadjidakis's O Megalos Erotikos for a more refined, art-song approach to the same period's ideals."],"saban-bajramovic-a-gypsy-legend":["A collection that introduced Šaban Bajramović to Western listeners, gathering his Romani songs in a Balkan band setting. His voice is the attraction: deep, husky, full of improvised slides and cries, both tender and rough. Behind him accordion, clarinet and violin trade quick, ornamented lines over dance rhythms, some fast and stamping, others slow and swaying, and the songs move between Romani, Serbian and occasional other languages.","Bajramović, born in Niš in southern Serbia in 1936, was already a legend in Yugoslavia for recordings made from the 1960s onward. He began singing seriously while serving a prison sentence on the island of Goli Otok, imposed for desertion from the army. By the 2000s, the world-music market and the success of Balkan brass and Bregović's scores opened Western Europe to his older style.","Listen to how freely Bajramović treats the beat. In the slower songs he lags behind or rushes ahead of the band, stretching some syllables and compressing others, a jazz-like freedom he was proud of; he loved jazz and spoke of it often. In the faster tracks, the accordion and clarinet often play in parallel, ornamenting the same melody differently, creating a shimmering, slightly blurred unison.","Western critics hailed the record as an introduction to one of the great Romani voices, and Bajramović was nicknamed the King of Gypsy music. Several of his songs were taken up in film soundtracks and by other artists, sometimes without credit, a recurring grievance in Romani music. He died in poverty in 2008, which drew attention to how little he had earned from his fame.","Start with a slow song and listen for the way the voice moves around the beat. Then pick a fast dance number and follow the clarinet. Compare Esma Redžepova for a female Romani star of the same generation, and Bregović's Ederlezi for how Romani song entered Balkan film music."],"esma-redzepova-caje-sukarije":["Esma Redžepova's most famous song, sung in Romani, praising the beauty of a young woman. It moves in a quick, bouncing dance rhythm with an accordion and clarinet leading the instrumental line and a hand drum driving the beat. Redžepova's voice is bright, powerful and full of quick ornaments, and the whole band builds a sense of festive joy that keeps rising toward the end.","Redžepova was born in Skopje in 1943 and was discovered as a teenager by the musician Stevo Teodosievski, who became her husband and bandleader. Together they built a career that made her the first Romani star of Yugoslav radio and records, at a time when Romani artists were usually treated as anonymous entertainers. Yugoslav state media under Tito encouraged the performance of minority folk traditions.","Listen to the way the rhythm is felt rather than counted: the dance is in a lilting pattern that Western ears tend to hear as straight but which has a slight lengthening on certain beats. Redžepova's ornaments also follow traditional Romani vocal technique, quick turns and catches in the throat that are almost impossible to notate, which gives the song its shine.","The song became one of the best-known Romani melodies in the world and was later used in the 2006 film Borat without her permission, which led to legal action. Redžepova's reputation grew from local star to international ambassador of Romani music; she was celebrated as the Queen of the Gypsies and represented Macedonia at the Eurovision Song Contest in 2013.","Listen for the moment the clarinet joins the voice and how the tempo seems to push forward. Then compare Šaban Bajramović for the male Romani voice of the same generation and Fanfare Ciocărlia for brass versions of this dance energy. Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances offer a classical composer's response to similar music."],"ivo-papasov-orpheus-ascending":["Ivo Papasov's first internationally released album, recorded with his band Trakiya. The music is Bulgarian wedding music at full throttle: lightning-fast clarinet improvisations over dance rhythms in uneven metres, groups of seven, nine and eleven beats, driven by electric bass and drum kit. Saxophone and accordion join in breakneck unisons, and there are moments of jazz-like freedom amid the folk melodies.","Wedding bands in communist Bulgaria played for days at a time, mixing folk, Turkish, Romani and Western pop elements. The regime disapproved of this hybrid music and, during the 1980s campaign against Bulgaria's Turkish minority, harassed musicians who played Turkish-flavoured styles; Papasov, of Romani-Turkish origin, was briefly imprisoned. This album appeared on Hannibal Records in the year the communist government fell.","Listen to how the rhythms are counted. A tune in eleven might feel like a stumble at first, but the band locks into it with total ease, and Papasov's clarinet phrases often run across the bar lines, as if he were deliberately disguising the beat. The tunes also borrow freely from Turkish modes, a fact politically charged at that time in Bulgaria.","Western critics responded with amazement; the record was compared with the most virtuosic jazz and introduced the term Bulgarian wedding music to Western listeners. Some world-music critics debated whether the Western interest exoticised the music, but Papasov's own technical command made such arguments almost irrelevant. He remains regarded as one of the great clarinettists of any tradition.","Pick one fast track and try to clap along; you will quickly feel the uneven groupings. Then listen to the clarinet improvisations for their blurring speed and their sudden lyrical turns. Compare Bartók's Six Dances in Bulgarian Rhythm for a composer's take on the same metres, and Naftule Brandwein for an older clarinet virtuoso from a neighbouring tradition."],"ivo-papasov-balkanology":["The follow-up to Orpheus Ascending, again with Papasov's band, in a fuller, more polished production. The pieces are longer and more varied: some fast dance tunes in complex metres, some slower improvisations where the clarinet sings in long, ornamented phrases over a drone. Electric guitar and saxophone take more solo space, and the record pushes further toward jazz fusion while keeping wedding-music energy.","Released two years after the fall of communism in Bulgaria, the album came as Papasov was touring Western Europe and America for the first time to enthusiastic audiences. Bulgarian music was briefly fashionable in the West, thanks also to the success of the Mystère des Voix Bulgares choral records. Wedding bands at home were now free to play what they liked, and the style was evolving fast.","Listen in the slow pieces for the free, unmetered introductions where Papasov plays alone or over a single sustained note; these are the taksim tradition of Turkish and Balkan music, an improvised prelude that explores the mode before the dance begins. Notice how he often hints at the coming dance tune near the end of the improvisation.","Critics praised the record for its ambition and polish, though some preferred the rawness of Orpheus Ascending. His reputation as a virtuoso was cemented by the tours that followed, where Western audiences heard the band's speed at first hand. Today both albums are considered essential documents of the Balkan wedding music scene at the moment it reached the world.","Start with a slow track to hear the improvised introduction, then put on a fast one and listen for the moment the full band crashes in. Compare Le Mystère des Voix Bulgares for the other Bulgarian music fashionable at the time, and Mahavishnu Orchestra for jazz-rock virtuosity in unusual metres."],"bijelo-dugme-kad-bi-bio-bijelo-dugme":["Bijelo Dugme's debut album, its title meaning If I Were a White Button, a play on the band's name. The record mixes hard rock in the style of early-seventies British bands with melodies and rhythms drawn from Bosnian and Balkan folk song. Goran Bregović's guitar riffs are loud and blues-based, Željko Bebek's high voice rides above them, and between the rock tracks come ballads with a folk lilt.","Bijelo Dugme formed in Sarajevo and quickly became the biggest band in Yugoslavia. Tito's Yugoslavia was more open to Western culture than other socialist states, and rock had a large youth audience, but local bands had mostly copied foreign models. Bregović's idea of mixing rock with folk, which critics called shepherd rock, gave Yugoslav rock its own voice and made the album a phenomenon.","Listen to the guitar figures in the faster songs: some riffs are built on folk scales rather than blues scales, and certain melodic turns would be at home in a Bosnian village song. That small shift is what the press meant by shepherd rock. Listen too for Bebek's ornaments at the ends of phrases, little catches borrowed from folk singing rather than from Robert Plant.","The album sold in unprecedented numbers for a Yugoslav rock record and launched a decade of Bijelo Dugme dominance. Some rock purists mocked the folk element, but it became the template for much later Yugoslav rock. Today it is regarded as one of the most important debuts in the history of the region's popular music.","Listen first for the loud, riff-driven opener and then for the folk-tinged ballads, noticing how the same band shifts style. Compare Led Zeppelin III for a British band blending hard rock and folk at the same time, and Bregović's later film music to hear what became of his folk instincts."],"bijelo-dugme-bitanga-i-princeza":["An album whose title means The Rascal and the Princess, showing Bijelo Dugme at the height of their power. The sound is fuller than on their debut: big, layered guitars and keyboards, orchestral strings on the ballads, and a more dramatic vocal performance by Željko Bebek. The record alternates hard-rock tracks with sweeping, melancholy ballads that became among the most sung songs in Yugoslavia.","By 1979 the band had survived a pause in activity, probably while Bregović completed his military service, and had returned more popular than ever. Yugoslav rock was diversifying, with punk and new wave rising in Zagreb and Belgrade, and Bijelo Dugme responded with a more polished, ambitious record. The country was nearing the end of the Tito era; he died the following year.","Listen to the ballads for their strings. Bregović was beginning to write for orchestra, and the string arrangements here anticipate the grand, cinematic style of his later film scores. Notice too how some melodies use repeated small phrases that rise step by step, a technique he would use again and again in his scores for Kusturica.","The album was a massive commercial success and is often named one of the greatest Yugoslav rock albums. Some critics in the new-wave scene saw Bijelo Dugme as old-fashioned arena rock, but the ballads proved lasting, and many are still sung across the former Yugoslav states. Its reputation has only grown with nostalgia for a shared musical culture.","Start with one of the ballads and listen for the moment the strings enter. Then hear a heavy track to compare the two faces of the band. Compare Bregović's Underground for how these orchestral instincts later grew, and Pink Floyd's The Wall from the same year for another epic, string-laden rock statement."],"bijelo-dugme-pljuni-i-zapjevaj":["An album whose title means Spit and Sing, My Yugoslavia, a deliberately pan-Yugoslav record mixing rock, folk melodies and Romani songs. Singer Alen Islamović leads a sound that blends synthesisers and rock rhythm with brass, folk instruments and choruses. The best-known track, Đurđevdan, is a Romani song for St George's Day, set to the same traditional melody that Bregović later arranged as Ederlezi.","By the mid-1980s Yugoslavia was in political and economic crisis after Tito's death, with nationalist tensions growing among its republics. Bregović designed the album as a plea for unity, drawing on music from across the country's peoples. The band recorded in this spirit of national togetherness just a few years before the federation collapsed into war in the early 1990s.","Listen to Đurđevdan and then to Bregović's Ederlezi: they share a melody, and hearing both shows how Bregović returned to the same Romani tune in rock and film settings. Notice also how the album places folk and Romani music side by side with rock, reflecting the multi-ethnic idea of Yugoslavia that the record tried to celebrate.","The album was a big success, and Đurđevdan became one of the most recognisable songs in the region. Its message of unity has been read since as poignant and naive, a final statement of a shared Yugoslav identity before the war. Critics have also raised the question of credit for the traditional Romani material, a recurring issue in Bregović's career.","Start with Đurđevdan and hear how the Romani melody sits within rock production. Then listen to Bregović's Ederlezi for the film version of the same tune. Compare Sezen Aksu's Düğün ve Cenaze for a later collaboration across borders, and Fanfare Ciocărlia for the brass tradition behind much of this music."],"mercan-dede-su":["An album whose title means Water, in which Mercan Dede blends the sound of Sufi music with electronic beats and ambient textures. The breathy, haunting ney flute, the frame drum bendir and the plucked kanun weave over programmed rhythms and synthesiser pads, and guest singers bring folk and devotional melodies. The tracks flow into one another, building from meditative calm to hypnotic, dance-floor intensity.","Dede, born Arkın Ilıcalı in Turkey, moved to Canada and worked as a DJ in Montreal while studying the ney and Sufi traditions. He built a career split between Istanbul and North America, becoming one of the leading figures of world-music electronica in the early 2000s. His concerts often featured whirling dervishes alongside DJ sets, a provocative mix of the sacred and the club.","Listen to how the ney's breath sound is treated. Rather than cleaning it up, Dede often lets the breath and air noise come forward in the mix, making the flute's human texture part of the electronic soundscape. Notice too how the rhythms often follow Sufi ceremonial metres rather than club patterns, gradually accelerating as in a Mevlevi ritual.","The album was praised internationally for its seamless blending of traditions and became one of Dede's best-known records. Some traditionalists disapproved of placing sacred music in club settings, while others welcomed it as a way to bring young listeners to Sufi sounds. Critics generally rank it among the most successful marriages of electronic music and a living tradition.","Listen with good headphones and follow the ney's breath through the electronic layers. Notice where the beat first enters. Compare Dede Efendi's Mevlevi Âyin for the sacred music Dede draws on, and Brian Eno's ambient albums for the electronic atmosphere he works within."],"mercan-dede-800":["An album named for the 800th anniversary of the birth of Rumi, the thirteenth-century mystic poet whose followers founded the Mevlevi order. Dede again blends ney, frame drums and Turkish strings with electronic beats, but the mood is more devotional and the textures more orchestral. Long tracks build from quiet ney meditations into pulsing grooves, with chanted voices and swelling strings at their peaks.","2007 marked Rumi's 800th birth anniversary, celebrated internationally, with UNESCO declaring a year in his honour. Dede, whose musical identity was built on the Mevlevi tradition, made the album as a tribute. By then he was a familiar figure at international festivals, and his fusion style had influenced a wave of Turkish and Middle Eastern electronic musicians.","Listen for references to the structure of the Mevlevi ceremony, which moves through distinct sections with a gradual increase in intensity. Dede mirrors this arc across the album, so that the whole record works like a modern version of the whirling ritual. The ney, the central instrument of the Mevlevi rite, is always the first or last voice heard.","The album was well received, especially for its ambition in shaping a whole record around Rumi and the ceremony. Some critics felt the formula had become predictable compared with Su, while others found it his most spiritually coherent work. It remains a reference point in the genre of Sufi-inspired electronica.","Listen from start to finish to hear the ritual arc. Focus on the opening ney and then notice the first beat. Compare Dede Efendi's Ferahfeza Âyin for an original Mevlevi ceremony and Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan for another Sufi tradition pushed into new forms."],"altin-gun-gece":["The second album by the Amsterdam band Altın Gün, its title meaning Night. It consists of Turkish folk songs and classics of Anatolian rock reimagined with a modern psychedelic sound: electric saz, wah guitar, vintage keyboards and a tight, groovy rhythm section. The vocals alternate between Merve Daşdemir and Erdinç Ecevit, and the songs combine dreamy atmosphere with dance-floor energy.","Altın Gün was formed by Dutch bassist Jasper Verhulst, a fan of Turkish psychedelic records who sought out Turkish musicians in the Netherlands to form a band. Their sound drew on the Anatolian rock revival of the 2000s and 2010s, when Western labels reissued Turkish records from the 1970s. Gece was nominated for a Grammy for Best World Music Album, raising their international profile.","Listen to how the band adapts traditional melodies to modern grooves without losing their original modal character. Some songs are arranged from folk material that Erkin Koray, Selda Bağcan or Barış Manço had recorded decades earlier, so the album is partly a tribute and partly a reinterpretation. The electric saz gives a continuity with those older records.","The album was praised for its warmth and energy, and its Grammy nomination brought new attention to Anatolian rock. Some critics questioned whether a mostly Dutch band could represent Turkish music authentically, but the band's Turkish members and their care with the material won over most skeptics. It is now seen as a key album of the Anatolian rock revival.","Listen first for the electric saz and the interplay between the two singers. Then compare Erkin Koray's Elektronik Türküler or Selda Bağcan's 1976 album to hear the originals, and Tinariwen's Amassakoul for another band turning a regional tradition into hypnotic guitar music."],"altin-gun-yol":["Altın Gün's third album, its title meaning Road, shifts their sound toward synth-pop and electronic textures while keeping Turkish folk songs at the core. Drum machines, vintage synthesisers and dreamy production replace some of the earlier rock drive, and the electric saz takes a smaller role. The album feels lighter and more hypnotic, with repetitive grooves and soft, layered vocals.","Made largely during the COVID-19 pandemic, when the band could not tour or record together in the usual way, Yol was assembled through home recording and remote collaboration. The band drew on Turkish synth-pop and disco of the 1980s as well as folk and Anatolian rock. It was released to wide acclaim and further established them as a major name in global psychedelic music.","Listen for the way the drum machines echo the rhythms of Turkish folk dances in electronic form. Many tracks use repetitive patterns that recall both 1980s synth-pop and the trance-like repetition of folk music. The production also hides traditional instruments deep in the mix, so you may hear a saz or bağlama only on close listening.","The album was praised for its bold change of direction, with critics comparing it to classic synth-pop while noting its Turkish roots. Some fans missed the rock energy of Gece, but most saw Yol as a creative evolution. It cemented the band's reputation as more than revivalists, capable of reinventing their source material.","Listen with headphones to catch the hidden saz and the layered vocals. Notice how the synthesisers replace guitar riffs. Compare Gece for the band's rock sound, Selda Bağcan for the original folk-protest energy that some songs recall, and Kraftwerk's Radio-Activity for the drum-machine pulse behind it."],"ades-the-tempest":["A three-act opera on Shakespeare's play, sung to a rhymed libretto by Meredith Oakes that compresses the verse into short couplets. Prospero's anger drives the first act in thick, clanging brass; Caliban gets the warmest lyrical music; the lovers Miranda and Ferdinand sing in plain, rising lines. The sound everyone remembers is Ariel, a coloratura soprano kept almost permanently in the stratosphere, flickering above the orchestra like a spirit who cannot come down to human pitch.","The Royal Opera House commissioned it, and Adès conducted the first performance at Covent Garden in February 2004, with Simon Keenlyside as Prospero. He was thirty-two, already notorious for the chamber opera Powder Her Face, and this was his first full-scale work for a major house. Shakespeare's play had tempted and defeated many composers before him; Adès chose to have the text rewritten in short rhymed lines rather than set the original verse.","Adès builds much of the score on chains of intervals that widen or narrow step by step, so harmonies seem to drift between tonal gravity and weightlessness. Listen for how Prospero's music changes once he chooses forgiveness: the clangour thins, and Adès saves some of his plainest, most consonant writing for that moment. Ariel's first entry sits higher than almost any operatic role written before it.","The premiere was a triumph and the opera quickly travelled to Copenhagen, Strasbourg, Santa Fe and, in Robert Lepage's 2012 production, the Metropolitan Opera. Some critics regretted the loss of Shakespeare's language in Oakes's doggerel-like couplets; others argued it freed the music to carry the poetry. It is now one of the most performed British operas of its century, and the standard by which Adès's later stage works are measured.","Begin with the final scene of act three, where Caliban is left alone on the island singing a tender farewell that the orchestra dissolves around him. Then go back to Ariel's first appearance to hear how extreme the writing is. The Metropolitan Opera video with Adès conducting is the clearest introduction; compare Britten's Turn of the Screw for another English opera haunted by spirits."],"ades-violin-concerto-concentric-paths":["A twenty-minute concerto in three movements titled Rings, Paths and Rounds. The first is a fast, glittering spin in which the violin circles at high speed above flickering strings and wind. The long central movement, Paths, is the heart: a slow, grave procession in which orchestral lines move at different speeds and keep colliding with the soloist. The finale is a brisk, almost dance-like round that clears the air with surprising lightness.","Adès wrote it for the violinist Anthony Marwood, a longtime collaborator, and it was first played at the Berlin Festival in September 2005 with the Chamber Orchestra of Europe, conducted by the composer. It came in the year after The Tempest, when Adès was turning from large stage forms back toward compact abstract structures, and it rapidly became one of the most programmed new concertos of the decade.","The title is literal. Throughout Paths, groups of instruments trace cycles of different lengths, like planets on orbits of different sizes, so that the same material recurs out of phase and alignments happen only occasionally. The climax of the movement is one of those rare alignments, when the cycles coincide and the orchestra suddenly speaks with one voice. The solo part is less a melody than a route through these circles.","Critics welcomed it as one of the most substantial violin concertos by a British composer in decades, and it was taken up by orchestras across Europe and America within a few seasons. Some listeners find the outer movements slight beside the central one; most regard that imbalance as the design, the brief frames throwing the slow movement into relief. Other violinists have since taken it into their repertoire beyond Marwood.","Go straight to Paths and listen to how the slow orchestral layers seem to walk past each other before locking together. Then hear the first movement as a whirling prelude to it. Marwood's recording with Adès is the reference. Compare Ligeti's Violin Concerto for another late-twentieth-century concerto that treats the orchestra as a machine of interlocking cycles."],"ades-tevot":["A single span of about twenty-two minutes for a very large orchestra. It begins in the highest register, with violins and piccolos swarming in tiny overlapping figures, and slowly descends through the orchestra as if a vast vessel were settling into water. Waves of sound rise and break; brass chorales surface and sink; the ending is a long, warm, tonal glow that feels like reaching land after a voyage.","The Berlin Philharmonic and Simon Rattle commissioned it, and they gave the premiere in Berlin in February 2007 before taking it on tour. Adès was by then a regular partner of Rattle, who had championed Asyla in Birmingham a decade earlier. The title is a Hebrew word that can mean both the bars of music and an ark, including Noah's ark, and Adès described the orchestra as a ship carrying its world.","Almost everything grows from cascading lines, scales that fall in many parts at slightly different speeds, so the orchestra is constantly moving downward while seeming to stand still: an acoustic illusion of endless descent. Listen near the end for a broad melody in the brass that sounds like a quoted hymn but is entirely Adès's own, built from the same falling patterns slowed almost to stillness.","The premiere was warmly received, though some critics found the work's lush ending close to film music and questioned its earnestness after the irony of Asyla. Others heard it as Adès's most generous orchestral statement. It has entered the repertoire of orchestras on both sides of the Atlantic, and Rattle's recording with Berlin is often cited as one of the finest documents of their partnership.","Listen to the first two minutes twice: once for the dizzying high texture and once to notice how it slowly sinks. Then let the last five minutes play without interruption; the arrival of the final warm sonority is the point of the whole piece. Rattle's Berlin recording is the obvious choice. Compare Sibelius's Tapiola for another single-movement orchestral voyage."],"ades-the-exterminating-angel":["A three-act opera after Luis Buñuel's 1962 film, in which guests at a grand dinner party find they cannot leave the drawing room, for no reason anyone can name. Over days they starve, quarrel, fall ill and turn savage. Adès writes for a huge ensemble cast, each singer with a distinct vocal type, and threads the score with the eerie gliding tone of the ondes martenot, an early electronic instrument that seems to voice the invisible barrier itself.","The Salzburg Festival gave the premiere in July 2016, co-commissioned with the Royal Opera House and the Metropolitan Opera, where it arrived in 2017. Tom Cairns wrote the libretto with Adès and directed. Adès had wanted to set the film since his student years; its blend of surrealism, social satire and claustrophobia matched the darker, more ritualistic turn his music had taken since The Tempest.","The opera singer Leticia, one of the guests, is written so high that at the Met her opening phrase was reported as the highest note ever sung on that stage. The trap is musical as well as dramatic: whole passages recur with small variations, mirroring the repeated shots in Buñuel's film. Listen for distorted Spanish dances and tolling bells, and for the way the guests finally escape by re-enacting a moment from the start.","Reviews were mixed to enthusiastic. Many praised the orchestral writing and the ensemble scenes as Adès's most ambitious music; some found the opera long and the characters hard to distinguish, a problem inherited from the film's crowd of guests. Its staging by three major houses within eighteen months made it one of the most visible new operas of the decade, and debate continues over whether its coldness is a flaw or the point.","Start with the opening of act one, where guests arrive twice and the orchestra hints that something is wrong. Then jump to the final act's release and its bleak coda. The Royal Opera recording conducted by Adès is the best guide. Watch Buñuel's film first if you can, and compare Sciarrino's Luci mie traditrici for another opera of aristocratic claustrophobia."],"unsuk-chin-piano-etudes":["A set of short, ferociously difficult studies for solo piano, each built on one idea pushed to its limit. In C hammers a single pitch area until it splinters into resonance; Sequenzen sends patterns racing up and down at shifting speeds; Scherzo ad libitum lets the pianist reshuffle fragments; Toccata is a relentless machine of repeated notes; Grains breaks the piano's sound into tiny rattling particles. Together they last about twenty-five minutes and sound like a laboratory of piano colour.","Chin studied with György Ligeti in Hamburg in the 1980s, and Ligeti's own piano Études, begun in 1985, set the challenge she was answering. She wrote the first of hers in 1995 while living in Berlin, adding to the set over the following years. Young pianists took them up quickly, and they became a calling card for virtuosos interested in the repertoire that followed Ligeti and in music that treats the piano as a source of colour.","Chin's background in electronic music shows in the way the études treat the piano as a sound source to be processed. In several of them, the sustaining pedal is used to build up clouds of overtones that the hands then filter, so you hear notes the pianist never plays. Listen in Grains for the way fast repeated figures blur into an almost electronic granular texture.","The études were initially known only to specialists, but recordings and competition use have made them among the most performed new piano works of their generation. Some critics hear them as too dependent on Ligeti's model; others note that Chin's interest in timbre rather than rhythmic illusion gives them a separate identity. They are now regularly taught in conservatories as a bridge between Ligeti and later piano writing.","Start with the Toccata for sheer energy, then listen to Grains with headphones to catch the fizz of overtones. Play one of Ligeti's études alongside, perhaps L'escalier du diable, and notice how Chin cares less about rhythmic paradox and more about the colour of the sound. A recording that pairs Chin with Ligeti makes the ideal starting point."],"unsuk-chin-su":["A single-movement concerto for the sheng, the Chinese mouth organ of bamboo pipes, and orchestra. The sheng can sustain clusters of notes as long as the player breathes, inhaling or exhaling, so the piece opens in a shimmering haze in which the soloist and the strings are almost indistinguishable. Gradually the sheng asserts itself with sharp, reedy chords and fast pulsing figures, and the orchestra answers with glassy percussion and bright wind.","Chin wrote it for the sheng virtuoso Wu Wei, whose playing had transformed the instrument's profile in European new music. It was premiered in 2009 and quickly taken up by orchestras in Europe and Asia. Chin was then composer-in-residence with the Seoul Philharmonic and artistic director of its new-music series, and her interest in East Asian instruments within a Western orchestra was an explicit part of her programme.","The sheng was one of the instruments that, according to tradition, inspired the invention of the harmonium and accordion in Europe, so the concerto quietly closes a historical circle. Chin exploits the instrument's ability to sound while breathing in, so there are no breaks for air. Listen for passages where orchestral strings imitate the sheng's breathing cluster so closely that you lose track of where the soloist is.","Critics praised it as one of the few concertos for an Asian instrument that avoids decorative exoticism, treating the sheng as a modern instrument rather than a folk colour. Some found its middle stretches diffuse. Wu Wei's performances established it, and it is now one of the standard works in a growing repertoire for the sheng in symphonic settings.","Listen first for the opening haze and try to tell where the sheng ends and the orchestra begins. Then follow the soloist as it hardens into rhythmic chords. Wu Wei's recording with the Seoul Philharmonic is the obvious choice. Compare Chin's Violin Concerto for her treatment of a Western solo instrument, and Saariaho's Petals for another composer working with breath-like sustained sound."],"caroline-shaw-valencia":["A six-minute string quartet about an orange, specifically the Valencia variety. It begins with bright, pulsing chords, then breaks into scrubbing, glinting textures that suggest peeling, the spray of juice, the segments falling apart. Repeated rhythmic cells pass around the quartet like a game; by the end, the music has distilled to a gentle, sweet harmony that seems to hang in the air like a scent. It is direct, playful and surprisingly tender.","Shaw wrote it in 2012, the year she was finishing the Partita for 8 Voices that would win her the Pulitzer the next year. She plays violin and wrote it close to the instrument. The Attacca Quartet later made it part of their album Orange, devoted entirely to Shaw's quartets, which won the Grammy for Best Chamber Music Performance; the album title nods to this piece.","Shaw says the piece tries to capture the inside of an orange, its cells and structure, rather than its colour. Listen for the way the quartet splits into tiny repeated figures, like the vesicles inside each segment, that fit together into larger chords. The bowing techniques change constantly, pressure, position and speed, so the same harmony keeps changing colour.","It has become one of the most performed new string quartet pieces of its decade, programmed by student and professional quartets alike. Critics praise its economy and charm; a few find Shaw's quartets slight beside her larger works. Its success, with the rest of the Orange album, helped establish her as a chamber composer and not only a vocal one.","Put on the Attacca Quartet recording and simply follow the energy: the opening pulse, the splintering, the final calm. Then listen again for the different bowing sounds. Compare her Entr'acte, another quartet that uses old forms and textures in new ways, and Reich's Different Trains for another quartet built on pulse."],"caroline-shaw-narrow-sea":["A thirty-minute song cycle for soprano, piano and percussion quartet, built from texts in The Sacred Harp, the nineteenth-century American shape-note hymnal. Its sections drift through images of crossing water to a promised land. The percussion is gentle and resonant, metal and wood bowed, struck and rubbed, sometimes as soft as water. The voice moves between plain hymn-like melody and floating lines, while the piano provides quiet harmonic anchoring.","Shaw wrote it for the soprano Dawn Upshaw, the pianist Gilbert Kalish and Sō Percussion, who first performed it and recorded it for Nonesuch in 2021. It grew out of Shaw's long collaboration with Sō Percussion and her fascination with shape-note singing, a tradition of loud, open congregational hymnody still practiced in the American South and elsewhere.","The 'narrow sea' of the title comes from the hymn imagery of death as a short stretch of water between this life and the next. Listen for fragments that sound like actual shape-note tunes surfacing in the vocal line, transformed into quiet solos rather than the full-throated congregational sound of their origin, where dozens of singers face each other in a hollow square and sing at full volume.","Critics welcomed it as one of Shaw's most personal works and praised Upshaw's late-career performance, the recording drawing wide attention. Some listeners found its tranquillity monotonous over thirty minutes; others heard it as deliberately meditative. Its use of Sacred Harp material places it in a lineage running from Ives and Copland to more recent American explorations of vernacular sacred song.","Listen to it in one sitting with the lights low. Notice first the percussion colours, then the way the voice moves between hymn and murmur. If you can, hear an actual Sacred Harp singing afterwards to feel the contrast. The Nonesuch recording with Upshaw, Kalish and Sō Percussion is the definitive version. Compare Crumb's Ancient Voices of Children."],"julia-wolfe-lad":["Fourteen minutes for nine Highland bagpipes, often performed by a single piper playing live over a recording of the other eight. It starts with the drones settling in, then the chanters begin to overlap in tightly spaced, buzzing intervals, building a dense, roaring wall of sound that pulses and wavers. The effect is less a march than a slowly churning landscape, as raw and loud as any rock band.","Wolfe wrote it in 1995 for the piper Matt Welch, during the years when she, Michael Gordon and David Lang were running the Bang on a Can festival in New York and seeking a sound between minimalism and rock. Her interest in loud, physical music led her to the bagpipe, an instrument almost absent from concert music, with its fixed volume and unstoppable drone.","Because bagpipes cannot get softer or louder, Wolfe creates dynamics through density alone: the more pipes playing in close intervals, the more the sound seems to swell. Listen for the slow beating that arises when two chanters sit almost, but not quite, on the same pitch; it pulses like a heartbeat. The instrument's long association with military laments and funerals gives the roar an undertone of mourning.","The piece became something of a cult favourite, valued for its sheer acoustic force and as an emblem of Bang on a Can's commitment to unusual forces. Critics who found it one-dimensional were outweighed by those who heard in it a powerful combination of folk instrument and post-minimalist process. It is a regular programme choice when pipers explore contemporary repertoire.","Play it loud and let the wall of sound wash over you. Notice the slow beating between the pipes, almost like a heartbeat. Compare it with Wolfe's Cruel Sister for her use of a folk source, and with Reich's Drumming for another minimalist work built on accumulation. Seek a recording with a single piper over track for clarity."],"julia-wolfe-cruel-sister":["Half an hour for string orchestra, retelling an old British ballad in four connected sections. Two sisters walk by the sea; the elder pushes the younger in to drown out of jealousy; a minstrel makes a harp from the drowned girl's bones, and the harp sings out the murder. Wolfe never quotes the tune but builds the story from repeating rhythmic patterns, rising, swirling and churning, until the strings sound like waves, wind and finally the eerie voice of the bone-harp.","The Munich Chamber Orchestra commissioned it, and they premiered it in 2004. Wolfe had been exploring American and British folk sources and stories for years. The ballad, known as The Twa Sisters, survives in many versions throughout the Anglo-Celtic world, and Wolfe's version draws on the folk revival recordings of the 1960s as well as the old texts.","The ballad's refrain in many versions is a nonsense line repeated after each verse. Wolfe translates that recurrence into orchestral cells that return again and again, like the refrain heard across centuries. Listen for the section where the strings slide in glissandi as the younger sister drowns: the most graphic moment in the piece, and the turning point toward the bone-harp.","Reviewers praised its narrative energy and its demonstration that post-minimalist techniques could carry a story. Some found the length challenging. It has become one of Wolfe's most performed orchestral works and is often paired with her Fuel. Critics now see it as a key step toward the documentary oratorios, Steel Hammer and Anthracite Fields, that followed.","Read the ballad first, perhaps in a version sung by a folk revival singer, then follow the story through the music. Listen for the transformation from smooth waves to violent glissandi. The Ensemble Resonanz recording is excellent. Compare it with the traditional Twa Sisters to see how Wolfe turns a refrain into a structure."],"mazzoli-sinfonia-for-orbiting-spheres":["About eleven minutes of slowly turning orchestral sound. Strings, wind and percussion circle around a central harmonic pole, rising and falling in long arcs, while harmonicas played by orchestral musicians add a wheezing, breathy glow. The music feels like planets on elliptical orbits: no clear beginning or end, only waves that recur at different speeds and occasionally align in a bright, consonant swell.","Mazzoli first wrote it for chamber orchestra in 2014, then expanded it for full orchestra in 2016. Her music bridges the concert hall and the indie-rock scene of Brooklyn, where she also leads the band Victoire, and she was soon to become composer-in-residence with the Chicago Symphony Orchestra. The image of the solar system, she said, was a way to write a piece that never stops moving but never quite arrives.","Listen for the harmonicas, which most audiences do not notice until halfway through: they are handed to string or wind players and blown softly to create a reedy, slightly out-of-tune halo. The piece is a kind of passacaglia, built on a repeating chord pattern that keeps returning in new orchestral clothing, like a planet returning to the same place in its orbit.","Critics praised its warmth and orchestral imagination, and it has become one of Mazzoli's most performed orchestral works, played by major American orchestras. Some find its gentle surface too comfortable; others hear in its patient circles a rare confidence. It belongs to the run of work that led to Mazzoli's commission from the Metropolitan Opera.","Listen with headphones to catch the harmonica glow and the overlapping orbits. Notice how the piece seems to slow time down without ever stopping. Compare Ligeti's Lontano for another orchestral work of slowly shifting harmonies, and Adès's Tevot for an orchestral voyage. The Chicago Symphony performances are the reference."],"mazzoli-song-from-the-uproar":["A seventy-five-minute chamber opera about the Swiss explorer Isabelle Eberhardt, who dressed as a man, travelled across North Africa, converted to Islam and died in a flash flood at twenty-seven. The mezzo-soprano lead is surrounded by a vocal quintet acting as a chorus of inner voices, while a small ensemble of strings, piano, guitar, percussion and electronics creates layered, dreamlike textures. Projected films echo the desert landscapes and Eberhardt's journals.","Beth Morrison Projects produced the premiere in New York in 2012, part of a wave of new chamber operas emerging from New York's indie music scene. Mazzoli wrote the libretto with Royce Vavrek, who would later write her larger operas. She based it on Eberhardt's diaries and the ambiguities of her life, a woman crossing borders of gender, religion and nation decades before such stories were widely told.","The opera's title comes from Eberhardt's own journals, where she describes seeking a kind of music within the chaos of her life. Listen for the electric guitar and the softly droning electronics that mark the desert scenes, and for the way the chorus voices sometimes emerge from the ensemble itself. The final flood is written not as catastrophe but as a dissolving.","Critics praised its fusion of indie rock sound and operatic narrative, though some found the dramatic structure loose. It has been revived several times and recorded, and it marked Mazzoli as one of the leading voices of the new American chamber opera movement. Its success led directly to commissions for Breaking the Waves and the Metropolitan Opera.","Listen first to the opening, where the guitar and voices set a hazy, unmoored mood, then follow Eberhardt's journey to the final flood. The studio recording of the original production is the best guide. Compare it with Saariaho's L'Amour de loin, another opera about longing across distance, and with Reid's p r i s m for another new American chamber opera on a woman's inner life."],"thorvaldsdottir-metacosmos":["Thirteen minutes of vast, slowly shifting orchestral sound. It begins with low rumbling and the breath of brass instruments without pitch, then grows into huge sustained chords that swell and recede like a body breathing. Strings scrape and glide, percussion adds metallic shimmer, and at the climax the whole orchestra seems to be falling into a gravitational pull before the sound finally drains away into silence.","The New York Philharmonic commissioned it, and Esa-Pekka Salonen conducted the premiere in April 2018. Thorvaldsdóttir, who grew up in Iceland, had been gaining international attention since Aeriality. She described Metacosmos as inspired by the idea of falling into a black hole: the attraction, the fear and the beauty of an abyss, and the balance between chaos and order.","Thorvaldsdóttir composes from detailed hand-drawn sketches that look like landscapes, mapping density and texture before notes. Listen for the moment roughly two-thirds through when a soft, consonant chord emerges in the strings beneath the turmoil; it is the closest the piece gets to a melody, and it is quickly swallowed, as if by gravity.","Critics praised its scale and atmosphere, and it was quickly taken up by orchestras in Europe and America. Some find Thorvaldsdóttir's music static; supporters hear it as a deliberate rejection of conventional development in favour of a sense of geological time. Metacosmos is now one of the most frequently performed orchestral works by a living Nordic composer.","Listen in the dark and at a good volume, so the low rumbles and quiet breaths register. Notice how the orchestra behaves like a single organism. Compare her Aeriality for an earlier exploration of the same approach, and Ligeti's Atmosphères for the ancestor of this kind of orchestral cloud. The Iceland Symphony recording is the reference."],"thorvaldsdottir-catamorphosis":["Twenty minutes of slowly gathering orchestral weather. Low strings and brass build long, shifting chords; breathy noise from wind and percussion swirls around them; occasional shafts of light appear in the high strings, only to be overwhelmed. The music moves in vast arcs, growing denser and more unstable, until a final quiet passage hangs suspended, as if the world were waiting to see whether it would survive.","The Berlin Philharmonic co-commissioned it with other orchestras, and Kirill Petrenko conducted the premiere in Berlin in January 2021, in a hall emptied by pandemic restrictions and streamed online. Thorvaldsdóttir described it as being about the fragile relationship between humanity and the planet: the forces that hold the earth together and the ones that are pulling it apart. It came at a moment when concert life itself had nearly stopped.","The title combines Greek roots for 'down' and 'form' and suggests a transformation toward a lesser state, a decline. Listen for a rising figure in the strings that returns several times; each time it reaches a little less high, as if the energy for ascent were running out. The final pages are among the quietest she has written for orchestra.","The premiere drew wide attention despite the absence of an audience, and critics praised it as her most powerful orchestral statement. Some found its environmental programme too explicit; others felt it was deeply felt rather than didactic. Performances by other orchestras quickly followed, and it confirmed Thorvaldsdóttir's position as one of the leading orchestral composers of her generation.","Listen to it in one sitting and let the arcs build. Pay attention to the rising string figure and its gradual exhaustion. Compare Metacosmos for the same composer's vision of cosmic gravity, and Sibelius's Tapiola for an earlier Nordic orchestral work about elemental forces. The Iceland Symphony Orchestra's recording is an excellent guide to the textures."],"abrahamsen-let-me-tell-you":["A half-hour cycle for soprano and orchestra in three parts, in which Ophelia from Hamlet tells her own story. The orchestra is luminous and fragile: high strings, celesta, soft brass, and percussion brushed with paper and fingers. The voice moves between stammering, repeated syllables and long, floating lines. In the last part, Ophelia walks out into a snowy landscape, and the music dissolves into whispering white noise and tiny glints of sound.","Abrahamsen wrote it for the Canadian soprano Barbara Hannigan, who premiered it with the Berlin Philharmonic and Andris Nelsons in December 2013. The text is by the writer Paul Griffiths, from his 2008 novel of the same name, in which Ophelia is allowed to use only the words she speaks in Shakespeare's play. The piece won the Grawemeyer Award in 2016 and quickly became Abrahamsen's best-known work.","Because Griffiths restricted Ophelia's vocabulary to her lines in Hamlet, her sentences have an oddly stilted, reaching quality; Abrahamsen mirrors this by having her stutter and repeat words as if searching for meaning. Listen in the last section for the sound of snow: paper rustled, brushes on drums, high harmonics, the closest music gets to silence.","Critics greeted it with unusual unanimity, calling it one of the finest vocal works of its century. Hannigan's performances and her recording with the Bavarian Radio Symphony made it famous. Some find its fragility precious; most hear it as a perfect fusion of text, voice and orchestra. It is now one of the most performed new works for soprano and orchestra.","Read a few of Ophelia's lines from Hamlet first, then listen to the opening to hear how the singer stumbles over them. Let the final section play in silence. Hannigan's recording is essential. Compare Kurtág's Kafka Fragments for another fragmented vocal work, and Abrahamsen's Schnee for the snow imagery in purely instrumental form."],"abrahamsen-schnee":["Fifty-five minutes of ten canons for nine players, arranged in pairs, each pair exploring a different type of musical repetition. The sound is hushed and glacial: two pianos, strings in muted harmonics, wind instruments breathing softly, all moving in slow, calm patterns like snow drifting across a frozen landscape. The pairs grow shorter and more fragile as the work proceeds, until a final pair that seems almost to melt away into silence.","Abrahamsen wrote it after a long creative silence in the 1990s, during which he arranged other composers' music rather than writing his own. It premiered in Germany in 2008 and was soon recognised as a turning point in his career. The idea came from his fascination with canons and with the image of snow, which he associated with childhood in Denmark and the stillness of winter.","The canons are not strict imitations but explore different ways of repeating material: forwards, backwards, at different speeds, with different instruments. Listen for the moments between canons, when the players perform short intermezzos with paper rustling and soft bowing. One piano is tuned a quarter-tone lower than the other, giving the whole piece a subtle, uncanny glow.","Critics hailed it as one of the major chamber works of the century, and the Ensemble Recherche recording brought it to wide attention. Some find its length and stasis demanding; admirers hear in it a profound meditation on time and repetition. It established Abrahamsen as a major voice, paving the way for let me tell you.","Listen to the first pair of canons, then step away and return to another pair. The piece rewards patient, unhurried attention. Notice how the slightly mistuned pianos create a shimmer. Compare Feldman's Triadic Memories for another work of extended quiet repetition, and Abrahamsen's let me tell you for the snow imagery in vocal form."]});